HT_DATA.load("detail", {"AJdEqssNZ-U":{"wy":"A perennial recommendation for anyone learning game feel and juice, praised for showing concrete steps that turn a bland prototype into a satisfying game.","q":{"x":"","i":39642436,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":45211145,"author":"auto","month":"2025-09","story":"DOOMscrolling: The Game","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Teaches adding juice to a game's core loop.","quote":"One of my favorite game dev talks of all time would be perfect for you to watch and iterate with","text":"Once I gave this sometime I actually enjoyed the concept quite a bit. I think it's a novel twist on the bullet hell genre, and I'm a sucker for single button/input style games. I'm playing on a mouse, specifically a Logitech G502 that I can \"unlock\" the screen wheel to be almost frictionless, which to me feels like the right meta for a game like this. Couple constructive criticisms: - Is there a pause button that I'm missing? If not, add one, I want to actually play this more but I need to be able to take breaks - The beginning is too easy. I'm biased as I like difficult games that encourage mastery, but I found myself dying early on runs because I was getting impatient with how slow the early progression is - I haven't played enough yet to know whether monsters eventually come from the top, but eventually they should, it would greatly increase the difficulty curve - Hitboxes feel a little meh, and don't seem to match the sprites - If there's sound, it wasn't working for me, which leads to... - Pump up the juice. You've got a great core loop, but you need the juice. One of my favorite game dev talks of all time would be perfect for you to watch and iterate with ( [VIDEO] ) - Monster diversity. Again, maybe I didn't play long enough yet to get to newer monsters, but from what I can tell the only diversity is their looks, health, and approach angle. Monsters that are faster and incentivize different movement, or that shoot back at you, or blow up after shooting so you have to a"},{"id":44947943,"author":"abetusk","month":"2025-08","story":"Starting game development in JavaScript with no experience","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fills the gap between finished tutorial and a game that looks polished.","quote":"was what helped fill in the steps to \"making the owl","text":"For me, the \"owl\" was all the polish and game feel. \"The art of screenshake\" by Jan Nijman of Vlambeer [0] was what helped fill in the steps to \"making the owl\". I think most people with reasonable know-how can make a basic game, whether it's asteroids, flappy bird, breakout, etc. For me, I never understood how to get past the \"finished the tutorial\" to \"looks like an actual game\". Screenshake, aka game feel/polish/production effects, was what made it gel. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39642436,"author":"abetusk","month":"2024-03","story":"How do you make an addictive video game?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Walks through adding effects to a basic shooter until it feels fun.","quote":"one of the best videos I've seen on this is \"The art of screenshake\" by J. Nijman of Vlambeer","text":"Ultimately pretty shallow. This is more an exploration of how it feels to make a \"sticky\" game rather than how to actually make a sticky game. I certainly don't have any expertise or deep insight but I will say that one of the best videos I've seen on this is \"The art of screenshake\" by J. Nijman of Vlambeer [0]. There, he walks through what's a very basic 2d sidescroller shooter and adds in effects until it looks like an actual fun game. The idea being that layering feedback and effects so that every aspect of the game becomes more fun to play. It sounds like this podcast was focusing more on Hearthstone and Marvel Snap, both games which I haven't played but are more strategy and luck oriented than the kind of games Vlambeer makes. I think one of the insights there is that there's a non-commutative aspect of the game that allows for different strategies based on context. For example, A > B, B > C but C > A, like in rock paper scissors, or some of its generalizations [1]. I think this is what Magic the Gathering did with each of the colors. Maybe the keyword here is \"mixed strategies\"? Nijman's video highlights, to me, the importance of polish and how we basically understand that many core mechanics are fun and so don't need to be innovative if you add in enough polish or support around it. One other thing I'll mention is that A. Bruce who developed Antichamber had a GDC talk about his process of development [2]. One of the interesting points he made was that he got it in fro"},{"id":34045254,"author":"ncallaway","month":"2022-12","story":"Twitter Suspends PG's Account","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows the high ceiling for polish and feel in interactions","quote":"I absolutely love their \"art of screenshake\" lecture.","text":"> vlambeer, if anyone knows them I absolutely love their \"art of screenshake\" lecture. [VIDEO] It really shows how there's a very high ceiling for polish and _feel_ when it comes to interactions. \"Screenshake\" is something that I'm sure a lot of people might look at and say: \"oh, that would take 15 minutes to add\", and if you're just talking about shaking the screen a bit, absolutely! But if you want to make the screenshake _really_ communicate a feeling and an emotion, there's so much more you can do."},{"id":43208500,"author":"modernerd","month":"2025-02","story":"Ask HN: Recommend resources that helped your game dev journey?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows execution and polish make games fun; slow start but worth it.","quote":"The art of screenshake (starts a little slow, but stick with it!)","text":"I don't think books and tutorials help that much. Playing and making games — a lot of games — might help you grow more as a designer than reading and watching YouTube. 1. Do master studies. Take a tiny environment or mechanic of a game you love and reproduce it for education, knowing you'll throw it away. Do this often. Get faster at it. 2. Ask people what their favourite game is. Play it. If it's fun to you, why? If it doesn't resonate with you, why? 3. Internalise the idea that ideas don't matter that much, execution is what matters. Good game designers can make otherwise boring games feel fun to play. (See \"the art of screenshake\" below.) 4. Make a lot of games. Make them small enough to finish. You don't have to release them all, but you should watch other people play them sometimes. 5. Don't listen to people who tell you not to watch YouTube for game design ideas, there are plenty of great videos out there that have positively changed how I think about game design: The art of screenshake (starts a little slow, but stick with it!): [VIDEO] Designing to reveal the nature of the universe: [LINK] Game design is a search algorithm: [LINK]"},{"id":35287672,"author":"doomlaser","month":"2023-03","story":"Juice","stance":"rec","why":"Classic talk on juice and screenshake","quote":"Another classic talk on the same topic comes from Jan Willem Nijman in 2013","text":"I believe the term comes from the game development sphere originally. Martin Jonasson & Petri Purho (creator of Noita and Crayon Physics Deluxe ) gave a famous talk about it in 2010, Juice it or lose it : [LINK] Another classic talk on the same topic comes from Jan Willem Nijman in 2013, The art of screenshake : [VIDEO] It's nice to see these terms being adopted by the wider software development industry, though I doubt we'll be seeing much screenshake on websites or utility apps any time soon."},{"id":32383293,"author":"abetusk","month":"2022-08","story":"In-browser retro-futuristic tank game, open source","stance":"rec","why":"Most enlightening video on adding juice to games","quote":"the most enlightening one I found was from Nijman of Vlambeer","text":"Almost completely unrelated but I have long wanted an in-browser/real-time/javascript/FOSS 'synthwave screensaver' in the style that you sometimes see on YouTube [0]. Maybe I'll use your code as a baseline for it if I get the motivation. In terms of actual feedback, the game itself has a lot going for it. I would, at the very least, add some 'juice' to it, which would probably add a lot. There are many videos on it but the most enlightening one I found was from Nijman of Vlambeer [1]. You might need to take care with literal screenshake as it's 3d but there are many other suggestions that are relevant (kick back from firing, enemy blood/armor splatter, detritus persistence, small variations in bullet direction, etc.). [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32481291,"author":"yetihehe","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: How do I get started to game devevelopment/design?","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested for making bland games more engaging","quote":"Art of screenshake","text":"> I don't consider myself a person that good at writing stories (or anything other than programming) Read a book [LINK] > often find my games bland and boring pretty fast. Art of screenshake [VIDEO]"},{"id":12273400,"author":"jean_claude","month":"2016-08","story":"How to Prototype a Game in Under 7 Days (2005)","stance":"rec","why":"Iterative process of improving game mechanics and feel","quote":"talks about it through a simple iterative process of improving game mechanics","text":"The core essence of a video game is the game feel (which not everyone likes, because it's ill-defined). How does a player interact with a game? Via game mechanics. No amount of polish is going to make up for horrible game mechanics. Jan Willem (of Vlambeer), who hates the phrase \"game feel\", talks about it through a simple iterative process of improving game mechanics. See: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11804468,"author":"polm23","month":"2016-05","story":"I Have Created 50 Games in 2014","stance":"rec","why":"Many small improvements that make games feel good","quote":"Here's Jan Willem Nijman of Vlambeer discussing a large number of small improvements that make games feel good","text":"Here's Ryan Clark (Crypt of the NecroDancer) writing about what makes a game commercially viable: [LINK] Here's Jan Willem Nijman of Vlambeer discussing a large number of small improvements that make games feel good: [VIDEO] Here's Rami Ismail, also of Vlambeer, explaining how making a game a week helps you develop an intuition for some parts of game making: [LINK] Here's a talk by Sid Meier (Civilization) about Interesting Decisions: [LINK]"},{"id":7625748,"author":"Vermeulen","month":"2014-04","story":"Game Mechanic Explorer – examples for game mechanics, algorithms, and effects","stance":"rec","why":"Vlambeer's demo of game effects in the screenshake talk","quote":"Another great one is this Aztez post on fighting game effects","text":"I love these interactive demos of game dev techniques. Another great one is this Aztez post on fighting game effects - [LINK] - and Vlambeer's demo in their 'art of screenshake' talk [VIDEO]"},{"id":7037658,"author":"drhayes9","month":"2014-01","story":"Ask HN: I'm building one HTML5 game per week, need feedback","stance":"rec","why":"Making action games feel more responsive and fun","quote":"it's a bunch of interesting points on how to make action games feel more responsive and fun","text":"Great games! Great start! Here's a talk you might be interested in from one of the guys who made \"Ridiculous Fishing\" on the Art of Screenshake: [VIDEO] Basically, it's a bunch of interesting points on how to make action games feel more responsive and fun."},{"id":32482549,"author":"badpun","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: How do I get started to game devevelopment/design?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Adding screenshake etc. for boring games is essentially putting lipstick on a pig","text":"> Art of screenshake [VIDEO] I'm actually opposed to that. Adding screenshake etc. for boring games is essentially putting lipstick on a pig, i.e. adding a (short-lived) illusion that the game is fun while it actually isn't. The best game designs don't require any screenshake to captivate players for a long time. I mean, I'm not opposed to adding visual gimmicks during the polish phase. It should be used as a way to rescue a bad game though."}],"r":[["N4UFC0y1tY0",1],["HPqGaIMVuLs",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["YUU7_BthBWM",1],["S-Xm7s9eGxU",1],["re96UgMk6GQ",1],["UF8uR6Z6KLc",1],["lIFE7h3m40U",1],["72y2EC5fkcE",1],["0ifChJ0nJfM",1],["sPhpelUfu8Q",1],["9XkHBmE-N34",1],["OkmNXy7er84",1],["sWblpsLZ-O8",1]]},"1Jwo5qc78QU":{"wy":"The default link in copyright and takedown threads, recommended as an excellent, comprehensive explanation of why automated enforcement and the law work as they do.","q":{"x":"Tom Scott explains the situation and the solution best.","i":25090236,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":27660215,"author":"Laremere","month":"2021-06","story":"Dear Google: Public domain compositions exist","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains aspects of YouTube's copyright system most commenters miss.","quote":"I recommend the excellent video by Youtuber Tom Scott","text":"I recommend the excellent video by Youtuber Tom Scott \"YouTube's Copyright System Isn't Broken. The World's Is.\" [VIDEO] While I agree with the OP in broad strokes (they clearly have the right to post and profit off their performance), I find that the vast majority of comments on Youtube's copyright claim system fail to even acknowledge major aspects of the problem. Perhaps Youtube's practical monopoly does it a disservice here, and it's hard to separate \"big self publishing video platform\" problems with Youtube specific problems. Without a system like YT currently has, it would get absolutely sued into oblivion by rightful copyright complaints. This is in many ways is a problem of copyright law not being designed with modern technology in mind. I think a good first step would be a way for copyright owners to be punished for broadly overreaching with their claims."},{"id":27005230,"author":"0xTJ","month":"2021-05","story":"Piano teacher gets copyright claim for Beethoven’s Moonlight Sonata [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Really great, hour-long video on copyright claims.","quote":"A really great video (if you're willing to spend the better part of an hour watching it)","text":"A really great video (if you're willing to spend the better part of an hour watching it) about this is YouTube's Copyright System Isn't Broken. The World's Is.[1] by Tom Scott. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25090236,"author":"jimbob45","month":"2020-11","story":"A Modest Proposal in the New Age of DMCA Takedown Aggression","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the situation and proposes shorter copyright.","quote":"Tom Scott explains the situation and the solution best.","text":"[VIDEO] Tom Scott explains the situation and the solution best. Just shorten copyright to 20 years (instead of life + 70 years). .......that said, we did just strong-arm the world into matching US copyright law and I’m not sure we could do that now even if we wanted to."},{"id":23331904,"author":"peterkos","month":"2020-05","story":"Trump threatens to 'close' down social media platforms","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent video on automated copyright checking.","quote":"Tom Scott did an excellent video on the automation of the copyright system on YouTube","text":"> youtube algo's screwing up content creator A slightly different problem, but Tom Scott did an excellent video on the automation of the copyright system on YouTube: [VIDEO] In summary, auto copyright checking solves Most copyright issues, to a degree that no other solution can really provide. And while there are a small (yet very unfair) number of false positives, the benefit that the system as a whole allows far outpaces that downtrend: for YouTube to exist! With regards to fact checking, I'd be more interested to hear how many people who read those kind of \"fact flags\" actually change their opinion in an easy case (flat earth, climate change, etc.). Honestly, the problem of \"true truth\" might never be solved, but so will the cause of incompetence never disappear."},{"id":22718892,"author":"Hokusai","month":"2020-03","story":"Internet Archive’s National Emergency Library Harms Authors","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why copyright law is outdated.","quote":"Great video about Copyright by Tom Scott","text":"> many unintuitive parts I think a more concrete term for unintuitive is outdated. The use cases for Copyright law were defined a century ago when society and technology was different. It is difficult for current citizens to understand copyright law because has nothing to do with their own day to day experiences and needs. Great video about Copyright by Tom Scott: [VIDEO] Too long didn't see: Copyright is broken needs to be fixed in a way that fits the current situation and needs, but, it is going to be damn hard to do."},{"id":39840731,"author":"HaZeust","month":"2024-03","story":"Thoughts on Vision Pro","stance":"rec","why":"Great video arguing copyright needs a revamp.","quote":"Tom Scott did a great video on copyright [1] - it's long overdue to revamp.","text":"Always has been. Tom Scott did a great video on copyright [1] - it's long overdue to revamp. 1 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":38747068,"author":"happytiger","month":"2023-12","story":"AI companies required to disclose copyrighted training data under new bill","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how flawed copyright enforcement is on YouTube.","quote":"Tom Scott did a piece on it that’s worth watching","text":"The copyright system has been broken for a long time. AI is just making the system’s inadequacy more obvious. Just look at YouTube copyright strike and understand how screwed up the enforcement of a law like this is going to be. Tom Scott did a piece on it that’s worth watching: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27007326,"author":"I_Byte","month":"2021-05","story":"GitHub repository for Sedgewick's Algorithms is taken down","stance":"rec","why":"Shows why companies are legally required to act on takedowns.","quote":"I recommended checking out Tom Scott’s video on DMCA (0).","text":"I recommended checking out Tom Scott’s video on DMCA (0). It’s 40 minutes long and rather YouTube centric however it paints a very clear picture as to why these companies are engaging in a seemingly broken practice. It’s not because they want to, it’s because they are legally required to do so. (0) - [VIDEO]"},{"id":24910253,"author":"ehsankia","month":"2020-10","story":"RIAA’s YouTube-dl takedown ticks off developers and GitHub’s CEO","stance":"rec","why":"Comprehensive video on the DMCA problem.","quote":"I do like Tom Scott's comprehensive video on the subject","text":"Absolutely, especially with all the mess on Twitch recently, and with Youtube for the past few years, it's very clear that DMCA needs to be updated. I do like Tom Scott's comprehensive video on the subject [VIDEO]"},{"id":24574903,"author":"gfody","month":"2020-09","story":"They're deleting my channel, but they don't know why? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Informative summary of YouTube and copyright, with alternatives to channel shutdowns.","quote":"Tom Scott's very informative summary of all things youtube and copyright","text":"according to Tom Scott's very informative summary of all things youtube and copyright [1] there are seemingly reasonable alternatives to suddenly shutting down a whole channel - it seems like we're not getting the whole story as to why those were rejected or aren't being considered in this case? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22917510,"author":"nayuki","month":"2020-04","story":"Copying is not theft, but censorship is (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the copyright situation and YouTube's Content ID.","quote":"Tom Scott explains the copyright situation and YouTube's Content ID","text":"Relevant - Tom Scott explains the copyright situation and YouTube's Content ID: [VIDEO] (42 minutes)"},{"id":23012818,"author":"anonymfus","month":"2020-04","story":"With questionable copyright claim, Jay-Z orders deepfake parodies off YouTube","stance":"rec","why":"Covers parody and common copyright misconceptions.","quote":"There is a good Tom Scott's video about this distinction and other common misconceptions about copyright","text":"Yes but an imitation is not necessary and usually a parody. There is a good Tom Scott's video about this distinction and other common misconceptions about copyright: [VIDEO] (42 interesting minutes)"},{"id":22683625,"author":"oakesm9","month":"2020-03","story":"I got my file from Clearview AI","stance":"rec","why":"Content ID as an imperfect workaround for broken copyright.","quote":"ContentID is a workaround for a broken copyright system.","text":"Related Tom Scott video [VIDEO] tl;dr ContentID is a workaround for a broken copyright system. It's not perfect, but it's better for everyone than falling back to the default of copyright through court."},{"id":40436056,"author":"Lerc","month":"2024-05","story":"Sam Altman is showing us who he really is","stance":"reference","why":"Recalls it as having covered the topic.","quote":"It was covered in a Tom Scott video.","text":"Now you've got me doubting myself. It was covered in a Tom Scott video. I'll have a look for it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38878749,"author":"amiga386","month":"2024-01","story":"YouTube demonetizes public domain 'Steamboat Willie' video after copyright claim","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to correct what Tom Scott actually said about court.","quote":"A TV channel from Thailand took one of my videos","text":"> I vaguely remember Tom Scott saying something similar as an example of the one time he used a small claims court? Tom Scott said two things about himself in YouTube's copyright system isn't broken. The world's is. [0] 1. \"A TV channel from Thailand took one of my videos, played it out without permission in one of their big television shows, and then put that entire television show into Content ID. I got a Content ID hit on my original video from them, and that took a long time to sort out.\" 2. \"The UK has [...] the Intellectual Property Enterprise Court [1][...] and that court has a small claims track. If you're an individual photographer or video maker, you can [...] issue a claim as a \"litigant in person\", which is the fancy British term for \"without a lawyer\". I've done it, because a major company ripped off one of my videos. [...] And there I was, walking in, no lawyer, thinking I had a case, thinking I had a grip on reality. Statistically, the odds weren't in my favour, but I filed successfully. And the company settled with me, they paid me to drop the claim, because they were in the wrong, and they knew they were in the wrong, and they knew I could actually get it to a judge in exchange for a bit of work and a court fee of just over £100.\" He did not say these two incidents were related. He did not say he used a court to resolve a Content ID claim. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":38826392,"author":"hn_acker","month":"2023-12","story":"Copyright is not a moral right, it's a monetization strategy","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in discussion of DMCA reform; no endorsement given.","quote":"","text":"Copyright law in general, including online-oriented parts such as DMCA 512 (17 U.S. Code § 512 [1])), needs meaningful penalties for false copyright infringement claims (including automated mass reports with negligent/no human review) that are no more than one magnitude smaller than what would be the corresponding penalties for infringement. (Equal magnitude might intimidate too many lower-wealth authors from filing rightful infringement suits.) Currently, successfully fighting an infringement lawsuit with a fair use defense will still ruin you because of the attorney fees and court fees. A DMCA infringement notice requires only good faith from the rightsholder even if the claim turns out to be patently false, yet someone filing a counter notice against a false infringement claim does so under penalty of perjury [2]. Meanwhile, the current safe harbor standard requires service providers with user-uploaded content to remove material as soon as possible solely based on a claim of infringement with respect to that material [1]: > upon notification of claimed infringement as described in paragraph (3), responds expeditiously to remove, or disable access to, the material that is claimed to be infringing or to be the subject of infringing activity. Again, these infringement notices can be automated or BS [3] (whether intentional or not), but the service provider has to comply to avoid third-party liability. The obvious result is overblocking. The safe harbor standard should be modi"},{"id":38442171,"author":"sillysaurusx","month":"2023-11","story":"Google Play keeps banning the same web browser due to vague DMCA notices","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the system not being designed for the modern era.","quote":"the system was not designed for the modern era.","text":"The remedy isn’t (currently) to punish the issuer. It’s to file a counter notice. I’ve been through this process against Meta. It sucked, and killed my project’s early momentum. But it was restored within a week or two after we filed. It may seem like this system isn’t fair. It certainly isn’t. But it’s the best we have, because the system was not designed for the modern era. [VIDEO] It’s lucky we even have a recourse like filing a counter notice. I’ve wanted to make a non profit that offers up counter notices as a service. Basically, let people donate, and whenever someone’s project (like this unfortunate web browser) gets issued a DMCA claim, they’d be able to get a counter notice drafted and filed. It’s not cheap to hire a lawyer to do it, but you pretty much have to, which was the most unfair part. The companies filing the takedowns have lots of resources, but the targets often have substantially none. That idea is probably too much work, but someone should do it. We need an organization to go to bat for creators. Or at least an organization that gives the creator enough legal rope to hang themselves if the entity making the claim decides to file a lawsuit, which is sometimes a real possibility. But the creator should get to decide whether to take on that risk without having to pay thousands of dollars first."},{"id":38475230,"author":"askiiart","month":"2023-11","story":"'Weird Al' Roasts Spotify's Artist Payout System in Year-End Wrapped Video","stance":"reference","why":"Source for the view that parody may not clearly be fair use.","quote":"most of my knowledge of copyright law comes from Tom Scott's video on the subject","text":"I'm not a lawyer, and most of my knowledge of copyright law comes from Tom Scott's video on the subject [1], but it's worth noting that it's not universally agreed on that Weird Al's parodies fall under fair use. Again, I am not a lawyer, at best I'm a novice when it comes to copyright law, but what I do know, I agree with what Tom says: I wouldn't expect Weird Al to win if he was taken to court (if he didn't ask permission). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28520003,"author":"jchw","month":"2021-09","story":"YouTube takes down the Ig Nobel show because of a 1914 recording","stance":"reference","why":"Backs the argument that copyright itself is broken.","quote":"the answer to that is that copyright is broken","text":"That is absolutely the battle YouTube fought that lead to Content ID and the claims system that doesn’t have the leniency you wish that it did. It’s not even ambiguous; this is the system that we got. In fact, the system actually improved vastly since it was initially implemented. Really. It was all a direct reaction to the lawsuits: [LINK] When I say it has improved, I mean it genuinely has. Back in 2008 the usual outcome of copyright strikes were deleted videos and deleted channels. Copyright cartels monetizing videos they didn’t make due to dubious Content ID matches may seem like an unreasonable response, but it is still a better compromise than content flying down blackholes. And yes. It would be great if all of these problems could just be solved. I’m sure they’ve heard every idea imaginable. There is no one obvious way to solve everything. So asking “why don’t they just do this?” is not productive. What we could ask is “how did we get here?” And the answer to that is that copyright is broken. And if we could fix that, or at least make it less broken, some of these problems would literally disappear. And if you don’t believe me, there’s tons of backup from people more persuasive who have perspectives of being in the direct line of fire. Like Tom Scott: [VIDEO] And yeah YouTube could do better, but all too often you get the perspective that this is all their fault because they could solve it with one easy trick. It’s easy to say that when none of the competition managed "},{"id":23587001,"author":"Wingy","month":"2020-06","story":"Gameloft took down Marvel's SpiderMan trailer (11M views) with a copyright claim","stance":"reference","why":"Posted as relevant to a copyright claim story.","quote":"","text":"YouTube's Copyright System Isn't Broken. The World's Is. [VIDEO] Edit: Oh someone already posted it here, sorry."}],"r":[["cZYNADOHhVY",1],["dnHdqPBrtH8",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["P7GKK3liv8M",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["43XKfuptnik",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["kdz6FeQLuHQ",1],["d4EgbgTm0Bg",1],["dqQw05Mr63E",1],["Y99Wj-NStok",1],["-opBifFfsMY",1],["AcS3NOQnsQM",1],["f7MzFfuNOtY",1],["X-iSQQgOd1A",1],["iphcyNWFD10",1],["ObhK8lUfIlc",1]]},"izxXGuVL21o":{"wy":"It is one of the most frequently recommended videos on HN for old-school game development hacks under tight hardware constraints.","q":{"x":"Immensely interesting video!","i":22932418,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":48500887,"author":"mscdex","month":"2026-06","story":"How we made hit video game Prince of Persia","stance":"strong_rec","why":"War Stories episode on the making of Crash Bandicoot.","quote":"I also highly recommend the \"War Stories\" video for the making of Crash Bandicoot","text":"If you haven't already seen it, I highly recommend watching the \"War Stories\" video on the making of Prince of Persia: [LINK] On a related note, I also highly recommend the \"War Stories\" video for the making of Crash Bandicoot: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41483527,"author":"zzanz","month":"2024-09","story":"Graphics Tricks from Boomers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Naughty Dog developer on unconventional tricks for 3D on PS1.","quote":"There is a fantastic video of one of the original Naughty Dog developers","text":"I really do miss this old regime of utilizing the entire machine in games. There is a fantastic video of one of the original Naughty Dog developers talking about the optimizations they did for the original Crash Bandicoot ( [VIDEO] ), and some of the less conventional tricks they did to enable 3D gameplay on the PS1. I've always wondered what the true limit of performance optimizations is in games. Obviously modern systems have a lot more systems to compete and share memory between, but there have been obvious cases where performance has had low priority. Also under consideration is the constantly morphing landscape of PC hardware and software architecture. Given a fixed hardware, like the PS5 or Xbox, with the hardware of a high end gaming computer, what is the true limit a game could reach."},{"id":29989968,"author":"bmitc","month":"2022-01","story":"OpenGOAL: Port of Jak and Daxter, written in GOAL, a custom Lisp by Naughty Dog","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Awesome episode featuring Andy Gavin as a hacker's hacker.","quote":"his episode on Ars Technica's War Stories was awesome.","text":"In the cause of Naughty Dog, it was Andy Gavin who introduced Lisp and GOAL and was one of the co-founders of Naughty Dog. He came from MIT and was a student of Rodney Brooks. [LINK] [LINK] If someone hasn't seen it, his episode on Ars Technica's War Stories was awesome. He seems to be a hacker's hacker. It's a fascinating story because it's not really how I personally work (internally), so it's fun hearing someone describe just diving in like that. [VIDEO] [LINK] It was my understanding that Racket wasn't in use anymore at Naughty Dog. It would be cool if there was an update or someone who knows (I know Dan is no longer at Naughty Dog), because unfortunately the tale of Lisps in companies is normally that it eventually gets replaced or set aside (where new stuff is built in something else)."},{"id":24568052,"author":"mmm_grayons","month":"2020-09","story":"Amnesia is now open source","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great interview with Andy Gavin on Crash Bandicoot.","quote":"The interview with Andy Gavin on Crash Bandicoot is great, even though I never played the game","text":"For anyone that hasn't yet seen it, I'd highly recommend watching Ars Technica's video interview on Amnesia and how it created its unique atmosphere: [LINK] Along with the rest of their War Stories videos, for that matter. The interview with Andy Gavin on Crash Bandicoot is great, even though I never played the game: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22819840,"author":"lathiat","month":"2020-04","story":"Myst Almost Couldn't Run on CD-ROM [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Crash devs overwrote Sony libraries in RAM to gain memory.","quote":"I'm loving this series from ARS, another great one about Crash Bandicoot","text":"I'm loving this series from ARS, another great one about Crash Bandicoot where apparently they needed extra RAM so selectively overwrote the sony libraries in RAM with their own data, by experimenting which areas were needed on whether it crashed or not. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22819857,"author":"jonas21","month":"2020-04","story":"Myst Almost Couldn't Run on CD-ROM [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Part of a great series of developer interviews on influential games.","quote":"This is part of a great series of interviews with the developers of some of the most influential games in history.","text":"This is part of a great series of interviews with the developers of some of the most influential games in history. Two of my favorites are Andy Gavin on Crash Bandicoot: [VIDEO] and Sid Meier on Civilization: [LINK]"},{"id":22932418,"author":"dzeix35wc363CzC","month":"2020-04","story":"Playstation Architecture: A Practical Analysis","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Immensely interesting look at developing for original PlayStation under constraints.","quote":"Immensely interesting video!","text":"For anyone interested how it was developing for the original PSX (and therefore under hardware constraints) How Crash Bandicoot Hacked The Original Playstation [VIDEO] Immensely interesting video! \"Old\" hardware makes me feel so humble regarding what we have now. Hardware limitations back then really pushed developers towards novel approaches and solutions."},{"id":41414872,"author":"2600","month":"2024-09","story":"Playstation 2 GS emulation – the final frontier of Vulkan compute emulation","stance":"rec","why":"Ars interview with Andy Gavin on developing Crash Bandicoot.","quote":"I very much enjoyed this video that Ars Technica did with Andy Gavin","text":"I heard something similar with Jak and Daxter, where they took advantage of the embedded PS1 hardware on the PS2 to scrape together some extra performance. I very much enjoyed this video that Ars Technica did with Andy Gavin on the development of Crash Bandicoot: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36370436,"author":"gnatman","month":"2023-06","story":"Reddit 1.0 was written in Lisp","stance":"rec","why":"Andy Gavin on pushing the PlayStation's envelope for Crash.","quote":"Andy Gavin talking about pushing the envelope of the Playstation for Crash Bandicoot.","text":"I'm sure the commenter above has seen this but for anyone who hasn't: Andy Gavin talking about pushing the envelope of the Playstation for Crash Bandicoot. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32191161,"author":"hotpotamus","month":"2022-07","story":"Jurassic Cloud","stance":"rec","why":"Hacky, brilliant tricks to load Crash quickly into limited RAM.","quote":"all the hacky (and brilliant) things they had to do to get the game to load quickly","text":"There's a talk by one of the developers of Crash Bandicoot of all the hacky (and brilliant) things they had to do to get the game to load quickly off the (2X?) CD-ROM into the very limited RAM of the Playstation 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29127188,"author":"georgewsinger","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: How were video games from the 90s so efficient?","stance":"rec","why":"Andy Gavin discusses hacks to make Crash Bandicoot run smoothly.","quote":"I recommend watching this","text":"I recommend watching this: [VIDEO] Naughty Dog Co-founder Andy Gavin discusses various hacks that were used on the Playstation to get Crash Bandicoot to run smoothly. The fuller version is also worth watching."},{"id":29128261,"author":"de6u99er","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: How were video games from the 90s so efficient?","stance":"rec","why":"Cool story of hacking PlayStation to free memory.","quote":"This is a cool story on how the Crash Bandicoot developers hacked toe PlayStation to get free up more memory for their game.","text":"This is a cool story on how the Crash Bandicoot developers hacked toe PlayStation to get free up more memory for their game. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22571363,"author":"eindiran","month":"2020-03","story":"The Polygons of Another World: Atari Jaguar","stance":"rec","why":"Great interview on technical side of developing Crash Bandicoot.","quote":"Andy Gavin has a great interview on the technical-side of developing Crash Bandicoot [0]","text":"Andy Gavin has a great interview on the technical-side of developing Crash Bandicoot [0], which they wrote in GOOL, the precursor to GOAL. In fact, they wrote GOOL explicitly for Crash Bandicoot. Later, when writing Jak & Daxter, Naughty Dog developed GOAL. Sony ended up buying ND -- among other things, they were hoping to use ND's code, and were quite surprised to discover that it was all written in a homebrewed Lisp dialect. You can see some GOAL here: [LINK] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46050802,"author":"bsimpson","month":"2025-11","story":"Making Crash Bandicoot (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Video interview of the author from years ago.","quote":"If you'd prefer to watch a video, the author was interviewed on YouTube 5y ago","text":"If you'd prefer to watch a video, the author was interviewed on YouTube 5y ago: [VIDEO] I thought that video included one of my favorite programming legends, and apparently a bunch of people on Reddit remembered that too. Instead, it was published on Gamasutra about a \"late 90s PC title.\" Still very worth the quick read: [LINK]"},{"id":30403822,"author":"voussoir","month":"2022-02","story":"Game Boy Wordle clone: How to compress 12972 five-letter words to 17871 bytes","stance":"reference","why":"Domain-specific animation compressor made for Crash Bandicoot.","quote":"he talks about a domain-specific compressor they made for storing Crash Bandicoot's animations","text":"Here's another example from a constrained environment: in Andy Gavin's interview with Ars Technica, he talks about a domain-specific compressor they made for storing Crash Bandicoot's animations, because storing each coordinate of each vertex for each frame would have been too large. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27000979,"author":"Wxc2jjJmST9XWWL","month":"2021-04","story":"Internal Combustion Engine","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Nope ; Motorsport is always drivers' skills coupled with engineering ingenuity. It's always about \"what can I come up with, which gives me an edge, and still somehow is within the rules?\" I don't know anything about Nascar, but the history of Formula 1 is full of such little tricks as well. It's just easier to regulate \"other sports\" than it is to regulate sports that come coupled with a lot of technological involvement. If sth gives you an edge for half a season until rules are adjusted, that might be enough to win a championship. It's a cat-and-mouse game, but it's also exciting, and important for the whole thrill of it. Decades past Gordon Murray designed a fan quite literally sucking cars to the ground, which somehow was within regulations, because no one even considered something like that [LINK] In rally driving, they would sometimes come up with fake reasons for a start to be delayed, so they wouldn't have to drive in the front car's dust all the time. Audi entering with their 4-wheel car back in the days was only possible, because they pushed for a rule change and no one else really knew what was coming. Sometimes manufacturers straight up \"cheated\" (almost, sometimes for real) [LINK] ; it's a thin line, but also what makes it exciting. I would say that it's the hacker's / engineering ethos almost. What can I do within the framework? Whether it's building a bridge (to make it more stable while still following this brash design), a road car (how can I create something "},{"id":23527504,"author":"mikepurvis","month":"2020-06","story":"Accurate N64 emulation gets resolution upscaling powered by Vulkan (RetroArch)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk covers asset streaming effort around seek and read times.","quote":"the fascinating Andy Gavin talk that Ars published a few months ago","text":"> no additional cost The additional cost was an enormous step up in seek time and read time, which for some games manifested as load times everywhere, and for others meant a herculean effort in managing asset streaming, per the fascinating Andy Gavin talk that Ars published a few months ago: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["YjjTPV2pXY0",1],["MFzDaBzBlL0",1],["Q13CishCKXY",1],["ph8LyNIT9sg",1],["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["pSHj5UKSylk",1],["GOGru_4z1Vc",1],["wJyUtbn0O5Y",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["-J_xL4IGhJA",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["2KSahAoOLdU",1],["pOmXiapfCs8",1],["oP3c1h8v2ZQ",1],["sw0VfmXKq54",1],["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1]]},"d8RRE2rDw4k":{"wy":"Recommended as the argument that better alternatives to driving also improve things for drivers, and as an introduction to Dutch urban planning.","q":{"x":"One of the many excellent videos on the Not Just Bikes YouTube channel [1] shows how the Netherlands has done it.","i":37836886,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":47795537,"author":"nehal3m","month":"2026-04","story":"The Future of Everything Is Lies, I Guess: Where Do We Go from Here?","stance":"rec","why":"Describes Dutch urban planning","quote":"Check out this video, it describes Dutch urban planning pretty well","text":"You might think so, but a flat number comparison doesn't do justice to the vast differences in urban planning. Check out this video, it describes Dutch urban planning pretty well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47914736,"author":"tmsbrg","month":"2026-04","story":"Waymo says expecting driverless taxis to stay out of bike lanes is unrealistic","stance":"rec","why":"Better cycle infrastructure benefits everyone","quote":"Would recommend some people to watch some Not Just Bikes videos.","text":"Kind of weird article. If you want cars to stay out of bike lanes, make them protected like in here in the Netherlands. But also why do people expect to be taken to a bike lane rather than a parking space? Also seeing a lot of ignorance about cycling here in the comments. Would recommend some people to watch some Not Just Bikes videos. Building better cycle infrastructure is better for everyone, cars and cyclists included. Less people die, and cars don't have to deal with cyclists on the road. Ex [VIDEO]"},{"id":41106956,"author":"LeChuck","month":"2024-07","story":"If we want a shift to walking, we need to prioritize dignity","stance":"rec","why":"Cities for other modes also help drivers","quote":"If you have 15 minutes to spare, watch this video","text":"A city designed for other modes of transport is also better for drivers because only those who need (or really want) to drive need to do so. Result, less congestion and more relaxed driving. If you have 15 minutes to spare, watch this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37836886,"author":"mitchbob","month":"2023-10","story":"A global climate solution – if it can get past conspiracy theories and NIMBYs","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how the Netherlands did it","quote":"One of the many excellent videos on the Not Just Bikes YouTube channel [1] shows how the Netherlands has done it.","text":"One of the many excellent videos on the Not Just Bikes YouTube channel [1] shows how the Netherlands has done it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35246705,"author":"_dain_","month":"2023-03","story":"Cyclists Now Outnumber Motorists in City of London","stance":"rec","why":"Reducing car traffic benefits drivers directly","quote":"See this video for how these kinds of policies benefit drivers directly[1].","text":"Most (or at least, a plurality of) people who cycle or take the tube in London also drive a car sometimes; it's not like you have to be wedded to one particular transport mode. Drivers aren't drivers 100% of the time. Reducing motor traffic makes for safer, more aesthetically pleasant streets. You can expand the pavement, have flowers, landscaping, public art, outdoor dining, etc. The air quality is better, the surroundings are quieter, people don't get run over and maimed in collisions. And when you really want or need to drive, there's less traffic around. Nobody likes sitting in traffic jams! See this video for how these kinds of policies benefit drivers directly[1]. The carrot is having a more pleasant city. Everyone wins from that. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32815832,"author":"Kim_Bruning","month":"2022-09","story":"Bikes, not self driving cars, are the technological gateway to urban progress","stance":"rec","why":"Why drivers benefit from bike lanes","quote":"For more arguments than can fit in this margin, see","text":"So the actual challenge is to convince those car drivers that they really should be pushing for more bike lanes, since it really is in their best interest. O:-) For more arguments than can fit in this margin, see: [VIDEO] \"The Best Country in the World for Drivers\" (edit: +if you don't have time for an entire video, just watch this one traffic light for single example that exemplifies the story: [LINK] ... comments on that video are good too!)"},{"id":32188489,"author":"xputer","month":"2022-07","story":"Why we ignore thousands of daily car crashes","stance":"rec","why":"Explains forced driving in America","quote":"Here is a video that explains it further","text":"Both are definitely a factor, but don't forget that a lot of the irritate driving behavior is because in America people are forced to drive. Even if they don't want to or really shouldn't be driving, they still have to drive to get around town. Here is a video that explains it further: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29736938,"author":"Improvotter","month":"2021-12","story":"Changes in Dutch streets throughout the years (photo album)","stance":"rec","why":"Car-independent countries are still best to drive in","quote":"I would recommend you have a look at Not Just Bikes on YouTube","text":"I think this is due to how car dependent North America is. There are so many aspects that are missed in these pictures or what you are for example experiencing. It's not that you're situation is improving, it's most likely not. But that's not due to car independent cities being bad, it's because changing from being car dependent to car independent is hard and takes time. I would recommend you have a look at Not Just Bikes on YouTube, a Canadian that moved to the Netherlands. He talks about car independent cities in Europe, more specifically The Netherlands and compares it to car dependent North America. Here is a video that talks about how these \"car independent\" countries are still the best to drive in compared to others: [VIDEO] . Regardless, I would recommend you check out a few other of his videos, most importantly his series on the US non profit organisation \"Strong Towns\": [LINK] . It think it's about sharing the message that placing public transport, cyclists, and pedestrians in the forefront is better for almost everyone involved, including drivers, and especially people who live in cities and towns."},{"id":29282902,"author":"cultofmetatron","month":"2021-11","story":"Designing transport for humans, not econs","stance":"rec","why":"Channel on American urban planning problems","quote":"a channel that really opened my eyes on how terrible urban planning is in america.","text":"I've been watching a channel that really opened my eyes on how terrible urban planning is in america. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39745658,"author":"dublinben","month":"2024-03","story":"Paris cycling numbers double in one year thanks to investment","stance":"reference","why":"Fewer cars helps drivers","quote":"An enlightened self-interested driver would want to increase investment in alternatives to driving","text":"Even as a driver, if you think about the second-order effects of getting more (other) people out of their cars, it becomes obvious that it would improve traffic for you. An enlightened self-interested driver would want to increase investment in alternatives to driving, since that is the only proven solution for congestion. As a video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37190294,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-08","story":"Amsterdam to use “noise cameras” against too loud cars","stance":"reference","why":"Fewer needed cars helps those who drive","quote":"He does have a video called \"The Best Country in the World for Drivers","text":"> I kind of like his ideas (I know the channel), but he’s heavily biased against cars. He’s the “everything could be done on a bike” type of guy. It's kind of hard to over emphasize the the damage they've done, specifically as forms of personal transportation (versus more commercial transport). He does have a video called \"The Best Country in the World for Drivers\", in which he shows that by reducing the need for cars for most people, actually helps the optimize things for operating a car: * [VIDEO] And until the ~1920s, societies managed to function pretty well without the streets being taken over by cars: * [LINK] I don't see why they need to have total priority with-in cities to the extant they currently do."},{"id":35396565,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2023-04","story":"Maps show how parking lots “eat” U.S. cities","stance":"reference","why":"Non-car improvements help drivers too","quote":"Improving things for non-car users can actually make things better for drivers","text":"> […] considering the transit with the lowest capital investment, the bus, is also benefited by car oriented development like a road network with more connections between nodes. 'Improving' car-oriented development has been observed to make things worse: > It is a paradox in that improvements in the road network will not reduce traffic congestion. Improvements in the road network can make congestion worse if the improvements make public transport more inconvenient or if they shift investment, causing disinvestment in the public transport system. * [LINK] Improving things for non-car users can actually make things better for drivers: * [VIDEO] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":35232395,"author":"CalRobert","month":"2023-03","story":"Cyclists Now Outnumber Motorists in City of London","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch infrastructure makes driving pleasant","quote":"the excellent infrastructure in the Netherlands makes it a very pleasant place to drive.","text":"That makes sense, the excellent infrastructure in the Netherlands makes it a very pleasant place to drive. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33864363,"author":"oblio","month":"2022-12","story":"Tesla Semi, a Deep Dive","stance":"reference","why":"Drivers benefit when others walk or ride","quote":"As a driver you want everyone to be walking, cycling, taking the bus/trolley/tram/train/...","text":"[VIDEO] As a driver you want everyone to be walking, cycling, taking the bus/trolley/tram/train/... Why? Because you'd be alone on the road."},{"id":32814476,"author":"ackfoobar","month":"2022-09","story":"Bikes, not self driving cars, are the technological gateway to urban progress","stance":"reference","why":"Average car speeds versus bikes","quote":"Yet another video from NJB on this topic.","text":"Yet another video from NJB on this topic. [VIDEO] Chuck Marohn measured his average speed at rush hour, and found it was about 16km/h ... So despite the roads having a speed limit of 50 km/h or more, his average speed was less than 16, because of all the traffic lights. And this was in a tiny town of only 14,000 people. Incidentally, 16 km/h is the average speed of a cyclist in Amsterdam"},{"id":32853125,"author":"shlurpy","month":"2022-09","story":"Should traffic lights be abolished? (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Bike-friendly cities are car friendly too","quote":"bike and pedestrian friendly cities are also some of the most car friendly ones for this reason.","text":"Empirically, most people care more about making the trip efficiently then how exactly it's done. If all those people are motivated to not use cars, think of how much less traffic there will be for everyone else? It turns out that bike and pedestrian friendly cities are also some of the most car friendly ones for this reason. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32386609,"author":"wbsss4412","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: What's the next big thing that few people are talking about?","stance":"reference","why":"Driving is more pleasant in Amsterdam","quote":"he made an interesting point that driving is more pleasant in Amsterdam.","text":"That’s why it’s important to help people understand that’s it’s not about eliminating cars entirely, but about substituting significant numbers of trips. The YouTube channel NotJustBikes has gained a lot of notoriety over the last couple of years, and he made an interesting point that driving is more pleasant in Amsterdam. [0] The issue is mainly car dependency not cars per se. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31853259,"author":"noahtallen","month":"2022-06","story":"Investigating the effects of bike lanes in Minneapolis","stance":"reference","why":"Amsterdam also great for driving","quote":"And over the past several decades, they have made vast changes and redevelopments","text":"I definitely agree with the latter paragraph. But it’s important to note that places like Amsterdam did invest heavily into car infrastructure as well. It’s not completely true that car dependency was staved off by existing road sizes. Like you allude to, the US was bulldozed for the automobile. We didn’t develop around it. Amsterdam was following a similar path until mass protests in the 70s about people being slaughtered by cars. ( [LINK] ) And over the past several decades, they have made vast changes and redevelopments that we should have been following as well. And now Amsterdam isn’t just amazing for cyclists and pedestrians… it’s also great for driving. ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":29505426,"author":"Rendello","month":"2021-12","story":"How do you fix a stroad?","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch driving is faster and pleasant","quote":"driving is actually much faster and more pleasant","text":"My observations: I grew up in Northern Ontario, where the cities are basically long stroads. Inside of a car, they can be stressful to drive in, as they combine high-speed travel with frequent and awkward turns. God forbid you want to walk or bike next to one of them. The noise is extreme, and the infrastructure surrounding them is horrid. Whether in a car, on a bike, or on foot, they are ugly and soul-crushing. The patio bar next to a stroad isn't going to be people's first choice. I haven't been to the Netherlands myself, but it appears that after moving away from stroads and \"car-centric\" infrastructure, the driving is actually much faster and more pleasant [1]. It gives me hope at least! 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29312928,"author":"drivers99","month":"2021-11","story":"The Stroad","stance":"reference","why":"Why the Netherlands is good for drivers","quote":"Have you seen Not Just Bikes' latest video?","text":"Have you seen Not Just Bikes' latest video? \"Why the Netherlands is the Best Country for Drivers\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lcjdwSY2AzM",1],["IgF3OX8nT0w",1],["GVsUOuSjvcg",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["bnKIVX968PQ",1],["uxykI30fS54",1],["42oQN7fy_eM",1],["oHlpmxLTxpw",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["agZ0xISi40E",1],["vGAqYNFQdZ4",1],["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["ovJcsL7vyrk",1],["EYPapE-3FRw",1],["u6XAPnuFjJc",1],["R2paSGQRwvo",1],["QM1iUe6IofM",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["y_SXXTBypIg",1],["xoVJKj8lcNQ",1]]},"AxFzf6yIfcc":{"wy":"HN cites it repeatedly in debates about in-app purchases and monetization corrupting games.","q":{"x":"how the business model deeply corrupts the game through the metaphor of bad 70's TV shows vs HBO","i":6926241,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6673256,"author":"DanielRibeiro","month":"2013-11","story":"On Game Development","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Parallel between evolution of television and games.","quote":"recently gave an amazing presentation on the evolution of Television","text":"Jonathan Blow (Braid and Indie Fund) recently gave an amazing presentation on the evolution of Television, and he made an interesting parallel to the evolution of games: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23658900,"author":"hnick","month":"2020-06","story":"Facebook announces policy changes ahead of 2020 elections","stance":"rec","why":"How commercial constraints shape TV and mobile game design.","quote":"There's an interesting talk about that by Jonathan Blow","text":"There's an interesting talk about that by Jonathan Blow, using the common phrase 'The Medium is the Message'. The eventual topic is mobile game design but he starts off with TV and how commercial and other constraints influence show (and game) design. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14273178,"author":"emsy","month":"2017-05","story":"So You Want to Make Games","stance":"rec","why":"Blow's analysis rebutting a Candy Crush comparison.","quote":"Jonathan Blow nailed it in this presentation","text":"While I disagree with the word dumb and find \"ignorant\" more suitable, your Candy Crush comparison is bad. Jonathan Blow nailed it in this presentation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10240576,"author":"aethertron","month":"2015-09","story":"“We Own You” – Confessions of an Anonymous Free to Play Producer","stance":"rec","why":"Blow on how payment model shapes game quality, including mobile Skinner boxes.","quote":"Jonathan Blow has some interesting points to make on how the payment form of games","text":">It is almost certain you have or are playing a game I have produced or worked on in a major capacity. Maybe for a few minutes. I like to think I'm good at analysing game mechanics, as a long time gamer and sometimes amateur game designer. So I mostly avoid F2P games. The tactics are repulsive to my tastes, and I can smell them from a mile away. When there's a choice between struggling to meet a challenge by improving my skill, or paying my way though, my inclination is to take a 3rd choice: stop playing this and do something else. So I much prefer paying up front for a game. I'm old-school that way. Maybe this option is viable, as a 'serious' gaming hobbyist, only temporarily. But right now, the best games (in my genres of choice) are sold on the traditional purchase model. They are PC and console games. Generally, I don't like 'endless' games; I prefer a game to have an ending: I play it, experience it 'fully', finish it, then move on to something else. (But... just to undermine everything I've stated above, I am playing Pac Man 256. It has good gamefeel. I'll play it until I get all the powerups, perhaps. No interest in high-scores...) tl;dr: IMO F2P games are inferior instances of the artform. Be cool like me and play superior PC and console games. Also, Jonathan Blow has some interesting points to make on how the payment form of games (and entertainment in general) affects their structure/quality. This is the one where he talks about addictive mobile Skinner box games [x"},{"id":9011409,"author":"kelukelugames","month":"2015-02","story":"‘Braid’ creator sacrifices his fortune to build his next game","stance":"rec","why":"Blow's talk comparing games to television.","quote":"But he has given some amazing talks.","text":"I've followed Jonathan Blow for a while. He has a reputation for being condescending. If you follow him a Twitter then you might come to the same conclusion. But he has given some amazing talks. [VIDEO] <-compares games to televison [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":7162159,"author":"wsc981","month":"2014-02","story":"How in-app purchases have destroyed the game industry","stance":"rec","why":"Critique of games designed around micro-payments, starting around minute 20.","quote":"The whole talk is interesting, but he starts discussing the issues around the 20 minute mark","text":"Jonathan Blow -creator of Braid and working on The Witness- also has a very nice critique on the problems with games that are designed around micro-payments. The whole talk is interesting, but he starts discussing the issues around the 20 minute mark: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6926241,"author":"po","month":"2013-12","story":"The case for an IAP off button in mobile games","stance":"rec","why":"How free-to-play business models corrupt games, via TV analogy.","quote":"how the business model deeply corrupts the game through the metaphor of bad 70's TV shows vs HBO","text":"If anyone wants to see a good example of why you can't just toggle off IAP you should check out Jonathan Blow's talk about \"Free to Play\" games and how the business model deeply corrupts the game through the metaphor of bad 70's TV shows vs HBO: [VIDEO] The solution isn't to toggle IAP off in the game, it's the ability to toggle it off in the App Store. The difficulty is some IAP is legitimate and some is not. For example, Carcassonne has an IAP to upgrade the game into a slightly different game with additional tiles. It's only when the IAP is part of the core game mechanic that is troubling. I think categorising them and relegating them to a particular category like we do with FPS, RPG, RTS, etc.. would help."}],"r":[["JjDsP5n2kSM",1],["SqFu5O-oPmU",1],["GNO_CYUjMK8",1],["PUv66718DII",1],["zDZFcDGpL4U",1],["ez4DgdurRPg",1],["_L3gNaAVjQ4",1],["RCCigccBzIU",1],["QeLu_yyz3tc",1],["GnO7D5UaDig",1],["FssULNGSZIA",1],["pXOK-ZVJMaU",1],["Uo08uS904Rg",1],["i_yJTCDU4uE",1]]},"BzB5xtGGsTc":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an in-depth, accessible look at airline class economics and revisit it in many discussions about airlines.","q":{"x":"Would highly recommend the YouTube video below [0] from Wendover","i":19338428,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":29153517,"author":"filereaper","month":"2021-11","story":"Billion-dollar airline routes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Wendover's airline economics videos, this one on seat classes","quote":"Highly recommend Wendover's videos on Airline economics","text":"Highly recommend Wendover's videos on Airline economics, this specific one is about the various classes in Airline seats [VIDEO]"},{"id":19338428,"author":"alehul","month":"2019-03","story":"First-class air travel is in decline","stance":"strong_rec","why":"In-depth look at the economics of airline classes","quote":"Would highly recommend the YouTube video below [0] from Wendover","text":"Would highly recommend the YouTube video below [0] from Wendover, which went pretty in-depth on the economics of airline classes a while ago, and how first-class was the least profitable for the airlines. :) [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16100443,"author":"sillysaurus3","month":"2018-01","story":"As of today, no US passenger airlines operate the Boeing 747","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Wendover channel on airline operations; this one on class economics","quote":"I just recently found the channel. So much good stuff.","text":"If anyone finds the operation of airlines a fascinating topic, you might like Wendover Productions: [LINK] How airlines price flights [LINK] The little plane war [LINK] Why planes don't fly faster [LINK] The economics of Airline Class [VIDEO] How airlines schedule flights [LINK] I just recently found the channel. So much good stuff. (My unrelated favorite is [LINK] How the US government will survive doomsday.)"},{"id":20099721,"author":"mynameisvlad","month":"2019-06","story":"Flying-V: Flying long distances energy-efficiently","stance":"rec","why":"Explains which seat classes earn airlines the most","quote":"Wendover Productions had a great video on this a few years ago","text":"Economy class seats generally speaking will earn the least amount of money to an airline, so this idea would actually likely be the most unprofitable option. To make it truly profitable, you convert the plane to First class, but then you'll need to fill all those seats. Wendover Productions had a great video on this a few years ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17575235,"author":"caio1982","month":"2018-07","story":"Is killing the boom the key to supersonic air travel?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains airline class economics and why supersonic flight died","quote":"it talks about specifically why supersonic flights went into oblivion","text":"There is also another Wendover one explaining the economics or airlines classes and it talks about specifically why supersonic flights went into oblivion: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14132216,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-04","story":"How the Airlines Became Cartels","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on airline classes","quote":"There's a good Wendover video on airline classes","text":"There's a good Wendover video on airline classes: [VIDEO] And another great one about AmTrak in relation to flight: [LINK]"},{"id":45431646,"author":"Thorrez","month":"2025-09","story":"Boeing has started working on a 737 MAX replacement","stance":"reference","why":"Used to challenge a claim about class profitability","quote":"This video is basically saying the opposite of you: that business/first class subsidizes economy.","text":"Do you have a source showing $/sqft of economy vs business/first class? This video is basically saying the opposite of you: that business/first class subsidizes economy. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44732853,"author":"meinersbur","month":"2025-07","story":"Why do some AI chatbot subscriptions cost more than $200?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of market segmentation in airline seating","quote":"Economy class fills the plane, but business and first class are the money makers","text":"There is also market separation in play: For the base service you only charge cost+small margin. For higher service levels you charge higher profit margins even though the additional service does not cost that much more to provide. Best example: flight seats. Economy class fills the plane, but business and first class are the money makers [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21040748,"author":"chiph","month":"2019-09","story":"End of an American tradition: Amtrak kills the traditional dining car","stance":"reference","why":"Premium seats analogy for rail","quote":"","text":"I don't understand why they won't run a platform car on the back of the trains and sell tickets as a super-premium class. It'd be the most profitable seats on the train because of what you'd charge for the experience, much like how the few 1st class seats on an jet earns the airline more than the entire economy class. [VIDEO] Sure, the product is basically the same - a ride from Point A to Point B. But if you offer a private dining room, an observation lounge, a steward who attends to just you and 3 other cabins, you can charge way more than an economy seat. By only focusing on the \"Point A to Point B\" aspects, they're entering a downward financial spiral."},{"id":17013566,"author":"clarkenheim","month":"2018-05","story":"Concorde ‘B’","stance":"reference","why":"Premium cabins as airlines' main profit source","quote":"First/business class seats are where the airlines make all of their money","text":"According to this: [VIDEO] First/business class seats are where the airlines make all of their money, so Concord would be economically viable today."},{"id":14106170,"author":"shimon_e","month":"2017-04","story":"United removes first-class passenger to make room for 'higher-priority' traveler","stance":"reference","why":"Source for premium passengers driving airline revenue","quote":"privileged passengers account for about 70% of airline revenue.","text":"Unfortunately, due to the economics of airline class[0], privileged passengers account for about 70% of airline revenue. 0. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UjZYMI1zB9s",1],["VPBH1eW28mo",1],["LLCF7vPanrY",1],["n1QEj09Pe6k",1],["_Qd3erAPI9w",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["lD9FAOPBiDk",1],["GFE4rkSaKOY",1],["ojm74VGsZBU",1],["KNWh6Kw3ejQ",1],["uI1eRy0uBI8",1],["fwjwePe-HmA",1],["pnv5iKB2hl4",1],["LrObZ_HZZUc",1],["-HFwok9SlQQ",1],["niKblgZupOc",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["Eco2s3-0zsQ",1],["2y5Pv4yN0b0",1]]},"xt1KNDmOYqA":{"wy":"HN commenters in memory management and language design threads cite it as the best explanation of group lifetimes versus per-object ownership.","q":{"x":"The best video on this might be from Casey Muratori","i":46457333,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48974141,"author":"jackclayton","month":"2026-07","story":"Claude Code uses Bun written in Rust now","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed commenter's view on borrow checking and lifetimes.","quote":"I used to think like you until I watched this video","text":"Above poster is talking about thinking in terms of grouped lifetimes and bulk allocations/deallocations, which is better for performance, and makes Rust borrow checking and other RAII style features completely pointless as they don't add any safety benefits. I used to think like you until I watched this video: [VIDEO] I can confirm after exploring programming like the above video explains, that everything he says is completely true."},{"id":46457333,"author":"gingerBill","month":"2026-01","story":"Odin: Moving Towards a New \"core:OS\"","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains locality affecting globality in language design.","quote":"The best video on this might be from Casey Muratori","text":"Unfortunately I don't think you've understand what I was I trying to say. Rust was designed from day-0 around explicit individual-value based initialization. Odin from day-0 was not designed around this (explicitly). This ever so minor choice might not seem like a big thing to you, as you have stated in your comment, but it leads to MASSIVE architectural decisions later on when the user programs. Odin could not \"just add\" non-nil pointers and it be \"fine\". It would actually not be Odin any more, and the entire language would not even be a C alternative any more, and feel much more like C++ or Rust. Rust and OCaml (which Rust is based off) are different kinds of languages to Odin and their approach does not translate well to what Odin (or C) is trying to do. Unfortunately I will not be able to explain this to you in a comment or article, and it is something that takes a while to understand since it is a subtle effect of locality affecting globality. The best video on this might be from Casey Muratori: [VIDEO] > Yes, initialization can be less efficient in Rust but then most of the time spent is spend in algorithms when they run, not during initialization of the data structure. Sadly this isn't as true as you think it is. I've written a lot of C++ code before and doing its boilerplate for ctors/dtors actually leads to much slower code in general, and I'd argue this does apply to Rust too. Most of the time isn't necessarily spent in \"algorithms\", especially when \"algorithms\" als"},{"id":41642906,"author":"bsder","month":"2024-09","story":"Committing to Rust in the Kernel","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Casey Muratori on grouped-element thinking versus RAII and smart pointers.","quote":"I think Casey Muratori hits the nail on the head here","text":"C and C++ are very different. I think Casey Muratori hits the nail on the head here: [VIDEO] In short, RAII and smart pointers and borrow checker are all signs of \"individual element thinking\" and that way lies madness. You smear out lifetime and ownership so badly that it practically by definition becomes a problem. Your goal is to think about this stuff in groups to make thinking about allocation way, way easier. Programming is all about abstracting to the next layer. I'm not sure he has quite gotten to the heart of the issue, yet. However, a bunch of smart people (Casey Muratori, Jonathan Blow, Andrew Kelley, etc.) are all dancing around something that Rust and C++ don't seem to fit the bill on. Hopefully they can crystallize it out so that everybody can see it."},{"id":48080557,"author":"ChrisTrenkamp","month":"2026-05","story":"Bun ported to Rust in 6 days","stance":"rec","why":"Casey on arena allocation and grouped lifetimes.","quote":"Casey Muratori has a good video talking about this.","text":"This is a hot take, but programming languages haven't progressed since the 90's. We've been conditioned to believe that if you want to be a serious programmer, you have to either use C++-style RAII (which includes Rust), or garbage collection, and there's no in-between, and C programmers are dinosaurs who can be ignored. Arena allocators are a great way to automatically manage memory allocations. You malloc a whole bunch of memory and release it all with a single free, which makes it much easier to reason about your program's memory safety. Casey Muratori has a good video talking about this. [VIDEO] And about Zig, you have an Arena Allocator out of the box: [LINK] . And it's not just limited to that, you have debug allocators that detects memory leaks and gives you stack traces where they occurred. This isn't to say that Zig is great at everything. I think Rust is great for things like kernels, high-frequency trading systems, and authentication servers where memory safety and performance is paramount. But for things like video games, memory leaks and buffer overflows aren't that big of a deal, and Zig's \"Good Enough\" approach is great for those types of applications."},{"id":46299559,"author":"thegeekpirate","month":"2025-12","story":"Zig's new plan for asynchronous programs","stance":"rec","why":"Manual memory management with groups.","quote":"is worth a watch.","text":"There's a GC library around somewhere, but I doubt anyone uses it. Manual memory management is generally quite simple, as long as you aren't using archaic languages. [VIDEO] is worth a watch."},{"id":43972066,"author":"slowmovintarget","month":"2025-05","story":"Odin: A programming language made for me","stance":"rec","why":"Casey on moving to zero-is-initialization.","quote":"Much better outcomes and failure modes than RAII.","text":"Here's Casey Muratori on his habit of moving to ZII: [VIDEO] Much better outcomes and failure modes than RAII. IIRC, Odin mentions game programming as one of its use cases."},{"id":43972218,"author":"gingerBill","month":"2025-05","story":"Odin: A programming language made for me","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the cost of per-object initialization.","quote":"A good little video on this is from Casey Muratori","text":"> It seems to me... that initialization time is unlikely to be significant The thing is, initialization cost is a lot more than you think it is, especially when it's done on a per-object level rather than a \"group\" level. This is kind of the point of trying to make the zero value useful, it's trivially initialized. And in languages that are much more strict in their approach, it is done at that per-object level which means instead of the cost of initialization being anywhere from free (VirtualAlloc/mmap has to produce zeroed memory) to trivially-linear (e.g. memset), to being a lot more nested hierarchies of initialization (e.g. for-loop with constructor for each value). It's non-obvious why the \"strict approach\" would be worse, but it's more about how people actually program rather than a hypothetical approach to things. So of course each style is about trade-offs. There are no solutions, only trade-offs. And different styles will have different trade-offs, even if they are not immediately obvious and require a bit of experience. A good little video on this is from Casey Muratori, \"Smart-Pointers, RAII, ZII? Becoming an N+2 programmer\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":42893666,"author":"budro","month":"2025-01","story":"Show HN: Uscope, a new Linux debugger written from scratch","stance":"reference","why":"Describes grouped element thinking.","quote":"","text":"> And this is where Rust differs; Rust will reduce the likelihood of bugs from happening in the first place. I'm not saying Zig doesn't also do that, Rust just does it more. I'd argue that it does this a LOT more. When it comes to spatial safety, Rust and Zig are on par. However in terms of temporal safety I think Zig lags far behind Rust... along with basically every other compiled systems language. If you had to come up with a single good reason to use Rust over Zig, it would definitely be temporal safety. A majority of applications don't need this though, or can be designed such that they don't. One of my main issues with Rust is that it makes it easy to write spaghetti code that uses reference counting all over the place. I don't recommend people to use Rust until they become a \"N+1 programmer\" that Casey Muratori describes as \"grouped element thinking\" [1]. At this point, most of that programmers problems are solved and suddenly the Zig vs Rust debate becomes much more nuanced. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46155578,"author":"Capricorn2481","month":"2025-12","story":"Thoughts on Go vs. Rust vs. Zig","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"extremely informative, but also stupidly gatekeepy","text":">> In Go and Rust and so many other languages, you tend to allocate little bits of memory at a time for each object in your object graph. Your program has thousands of little hidden malloc()s and free()s, and therefore thousands of different lifetimes. > Rust can also do arena allocations, and there is an allocator concept in Rust, too. There's just a default allocator, too. Thank you. I've seen this repeated so many times. Casey Muratori did a video on batch allocations that was extremely informative, but also stupidly gatekeepy [1]. I think a lot of people who want to see themselves as super devs have latched onto this point without even understanding it. They talk like RAII makes it impossible to batch anything. Last year the Zig Software Foundation wrote about Asahi Lina's comments around Rust and basically implied she was unknowingly introducing these hidden allocations, citing this exact Casey Muratori video. And it was weird. A bunch of people pointed out the inaccuracies in the post, including Lina [2]. That combined with Andrew saying Go is for people without taste (not that I like Go myself), I'm not digging Zig's vibe of dunking on other companies and languages to sell their own. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["7oWip00iXbo",1],["Cym4TZwTCNU",1],["5hVmeOCJjOU",1],["esQyYGezS7c",1]]},"6UsHHOCH4q8":{"wy":"HN uses it in TPP and trade-deal threads as a clear example of ISDS being used by corporations against governments.","q":{"x":"I really encourage you to check it out. It's about 18min long, but well worth it","i":9848209,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9848209,"author":"dailyrorschach","month":"2015-07","story":"CVS Health Quits U.S. Chamber Over Stance on Smoking","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tobacco companies doubling down on selling to the rest of the world","quote":"I really encourage you to check it out. It's about 18min long, but well worth it","text":"A great deal of the Chamber's funding comes from Tobacco companies. In fact there are conference rooms named for them. If you haven't seen Last Week Tonight's take on the international bad behavior of the Tobacco companies I really encourage you to check it out. It's about 18min long, but well worth it: [VIDEO] They've responded to a shrinking market in the US and other highly developed nations, by doubling down on their efforts to sell to everyone in the rest of the world. And woe to the country with a smaller GDP than Phillip Morris who decides to try to put restrictions on a pack of smokes."},{"id":9241552,"author":"hkarthik","month":"2015-03","story":"Latin America Is Quitting Smoking, and Africa Is Taking It Up","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How tobacco companies became more profitable worldwide","quote":"There was a great John Oliver segment on the tobacco industry","text":"There was a great John Oliver segment on the tobacco industry and how it's evolved to become even more profitable worldwide than it ever was in the US. Highly recommended to watch it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17731430,"author":"staz","month":"2018-08","story":"Smoking around the world","stance":"rec","why":"Covers tobacco companies targeting new non-smoking countries.","quote":"John Oliver did a good segment about that two years ago","text":"Most countries in the EU are taking steps to combat this and the number of smokers is steadily diminishing, even if too slowly. I'm more worried about the non smoker countries which the tobacco companies are seeing as new territories to conquer. John Oliver did a good segment about that two years ago [VIDEO]"},{"id":13453749,"author":"ggame","month":"2017-01","story":"US Announces Withdrawal from TPP","stance":"rec","why":"Shows effects of trade deals letting corporations challenge governments.","quote":"See this exert from John Oliver on the effect of such trade deals;","text":"Not a fan of Trump but this is a good thing. See this exert from John Oliver on the effect of such trade deals; [VIDEO] And that's the existing trade deals, TPP would have made it much worse. There is a reason why democratically elected governments need to protect their sovereignty. The scope of the legal provisions along with the requirement to use easily corruptible mediation is superfluous to free trade. In addition, I don't understand those that think boxing out Russia and China from the rest of Europe and Asia is a good thing."},{"id":10364113,"author":"walterbell","month":"2015-10","story":"The Final Leaked TPP Text Is All That We Feared","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Philip Morris ISDS suit against Australia over plain packaging.","quote":"caused such an outcry that TPP negotiators were forced to exclude the entire tobacco industry from ISDS.","text":"Those pre-TPP cases are nothing compared to the future combined scope of TPP (Pacific), TTIP (Europe) and RCEP (China, Korea, India, and other Asian countries). Never say there is nothing you can do. Tell other people about ISDS. Share the videos [1][2][3][4]. Jon Oliver's video [4] about the Philip Morris tobacco ISDS lawsuit against Australia (for brown paper packaging) caused such an outcry that TPP negotiators were forced to exclude the entire tobacco industry from ISDS. This is the first time such an industry-specific carve-out has appeared in a trade agreement. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9079684,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2015-02","story":"Go to Prison for File Sharing? That's What Hollywood Wants in the TPP Deal","stance":"rec","why":"Corporate threats against public-health laws start a few minutes in","quote":"Keep watching - the corporate stuff starts a few minutes in","text":"Keep watching - the corporate stuff starts a few minutes in, with the tobacco industry throwing around threats to various countries legislatures to stop those legislatures from passing Australian-style packaging laws and other public-health regulations. edit: if you're in a hurry, try starting at about 5:55 [VIDEO]"},{"id":29145801,"author":"ClumsyPilot","month":"2021-11","story":"Elite Underproduction","stance":"reference","why":"Cites British American Tobacco suing developing nations over smoking bans.","quote":"British American Tabacco has taken poor developing nations to court for trying to ban children from smoking (!!)","text":"Absolutely! When UK Parliament wanted Zuk to answer some tough question about his company facilitating electoral fraud, he simply refused to show up. Even first world nations struggle in dealing with American corporation, which often act as if they are above the law. God forbid you are a developing nation. British American Tabacco has taken poor developing nations to court for trying to ban children from smoking (!!) and got compenation and the law repealed [VIDEO] I am not even mentioning illegal stuff: bribes, threats, drone strikes, strong-arming, etc. Even the EU was strong armed when it comes to GPS: [LINK] Those that think that American economic advantage was won in a 'fair game' are super naive"},{"id":13175100,"author":"setrofim_","month":"2016-12","story":"Teen drug and alcohol use has fallen","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates tobacco industry pivot to global markets.","quote":"Global tobacco business is better than ever","text":"> I wonder if the cig companies have coped by pivoting into the global market and pushing hard that way. That is exactly what happened. Global tobacco business is better than ever: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12193270,"author":"josinalvo","month":"2016-07","story":"How the food lobby fights sugar regulation in the EU [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Example of corporations suing governments over lost profits from legislation.","quote":"Happened with cigarettes too","text":"Weird response, to say the least. And from a green account. (takes off tinfoil hat) Now, what is laughable is this idea of corporations suing governments because of lost profits from legislation. Happened with cigarettes too [VIDEO] [LINK] As for the idea of civil war: obvious hyperbole is obvious. Democracy needs fixing, or much larger problems -- but if there is no fixing, corporate corruption might ensure the problems."},{"id":11881077,"author":"gavinpc","month":"2016-06","story":"Google endorses the Trans-Pacific Partnership","stance":"reference","why":"Shows ISDS already used by tobacco companies against nations.","quote":"This is already happening, at least with tobacco companies. \"Last Week Tonight\" did a piece on this.[2]","text":"Are you referring to the part about corporations being able to sue nations if they take any measures that they can claim hurts their business? [0] I assume that the \"ISDS\" that Warren refers to in the linked article (which was written before the draft went public), is chapter 28, \"Dispute Settlement,\" in the draft at readthetpp.com. [1] This is already happening, at least with tobacco companies. \"Last Week Tonight\" did a piece on this.[2] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11608440,"author":"mikemoka","month":"2016-05","story":"TTIP Documents Revealed","stance":"reference","why":"Example of corporations acting against public wellbeing via state suits.","quote":"","text":"\"A random Company invests in a country, sets up a nice subsidiary starts doing business, everything is legal. But the country (the State) sees that it's taking up the market, uh-oh,\" when this has been done and when it has happened not in the interest of public well being? on the other hand here is an example of what some corporations actually do in the present [VIDEO]"},{"id":10330832,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2015-10","story":"Why Free Markets Make Fools of Us","stance":"reference","why":"Shows tobacco companies suing countries to protect advertising rights.","quote":"they actually sue countries [0] to keep their rights to advertise death.","text":"They adjust their strategies to local conditions. In EU they can do little. Elsewhere, they actually sue countries [0] to keep their rights to advertise death. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":10400111,"author":"walterbell","month":"2015-10","story":"TPP could thwart computer security research and tinkering","stance":"reference","why":"Tobacco ISDS segment raised public attention, leading to TPP carve-out.","quote":"That ISDS video got so much attention that the TPP excludes the entire tobacco industry","text":"Narratives of doom can lead to paralysis. Better to remember that the past is reversible by action, as described in this review of Public Citizen's yeoman effort to raise TPP awareness and decipher the implications of draft texts, [LINK] \"Enlisting a core band of labor, environmental, and community allies, the dozen trade-watching stalwarts at Public Citizen divided into five teams and went after the Brobdingnagians of global corporate power ... it's important to spread the story of the progressive coalition's successful confrontation with the Global Goliath. Its methods and achievements give us a new template for organizing (and winning) future populist challenges to the corporate order. And the breadth, depth, and intensity of this effort show what it will take to forge a real populist movement--multifaceted and with the long-term capacity to pursue our country's deep democratic principles. We can get there if we build on what we learn--and keep pushing.\" Do you remember Jon Oliver's video on tobacco lobbyists suing Australia for plain-paper packaging, [VIDEO] ? That ISDS video got so much attention that the TPP excludes the entire tobacco industry from suing government with lawsuits like [LINK] . To slow/stop the TPP, public interest stakeholders need to lobby corporations to take a stand and declare their position on freedom of speech, freedom to tinker, freedom to compete against all business models. Wikipedia can go dark for one day a week, to raise awareness of the TPP"},{"id":9306869,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2015-04","story":"Leaked TPP Investment Chapter Reveals Serious Threat to User Safeguards","stance":"reference","why":"Covers the Australia plain-packaging ISDS example well","quote":"this example is covered well by John Oliver","text":"The comic[1] that I posted in the previous thread has a nice overview about the situation. The specific issue the EFF article is pointing out is how the ISDS is designed to trumps national sovereignty, so business can strong-arm countries into removing any law they don't like. It works more or less like this: 1) pass TPP as a treaty , which by definition has the same power as a constitutional amendment. (this is probably a serious bug in the constitution). This means TPP trums congress. 2) TPP sets up an international tribunal (ISDS) that hears complaints from businesses. (i.e. \"Australia is hurting our tobacco sales with their plain packaging laws\"[2]) 3) The tribunal, as mentioned in this article, would be staffed by, \"three private-sector attorneys who take turns being the judge and the corporate advocate.\" This conflict of interest would be a serious violation of ethics requirements in most legal systems. 4) The tribunal rules that your local law violates some foreign requirement, as determined by the ISDS and their business-assigned judges, and the local legislature is forced to repeal the law. (\"Australia's plain packaging laws is seizing the trademarks of Philip Moriss, in violation of a 1993 trade agreement if we carefully abuse the agreement's language\") That's the short version, and I'm leaving out some details, of course. It should work as a decent overview of ISDS is a threat to national sovereignty. [1] [LINK] [2] this example is covered well by John Oliver: [VID"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"G1r55efei5c":{"wy":"HN commenters repeatedly share it because it is an eye-opening look at screen-reader use, and because at about 6:30 it addresses how inaccessible Hacker News itself is.","q":{"x":"There's an amazing 10min !!Con 2016 talk by Sina Bahram","i":21909159,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":32260142,"author":"jonah-archive","month":"2022-07","story":"For blind internet users, the fix can be worse than the flaws","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on blind computing covering HN's inaccessibility","quote":"At around 6:30 he specifically addresses how inaccessible HN is to a visually impaired user","text":"I would recommend that everyone go check out Sian Bahram’s 2016 talk on computing while blind. At around 6:30 he specifically addresses how inaccessible HN is to a visually impaired user: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26074375,"author":"jonah-archive","month":"2021-02","story":"Let's Bring Spacer GIFs Back","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on blind computing that covers HN's inaccessibility","quote":"At around 6:30 he specifically addresses how inaccessible HN is to a visually impaired user","text":"I recommend that everyone go check out Sian Bahram’s 2016 talk on computing while blind. At around 6:30 he specifically addresses how inaccessible HN is to a visually impaired user: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21909159,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2019-12","story":"Blind software development at 450 words per minute (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ten-minute talk on coding and computing with a screen reader","quote":"There's an amazing 10min !!Con 2016 talk by Sina Bahram","text":"There's an amazing 10min !!Con 2016 talk by Sina Bahram, How I Code and Use a Computer at 1,000 WPM!! I use a computer very differently than most people, because I’m blind. When I’m surfing the web, tweeting, checking email, reading the news, and writing code, I’m doing so because a program called a screen reader is reading me what’s on the screen. I happen to listen to it read me this text at a thousand words per minute! Join me in listening to how I experience some common user interfaces. Yes, I’ll slow it down for you. I also have a challenge for everyone in the audience. Can you get through a day only using the keyboard? What about not looking at your screen? Sina Bahram is an accessibility consultant, researcher, and entrepreneur. He is the founder of Prime Access Consulting (PAC), an accessibility firm whose clients include high-tech startups, fortune 1000 companies, and both private and nationally-funded museums. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20865180,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2019-09","story":"Ask HN: Is anyone here visually disabled?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Blind developer shows how he uses a computer via screen reader","quote":"inspiring and incredible","text":"Hopefully this will be helpful: I found Sina Bahram's talk at !!Con 2016 How I Code and Use a Computer at 1,000 WPM!! inspiring and incredible. He's blind and has his computer read everything out ridiculously quickly, and apparently gets input that way much faster than sighted people can read! Amazing. I use a computer very differently than most people, because I’m blind. When I’m surfing the web, tweeting, checking email, reading the news, and writing code, I’m doing so because a program called a screen reader is reading me what’s on the screen. I happen to listen to it read me this text at a thousand words per minute! Join me in listening to how I experience some common user interfaces. Yes, I’ll slow it down for you. I also have a challenge for everyone in the audience. Can you get through a day only using the keyboard? What about not looking at your screen? Sina Bahram is an accessibility consultant, researcher, and entrepreneur. He is the founder of Prime Access Consulting (PAC), an accessibility firm whose clients include high-tech startups, fortune 1000 companies, and both private and nationally-funded museums. Talk: [VIDEO] Text copied from this page: [LINK]"},{"id":28667079,"author":"jonah-archive","month":"2021-09","story":"Apple’s app review prevents developer from submitting fix to game for the blind","stance":"rec","why":"Blind speaker explains HN's accessibility problems","quote":"Nothing that he mentioned has been addressed","text":"Here's a talk by Sina Bahram from five years ago -- at around 6:30 he specifically addresses how inaccessible HN is to a visually impaired user. Nothing that he mentioned has been addressed: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30255983,"author":"exikyut","month":"2022-02","story":"Tell HN: You can see your vouched and flagged items","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how HN sounds with a screen reader","quote":"Here's a blind person showing how Hacker News sounds over JAWS","text":"I lost the video a while back, then finally re-found it. Here's a blind person showing how Hacker News sounds over JAWS: [VIDEO] (Headphone warning: the volume is unfortunately all over the place, the screen reader is way too loud and the speaker volume is way too soft.) TL;DR, the page design places so much emphasis on visual table layout that it doesn't incorporate a single <h1> or <h2> anywhere , and it is effectively impossible to read the site rapidly without unconditionally listening to all the Hacker News. Page has 224 links. Hacker News visited Unlabeled graphic Graphic y18 link Hacker News link new vertical bar link past vertical bar link comments vertical bar link ask vertical bar link show vertical bar link jobs vertical bar link submit. link login at the top of every single page. I just got mad and got JAWS working in demo/evaluation mode. Here's what blind users hear every time they visit HN: Table with 3 columns and 61 rows. 1. link upvote link Scientists raise alarm over ‘dangerously fast’ growth in atmospheric methane left paren link nature.com right paren 115 points by link jnord link 1 hour ago vertical bar link hide vertical bar link 46 comments. 2. link upvote link Wine bricks saved the U.S. wine industry during Prohibition link vinepair.com right paren 131 points by link harambae link 4 hours ago vertical bar link hide vertical bar link 42 comments. 3. link upvote link SoftBank’s Sale of Arm to Nvidia Collapses, Arm to IPO left paren link reuters.com righ"},{"id":28758087,"author":"exikyut","month":"2021-10","story":"Ask HN: Is HN having problem keeping up today?","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates screen reader use as motivation for accessible HN","quote":"","text":"For what it's worth, my own desire to see if I can help out somehow has definitely only increased over the past few months as I've noticed the messages at the tops of threads about pagination. I don't have any particular skills that would make me a hand-in-glove fit (eg, I'd need to learn Arc...), but surely there are tons of little boring things I could usefully do that would take the heat off. If I'm thinking this way, surely there are (many?) others...? For example, something tiny that I'd love to fix is to split vote() in half and move the UI update logic into an Image.onload handler, so that the UI only changes if the upvote absolutely committed. I'm OCD about this because I actually use voting as bookmarking, and recently realized that some unknown percentage of my upvotes/bookmarks have sadly been lost because the auth= value had expired (eg in the case of a weeks-old tab) or because I was on bad 4G on my phone, but the UI state has no bearing on the network response. Something else that I'd be very happy to put effort into, after being told how to approach the problem :), would be to make the site screen-reader-accessible: [VIDEO] (size-XL volume warning - unfortunately the speaker volume is like 5% of the screen reader volume). A longer-term project that I also think would also be very useful would be to implement OAuth2, so that users would be able to safely attach external logins to their accounts without needing to supply their actual user passwords (which thankfu"}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1],["mmfjM-SGlGs",1]]},"GOGru_4z1Vc":{"wy":"Frequently recommended as a delightful, influential way to think about story structure.","q":{"x":"Vonnegut is really special. I always like coming back to his Shape of Stories talks","i":25078089,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":28234096,"author":"urxvtcd","month":"2021-08","story":"Ask HN: What's the most life-changing blog post you've ever read?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Vonnegut talk that had a personal impact","quote":"a piece of Kurt Vonnegut's talk randomly found on youtube had an impact on me","text":"Not a blog post, but a piece of Kurt Vonnegut's talk randomly found on youtube had an impact on me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25078089,"author":"bmitc","month":"2020-11","story":"Kurt Vonnegut, The Art of Fiction No. 64 (1977)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Longer Shape of Stories talk showing Vonnegut late in life","quote":"Vonnegut is really special. I always like coming back to his Shape of Stories talks","text":"Vonnegut is really special. I always like coming back to his Shape of Stories talks: [LINK] Here's a longer one: [VIDEO] It's interesting to see the difference in him with age. He seems to giggle more as an older man."},{"id":39359464,"author":"plussed_reader","month":"2024-02","story":"The emotional arcs of stories are dominated by six basic shapes","stance":"rec","why":"Later-in-life version of Vonnegut's Shape of Stories talk","quote":"I like the later-in-life version of the talk.","text":"You can find variations of this talk across his life but Vonneguts 'Shape of Stories' kept ringing in my head while going over this paper. I like the later-in-life version of the talk. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29334113,"author":"f0e4c2f7","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: How do I learn how to tell a good story?","stance":"rec","why":"Vonnegut on story shapes as a guide to telling stories","quote":"There is a video of Kurt Vonnegut talking about story telling called Shape of Stories thats pretty good.","text":"There is a video of Kurt Vonnegut talking about story telling called Shape of Stories thats pretty good. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27747911,"author":"sam_0123","month":"2021-07","story":"No More Movies","stance":"rec","why":"Explores the idea that stories have certain shapes","quote":"beautifully explored here among other places","text":"Yes, Hero's Journey plot ( [LINK] ) is very well understood and yet continues to sell well and generally the idea of certain shapes to stories is beautifully explored here among other places: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18851814,"author":"iaw","month":"2019-01","story":"At the Blackboard:  Kurt Vonnegut diagrams the shapes of stories (2005)","stance":"rec","why":"Vonnegut walks through his story shapes on a blackboard","quote":"There's an excellent video where he goes through these [0], worth a watch if you have the time.","text":"There's an excellent video where he goes through these [0], worth a watch if you have the time. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27752780,"author":"bmitc","month":"2021-07","story":"No More Movies","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture presentation of Vonnegut's story shapes","quote":"There are a couple of lectures posted on YouTube.","text":"That's what I meant by presentations of his shapes of stories. There are a couple of lectures posted on YouTube. [LINK] [VIDEO] The thinking originated in his (rejected) master's thesis at the University of Chicago. Here are some photos of some of the graphs: [LINK] Even there, in an incomplete copy, there are seven unique story shapes. And there's a major difference between saying here are some shapes of some stories and here are the only story shapes."}],"r":[["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["pSHj5UKSylk",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["wJyUtbn0O5Y",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["-J_xL4IGhJA",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["2KSahAoOLdU",1],["pOmXiapfCs8",1],["oP3c1h8v2ZQ",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["xguam0TKMw8",1],["BDqvzFY72mg",1],["taZJblMAuko",1],["T71ibcZAX3I",1]]},"bHIhgxav9LY":{"wy":"Often recommended as an eye-opening field-based explanation of electricity, though it was also controversial and criticized as pedantic.","q":{"x":"It’s an eye opening alternative explanation","i":43149571,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":39173600,"author":"lchengify","month":"2024-01","story":"Why is Maxwell's theory so hard to understand? (2007) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how fields, not electrons, carry energy in circuits","quote":"I highly recommend watching Veritasium's YouTube video on electricity","text":"Only tangentially related, but I highly recommend watching Veritasium's YouTube video on electricity if you're curious as to how Maxwell's fields create the current / amp abstractions in EE [1]. It's a common misconception that electrons or current transfer energy. In reality it's the electric field that exists between the wires that is doing the heavy lifting, the electrons in the wires are just controlling the field. This has always confused me and I was very irritated when I first learned electromagnetics about how rote all the initial learnings are. I wish more work was put earlier into making everything relate back to Maxwell's equations to make it make sense. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37638444,"author":"jerf","month":"2023-09","story":"How the Most Useless Branch of Math Could Save Your Life","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Channel's electricity videos proved correct despite controversy and debunking attempts","quote":"some of his recent videos have rocked the boat even among physicists and IMHO he was ultimately proved to be correct for them","text":"The video in question for reference: [LINK] Interestingly, when I saw that I figured this channel for a goner because he was going to turn to the darkside, but quite contrary to expectations I feel like his channel has actually blossomed since then. His older videos are fine, but often just explaining concepts that you can find a lot of other explanations on, but some of his recent videos have rocked the boat even among physicists and IMHO he was ultimately proved to be correct for them. I particularly point at his videos about how nobody has measured the one-way speed of light ( [LINK] ), which was well-known among people very very deep in the field of relativity but not very well known outside of that (even amongst physicists in other fields, let alone lay people) and the electricity videos he's done in the last year ( [VIDEO] , [LINK] ) which attracted a whole whackload of \"debunking\" videos, which were themselves wrong (the best \"bunking\" video, if I may call it that, is probably AlphaPheonix's: [LINK] , since he actually set up the experiment, albeit on a smaller scale). They aren't all of that caliber, but the knot theory video was very good too and not something I've seen a hundred times elsewhere, and the airships video that preceded that one clearly had a high degree of research put into it beyond just the hype."},{"id":49106407,"author":"leonidasrup","month":"2026-07","story":"A.I. Companies Are Recruiting Electricians and Carpenters by the Thousands","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction to how electricity works","quote":"Good intrpduction are Veritasium videos","text":"Good intrpduction are Veritasium videos: \"How Electricity Actually Works\" [LINK] \"The Biggest Misconception About Electricity\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":43149499,"author":"H8crilA","month":"2025-02","story":"But good sir, what is electricity?","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible explanation of electricity beyond the water analogy","quote":"there is an accessible video that explains electricity pretty well.","text":"The problem with (or the advantage of) the water flowing analogy, or even more broadly the discrete element model, is that it explains reality good enough to be used in most practical situations. Schematics are ubiquitous, yes they are \"fake\", but they are also usually \"correct enough\". Kind of like the incorrect Bohr's model of electrons orbiting the nucleus actually does explain the emission spectra (up to a point). But there is an accessible video that explains electricity pretty well. Veritasium - The Big Misconception About Electricity: [VIDEO] There is one commonly used concept that requires understanding electricity correctly, and not just as a combination of waterhoses and gizmos. It's impedance, and it directly corresponds to the \"controversial\" experiment that Veritasium is proposing in his video. Impedance breaks the pipe-of-electrons analogy."},{"id":43149571,"author":"djsamseng","month":"2025-02","story":"But good sir, what is electricity?","stance":"rec","why":"Eye-opening field-theory explanation of electricity","quote":"It’s an eye opening alternative explanation","text":"There's also the field theory of electricity: [VIDEO] It’s an eye opening alternative explanation to the electrons flowing like a chain theory of this article."},{"id":40750133,"author":"poorman","month":"2024-06","story":"Wireless power transfer system achieves 270-kilowatt charge","stance":"rec","why":"Explains electricity as field around the wire rather than flow","quote":"This video explains it really well","text":"I dove deep into this topic after reading your post and discovered something fascinating: electricity doesn't actually flow through wires in the conventional way I was taught. It's more about the electromagnetic field around the wire. This video explains it really well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29922375,"author":"123pie123","month":"2022-01","story":"5.5 mm in 1.25 nanoseconds","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting take on speed of electrons versus fields","quote":"this is an interesting video (to me anyway!!) on the speed of electrons vs fields","text":"this is an interesting video (to me anyway!!) on the speed of electrons vs fields The Big Misconception About Electricity.. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43157620,"author":"fransje26","month":"2025-02","story":"But good sir, what is electricity?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"So if I understand this correctly, the information is moving along the wire, at a speed between 60 to 100% of c. This brings me to a Veritasium video of a few years back that I didn't quite understand at the time. [1] The claim being made was that if you connected a light bulb to a switch, with 300'000 km of wire left and right, and if the switch and the light bulb were 1 m away, the light would turn on in 1/c seconds. But this would imply that the information travel is not along the wires, but straight from the switch to the light bulb? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36962080,"author":"dlivingston","month":"2023-08","story":"A Room-Temperature Superconductor? New Developments","stance":"reference","why":"Current is a state of the electromagnetic field, not literal electron flow","quote":"current flow is not a literal flow of electrons, but a state of the electromagnetic field","text":"Sorry, can you explain? My understanding is that transistors don't \"dump\" electrons anywhere. The gate controls the voltage, which in the `0` state forces resistance to be high enough s.t. current flow through that transistor stops. As the Veritasium video explains [0], current flow is not a literal flow of electrons, but a state of the electromagnetic field (or something to that effect... it's been many years since my electromagnetism university courses). [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36919491,"author":"H8crilA","month":"2023-07","story":"U.S. Marine Corps Antenna Handbook (1999) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Energy flows around the wire, not inside it","quote":"The energy doesn't flow inside of a wire, it flows around the wire.","text":"Circuit theory is demonstrably wrong. The energy doesn't flow inside of a wire, it flows around the wire. [LINK] [VIDEO] It is a very useful tool in practica."},{"id":31137219,"author":"4oo4","month":"2022-04","story":"The Man Who Accidentally Killed the Most People in History","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I suppose this one isn't too bad in that regard, I think I was mainly thinking of this video: [VIDEO] This video about leaded gasoline makes a solid case and the science and history are there to back up that Kettering caused the lead poisoning on this scale, but I think it's a stretch, or a gross oversimplification to say he killed the most people in history. I guess I expect slightly more scientific rigor and caution from someone like him (though, I understand that YouTube videos aren't expected to withstand the scientific method). With this video, I think it comes down to the title. Saying he killed the most people is a reach, I think saying he poisoned the most people is probably more accurate. That's not saying anything to discredit how bad lead poisoning is and that it does kill people, but if you're going to accuse someone of being a mass murderer I think you need to make your claims really precisely and have data that really clearly shows a causation and not just correlation. The video titles ironically seemed to get more clickbait-y after he released this video: [LINK]"},{"id":30860445,"author":"alberth","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: How does a CPU communicate with a GPU?","stance":"reference","why":"Signals travel through fields around wires","quote":"Veritasium explains","text":"Nit pick… Technically it’s not “through” the electrical wires, it’s actually through the electrical field created around the electrical wires. Veritasium explains [VIDEO]"},{"id":30064975,"author":"tzs","month":"2022-01","story":"DC Circuit Water Analogy (1998)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Veritasium did a video on the topic in late 2021 that didn't really present things as well and ended up being quite controversial","text":"> Anything involving the electrical energy not inside wires has no water analogue (ie the electromagnetic waves inside a transformer) You don't even need fancy things like transformers for energy not in the wires to be important. Even a simple DC circuit consisting of a battery in series with a light bulb mostly involves an electromagnetic field to transfer energy from the battery to the light bulb, which mostly takes place outside the wires. The function of the wires when it comes to energy transfer is to carry moving charge which creates the magnetic part of the electromagnetic field that actually carries the transferred energy. Here's a pretty good explanation [1]. That video was from January 2019, and not controversial. Veritasium did a video on the topic in late 2021 that didn't really present things as well and ended up being quite controversial [2]. Other well-known YouTube channels such as EEVblog [3] and ElectroBOOM [4] responded. There was also someone who bought a bunch of wire and did the experiment as described in the Veritasium video (although scaled down). That was discussed on HN and that discussion contains some very interesting links [5]. In particular the link to a talk by Rick Hartley in this subthread is very informative [6]. He talks about how most EMI problems in PCB designs are due to people not taking into account the the energy is not in the wires. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK]"},{"id":29441122,"author":"VHRanger","month":"2021-12","story":"ADSL works over wet string (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Energy flows via fields around wires, not electrons in them","quote":"","text":"That's not exactly correct. Electrons don't flow through wires - there's a one-way flow of the ambient electrons around the wire from the field created. See here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29604352,"author":"Aengeuad","month":"2021-12","story":"I bought 1000 meters of wire to settle a physics debate [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"its omission does lend some credibility to the complaints in the parent comments that the video style is intentionally pedantic","text":"The term 'transmission line theory' (or even 'transmission' for that matter) doesn't appear to have been mentioned in the video at all[0], and this is a complaint that was also raised by Dave Jones[1] in his critique of sorts. The video is fundamentally about transmission line problems and the term is mentioned both in the description and the word document containing further analysis, but its omission does lend some credibility to the complaints in the parent comments that the video style is intentionally pedantic (it is a pure physics vs practical electrical engineering take after all) and presented as a controversy which comes off as a bit shallow as a result. If one were to put on a tinfoil hat it doesn't seem like much of a stretch to imagine that the plan was to have a follow up video to settle the controversy with the gist of the video being about transmission line theory and concluding with a practical demonstration of the effect. [0] I don't have the time to carefully watch a 15 minute video to verify this, and demonstrating a lack of evidence seems difficult, but the subtitles for the video can easily be downloaded with youtube-dl and then grepped or opened with a regular text editor. Note that these subtitles are manually written and not auto-generated by youtube, and while it's possible there's some differences in the script and what is said it seems unlikely. youtube-dl --write-sub --sub-lang en --skip-download [VIDEO] grep -i 'transmission' 'The Big Misconception"}],"r":[["oI_X2cMHNe0",2],["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["r6Rp-uo6HmI",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["bL6hp8BKB24",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1],["yL_-1d9OSdk",1],["kPRA0W1kECg",1],["cefnmjtAolY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["GFMyMxMYDNk",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"0SARbwvhupQ":{"wy":"HN regulars recommend it as a career-shaping talk on code review, humility and collaborative development.","q":{"x":"One of the best talks about code reviews and similiar things","i":12637602,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":39034779,"author":"sethops1","month":"2024-01","story":"FAQ on Leaving Google","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk that shifted a programmer toward professional engineering thinking.","quote":"Two talks given by Ben Collins-Sussman absolutely changed my career path","text":"Two talks given by Ben Collins-Sussman absolutely changed my career path from being a hot headed programmer to thinking like a professional engineer. The Myth of the Genius Programmer: [VIDEO] The Art of Organizational Manipulation: [LINK] I rewatch these every few years, or before an interview. Puts me back in the right headspace. If you're reading this Ben, thank you."},{"id":12637602,"author":"oleksiyp","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Top talk on code reviews and collaboration.","quote":"One of the best talks about code reviews and similiar things","text":"Google I/O 2009 - The Myth of the Genius Programmer One of the best talks about code reviews and similiar things [VIDEO]"},{"id":2489085,"author":"bajsejohannes","month":"2011-04","story":"Your taste is why your work disappoints you.","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk that changed their view on publishing unpolished code.","quote":"I recommend The Myth of The Genius Programmer","text":"I recommend The Myth of The Genius Programmer: [VIDEO] What you see on Github is people's polished code - you don't see what they are throwing away. They might be better, but you are probably looking at some of the code they are most proud of. (Actually, for my github/bitbucket page this is no longer the case. After I watched this video, I started publishing as much of my code as possible, if it actually did what it was supposed to)"},{"id":16375282,"author":"maze-le","month":"2018-02","story":"Ask HN: Is there no place for people, who are average at something?","stance":"rec","why":"Touches on being average and the genius programmer myth.","quote":"Interesting video, that touches the issue","text":"Most people are average, thats kind of the meaning of average. I consider myself an average programmer advancing on an average career path and had plenty of recruiters contact me with interesting opportunities recently. Sure its not Google or Facebook, but so what, I wouln't like to work for a mega coorporation like that anyway. If you doubt yourself because you consider yourself average, consider that the constant talk of excellence, highest skilled people and so forth is mostly HR PR bullshit. Sure, there are extremeley talented people out there, but they are pretty rare. Interesting video, that touches the issue: \"The myth of the genius programmer\"[0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8272883,"author":"coppolaemilio","month":"2014-09","story":"Ask HN: What do you do when you realize you are not a programmer?","stance":"rec","why":"Reassurance that you needn't be a genius programmer.","quote":"keep enjoying creating things. A google talk","text":"\"I hated discussing what OO pattern should be used\" \"they submit patches to major frameworks, writes things in assembly for fun\" You don't have to do this kind of things to be a programmer. Of course discussing OO patterns with your team is a need but submitting patches to major frameworks is on your free time! If every programmer would do so we wouldn't have so many incomplete open software. Try not to be chased by the \"The Myth of the Genius Programmer\" and keep enjoying creating things. A google talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18543785,"author":"octosphere","month":"2018-11","story":"Ask HN: As a programmer, how do you know if you're a good one or not?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for further research on the bus factor.","quote":"Further research into the Bus Factor can be found here","text":"Some basic heuristics I use for determining the capability of other programmers and myself: 1.) Does the code have a good bus factor i.e If the maintainer dies tomorrow by being knocked down by a bus, can other people pick up where he/she left off? Further research into the Bus Factor can be found here: [VIDEO] 2.) Is the code well documented? This is so obvious that it is painful to write this comment, but it is a very simple heuristic for determining capability. Documentation can include code comments. 3.) Is the code resilient to weird input? The code should perform well under heavy fuzzing and where possible, sanitize different input. It should be tested against this: [LINK]"},{"id":7687461,"author":"mqsiuser","month":"2014-05","story":"What Programming is Like","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"So, just as a reference: \"Google I/O 2009 - The Myth of the Genius Programmer\": [VIDEO] There are quite a couple of possible views in IT, which makes it hard for us"},{"id":3210422,"author":"RyanMcGreal","month":"2011-11","story":"Nimbus.io: Open-source alternative to Amazon S3","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's open source insofar as the code, when it is released, will be released under an open licence. However, it fails the 'unofficial' criterion of a project that is developed in the open . See also: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JjDsP5n2kSM",1],["e3K5UxWRRuY",1],["r6sGWTCMz2k",1],["WRdJCFEqFTU",1],["J-M98KLgaUU",1]]},"Y9FZ4igNxNA":{"wy":"Recommended as one of the best explanations of Fourier transforms and a clear demo of analog optical computing.","q":{"x":"The video happens to have one of the best explanations of Fourier transforms I've yet encountered.","i":28666285,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":32581661,"author":"jstrieb","month":"2022-08","story":"AI-designed camera only records objects of interest while being blind to others","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fourier optics pattern recognition with a great Fourier transform explanation.","quote":"The video happens to have one of the best explanations of Fourier transforms I've yet encountered.","text":"Reminds me of this really cool video about using Fourier Optics for optical pattern recognition.[1] The video happens to have one of the best explanations of Fourier transforms I've yet encountered. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31658351,"author":"orlp","month":"2022-06","story":"Photonic Chip Performs Image Recognition at the Speed of Light","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Analog image recognition using Fourier optics.","quote":"I can highly recommend this video","text":"For a somewhat related topic (analog image recognition), for those interested, I can highly recommend this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28666285,"author":"jstrieb","month":"2021-09","story":"Moiré No More","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fourier optics for pattern recognition with great Fourier transform explanation.","quote":"The video happens to have one of the best explanations of Fourier transforms I've yet encountered.","text":"I was introduced to this idea in a really cool video about using Fourier Optics for optical pattern recognition.[0] The video happens to have one of the best explanations of Fourier transforms I've yet encountered. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42877977,"author":"infogulch","month":"2025-01","story":"Detecting edges of images at the speed of light","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Fourier transform, then demos a physical filter for matching.","quote":"Huygens Optics has a great video","text":"Huygens Optics has a great video \"Fourier Optics used for Optical Pattern Recognition\". He starts with explaining the Fourier Transform wrt audio, then demonstrates a physical filter that screens the resulting convolution pattern to identify matches. Very cool: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40429183,"author":"CamperBob2","month":"2024-05","story":"AI Needs Enormous Computing Power. Could Light-Based Chips Help?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows a lens acting as a Fourier transformer.","quote":"See the excellent videos by Huygens Optics including this one","text":"You could think of a lens as a \"Fourier transformer,\" not in the LLM sense directly but in the sense of something that executes a Fourier transform. See the excellent videos by Huygens Optics including this one: [VIDEO] entitled \"Fourier optics used for optical pattern recognition.\" Having established that, well, it's not hard to imagine that something that's good at running Fourier transforms is probably good for other situations where you need to run a lot of dot products in parallel. That, in turn, should sound like an awfully familiar problem..."},{"id":31586515,"author":"ulnarkressty","month":"2022-06","story":"Physicists are building neural networks out of vibrations, voltages and lasers","stance":"rec","why":"Lens produces a Fourier transform, used for analog pattern matching.","quote":"There is an interesting video explaining this on the Huygen Optics Youtube channel[2].","text":"An even better one - holding an image at the focal point of a lens produces its Fourier transform at the focal point on the other side of the lens[0]. It is used for \"analog\" pattern matching[1]. There is an interesting video explaining this on the Huygen Optics Youtube channel[2]. [0] - [LINK] [1] - [LINK] [2] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":42963400,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2025-02","story":"Pre-Trained Large Language Models Use Fourier Features for Addition (2024)","stance":"reference","why":"Diffraction pattern as Fourier transform of aperture.","quote":"","text":"IDK, the more I learn the more it seems to me that Fourier transform is reality's cheat code. It keeps showing up everywhere. Like, the other day I learned[0] that if you shine a light through a small opening, the diffraction pattern you get on the other side is basically the Fourier transform of the aperture outline. (Yes, this also implies that if you take a Fourier transform of an image and make a diffraction grating off the resulting pattern, projecting light through it should paint you the original image.) -- [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":39029690,"author":"Balgair","month":"2024-01","story":"Fluorite lenses: Corrective capabilities beyond ordinary optical glass","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Upping a cousin comment. [VIDEO] Adding that, again, optics is a difficult and frustrating field. Don't worry that you're struggling to connect it. It took me a few years in an optics lab working hands on with light every day to really come around."},{"id":32585122,"author":"aaaaaaaaaaab","month":"2022-08","story":"AI-designed camera only records objects of interest while being blind to others","stance":"reference","why":"Example of optical pattern recognition.","quote":"","text":"Optical pattern recognition is an established field. Here's a simple version via Fresnel zone plates: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5MxH1sfJLBQ",1],["2A2NY29iQdI",1],["j5v8D-alAKE",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["p8u_k2LIZyo",1],["Ecy1FBdCRbQ",1],["htYh-Tq7ZBI",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["ne99laPUxN4",1],["RjD1aLm4Thg",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["5RpPTRcz1no",1],["KdfHVcU8U7U",1],["tX4H_ctggYo",1]]},"jN7mSXMruEo":{"wy":"It is HN's default link whenever vehicle size comes up, with commenters recommending it for the history of the light-truck loophole and the safety effects of tall bumpers.","q":{"x":"a very relevant video about the very real problems that SUVs and modern trucks have on urban environments.","i":35049727,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46649903,"author":"culi","month":"2026-01","story":"Canada slashes 100% tariffs on Chinese EVs to 6%","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep dive on how US light-truck rules led to ballooning vehicles and deaths.","quote":"Here's a video I wish I could make every American watch","text":"Here's a video I wish I could make every American watch [VIDEO] It provides a deep-dive video into the history of how we got to the situation we're in today with American cars exploding in size. It actually has its origins in Obama-era legislation for emissions standards that made an exception for \"light trucks\". SUVs are legally classified as light trucks so the industry has massively pushed these tanks onto the consumer promising more safety. It has led to a dramatic decrease in public safety and pedestrian deaths that is unique to the US. One contributor to these deaths is literally parents running over their own children in their own driveways. THIS IS NOT SOMETHING THAT IS HAPPENING IN ANY OTHER COUNTRY. The video goes over the visibility issues with these trucks, how our safety regulations fail to account for them (light trucks only need to be tested in collision with other light trucks) and also covers how modern trucks have the same carrying capacity as pickup trucks from 30 years ago (the main thing that's increased is the hull and cabin size) while being harder to use for actual work since the bed is higher offer the ground"},{"id":35584996,"author":"pastacacioepepe","month":"2023-04","story":"SUVs more dangerous to cyclists than other cars, study suggests","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why tall bumper heights make cars more dangerous","quote":"Just do yourself a favour and watch this video[0]","text":"Just do yourself a favour and watch this video[0], you might learn why SUVs and in general cars with a taller bumper height are more dangerous. .0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35094573,"author":"eska","month":"2023-03","story":"Building a city optimized for bikes is a choice","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why SUVs are popular in the US","quote":"This documentary was quite eye-opening for me","text":"As a European I was really surprised why SUVs are so popular in the US, making biking extremely dangerous. This documentary was quite eye-opening for me: [VIDEO] Similarly in Germany we’re building a new Autobahn in the middle of a city (Berlin) under extreme costs, while still neglecting investment in trains, because we’re a car country."},{"id":38754424,"author":"turquoisevar","month":"2023-12","story":"Tesla Has a Design Problem","stance":"rec","why":"Extensive look at light truck regulations","quote":"Not Just Bikes has a pretty extensive video on it","text":"I’m not sure how it relates to passenger safety regulations, but it is true that in the US there is a lower set of fuel efficiency and safety requirements for light trucks. And SUVs are classified as light trucks, hence the push by manufacturers for people to buy SUVs. Not Just Bikes has a pretty extensive video on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37325443,"author":"rurounijones","month":"2023-08","story":"Weight-based motor vehicle tax","stance":"rec","why":"Intro to light truck loophole and SUV popularity","quote":"NotJustBikes did a good intro video on the subject.","text":"> That's the first time I hear this argument. SUVs and the like exploit a loophole in various standards like fuel efficiency requirements because they are not \"cars\" but \"Light trucks\" and therefore those standards don't apply to them; which makes them cheaper to design and build for automotive companies. The \"Light trucks\" category was supposed to be used for light commercial vehicles, not personal vehicles but then the law of unintended consequences had a love-child with profit seeking corporations and their linguistic games. Literally the only reason SUVs and trucks became popular is because the automotive manufacturers could make more money doing it that way and convinced the consumers to buy them. NotJustBikes did a good intro video on the subject. * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":35417799,"author":"crooked-v","month":"2023-04","story":"Giving kids no autonomy at all has become a parenting norm","stance":"rec","why":"Statistics and visuals on modern vehicle grill heights","quote":"which includes various statistics and some striking visual examples of the absolutely absurd grill heights of modern production vehicles.","text":"On that note, there's the video essay \"These Stupid Trucks are Literally Killing Us\" [VIDEO] , which includes various statistics and some striking visual examples of the absolutely absurd grill heights of modern production vehicles."},{"id":35650037,"author":"kinjba11","month":"2023-04","story":"Rural Americans are importing tiny Japanese pickup trucks","stance":"rec","why":"Covers increasing vehicle sizes in the US","quote":"made a video about increasing vehicle sizes in the US","text":"Not Just Bikes, a YouTuber made a video about increasing vehicle sizes in the US and why you may be interested in [VIDEO]"},{"id":35650950,"author":"m000","month":"2023-04","story":"Rural Americans are importing tiny Japanese pickup trucks","stance":"rec","why":"How trucks overtook cars in the US, and the legislation","quote":"explains in detail how \"trucks\" overtook \"cars\" as the most popular vehicle in the US, and legislation that lead to that.","text":"These Stupid Trucks are Literally Killing Us [1] explains in detail how \"trucks\" overtook \"cars\" as the most popular vehicle in the US, and legislation that lead to that. (Quotes because the words refer to the legal definition of trucks/cars.) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35046671,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-03","story":"Pickup Trucks: From Workhorse to Joyride","stance":"rec","why":"Covers CAFE and chicken tax effects on truck sizes","quote":"which does go over those things","text":"> Article didn't mention CAFE (which is the reason for larger sizes) nor chicken tax (which makes, even if they qualified under CAFE, foreign small trucks economically non-viable). I was pointed to the article from the just-posted Not Just Bikes video \"These Stupid Trucks are Literally Killing Us\", which does go over those things: * [VIDEO] The point of the Axios article, which comes up at the 25m25s point in the NJB video, goes towards the point that the pickup truck has actually become less practical over the decades. And to the idea that most folks would be better served with a station wagon or perhaps a minivan. Trucks have gone from the cargo bed being >60% of the usable length (ignoring the engine), to the (crew) cab being >60% of the useable length. Pickup trucks gone from thing-haulers to people-haulers, in which case, why bother with that form factor?"},{"id":35046720,"author":"vanilla_nut","month":"2023-03","story":"Pickup Trucks: From Workhorse to Joyride","stance":"rec","why":"Video on the growth of trucks","quote":"Not Just Bikes video on the same subject today","text":"Not Just Bikes video on the same subject today: [VIDEO] It's crazy to see how much trucks have blown up in the last few years. They clog up parking lots, barely fit in parking garages (let alone personal garages), blind me at night with their too-high too-bright headlights, and completely block cross-street vision for anything except other trucks when they park on the street. A complete failure of US govt regulation. At this point I hope gas prices go through the roof and punish these truck owners for their selfish decisionmaking."},{"id":35049727,"author":"estebank","month":"2023-03","story":"Pedestrian Traffic Fatalities by State: 2022 Preliminary Data","stance":"rec","why":"Problems SUVs and trucks cause in urban environments","quote":"a very relevant video about the very real problems that SUVs and modern trucks have on urban environments.","text":"Not Just Bikes just released a very relevant video about the very real problems that SUVs and modern trucks have on urban environments. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48649252,"author":"genxy","month":"2026-06","story":"The Deadly Rise of Giant Trucks and S.U.V.s","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as previously discussed video on dangerous truck design.","quote":"","text":"The Grille Trend that Kills 509 People per Year [LINK] video discussed previously [LINK] These Stupid Trucks are Literally Killing Us [VIDEO] The original designer wanted the truck to feel like you were driving a fist, punching through the air. So we are killing people so that the driver can have an aggressive aesthetic. And the design has spread like a contagious meme, even BMWs have it now."},{"id":46131554,"author":"CalRobert","month":"2025-12","story":"Accepting US car standards would risk European lives","stance":"reference","why":"Broader video on oversized trucks, cited with a newer related video.","quote":"A couple years ago he also made a video about these trucks more broadly","text":"NotJustBikes just put out a video about this issue - [LINK] A couple years ago he also made a video about these trucks more broadly - [VIDEO] What's truly maddening is how many of these vehicles which _do not_ meet European safety standards are _already_ in Europe. Walk around Hilversum in the Netherlands and you will see plenty of Dodge Rams (mostly 1500's, but there's even a 2500 Dually usually parked on the sidewalk (\"pavement \"for Brits) where my kids used to go to school). They're imported under \"Individual Vehicle Approval\" rules, exempting them from type safety requirements, and on top of that are almost always registered as \"business vehicles\" (you can tell from the V plate) which means they pay an absolute pittance in tax. I moved here to get away from American kindercrushers (among other reasons) and I am profoundly concerned that Europe is being invaded by these machines. (Edit) Worth noting is that a lot of Dutch street design is based on the idea that people _can_ share space with cars in dense, low speed environments, but that assumption flies out the window when the vehicles are so large you can't even see a kid walking or biking to school. Further edit - source - [LINK] 5,000 Dodge Rams imported in to Europe in 2023 alone."},{"id":41626683,"author":"vfclists","month":"2024-09","story":"London saw a surprising benefit to ultra-low emissions zone: More active kids","stance":"reference","why":"Linked alongside argument about oversized vehicles endangering lesser road users.","quote":"","text":"Why don't cyclists use cycle lanes - [LINK] [VIDEO] Can you think of why it is a good idea for vehicles which weigh 2000kg and more to be used to transport 90kg loads all day long at needless risk to lesser road users? Why can't the motor vehicle industry develop smaller powered vehicles sheltered from the elements for personal transport, something not much more than a 3 wheeled scooter with a canopy? As a technically aware guy does that really make sense? Motor vehicles as they are are primarily recreational vehicles and status symbols, not means of moving 100kg individuals and their handbags or briefcases if they are carrying any around town."},{"id":40856753,"author":"hayertjez","month":"2024-07","story":"Car Crash Deaths Involving Cannabis on the Rise","stance":"reference","why":"Shows cars and trucks growing bigger, heavier and more dangerous.","quote":"cars are allowed to get bigger, heavier and thus more dangerous (SUV and Pickup trucks)","text":"Meanwhile cars are allowed to get bigger, heavier and thus more dangerous (SUV and Pickup trucks). See [VIDEO] Not as an excuse for a driver to take seat after drinking too much alcohol or taking cannabis. I find it a rearguard action by concentrating too much on the driver and not on the weapon (car) itself. In my opinion a more difficult but more worthwhile is to make infrastructure more safe in general."},{"id":38835265,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2024-01","story":"In a win for the climate (and safety), urban US speed limits are dropping","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"How would you force automakers to do so if Congress or executive branch agencies won’t change light vehicle and truck regulations? Some change is happening, albeit slowly (with regards to the Manual on Uniform Traffic Control Devices for Streets and Highways). [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":38990449,"author":"socksy","month":"2024-01","story":"Cars suck, man","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as backing for claim about SUV regulation and manufacturer incentives.","quote":"","text":"SUVs are pushed by manufacturers due to their relative lack of regulation in the US, and so the more money they can make from them. Safety and car seat sizes have nothing to do with it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36042486,"author":"Cntrl-Frk","month":"2023-05","story":"An auto CEO came close to saying the right thing about heavy EV batteries","stance":"reference","why":"Heavy truck trend and legislation behind it","quote":"the heavy truck trend has been going for some time","text":"As presented by 'Not Just Bikes' [1] the heavy truck trend has been going for some time. The legislation around larger trucks is different and has a stronger profit motive. Curious to see an exec talking about the trend as a negative. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35714375,"author":"jterrys","month":"2023-04","story":"Chevy Bolt EV and Bolt EUV production is ending this year","stance":"reference","why":"Manufacturers build trucks and SUVs to avoid emission regulations","quote":"many U.S manufacturers opt into building Trucks and SUVs to avoid emission regulations","text":"But that's not at all the reason why. Even the article explains it. They sold well but Chevrolet's quality control is bad. They lost money because of recalls and lack of good control processes to make these cars. They ran a cost benefit analysis and said, \"fuck it, we'll continue making shittier cars\". It also doesn't help that many U.S manufacturers opt into building Trucks and SUVs to avoid emission regulations: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35203437,"author":"VoodooJuJu","month":"2023-03","story":"These Stupid Trucks are Literally Killing Us","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I can't listen to videos like this where the narrator uses emotional affectations in a scripted report.","text":"I can't listen to videos like this where the narrator uses emotional affectations in a scripted report. > [VIDEO] >They're just... BAD for cities. Really bad. > [VIDEO] >We're constantly being told that the astronomical growth in the average size of motor vehicles would infringe on the \"\"\" FREEDOMS \"\"\" of people to do whatever they want. It's cringe, unnatural, and distracts from the message. Present the logic of your argument in a neutral manner and leave it to the listener for emotional interpretation - don't force it."}],"r":[["040ejWnFkj0",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["S0dSm_ClcSw",1],["wVp5koAOu9M",1],["86gyW0vUmVs",1],["ZD_3_gsgsnk",1],["vSSkDos2hzo",1],["gUnxzVOs3rk",1],["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1]]},"s_fkpZSnz2I":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a funny, fascinating talk on cuneiform tablets and the ancient world by an engaging curator.","q":{"x":"equal parts fascinating and hilarious","i":37343591,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":45924410,"author":"Jun8","month":"2025-11","story":"Why I'm Learning Sumerian","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious talk on Noah's Ark by a British Museum curator","quote":"Irving Finkel, a British Museum curator, is a treasure.","text":"If you are interesting anything related to cuneiform writing or ancient Mesopotamian culture , Irving Finkel, a British Museum curator, is a treasure. Here he is teaching how to write cuneiform: [LINK] Here is a hilarious talk he gave at Chicago's Oriental Institute on Noah's Ark: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45537207,"author":"AndrewOMartin","month":"2025-10","story":"How to write in Cuneiform","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture on the cuneiform Noah's flood tablet and ark-building","quote":"Excellent lecture","text":"Not to mention his book, about when he found an Cuneiform tablet circa 1800BC telling the story of Noah and the flood, which included more detailed instructions on how to build an ark than the biblical accounts and so he went and built it. Nice article: [LINK] Excellent lecture: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37343591,"author":"cjs_ac","month":"2023-08","story":"7th century BCE Library of Ashurbanipal is the oldest recovered library","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Finkel on discovering the Babylonian Noah's Ark tablet","quote":"equal parts fascinating and hilarious","text":"This lecture by Dr Finkel on how he discovered the original Babylonian version of the Noah's Ark story is equal parts fascinating and hilarious: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44493518,"author":"cjs_ac","month":"2025-07","story":"Hymn to Babylon, missing for a millennium, has been discovered","stance":"rec","why":"Amusing story of finding the oldest Noah's Ark version","quote":"telling the surprisingly amusing story of how he discovered the oldest known version of the Noah's Ark story","text":"Somewhat related, since we're talking about cuneiform: Dr. Irving Finkel of the British Museum telling the surprisingly amusing story of how he discovered the oldest known version of the Noah's Ark story: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28064170,"author":"lalos","month":"2021-08","story":"Australian mathematician discovers applied geometry on 3,700-year-old tablet","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on clay tablets and ancient technology","quote":"Great talk for those interested in clay tablets and technology","text":"Great talk for those interested in clay tablets and technology: [VIDEO] In my opinion, I would bet that those old civilizations had more technology that we give them credit for. The only thing they lacked was to have a long lasting medium to share that knowledge. Whimsical stories or songs could've been a way to share advances. We're lucky to have one civilization picking clay tablets as a medium and luckier that those survived (some even by accident - fires destroying houses but cooking those tablets and burying them), who knows maybe we were writing on leather for centuries before that have all disintegrated by now (we hunted, we had access to paint, we naturally don't want to waste scarce resources)."}],"r":[["9KABcmczPdg",1],["q0RX59G2jaA",1],["03p2CADwGF8",1],["vtWp45Eewtw",1],["p33CVV29OG8",1],["yReDbeY7ZMU",1],["dpw9EHDh2bM",1],["mPn2AdMH7UQ",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1],["XVmsfL5LG90",1],["d4EgbgTm0Bg",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1]]},"x2H60ritjag":{"wy":"HN commenters cite her as the explanation for why soil biology, not added fertilizer, drives plant nutrition and farm results.","q":{"x":"Dr Elaine Ingham delivers a salient presentation.","i":34637433,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":41951604,"author":"schiffern","month":"2024-10","story":"In the US, regenerative farming practices require unlearning past advice","stance":"rec","why":"Healthy soil makes fertility from rock, air and rain","quote":"Direct from soil microbiologist Dr Elaine Ingham","text":"> Living soil consumes rock minerals making them available to plants. Thank you. This is one of those super-underappreciated biological facts. Industrial ag treats soil like it's a tank of fertility, and guess who sells the refills? Biologists know that healthy soil is a factory , making new fertility out of rock/air/rain/sun. Direct from soil microbiologist Dr Elaine Ingham: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38667231,"author":"vram22","month":"2023-12","story":"How the nutritional value of staple crops dropped by 50% since the 1960s","stance":"rec","why":"Soils have plenty of nutrients; soil structure and life limit","quote":"One of her most astonishing finds / claims is that","text":"Yes. I keep sharing Gabe Brown's three-part video series titled Treating the Farm as an Ecosystem, now and then, here on HN, whenever such threads come up. Some really solid practical and pragmatic stuff there, on regenerative agriculture, which, as I understand it [1], goes beyond organic farming, and is related to many of the issues talked about in this thread. [1] I had done organic gardening successfully, for a few years, some time earlier, so I have at least some practice and experience (and also a lot of reading and thinking about what I read) as the basis for my opinions of his work and those of others I mention, such as Elaine Ingham (a video by her is below too). One of her most astonishing finds / claims is that (IIRC, I saw the video a few years ago), almost all soils anywhere on earth have more than enough nutrients for plants for many many years. She said the real issue and limitation is the lack of soil organic content and soil structure and mycorrhiza ( [LINK] ), without which even applying tons of nutrients is useless. And, Gabe has a big farm, a few thousand acres (so not small scale) in North Dakota, USA, with field crops, green manure, livestock on pasture / prairie, etc. And is making profits from his farming operations, sustainably, even regeneratively, without government subsidies, and more than his conventionally-farming neighbors, some of whose soils are getting worse, while his get better over time, by objective measures (checked by external govt. age"},{"id":34637433,"author":"schiffern","month":"2023-02","story":"Microbes are 'active engineers' in Earth's rock-to-life cycle","stance":"rec","why":"Practical talk on biotic cycling in agriculture","quote":"Dr Elaine Ingham delivers a salient presentation.","text":"For a practical discussion of how this biotic cycling connects to agriculture, Dr Elaine Ingham delivers a salient presentation.[0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24828524,"author":"vram22","month":"2020-10","story":"No-till no-herbicide farming system in trial since 1981","stance":"rec","why":"Soil biology matters more than nutrient levels for farm results","quote":"Also see, very significantly, Elaine Ingham, soil (biology) scientist, Ph.D.","text":"Also see, very significantly, Elaine Ingham, soil (biology) scientist, Ph.D., also founder of startup soilfoodweb.com (to consult and get her research applied widely). Almost all the farmers I mentioned above, quote her work with respect and apply it in their operations, and consider it key to their results. She (along with many other researchers, over years, not a sudden thing) has come out with some rather startling results that, as I said, are being applied by these guys and many others. A key result is that it is the quantity and diversity of the life in the soil that is as (or more) important to soil health and hence farm results (now and long term) as the actual levels of plant nutrients in the soil. And this is because it is the soil life (they use the term \"biology\" for it, but okay, go with the prevailing term the experts use) that makes the chemical nutrients available in forms that plants can actually use, via a deep symbiosis and mutual helping that happens between plants and the soil biology, which includes bacteria, fungi, archaea, nematodes, arthropods and small animals. Another quite surprising / non-intuitive result is her saying that most or all soils on earth, already have many times more the amounts of nutrients than plants need, like 1000x more, including macro (e.g. N, P, K) and micro ones (like trace elements), so the limiting factor is really the soil biology, which depends a lot on soil organic matter content (all those critters need to eat), humidity"},{"id":15539067,"author":"schiffern","month":"2017-10","story":"Insects Are in Serious Trouble","stance":"rec","why":"Explains soil life unlocking nutrients from rock","quote":"This is all explained much better by soil microbiologist Elaine Ingham.","text":"> If I eat a banana that has potassium in it, it lends to the fact that it took potassium out of the soil. This is true. However the little-known fact is that healthy soil contains all the \"machines\" required to break down the abundant insoluble potassium (along with every other necessary element) present in rock particles into plant-soluble fertilizer. This \"insoluble pool\" contains enough nutrients for literally thousands of years of plant growth, if only the soil life is present to unlock it. The problem is that tillage kills this soil life, and that dead land now requires the constant import of external fertilizer to grow food. Pesticide and fertilizer application also kills these organisms, which out-compete pest species (as mentioned here: [LINK] ). Spraying becomes a vicious cycle for the farmer, and a lucrative cycle for their chemical supplier. In other words, functional soil isn't just an inert \"tank\" for holding nutrients. It's a two-dimensional factory for converting in-situ rock and air and rain into biomass. This is all explained much better by soil microbiologist Elaine Ingham. [VIDEO] > luckily engineers and farmers are looking at these things more closely now. I would add biologists (including soil scientists, botanists, mycologists, entomologists, ecologists, etc) and indigenous experts to that list."},{"id":10025205,"author":"schiffern","month":"2015-08","story":"U.S. honeybee colonies hit a 20-year high","stance":"rec","why":"Soil microbiologist explains how pesticides harm soil nutrient cycling","quote":"","text":">if neonicotinoids are a net win for agriculture The logic here seems to be that: * Farmers buy neonicotinoids. * Therefore, neonicotinoids have a net positive effect on agriculture. (in other words, like a good libertarian you are assuming that the market has complete/perfect/accurate information) Unfortunately that's just not true. Today's farmers simply don't have the required soil science/botany/ecology/hydrology knowledge. Worst, they are actively misled by biocide and fertilizer salesmen, who have a natural profit motive to convince farmers that their products are essential. Far from being essential, in reality pesticides destroy the nutrient harvesting ability of healthy soil. Soil microbiologist Dr. Elaine Ingham explains: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10025784,"author":"schiffern","month":"2015-08","story":"U.S. honeybee colonies hit a 20-year high","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how pesticides destroy soil microorganisms","quote":"Again, soil microbiologist Elaine Ingham explains the process better than I could","text":">Because the alternatives to neonicotinoids are broader-spectrum pesticides Well 'ere's yer problem! ;) You're starting off by assuming that agriculture must use pesticides to grow food. But is that really true? I submit to you that it is not. Furthermore, I submit that a food system that depends on pesticides is doomed to self-destruct (because of inevitable biogeophysical processes). Pesticides destroy the nutrient cycling microorganisms in the soil leading to infertility, erosion, and ultimately desertification. Again, soil microbiologist Elaine Ingham explains the process better than I could: [VIDEO] >I don't know why you insist on being so overtly hostile You're right. My apologies, and edited."},{"id":39859493,"author":"schiffern","month":"2024-03","story":"Vegetables are losing their nutrients. Can the decline be reversed?","stance":"reference","why":"Soil life mines nutrients for plants","quote":"","text":"I'll say it, because the article neglected to: \"It's the soil health, stupid.\" It's troubling how (at least in mass media) every environmental problem gets collapsed down to just CO2 and greenhouse gases. Yes it does play a role here, but it shouldn't eclipse the other existential environmental problems we also face: soil erosion and killing the soil life that 'mines' nutrients for plants.[0] People imagine soil as merely an inert sponge that only stores nutrients (to the delight of fertilizer companies), but healthy living soil will actually break down rocks and air to create a constant supply of nutrients manufactured in situ . [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34435074,"author":"schiffern","month":"2023-01","story":"Current and future role of Haber–Bosch ammonia in a carbon-free energy env(2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Microorganisms dissolve phosphorus and potassium","quote":"","text":"P and K are abundant in the world's agricultural soils. They just need to be 'eaten' (dissolved) by soil microorganisms.[1] The problem is that farmers have been taught to ignore the health of soil organisms and just apply a short-term bandaid chemical (vs biological) spray product, which causes more harm in the long-term. Take a wild guess who taught them that? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27852479,"author":"schiffern","month":"2021-07","story":"My take on the study from MIT that predicts “societal collapse”","stance":"reference","why":"Soil makes its own nutrients","quote":"It's eye-opening to realize that soil can make its own nutrients and fertilizer","text":"> soils have become depleted of nutrients and ammonium fertilizers are not enough It's eye-opening to realize that soil can make its own nutrients and fertilizer (unlike the common view of soil as an inert \"sponge\" which needs all the fertilizer added to it). Bacteria breaks down rock into minerals (including the all-important potassium and phosphorus) and also fix nitrogen. The problem is that applying synthetic fertilizers kills off this soil biology, making the farmer dependent on.... more synthetic fertilizers. It's a nice racket, until it kills the planet. [VIDEO] It's a huge biotech opportunity. Manufacture fertilizer biologically in-situ, vs blowing up some land, dissolving it in acid (along with tasty cadmium impurities[1]), and shipping it around the globe. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":26730539,"author":"Ma8ee","month":"2021-04","story":"Soil bacteria could improve crop yields, via fungi","stance":"reference","why":"Soil already has enough elements, needs microorganisms","quote":"","text":"I saw some another video where the claim was that there’s already enough elements in the soil to last thousands of years, but they require the right mixture of microorganisms to become available to the plants. It’s only the lack of microorganisms that forces us to add those nutrients. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17682867,"author":"schiffern","month":"2018-08","story":"Trash piles up in US as China closes door to recycling","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">However, it is usually organic food and it is typically much more expensive than regular, packaged food. I don't want organic. I just want to avoid packaging Just avoiding packaging is more expensive, because as this excellent article points out[1], packaging represents the automation of the distribution chain . > Some of the first packaging arose in the late 19th century. Some of the earliest packaging was for Uneeda Biscuits. They were packaged in a box with wax paper that sealed in the biscuits so they wouldn't go stale. Packaging was all about easing distribution for producers, because before that, everything was sold in bulk containers. The transformation of production and distribution as well as retail sales—going from mom and pop stores to the chain supermarkets we have today—means that packaging represents the automation of the distribution system. I think producers are very conscious of how helpful packaging is in helping them centralize their businesses. In doing that, they get to downsize and streamline and create economies of scale that they couldn't create if they didn't get to consolidate—which is what a lot of the drive behind switching from the refillable to the disposable bottle and can was about. The beer and soda industries consolidated massively in the post-war period, and the number of producers shrank dramatically. And this massive consolidation in both of those industries was facilitated by the switch to disposable containers. The industry no longer ha"},{"id":15515209,"author":"schiffern","month":"2017-10","story":"75% decline over 27 years in total flying insect biomass in protected areas","stance":"reference","why":"Beneficial soil ecosystem destroyed by poison","quote":"","text":">use pesticides to get rid of disease-carrying ticks Unfortunately this solution is a bit like using a nuclear bomb to get rid of gang violence. It does indeed do that, but at the expense of destroying the entire beneficial insect community as well. Generally pest species recover faster after catastrophe than beneficial species, so by eliminating all of the pest's competitors pesticides are sewing the seeds of future infestations. A much better, less toxic, and longer-lasting solution would be to provide pest-eating insect predators with habitats. In nature balance is achieved by predator/prey interactions instead of razing the beneficial soil ecosystem[1] with poison. To take just one example, ticks are naturally eaten by birds (eg chickens), and if productive bird species can be used this has the side effect of producing delicious chicken (vs surface water/aquifer pollution, soil degradation which necessitates fertilizers, and a proliferation of plastic \"keep children and pets off this lawn for X hours\" signs). People's instinct to protect themselves from disease-carrying pest species is a good one. The solutions handed to them by the post-war chemical industry are not. Who coulda thunk that redirecting all our WWII chemical weapons output to fighting nature wasn't such a good idea after all? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13457793,"author":"schiffern","month":"2017-01","story":"Turning CO2 to Stone","stance":"reference","why":"Soil manufactures fertilizer in situ from rock","quote":"","text":">the high nutrient requirements needed to grow the biochar We seem to have forgotten that natural forests are incredibly productive, and don't need artificial fertilizer. So we have a proof-of-concept. How do they do it, and how can we emulate those processes? Spoiler alert: soil (along with the root action of plants) is essentially a flat biological nanomachine that breaks down solid rock and fixes nitrogen, manufacturing fertilizer in-situ . [VIDEO] Of course the logical way to do this is not to create some vast new land use category (\"carbon forests\" or similar), but to transition our largest current land use category -- agriculture -- from a carbon-releasing to a carbon-sequestering mode. It also helps to increase biomass per hectare in suburbs, by transitioning our current low-carbon-density lawns over to a biome that buffers substantial amounts of carbon. This is the fundamental insight behind Permaculture btw, which has been working on figuring out exactly what this looks like. It's a hard problem yes, but one with existential importance for humanity. >Biochar is basically coal, which could be burned and converted into CO2 again. ...so don't do that. :) It's it obvious that burning coal also needs to stop for effective climate mitigation? In this application, density matters. Biochar is extremely low density, and high in surface area, which makes it an ideal soil amendment which buffers rainwater and provides microbial habitat (not to mention raw carbon for building in"},{"id":12577743,"author":"schiffern","month":"2016-09","story":"A MacArthur winner wants every child in the world to own a microscope","stance":"reference","why":"Soil microbiology identification guides soil rehabilitation","quote":"","text":">a piece of kit they use once or twice and then discard You're not thinking big enough. Microscopes aren't just a teaching tool, they're a powerful tool for observation of the world. One example is soil microbiology identification, which can be learned in a couple days and used to guide rehabilitation of degraded agricultural soils.[1] A microscope can reveal pathogens in polluted water. And not to mention all the medical procedures that boil down to \"prep it and look at it through a microscope.\" The kids of today are the scientists and technologists of tomorrow. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12609323,"author":"thatcat","month":"2016-09","story":"Atmospheric carbon dioxide passes 400ppm","stance":"reference","why":"Trees get nutrients from microbial life","quote":"","text":"You can convert the trees to biochar, then a large percentage won't decompose. You don't need to fertilize trees because they get their nutrients from microbial life[0], which is increased by fertilization with biochar. [0] elane ingham, food web talk - [VIDEO]"},{"id":11943581,"author":"schiffern","month":"2016-06","story":"Power plants that convert all of their CO2 emissions into carbon nanotubes","stance":"reference","why":"Soil life fertility","quote":"","text":">If we can incentivize people to make useful products out of all that CO2 just floating in the atmosphere, then maybe, just _maybe_, we can start fixing the mess we made. I'm sure most people are picturing factories here, but using low/appropriate tech as well is essential. Building soil and woody biomass can absorb enormous amounts of carbon. The question essentially becomes, how much biomass per hectare are we talking about? Absorbing the 350 teratonnes of human emitted carbon [1] over the 11.7 billion hectares of Earth's non-tundra land surface [2] works out to 30 tonnes of carbon per hectare. If you exclude deserts it's 42 tonnes per hectare, but the technology already exists to successfully \"green\" deserts. That's 30 trees per hectare, at 1 tonne per tree.[3] Or 3 kg per square meter of soil carbon, the equivalent of 25 cm of topsoil Soil carbon is stored up to 40 meters down by deep rooted plants, so in practice this isn't a problem. [4] Most of our land management is via agricultural, so agricultural reform is the only lever long enough to make a dent. Practically this means switching from soil destroying tillage to soil building cover crops, long-distance imported fertilizer to in-situ fertility produced by soil organisms[5], and ecologically unstable monocultures to resiliant polyculture and agroforestry systems. If you run the numbers trying to use factories alone the cost is astronomical. It's larger* than the world's global energy infrastructure, because thermodyn"},{"id":11585511,"author":"schiffern","month":"2016-04","story":"Devastating wheat fungus appears in Asia for first time","stance":"reference","why":"Soil organisms manufacture bioavailable nutrients from minerals and air","quote":"","text":"Topsoil erosion is already a massive problem.[1] So far we've been able to mask the damage by substituting fossil fuel inputs for soil fertility, but realistically soil husbandry is the only sustainable way to grow food. Heck, many people nowadays aren't even aware that soil organisms can manufacture bio-available nutrients from local minerals and the air.[2] The dominant view of soil seems to be as an inert tank that needs to be \"refilled\" with imported fertility. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11372632,"author":"schiffern","month":"2016-03","story":"Build a Personal Food Computer","stance":"reference","why":"Soil microbes make minerals bioavailable","quote":"","text":"Synthetic materials don't come from nothing. Following the supply chain backwards they're all[1] mined from somewhere, either as fossil fuels or minerals like potash and limestone. All the NPK fertilizers (which this system uses) come from mined materials. Far from \"not harming plants or animals,\" this material extraction comes with huge (but externalized) ecological, social justice, and wealth concentration problems. And of course it's completely unsustainable. Now compare that to how healthy soil functions: microorganisms leach minerals directly from grains of sand/loam/clay, essentially manufacturing in-situ minerals into bioavailable form right at the root zone. Nitrogen fixation is similar, performed by leguminous plants using otherwise wasted sunlight and ISRU. [VIDEO] [1] Since I know someone will bring it up, some do come from plants like soybeans. But mechanized soybean production is primarily either soil mining or (in areas with badly degraded soil) entirely supported by fossil fertilizers."}],"r":[["uUmIdq0D6-A",1],["QHPmRJIoc2k",1],["xKCuDxpccYM",1],["fjbWr3ODbAo",1],["2xcZS7arcgk",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["2lisQQmWQkY",1]]},"qsEjV8DdSbs":{"wy":"Pointed to as the clearest, evidence-based debunk of the Gimbal footage, and a model of how to analyze such claims.","q":{"x":"The summary video is concise and only 20 minutes long. I challenge you to watch all of it and respond specifically.","i":36235334,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36235334,"author":"mrandish","month":"2023-06","story":"US urged to reveal UFO evidence after claim that it has intact alien vehicles","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Concise 20-minute collaborative analysis of Gimbal video with all source data linked.","quote":"The summary video is concise and only 20 minutes long. I challenge you to watch all of it and respond specifically.","text":"> So you're telling me, some random game developer was more competent than the US military guys who literally are hired for such analysis? In the case of the most-cited \"Gimbal\" video, all the military has officially said is they can't determine for sure what the object the glare shown in the video is from. But everyone already agrees on that point. No one can tell from the video whether the glare shown is caused by an airplane many miles distant or some other object. No one representing the U.S. government has ever said the video could NOT be a magnified thermal glare from a distant airplane. Remember, the pilot never saw the object with his own eyes, it was far too distant to see visually even as a spec on the horizon. All the pilot ever saw was the same auto-tracking thermal video we've all seen. > I suppose, having coded some \"parallax effect\" is sufficient in your book? I don't know what you're referring to but it doesn't sound like Mick West's analysis video: [VIDEO] . Also, Mick only assembled the video. He acknowledges the evidence shown in the video is the work of many independent researchers working collaboratively over many months while iteratively sharing their data for peer review. There are links to all the source data shown as well as the entire open analysis discussion and review. The summary video is concise and only 20 minutes long. I challenge you to watch all of it and respond specifically. Which of the four points evidenced from the on-screen camera do yo"},{"id":45195923,"author":"trenchpilgrim","month":"2025-09","story":"Radar footage shows Hellfire missile fired by US Military bounce off UFO","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how IR cameras work before assuming footage shows an orb.","quote":"check out this video on how these cameras work","text":"an interesting youtube comment: \"2:42 Check out the readout on the bottom right . One of the numbers is the target height above terrain - and after it's hit that goes down from 86 to 59 over about 6 seconds before the video cuts off. So after being hit the object isn't continuing on course, it's falling at an increasingly fast rate. The video was sent to Rep. Eric Burlison with no chain of custody and no context, and it's a video taken of a screen which crops out 80% of the analytical data along the edges of the screen, but that's the MOST IMPORTANT PART which would tell us the distance, speed, size, range, angle and other critical information for interpreting the video. This could be drone to drone intercept footage from a training mission, we just don't know at this point.\" Also note that the article's title contains an error - this is not \"radar footage, it's IR camera footage. Before assuming this is even an \"orb\" at all, check out this video on how these cameras work: [VIDEO] I'm no expert but my most likely bet is someone cropped IR footage of a drone intercept and sent it without context or SME interpretation to a congressman."},{"id":37354527,"author":"thamer","month":"2023-09","story":"U.S. Department of Defense Launches All-Domain Anomaly Resolution Office Website","stance":"rec","why":"Thorough explanation of the Gimbal video's apparent anomaly.","quote":"Mick West's analysis seems to explain it pretty thoroughly","text":"How is the GIMBAL video still listed as \"unresolved\"? Mick West's analysis seems to explain it pretty thoroughly: [VIDEO] At the very least I would have hoped that there would be more than just a link to the video, and a section saying that some convincing explanations have been proposed. Right now it looks like on the first day it was released, as if this case was still just as mysterious."},{"id":36219018,"author":"shrimpx","month":"2023-06","story":"US urged to reveal UFO evidence after claim that it has intact alien vehicles","stance":"rec","why":"Analysis showing the rotating saucer was lens flare.","quote":"this analysis gave me new insight","text":"After taking the pilots' accounts on face value, this analysis gave me new insight: [VIDEO] The pilots claim they saw a flying saucer that was rotating in weird ways, and provide a grainy video that's kind of convincing. The analysis proves it was lens flare. There are additional debunked UAP videos released by the military, including one that is lens bokeh around starlight, which is debunked by showing the configuration of the \"triangle UAPs\" matches the positions of stars at that time, including additional evidence that the camera used on those ships has triangle aperture. This tells me there's a chance of a disappointing but realistic option 4: military incompetence. They take these videos, they don't know what they are, so they go into some data pipeline and categorized as UAP. Then people/congress become aware that there are \"UAP videos\", and we go through this declassification song and dance, only to get these bokeh and lens flare videos, that the military themselves do not know they are bokeh/lens flare, and they have to find out about it on YouTube. Giving the military more benefit of the doubt, the likely option is 3: psyop. They spread UAP rumors to confuse adversaries, knowing these UAP videos they have are BS. When they release these easily debunked videos, the adversaries could be further confused, still not knowing what they really have."},{"id":36219706,"author":"bragr","month":"2023-06","story":"US urged to reveal UFO evidence after claim that it has intact alien vehicles","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed analysis debunking the Gimbal UFO video as infrared glare.","quote":"there's some great analysis and debunks of the video the headline photo is taken from","text":"Tangential, but there's some great analysis and debunks of the video the headline photo is taken from: [VIDEO] Simulation: [LINK]"},{"id":36511710,"author":"Triesault","month":"2023-06","story":"US Congress doubles down on explosive claims of illegal UFO retrieval programs","stance":"rec","why":"Explains UFO as infrared glare rotating because the camera rotates while tracking.","quote":"This video does a good job at explaining how the \"UFO\" is probably an infrared glare","text":"tptacek could be referring to the following video: [VIDEO] This video does a good job at explaining how the \"UFO\" is probably an infrared glare, hiding the hot object behind it, and rotating only because the camera rotates when tracking the target from left to right."},{"id":34344629,"author":"agarren","month":"2023-01","story":"The Alien Grave of Aurora, Texas","stance":"rec","why":"Convincing analysis explaining the Gimbal UFO footage.","quote":"Mick West put together what I found to be a pretty enlightening and convincing analysis","text":"Mick West put together what I found to be a pretty enlightening and convincing analysis: [VIDEO] Ultimately, it remains an unidentified flying object, but between West’s conclusion and a physics defying anomaly, I’d lean squarely towards the former."},{"id":31216986,"author":"SemanticStrengh","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: Teach me something new","stance":"rec","why":"Example of the kind of analysis we should do.","quote":"that explanation is a great example of what kind of analysis we should do","text":"I forgot the names, don't remember wether nimitz is gimbal but anyway that explanation is a great example of what kind of analysis we should do [VIDEO]"},{"id":42396886,"author":"mrandish","month":"2024-12","story":"Mysterious New Jersey drone sightings prompt call for 'state of emergency'","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Pentagon misidentifying lens artifacts and gimbal rotation in its footage.","quote":"","text":"> The pentagon, for example, just declared that they do not know what they are I often hear those hyping UFO sightings citing this type of statement by the Pentagon. However, the Pentagon saying the don't know what it is doesn't mean anything. Of course, they don't know what it is. They weren't there. They didn't see it nor have any idea if there was anything unusual seen. The null hypothesis is the still the most likely: this is a result of media hype causing increased erroneous reports of aircraft and hobbyist drones along with false reports by social media attention seekers. Also, the Pentagon has a consistently terrible track record of failing to properly identify spurious internal lens reflections, digital stabilization artifacts, IR ghosting and gimbal rotation on their own footage. [VIDEO] ."},{"id":34670248,"author":"j0057","month":"2023-02","story":"As a US Navy fighter pilot, I witnessed unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP)","stance":"reference","why":"Mundane explanations for UAP videos.","quote":"","text":"There are plenty of explanations for these videos that don't involve alien species traveling multiple light years to troll American air force pilots, like [VIDEO] and [LINK] ."},{"id":34670645,"author":"godelski","month":"2023-02","story":"As a US Navy fighter pilot, I witnessed unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP)","stance":"reference","why":"Simulation debunking the Nimitz Tic Tac footage.","quote":"Short answer is that there was sense misreading and a gimbal lock. Some people even simulated it","text":"> The Nimitz Incident of 2004(?). The Tic Tac UFO? That's probably one of the best explained events (debunked). Short answer is that there was sense misreading and a gimbal lock. Some people even simulated it[0]. I wouldn't call this an elaborate hoax, but an optical illusion and an event that took some expertise to understand. Expertise that a pilot wouldn't have. I should add that finding that link was nontrivial. Faster through Google but searching through YouTube just came up with UFO videos and no debunking. This is probably part of the problem. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34746738,"author":"nostromo","month":"2023-02","story":"Pentagon Downed Object over Alaska, Officials Say","stance":"reference","why":"Explains UFOs as lens flares.","quote":"Those UFOs were lens flares.","text":"Those UFOs were lens flares. [VIDEO] Turns out we like to spend gobs of money even when we're just chasing our shadow."}],"r":[["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1],["Q7jcBGLIpus",1],["zjkBMFhNj_g",1],["m05abdGSOxY",1]]},"zduSFxRajkE":{"wy":"Recommended as the best deep dive on tokenizers, worth the two hours for anyone working with LLMs.","q":{"x":"Worth spending 2h for the deeper understanding if you deal with LLMs.","i":41932357,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41932357,"author":"cranium","month":"2024-10","story":"Probably pay attention to tokenizers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why tokenizers exist and how BPE works.","quote":"Worth spending 2h for the deeper understanding if you deal with LLMs.","text":"I finally understood the weirdness of tokenizers after watching the video Andrej Karpathy made: \"Let's build the GPT Tokenizer\" ( [VIDEO] ). He goes through why we need them instead of raw byte sequences (too expensive) and how the Byte Pair Encoding algorithm works. Worth spending 2h for the deeper understanding if you deal with LLMs."},{"id":45229908,"author":"vismit2000","month":"2025-09","story":"UTF-8 is a brilliant design","stance":"rec","why":"Good intro to Unicode byte encodings in the first 20 minutes.","quote":"also contains a good introduction to Unicode byte encodings, ASCII, UTF-8, UTF-16, UTF-32 in the first 20 minutes","text":"Karpathy's \"Let's build the GPT Tokenizer\" also contains a good introduction to Unicode byte encodings, ASCII, UTF-8, UTF-16, UTF-32 in the first 20 minutes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43610570,"author":"zellyn","month":"2025-04","story":"Why Does Claude Speak Byzantine Music Notation?","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth explanation of byte-pair encoding tokenization.","quote":"Understanding tokenization is surprisingly useful, even if that video seems awfully long to devote to such a tedious subject.","text":"Andrej Karpathy goes into quite some depth about how tokenization works here: [VIDEO] tl;dr many of the LLMs use byte-pair encoding to create tokens. You take a set of documents, and then form tokens by repeatedly merging the most common pair of tokens. The initial set of tokens is 256 raw bytes. And the text is typically represented in utf-8. I expect that although the LLMs can understand arbitrarily but cleanly offset unicode code points by (eventually) noticing the final byte of each sequence, they would do markedly worse on actually processing and completing on them, because they will not have been reduced to the normal set of tokens. However, if the text is actually output cleanly converted, either in internal thinking tokens or in the beginning of the response, they should do fine. Understanding tokenization is surprisingly useful, even if that video seems awfully long to devote to such a tedious subject. Even Karpathy doesn't like it!"},{"id":41304760,"author":"Chirono","month":"2024-08","story":"Artificial intelligence is losing hype","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why models struggle with letters in words.","quote":"this video is a great resource","text":"This is just an artefact of tokenisation though. The model simply isn’t ever shown the letters that make up words, unless they are spelled out explicitly. It sees tokens representing groups of words. This is a little like saying a human isn’t intelligent because they couldn’t answer your question that you asked in an ultrasonic wavelength. If you’d like to learn more this video is a great resource: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39557741,"author":"moozilla","month":"2024-03","story":"Ask HN: Did you encounter any leap year bugs today?","stance":"rec","why":"Two-hour deep dive on how GPT tokenizers work.","quote":"check out Andrej Karpathy's latest video if you have 2 hours for a deep dive","text":"ChatGPT is specifically bad at these kinds of tasks because of tokenization. If you plug your query into [LINK] , you can see that\"egregious\" is a single token, so the LLM doesn't actually see any \"e\" characters -- to answer your question it would have had to learn a fact about how the word was spelled from it's training data, and I imagine texts explicitly talking about how words are spelled are not very common. Good explanation here if this still doesn't make sense: [LINK] , or check out Andrej Karpathy's latest video if you have 2 hours for a deep dive: [VIDEO] IMO questions about spelling or number sense are pretty tired as gotchas, because they are all basically just artifacts of this implementation detail. There are other language models available that don't have this issue. BTW this is also the reason DALL-E etc suck at generating text in images."},{"id":40675716,"author":"pquki4","month":"2024-06","story":"Notebooks Are McDonalds of Code","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a high quality notebook.","quote":"Notable example of high quality notebook","text":"If there are complaints about code quality in a jupyter notebook, chances are that someone is doing it wrong -- either the author is using it for demonstration purpose, for production or near production environment, or the person looking at others' notebooks is taking them too seriously. Most code in jupyter notebook is bad, but that's fine, because nobody should waste time doing that unless they must reproduce the results or they are sharing the notebook with others. Notable example of high quality notebook: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39591306,"author":"anonymouse008","month":"2024-03","story":"Claude 3 model family","stance":"reference","why":"Background on tokenization issues in GPT.","quote":"For reference, Let's build the GPT Tokenizer","text":"I'm convinced GPT is running separate helper functions on input and output tokens to fix the 'tokenization' issues. As in, find items of math, send it to this hand made parser and function, then insert result into output tokens. There's no other way to fix the token issue. For reference, Let's build the GPT Tokenizer [VIDEO]"},{"id":39789967,"author":"dist-epoch","month":"2024-03","story":"Chronos: Learning the Language of Time Series","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why raw UTF-8 input to transformers works poorly.","quote":"People tried inputting UTF8 directly into transformers, but it doesn't work that well. Karpathy explains why","text":"Tokenisation turns a continuous signal into a normalized discrete vocabulary: stock \"went up a lot\", \"went up a little\", \"stayed flat\". This smooths out noise and simplifies matching up similar but not identical signals. > We tokenize text because text isn't numbers. Text is actually numbers. People tried inputting UTF8 directly into transformers, but it doesn't work that well. Karpathy explains why: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["OFgxAFdxYAQ",1],["O-2tpwW0kmU",1],["3VQ382QG-y4",1]]},"tD5NrevFtbU":{"wy":"A frequent citation in debates about SOLID and Clean Code, representing the performance-first side of the argument.","q":{"x":"Look at Casey Murrey's Clean Code horrible performance youtube video","i":41388106,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":48750530,"author":"xiphias2","month":"2026-07","story":"Solid and Clean Code never felt solid or clean to me","stance":"rec","why":"Shows abstractions make code hard to optimize later","quote":"If you want something real, look at Clean Code Horrible Performance youtube video if you haven't.","text":"If you want something real, look at Clean Code Horrible Performance youtube video if you haven't. I also found Bob Martin entertaining, but after trying to follow his suggestions I just got into the trap that Casey Muratori shows: even if I don't care about the performance of the code at the start, the amount of abstractions Uncle Bob advises makes it just too easy to make the code extremely hard to optimize later. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43289272,"author":"latexr","month":"2025-03","story":"Ask HN: Is Clean Code a waste of time?","stance":"rec","why":"Casey Muratori on clean code costs","quote":"You may enjoy","text":"You may enjoy “\"Clean\" Code, Horrible Performance”, from Casey Muratori. [VIDEO] Casey specialises in game engine research and development [LINK] And has an ongoing course on computer performance for programmers. [LINK]"},{"id":41388106,"author":"xiphias2","month":"2024-08","story":"Ask HN: What books should I read to improve as a software engineer?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows clean code hurting performance and readability","quote":"Look at Casey Murrey's Clean Code horrible performance youtube video","text":"Look at Casey Murrey's Clean Code horrible performance youtube video: [VIDEO] I also watched clean code videos and used it in practice, and why it _felt_ right my code size blew up and actually it got harder to understand by other people instead of easier. Nowdays my main guidelines are getting a balance between less lines of code (while still readable), readable variable names and better performance. Also I may get hate here for it, but I learned a lot from watching George Hotz programming tinygrad (available on YouTube)."},{"id":40115784,"author":"GuestHNUser","month":"2024-04","story":"How do you recognize an expert?","stance":"rec","why":"Reasons not to follow SOLID principles","quote":"I like this video on the topic for reasons not to follow SOLID principles","text":"The issue with those books is that they don't have any concrete data that affirms that they are worth following. And, in fact, that style of code is largely to blame for why modern software feels so sluggish. They often reduce performance by 10x if not 100x or worse. I like this video on the topic for reasons not to follow SOLID principles[0]. Muratori also has an excellent talk on writing APIs that are flexible and performant[1]. As for books on understanding the hardware performance, Computer Systems: a Programmer's Perspective, is the best example (not the international edition though, according to the author's recommendation). I'm not aware of any architecture books recommended by anyone that cares about performance unfortunately. Most high performance software is written iteratively, meaning they aren't assuming a code structure from the start. Andreas Fredriksson, a lead engine programmer at Insomniac Games, has an excellent quote on how he writes high performance software[2]: > Work backwards from hard constraints and requirements to guide your design. Don’t fall into the trap of designing from a fluffy API frontend before you’ve worked out how the thing will handle a worst case. Don’t be afraid to sketch stuff in while you’re proving out the approach. > The value is what you learn, never the code. Hack it and then delete the code and implement “clean” or whatever you need. But never start there, it gets in the way of real engineering. > As an industry we spend million"},{"id":38228963,"author":"GuestHNUser","month":"2023-11","story":"Ask HN: Learning new coding patterns – how to start?","stance":"rec","why":"Talks by Casey Muratori on data-oriented design","quote":"Casey Muratori has a number of talks well worth listening to","text":"> What resources do you recommend folks at my level look into? I'd argue that your best bet for learning is starting a project that drives you, then throwing that project away and starting it over again but try to make the code simpler. This way you can hone into what abstraction is actually necessary to solve your problem. I strongly recommend ignoring design patterns and instead honing in on what the essential data flow is in your problem space. Most design patterns will bloat your code base and, thus, slow both your program and your programming velocity down. As far as programming design and architecture goes, the best talks I have found come out of data orientated design talks. Casey Muratori has a number of talks well worth listening to[0][1][2]. I also enjoy Andrew Kelley's talk on Data Orientated Design specifically as it demonstrates DOD's power with actual numbers to back it up[3]. Tangent: I did my time with OOP design + trying to make my code 'clean' according to popular recommendations you'll see online. All of that time, I consider wasted because I focused too much on what the 'right' abstraction to use was, instead of solving my problem in the simplest way possible. I find now that often the best solution is the one that uses the least amount of abstraction and indirection. (E.g. One big function can be a lot easier to maintain than multiple little functions, you just have to develop a taste for when a function should be split up and why). (If you must have a pa"},{"id":46686726,"author":"jesse__","month":"2026-01","story":"Understanding C++ Ownership System","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on thinking about bulk processing versus single objects","quote":"","text":"Another commentor succinctly pointed out one argument against RAII+friends is that it encourages thinking about single objects, as opposed to bulk processing. In many contexts, the common case is in fact bulk processing, and programming things with the assumption that everything is a single, discrete element creates several problems, mostly wrt. performance, but also maintainability. [1][2] > The whole point of an arena allocator is the exact opposite of RAII Yes, agreed. And the internet is rife with people yelling about just how great RAII is, but comparatively few people have written specifically about it's failings, and alternatives, which is what I'm asking about today. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":43681010,"author":"immibis","month":"2025-04","story":"Zig's new LinkedList API (it's time to learn fieldParentPtr)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of the Casey Muratori school of programming","quote":"I call them the Uncle Bob vs Casey Muratori school","text":"bugs* ironic It depends on whether you approach programming from a perspective of writing piles of obviously correct abstraction layers (the total effect of which is to leak so much the solution ends up being wrong anyway), or from the direct unabstracted perspective of actually making the computer do what you want it to do (which does not preclude the solution from using abstractions). I call them the Uncle Bob vs Casey Muratori school, after Casey's lectures such as \"Clean Code, Horrible Performance\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":42598596,"author":"codetrotter","month":"2025-01","story":"One Dog vs. the Windows 3.1 Graphics Stack","stance":"reference","why":"Another video by the same author on the topic","quote":"See also this video of his","text":"See for example this playlist of videos of his with some videos from 2021 where he implements a terminal emulator. As he says himself, this project is not so much about terminals as it is about software development practices in general. [LINK] See also this video of his: “Clean” Code, Horrible Performance [VIDEO]"},{"id":38287316,"author":"GuestHNUser","month":"2023-11","story":"Push ifs up and fors down","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thanks for the thoughtful reply. > Well it's hard to argue about that tradeoff in general, but I think the existence of languages like Python, Ruby and PHP is compelling. Though I'd accept the argument that they help optimise for neither performance nor changeability! I see the point you're making, and I don't disagree with it. But I should be a bit more clear in what I really meant in my parent comment: Given whatever language a developer is working in, whether it is fast like C++ or a slow language like Python (or perhaps even Minecraft Redstone), I think the programmer that takes a data oriented approach (meaning they write their code thinking about the kinds of data the program can receive and the kind it will likely receive, along with what operations will be the most expensive) will have better code than a programmer that makes a nice object model following all the SOLID principles. The majority of the OOP code I've worked with spends too much time caring about abstractions and encapsulation that performance is lost and the code is no better to work with. > Regarding the DOD/OOP wars, I really love this article, which argues that OOP doesn't have to be bad[1]. I also think that when performance is a requirement, you just have to get more particular about your use of OOP. For example, the difference between Mesh and InstancedMesh[2] in THREE.js. Both are OOP, but have very different performance implications. Absolutely agree here. Classic gamedev.net article. ECS != DOD "},{"id":36588796,"author":"akasakahakada","month":"2023-07","story":"Ask HN: Resources for High Performance Computing?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"1. Single-thread performance This is hardly related to HPC. The closest thing maybe code optimization and competitive programming. First learn how to do anything fast in single process, then think about scale it up later. 2. Parallel programming Learn MPI. Specifically mpi4py. Once you go MPI, you never use python's multiprocessing module (it sucks). But stuffs are mostly about solving linear algebra problems, which I am not that interested in. 4. Distributed Computing Related to MPI. But nowaday distributed GPU computing become a thing, thing that I can think of is Dask. Don't know how to use that. [LINK] 5. Quantum Computing Completely unrelated to HPC, at least in the near future (10 to 30 years). Areas that is the most active in this field is molecular computation. If you don't have a phy/chem PHD, you are not able to read anything beyond college level stuff in this field. And those are only basics stuff like elementary schools' arithmetics. But write your own program to solve those quantum problems in classical computers is interesting, because people there only think about problems that cause scaling issue, not the others. That's why some may think that quantum computer is a replacement of supercomputers. If you can find ways and invent techniques that can reduce timecomplexity in terms of classical algorithm, you are welcome. And literally there is a lot of room for anyone because every quantum computer emulator is unoptimized and slow as hell. Google, Microsoft, IBM, "},{"id":42488722,"author":"whstl","month":"2024-12","story":"Ideas from \"A Philosophy of Software Design\"","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it produces code that is not only slow to run (as argued by Casey Muratori","text":"Not GP but: Personally, I find that book's advice is highly subjective and rooted on aesthetics rather than pragmatism or experimentation. It encourages an excessive number of very small methods and very small classes, and brushes off problems that it causes. Not about the book, but: Its influence is malignant. Even Uncle Bob mentioned in a recent interview that he will break the \"10 lines per method\" rule if need be. But practitioners influenced by the book lack his experience, and are often very strict. I even remember a specific Ruby linter that capped methods at 5 or 6 lines max IIRC. Working in such a fragmented codebase is pure madness. This comment from another user made me remind some of those codebases: [LINK] EDIT: After living in the \"Clean Code world\" for half a decade I can categorically say that it produces code that is not only slow to run (as argued by Casey Muratori [1]), but also slower to understand, due to the jumping around. The amount of coupling between incestuous classes and methods born out of \"breaking up the code\" makes it incredibly difficult to refactor. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["skos4B5x7qE",1],["lJ3NC-R3gSI",1],["7EmboKQH8lM",1],["LKtk3HCgTa8",1],["DpXy041BIlA",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1],["tHEOGrkhDp0",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["IRG-YubP1rw",1],["oz49vQwSoTE",1],["PmTUW-owa2w",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["eC7xzavzEKY",1]]},"N3bJnKmeNJY":{"wy":"HN points to it as a practical, well-researched DIY demonstration of passive radiative cooling.","q":{"x":"This YouTuber[0] does an excellent job recreating chemistry research from papers and improving on the techniques to be accomplished with household materials.","i":36843015,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36843015,"author":"teeray","month":"2023-07","story":"American universities have an incentive to seem extortionate","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Recreates chemistry research from papers using household materials","quote":"This YouTuber[0] does an excellent job recreating chemistry research from papers and improving on the techniques to be accomplished with household materials.","text":"This YouTuber[0] does an excellent job recreating chemistry research from papers and improving on the techniques to be accomplished with household materials. He documents his failures and takes an empirical approach to arriving at his successes. I’m not sure of his background, but it’s certainly research that he’s doing. Watching his channel always reminds me that the scientific method does not have “first obtain an expensive degree” as one of its steps. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46187062,"author":"jcims","month":"2025-12","story":"Mechanical power generation using Earth's ambient radiation","stance":"rec","why":"DIY radiative cooling paint with impressive performance","quote":"It has pretty impressive performance.","text":"DIY radiative cooling paint from YouTuber NightHawkInLight - [VIDEO] It has pretty impressive performance. Tech Ingredients did one or two vids as well - [LINK] Was thinking of whipping up a batch for my rv."},{"id":44358781,"author":"energ8","month":"2025-06","story":"NASA's Voyager Found a 30k-50k Kelvin \"Wall\" at the Edge of Solar System","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on passive radiative cooling paint","quote":"there are a couple good videos about it from NightHawkInLight","text":"It's a thing in from thousands of years ago [LINK] and today [LINK] for PDRC there are a couple good videos about it from NightHawkInLight [VIDEO] , [LINK] and Tech Ingredients [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":35536608,"author":"slimsag","month":"2023-04","story":"Energy-saving coating without pigment keeps the surface beneath cool","stance":"rec","why":"Home-made infrared cooling paint process","quote":"have been working on Infrared Cooling Paint for almost a year now and have been detailing how individuals can make it at home","text":"Two YouTube researchers[0][1] (they make science content that is very much not in the traditional form of YouTube) have been working on Infrared Cooling Paint for almost a year now and have been detailing how individuals can make it at home (although a fairly involved/lengthy process today.) There's definitely something here, hopefully someone sells it pre-made, I'd love to try some. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":34995893,"author":"jcims","month":"2023-03","story":"Just How White Can White Paint Get?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains passive cooling below ambient temperature","quote":"Couple of videos explaining the effect","text":">Only active cooling can cool anything below the ambient temperature. Not exactly: [LINK] Couple of videos explaining the effect [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":32121173,"author":"spyder","month":"2022-07","story":"Why rails buckle in Britain","stance":"reference","why":"YouTuber making paint that cools below ambient temperature","quote":"Here is a YouTuber trying to making such paint","text":"And for a better effect they could use paint that can cool below the ambient temperature by radiating infrared to space (if they aren't already using that, but it's probably more expensive): [LINK] Here is a YouTuber trying to making such paint: [VIDEO] Another article: [LINK]"}],"r":[["KDRnEm-B3AI",2],["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["DXYje2B04xE",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["E1AxVXt2Gv4",1],["aoczYXJeMY4",1],["bPhUhypV27w",1],["cdiD-9MMpb0",1],["krB0enBeSiE",1],["2hf9yN-oBV4",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1],["ta7nlkI5K5g",1],["UDtlvZGmHYk",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["3dl9Ovwmnxw",1],["URSWYvyc42M",1]]},"NXN7TOg3kj4":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an excellent beginner series that fills FreeCAD's gap in learning material.","q":{"x":"MangoJelly does an excellent work on that.","i":40722636,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40722636,"author":"deskr","month":"2024-06","story":"Versioning FreeCAD Files with Git (2021)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent beginner playlist addressing FreeCAD's lack of learning material","quote":"MangoJelly does an excellent work on that.","text":"Look at MangoJelly's beginner's tutorial playlist, start on first video and follow along: [VIDEO] There are definitely problems with FC (but then even expensive CADs have problems). One of the biggest problem I think was lack of good learning material. MangoJelly does an excellent work on that."},{"id":36055465,"author":"ranting-moth","month":"2023-05","story":"A blocky based CAD program","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Good beginner tutorials fill FreeCAD's educational gap","quote":"It will change your life.","text":"FreeCAD's arch nemesis has been lack of good educational material, until very recently. Checkout MangoJelly's \"FreeCAD 0.20 For Beginners Tutorials\" and follow along. It will change your life. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35067447,"author":"avaldez_","month":"2023-03","story":"FreeCAD User Book (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Commenter learned CAD for electronics cases from this series","quote":"I wanted to make cases for my electronics projects and just learned following this series","text":"My experience exactly. I wanted to make cases for my electronics projects and just learned following this series [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["t_yh_S31R9g",1]]},"PYylPRX6z4Q":{"wy":"Seen as the clearest introduction to the idea behind RLHF, and also borrowed by commenters as a metaphor for human motivation.","q":{"x":"I find this video by Rob Miles still the best presentation of the ingenious original 2017(!) paper","i":36862414,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36862414,"author":"cubefox","month":"2023-07","story":"What We Know About LLMs (A Primer)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best presentation of the original 2017 RLHF paper.","quote":"I find this video by Rob Miles still the best presentation of the ingenious original 2017(!) paper","text":"Specifically about RLHF, I find this video by Rob Miles still the best presentation of the ingenious original 2017(!) paper: [VIDEO] RLHF is actually older than GPT-1, which came out in 2018. It didn't get applied to language models until 2022 with InstructGPT, an approach which combined supervised instruction fine-tuning with RLHF."},{"id":21855733,"author":"sillysaurusx","month":"2019-12","story":"Autonomous DeLorean drives sideways to move forward","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Frames ML problem-solving options like human feedback.","quote":"I would highly recommend as a way of framing the problem.","text":"I would highly recommend [VIDEO] as a way of framing the problem. It seems like our options for ML are currently: self-play, dataset fitting, or human feedback. It's possible to solve any problem that can fit into one of those molds. (Unfortunately the opposite is true: if a problem doesn't fit into those categories, it seems very hard to solve with any form of ML.) But, now that I think about it a bit more, the sentence \"use a trained net as a tool for building a controller with known continuity properties\" seems neither obvious nor straightforward to do. I'm decently well-versed in AI techniques at this point, and I can't really think how I'd sit down and write a program that would generate such a system. Anyone have any leads? Basically, the goal is to make a neural net that helps you design a system that can be predictable. One way would be to write a loss function that penalizes unpredictability, and then let it run until it converges on a predictable control system. But that's a bit like saying \"Just draw an owl.\" Not so easy to think of the actual code to do that."},{"id":23909468,"author":"afarrell","month":"2020-07","story":"Ask HN: How do I get out of the procrastination abyss?","stance":"rec","why":"Reward model concept applied to understanding personal motivation.","quote":"To really understand what I'm getting at here, watch this video","text":"ADHDer here. Please steal some of the motivation-techniques I've learnt over the years: 1. Find a friend who is a software engineer or data analyst and cares about you as a person. Talk about this with them. Humans evolved being able to talk things over with their aunt or best friend. When you are perplexed by something, a rubber duck probably won't cut it -- you should talk with a human. 2. Make sure you're taking care of your body and brain: exercise, good food, good sleep (this is easier if you're getting exercise), meditation, etc. 3. Understand what your \"reward model\" is. Under what conditions do you recognize \"Aha! I've made progress!\"? To really understand what I'm getting at here, watch this video: [VIDEO] 4. Find internal things to strengthen your reward model's signals. Go back to when you originally started learning ML and Re-connect to your \"Why\". Do you still find those reasons compelling? If so, take some time to bring some vividness to your picture. -- Start With Why. 5. Find external punishments to remove. Now that you've spent 8 months working on it, you've noticed the things which are annoying you about your workflow. - After 8 months, it is totally healthy to take a week or two and improve your development environment to sand the edges off of things that give you friction. For a stranger's recommendation: [LINK] has made it dramatically easier for me to write little scripts that take away annoying tasks. - Maybe you feel like you've been \"in the weeds\" for"},{"id":25730178,"author":"afarrell","month":"2021-01","story":"Setting goals for 2021 – A brief guide about personal goal setting","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think your URL is a fantastic choice and hints at what a lot of \"accountability coaching\" really is: Camaraderie An easily-imaginable person who acts as a reward model[1] you can imagine saying \"yea! Make progress on the thing! You're on the right path[2]! Keep going.\" When you anticipate [social] rewards for an action, that action is motivating. If you watch Dr. Andrew Huberman's talks about the roles of Dopamine in the brain, he'll say it is not a signal of reward, but of anticipated reward. It does spike when you recognise a surprisingly strong reward, (like when you hit a jackpot or an unexpectedly interesting HN link) to help you learn to repeat the path to that reward[2], but mostly it signals \"Yep, you're on the right path. Now go make that test green\". [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":25872613,"author":"afarrell","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: How To Overcome Information Addiction?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A. Meet your basic biological needs. Your brain is a part of your body. A person with an iron deficiency can feel compelled to chew ice[1]. Ice does not cures anemia, brains are weird parts of the body and do weird things when the body is not getting what it needs. So, get better sleep, food, and exercise. Prof Andrew Huberman has some good tips[2]. B. Decide what few things you really value and allocate your time to those on purpose. If you find yourself repeatedly drawn to the news, maybe you are genuinely give a fuck about civic engagement. Your personality has a need for it. Trying to meet that need by scrolling twitter is like eating 3 bags of tortilla chips for dinner -- quick-access, but not healthy. If you be better off deciding that you value Mexican food, opening your calendar and honestly deciding to spend the time to learn to cook Mexican food. Look for the virtue underneath your vice -- What information are you most drawn to and what healthy activities does it point towards? Pick 3 and pursue those deeply. Why 3? You can only honestly give a fuck about a few things[3], so that requires deciding to not do other reasonable fun activities. If shallow engagement with your interests has been your primary mode of procrastination, then honest and deep engagement might deceptively feel like a waste of time. That makes this hard. Why arbitrarily avoid those other things? You are choosing to be the arbiter of your time. Take pride in grappling with the fear of procrastinat"},{"id":24259074,"author":"afarrell","month":"2020-08","story":"Persisting as a solo founder","stance":"reference","why":"Reward model concept applied to personal habits.","quote":"what is your 'reward model'? --","text":"as an ADHDer, one thing I've found key is figuring out what things in my environment and mind are the things I want to signal \"you're doing the right thing, keep going.\" Essentially, what is your 'reward model'? -- [VIDEO] Test-Driven-Development is one example of this."}],"r":[["42QuXLucH3Q",1],["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1],["NxvQPzrg2Wg",1],["YHLU0Uej1WA",1],["fCn8zs912OE",1],["Ggz_gd--UO0",1],["Ox5P7QyL774",1],["DpXy041BIlA",1],["S7TUe5w6RHo",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["Yocja_N5s1I",1],["nm1TxQj9IsQ",1],["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["bBC-nXj3Ng4",1],["NlIdzF1_b5M",1],["kjBOesZCoqc",1],["NQFqDKRAROI",1],["Sf036fO-ZUk",1]]},"ZPoqNeR3_UA":{"wy":"HN people use it as background noise for coding and focus, and share it as a personal favorite.","q":{"x":"I just listen to the Star Trek TNG Ambient Engine Noise[1] if I'm coding or trying to focus.","i":18502455,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18502455,"author":"octosphere","month":"2018-11","story":"Brain.fm: Music to improve focus, meditation and sleep","stance":"rec","why":"Used for coding and focus.","quote":"I just listen to the Star Trek TNG Ambient Engine Noise[1] if I'm coding or trying to focus.","text":"I just listen to the Star Trek TNG Ambient Engine Noise[1] if I'm coding or trying to focus. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12965748,"author":"TheSpiceIsLife","month":"2016-11","story":"Compulab Airtop – Natural Airflow Desktop","stance":"rec","why":"Starship Enterprise idling for 24 hours.","quote":"If you like those you might also like the star ship Enterprise idling, for 24hrs","text":"If you like those you might also like the star ship Enterprise idling, for 24hrs... for a more futuristic spin (haha!) on things. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9022331,"author":"getdavidhiggins","month":"2015-02","story":"Noisli – Improve focus and boost your productivity","stance":"rec","why":"24-hour engine hum for background listening.","quote":"This is a personal favorite too","text":"I listen to this whilst surfing the web: [LINK] Also handy: [LINK] [LINK] This is a personal favorite too - Star Trek TNG Ambient Engine Noise (Idling for 24 hrs): [VIDEO] Personally, whilst coding, I enjoy absolute terrifying silence."},{"id":7894092,"author":"avar","month":"2014-06","story":"Pixel Art + Star Trek = Pixel Trek","stance":"rec","why":"Background engine noise complementing a Star Trek pixel art page.","quote":"Having this on in the background compliments it","text":"Having this on in the background compliments it (\"Star Trek TNG Ambient Engine Noise (Idling for 24 hrs)\"): [VIDEO] Alternatively to mix BSG into it: [LINK]"},{"id":3984508,"author":"SkyMarshal","month":"2012-05","story":"Show HN: Daydream.fm - build and share your own world of soundscape and music.","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Fun app. I made one, have a few suggestions: [LINK] 1. The song title is overlaying my screenshot, munging it all up. Suggest moving the title to the top bar or somewhere it won't mess up the screenshot/background (same with the comments box on the right). 2. Music played when I previewed it, but it's not playing after publishing. Not sure why. 3. Due to #2 I want to edit my creation and change from this 'song' (white noise actually): [VIDEO] to this one: [VIDEO] to test if one can play and the other can't. But I don't see a way to edit a creation."},{"id":3985922,"author":"SkyMarshal","month":"2012-05","story":"Show HN: Daydream.fm - build and share your own world of soundscape and music.","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">regarding #1: how about hiding them when you're idle? Would this work? (Just pushed an update, please check it) Just checked, good enough. >regarding #2: Is it still not playing? I visited that url [LINK] and it seems to be playing. By the way, is it still not playing? It seems to work on mine. Also, the two youtube links you mentioned seem to be the same, aren't they? Still not playing though. And sorry, the two youtube links are supposed to be different. One is 24hrs, one is 1.5hrs. Here they are again: [VIDEO] (24hr) [LINK] (1.5hr)"},{"id":3730045,"author":"CGamesPlay","month":"2012-03","story":"Open-plan offices must die","stance":"reference","why":"Ambient engine noise used for focus.","quote":"is a personal favorite","text":"I really enjoy working in an open plan office. The distractions don't really bother me and I appreciate the energy level going around. I obviously use headphones to cancel out noise when I need to focus, sometimes just playing white noise tracks or no sound at all ( [VIDEO] is a personal favorite). Having a heavy distraction-oriented work flow forces you into a mode where you segment your tasks as atomically as possible, for example using the pomodoro technique. There are certainly times when a quiet space is necessary, and I will retreat away from my desk to a library or quiet corner where I'm not near people. I wonder how much of this is because I am young (24). My generation has grown up bombarded by distractions. So much so, in fact, that I find that there are equally many distractions inside of my computer as there are coming from the office plan."}],"r":[["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["PIPjmmmh_os",1],["N6xLMUifbxQ",1],["AbSehcT19u0",1]]},"v6jhjjVy5Ls":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an investigative look at CS skin gambling and the exploitation of minors.","q":{"x":"is well worth watching if you are interested in the subject","i":45683102,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45683102,"author":"pogue","month":"2025-10","story":"Counter Strike 2 update wipes nearly $2B off skin market value","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Exposes CSGO skin gambling scene and exploitation of minors.","quote":"is well worth watching if you are interested in the subject","text":"It's wild to me that people will use things like this as unregulated security investments. Coffeezilla did a great video series exposing the CSGO skin gambling scene and how it borders on straight up crime and exploitation (especially of kids to young to legally gamble) and is well worth watching if you are interested in the subject. It's a three part series & I can't remember which one is first... The Dark Side of Counter-Strike 2 [VIDEO]"},{"id":42792440,"author":"PinkSheep","month":"2025-01","story":"Reverse engineering Call of Duty anti-cheat","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Investigative series on Valve and skin gambling sites.","quote":"I highly recommend the videos.","text":"The above commenter was probably inspired by the recent investigative(!) video series by Coffeezilla (and decided not to mention it?). It was either in part 2 or 3, where C-z alleged/suspected Valve's legal actions to have been mostly about good PR and to send a signal to those ~websites~ businesses to keep them in check. However it is indeed the case, that Valve has introduced greater and greater restrictions on inventory handling. The measures obviously go far beyond just counteracting possible scammers and phishing. Still, I am inclined to believe, they could've implemented all these features many years ago, if only they had wanted to. I highly recommend the videos. You can maybe skip the first one. It's mostly about casino owners' drama. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":42492037,"author":"viraptor","month":"2024-12","story":"Ask HN: Predictions for 2025?","stance":"rec","why":"Investigation into CS skin casino business.","quote":"Coffeezilla just released two videos digging into the csgo skin casinos business","text":"Good timing! If you have a spare hour that is. Coffeezilla just released two videos digging into the csgo skin casinos business: [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["q58dLWjRTBE",2],["xMYm2d9bmEA",1],["Kr3quGh7pJA",1],["j2dHFC31VtQ",1],["lHcTKWiZ8sI",1],["OUNXFHpUhu8",1],["6c_LeIXrzAk",1],["ghUs-84NAAU",1],["7Hlb8YX2-W8",1],["13eiDhuvM6Y",1]]},"2aCOyOvOw5c":{"wy":"HN recommends it for its raw, mostly natural audio and for conveying the speeds and violence of a shuttle launch and booster descent.","q":{"x":"I immediately had to re-watch this pretty awesome video after reading the article","i":8564638,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8564638,"author":"0x0","month":"2014-11","story":"The Mysterious Mental Side Effects of Traveling into Space","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Liftoff to return in minutes with creepy real audio.","quote":"I immediately had to re-watch this pretty awesome video after reading the article","text":"I immediately had to re-watch this pretty awesome video after reading the article: [VIDEO] It goes from liftoff to space and back to earth in a handful of minutes, and the enhanced (but real) audio is pretty creepy."},{"id":8072257,"author":"ejr","month":"2014-07","story":"SpaceX Soft Lands Falcon 9 Rocket First Stage","stance":"rec","why":"Shows shuttle boosters descending, conveying the velocities involved.","quote":"Here's a very nice video of the shuttle boosters on their way down to give you an idea of the velocities involved.","text":"There's deceleration involved as well. Here's a very nice video of the shuttle boosters on their way down to give you an idea of the velocities involved. [VIDEO] An enormous amount of inertia to overcome so 30% doesn't sound so unlikely."},{"id":7390009,"author":"sk5t","month":"2014-03","story":"Drone Flies Into an Active Volcano [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Booster rocket footage with excellent natural sound.","quote":"This video of a booster rocket has excellent, mostly-natural sound","text":"This video of a booster rocket has excellent, mostly-natural sound: [VIDEO] It came to mind due to the airborne / hostile environment combination..."}],"r":[["-5wpm-gesOY",1]]},"T2OxO-4YLRk":{"wy":"Held up as an exemplary GDC postmortem and a first-hand account for fans of the original Fallout.","q":{"x":"GDC post mortems are amazing, especially the ones on Diablo[0] and Fallout[1].","i":30809987,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30809987,"author":"somedude895","month":"2022-03","story":"Wolfenstein 3D secrets revealed by John Romero in lengthy post-mortem chat","stance":"strong_rec","why":"GDC Fallout postmortem inspired post-apocalypse media exploration","quote":"GDC post mortems are amazing, especially the ones on Diablo[0] and Fallout[1].","text":"I can't wait for this to go up on Youtube. GDC post mortems are amazing, especially the ones on Diablo[0] and Fallout[1]. I've loved Fallout since I was 12 and it really got me into post-apocalypse fiction. I ended up watching and reading all the media that Tim cites in his presentation and discovered some wonderful things like On the Beach (the book by Nevil Shute AND the movie), A Canticle for Leibowitz, etc [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46124034,"author":"aresant","month":"2025-12","story":"Fallout 2's Chris Avellone describes his game design philosophy","stance":"rec","why":"Fallout creator Tim Cain's postmortem of making the game","quote":"worth a watch is the Classic Game Postmortem: Fallout video from Fallout creator Tim Cain","text":"Great find and also worth a watch is the Classic Game Postmortem: Fallout video from Fallout creator Tim Cain [VIDEO]"},{"id":17848059,"author":"Cognitron","month":"2018-08","story":"The Making of Prince of Persia (2011) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Fallout postmortem for fans of the first game","quote":"Also for Fallout fans (the first game)","text":"I like these too. Here's Jordan Mechner on Prince of Persia: [LINK] Also for Fallout fans (the first game): (EDIT: unrelated to Prince of Persia, I just like Fallout) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["tt2AYafET68",1],["7QmCUDHpNzE",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1],["Mlrrc_vy79E",1],["LsGZ_2RuJ2A",1],["0bcZb-SsnrA",1]]},"T5RmMNDR5XI":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an instructive, easy-to-follow look at Lucene internals from a core committer.","q":{"x":"I can super highly recommend talks by Adrien Grand on Lucene and Elasticsearch.","i":21989365,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":21989365,"author":"drej","month":"2020-01","story":"Elasticsearch from the Bottom Up (2013)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Instructive, easy-to-follow talks on Lucene internals","quote":"I can super highly recommend talks by Adrien Grand on Lucene and Elasticsearch.","text":"I can super highly recommend talks by Adrien Grand on Lucene and Elasticsearch. They are super instructive yet easy to follow. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":23655335,"author":"stereosteve","month":"2020-06","story":"Reducing search indexing latency to one second","stance":"rec","why":"Good review of Lucene concepts","quote":"found this video really good","text":"This is excellent. I was recently reviewing Lucene concepts and found this video really good: [VIDEO] Also this site has a series of Lucene articles that are pretty nice. The one on Term Vectors in particular: [LINK] Based on some quick research it seems like Lucene is already using a sorted skip data structure for the posting list, so I wonder why they had to do a custom implementation? Perhaps it has to do with their custom Document ID scheme and how they want to preserve order in the Posting List being different from the default behavior. It also sounds like searchers are searching on indexes as they're being written, and there is some custom coordination around visibility, which might require diverging from Lucene default behavior. Either way, pretty impressive!"},{"id":21017006,"author":"ddorian43","month":"2019-09","story":"Challenges in Implementing a Full-Text-Search Engine","stance":"rec","why":"Look at Lucene internals from a core committer","quote":"A nice view of how lucene internals work by core committer Adrien Grand","text":"This is extremely high level. A nice view of how lucene internals work by core committer Adrien Grand: [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["0mCsluv5FXA",1],["nAbCKa0FzjQ",1],["bSkpMdDe4g4",1],["4fFDFbi3toc",1],["MO8hZlgK5zc",1]]},"baxtyeFVn3w":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible demonstration of what Smalltalk environments offer and a reference for advanced IDE ideas.","q":{"x":"I think it is the most accessible explanation of the marvel of Smalltalk","i":23509075,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23509075,"author":"jasim","month":"2020-06","story":"Design Principles Behind Smalltalk (1981)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Most accessible explanation of Smalltalk's marvel for those who never used it.","quote":"I think it is the most accessible explanation of the marvel of Smalltalk","text":"If you're tired of hearing about the virtues of Smalltalk (like I was at one point) and ask \"if it is so good, why isn't it popular?\", then watch @deech's talk (Aditya Siram) - \"What FP can learn from SmallTalk\" - [VIDEO] I think it is the most accessible explanation of the marvel of Smalltalk, for those who were not lucky to work with it during the late 80-90s. Also I think Ruby is the mainstream language that is closest to Smalltalk today, with the idea that everything is an object and late-binding as much as possible. One thing I can't help point out is that Smalltalk / Alan Kay's vision of interconnected objects forming a recursive computing system is not the only \"true\" vision of OO out there. From Simula thru C++ and then Java and C# also are object-oriented (or class-oriented for those who care about the distinction), but they are statically typed. It is also OO because OO is a word, not a mathematical definition, and when people talk about OO, there is enough similarity between all these variations that we consider all of them to be in the same camp. Plus with the growing move towards strict static typing even in interpreted languages, which I think is driven by actual industry needs and programmer preferences than external marketing, it is becoming a disservice to think of statically typed OO languages as being somehow inferior to the true ideal of object-oriented programming."},{"id":26167627,"author":"nsm","month":"2021-02","story":"The Database Inside Your Codebase","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of code introspection and visualization around minute 17.","quote":"Check out a video demo of the code introspection and visualization at minute 17 in","text":"I am really glad they mentioned Smalltalk! I recently had a chance to experience Smalltalk and it was just mind blowing. Check out a video demo of the code introspection and visualization at minute 17 in [VIDEO]"},{"id":25642717,"author":"nsm","month":"2021-01","story":"On Repl-Driven Programming","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended talk alongside Glamorous Toolkit work.","quote":"I'd also add this talk","text":"I'd also add this talk \"Lambda World 2018 - What FP can learn from Smalltalk by Aditya Siram \" [VIDEO] as well as the whole series of things that Tudor Girba & co. are doing with Glamorous Toolkit [LINK] I'd still recommend folks use Pharo over GToolkit for most things, as GToolkit lacks a bunch of polish and documentation (not to say that even Pharo documentation approaches anything of the quality of the Rust and Python ecosystems)."},{"id":38792926,"author":"tcard","month":"2023-12","story":"IDEs we had 30 years ago","stance":"reference","why":"Pharo IDE demo illustrating advanced Smalltalk environments","quote":"Smalltalk(s) have had in many aspects the most advanced IDE in existence at every moment since its introduction","text":"I think it's fair to say Smalltalk(s) have had in many aspects the most advanced IDE in existence at every moment since its introduction. Demos for a couple old versions: [LINK] Demo for Pharo: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29307703,"author":"Supermancho","month":"2021-11","story":"When are enums not a code smell?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of IDE support for navigating small functions in gtoolkit","quote":"","text":"Not so much .NET/Java but Structured Programming (1972 Edsger W. Dijkstra, Ole-Johan Dahl, and Tony Hoare) has prompted the gradual backlash against switches. ( [LINK] , re: Duff's Device). Senior developers should watch the whole thing to understand how block scope and functions and classes relate. Modern development has drifted toward lots of small functions (which causes a mess because modern IDEs dont have supporting features like this: [VIDEO] , re: gtoolkit). A switch requires you to look inside and reason about the internals of a function to understand how it will behave before exiting, as the compiler. This doesn't matter so much if you dont care about early exits (no branch prediction, etc). Either way, it's extra cognitive overhead and error prone for a small improvement in readbaility. It's the line where programming becomes tricky programming. As a thought experiment, imagine there is ANOTHER keyword that acts like an if/else (or switch with mandatory breaks). If A. it's terse, B. it's structured, C. it's readable, would that be better? We primarily use switch because it's easy to read (versus a ton of if {}/else {}), as it gives us almost-block scoping without having to define another indirection/function. Python has this switch equivalent in match/case, but you have to use Python to get it. Switch use is a syntax problem. Lots of software is colored by bad syntax and language design^. ^ I think most people realize that type hierarchies are a brittle/limited form"},{"id":29331666,"author":"Supermancho","month":"2021-11","story":"What's the Future of IDEs?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows easy indirection resolution in gtoolkit","quote":"Back to easy indirection resolution","text":"To answer the question in a more practical sense than the plugging of a specific workflow and asking \"Is this the future?\", that the article posits: 1. Back to easy indirection resolution ( [VIDEO] , re: gtoolkit) 2. Comments that are not inline, but can point to various blocks/parts of code and overlap (multiple comments can apply to single parts of code). 3. More support for unit testing. function call tracking ( [LINK] ). 4. Conducting behavioral studies via opt-in submission of eye tracking data. A great deal of data is flowing through IDEs and not being leveraged to improve the daily tools of developers."},{"id":27306118,"author":"emilprogviz","month":"2021-05","story":"It's probably time to stop recommending Clean Code (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of Pharo's transient inlining editor feature","quote":"the Smalltalk editor Pharo already has a similar feature, demonstrated at","text":"Signed up just to say that I've also really, really wanted such a tool since forever. While for example the Jetbrains IntelliJ family of editors has the automatic \"inline function\" refactoring, they do it by permanently modifying the source code, which is not quite what we want. Like you say, it should be transient! So I recently made a quick&dirty interactive mock-up of how such an editor feature could look. The mockup is just a web page with javascript and html canvas, so it's easy to try here: [LINK] (Not mobile friendly, best seen on desktop) There are 2 different ways to show the inlining. You can choose between them if you click the cogwheel icon. Then I learned that the Smalltalk editor Pharo already has a similar feature, demonstrated at [VIDEO] I wish other editors would steal this idea. Microsoft, are you listening? My mock-up also shows an idea to improve code folding. When folding / collapsing a large block of code, the editor could show a quick summary of the block. The summary could be similar to a function signature, with arguments and return values."}],"r":[["-J8YyfrSwTk",1],["IcgmSRJHu_8",1],["DgVS-zXgMTk",1],["tISy7EJQPzI",1]]},"goePYJ74Ydg":{"wy":"HN recommends it as part of Primer's strong series of evolution and emergence simulations.","q":{"x":"Primer, who's had a great string of genetic/evolution simulation videos","i":26634585,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26634585,"author":"jedimastert","month":"2021-03","story":"Show HN: Play with Shaders Inspired by Slime Mold","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Primer's evolution simulations of emergent society","quote":"Primer, who's had a great string of genetic/evolution simulation videos","text":"Coding Aventure is one of my favorite intellectual(?) exploration channels, along with Primer, who's had a great string of genetic/evolution simulation videos exploring the emergent evolution of society [VIDEO]"},{"id":27475156,"author":"qvrjuec","month":"2021-06","story":"ALiEn – a GPU-accelerated artificial life simulation program","stance":"rec","why":"Videos demonstrating evolution of altruism","quote":"there are a bunch of really interesting videos made to demonstrate concepts like the evolution of altruism","text":"Also, \"The Selfish Gene\". Super fun read. Also, there are a bunch of really interesting videos made to demonstrate concepts like the evolution of altruism on youtube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26702399,"author":"ghgdynb1","month":"2021-04","story":"Yellen calls for global minimum corporate tax rate","stance":"rec","why":"Simplified exploration of green beard altruism, imposters section","quote":"The final section (imposters) of this [1] simplified exploration of “green beard altruism” might be of interest to you","text":"Happy you found it helpful! The final section (imposters) of this [1] simplified exploration of “green beard altruism” might be of interest to you if you want to explore this further. If you’re interested in thinking about things like this generally, these are Nash equilibrium problems and the field is called game theory. In answer: if we do discard the assumptions of game theory and we imagine nobody would ever think to “cheat,” it could work. But in reality it wouldn’t be just that some countries have huge advantages in some industries— they would have huge advantages in every industry. People and companies looking to maximize their opportunities would flee to the country with the lowest effective (nominal doesn’t matter) tax rate which could also guarantee their safety. This is actually part of how the US ended up burying the Soviet Union/eastern Bloc with economic productivity— we kept poaching all their best engineers, scientists, mathematicians, and entrepreneurs. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8PmM6SUn7Es",1],["3AtBE9BOvvk",1],["Oo0jm1PPRuo",1],["GsyhGHUEt-k",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["lIFE7h3m40U",1],["Fy0aCDmgnxg",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["LnzuMJLZRdU",1],["WZskjLq040I",1],["aVwxzDHniEw",1],["jGWFg7EDnyY",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1]]},"oYp5XuGYqqY":{"wy":"Cited in discussions of perception, consciousness and what is real, though some commenters find his conclusions hand-wavy.","q":{"x":"I encourage you to watch Donald Hoffman's 2015 TED Talk","i":27639049,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":21272882,"author":"patcon","month":"2019-10","story":"Depressed People See the World More Realistically (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Simulations show fitness-only perceivers outcompete those perceiving true reality.","quote":"This reminds me of this amazing TED Talk","text":"This reminds me of this amazing TED Talk: Do we see reality as it is? (Donald Hoffman, cognitive scientist) [VIDEO] The intellectual bomb in the video is this: Organisms that perceive only \"fitness\" drive to extinction all organisms that perceive reality as it is. (in model systems simulating environments and creatures navigating them) My take: at every level, it would seem, perceiving true reality is not favoured. Nevermind cognitive biases, that's just one level. Literally the fabric of the universe: spacetime, matter, language, etc. It means nothing that it might feel comfortable or intuitive -- we should question it all."},{"id":27639049,"author":"cartoonworld","month":"2021-06","story":"Ask HN: Words of encouragement for someone lost in life?","stance":"rec","why":"Australian jewel beetle example of misled perception, around 100 seconds in.","quote":"I encourage you to watch Donald Hoffman's 2015 TED Talk","text":"This is extremely relatable and true. Thank you. The Tinder game is so horrible, 5% of the women get the attention of 80% of the men or more, leaving both the men and women in the other group feeling like there is something wrong with them. People feel the need to edit themselves in order to fit the false narrative created by these gamified dating scenarios, or else experience the fear of dying alone . There is nothing wrong with you. Our complicated, 3-dimensional, deep , personalities and lives are not meant to be reduced to a catalog, best consumed on the toilet, dismissed or accepted instantly based upon a false presentation designed to harness the evolutionary biology of actual living beings. For money. This is not constrained to human society. There is an amusing anecdote of the Australian Jewel Beetle that also experiences the Tinder Effect. This nearly caused their extinction. I encourage you to watch Donald Hoffman's 2015 TED Talk \"Do we see reality as it is?\"[0] linked below for your convenience. I've timestamped the relevant section, skip forward about 100 seconds or so for the direct discussion of the Australian jewel beetle. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":12758883,"author":"wh-uws","month":"2016-10","story":"Image Synthesis from Yahoo's open_nsfw","stance":"rec","why":"Hoffman's hypothesis that perception does not show reality as it is.","quote":"fascinating stuff","text":"This is basically the exact hypothesis of a TED talk I saw by cognitive scientist Donald Hoffman. [VIDEO] fascinating stuff"},{"id":42492248,"author":"patcon","month":"2024-12","story":"GPT-5 is behind schedule","stance":"reference","why":"Hoffman's simulations of truth-seeing versus fitness-seeing creatures.","quote":"His lab runs evolutionary simulations","text":"There's something here that I feel is pretty deep, though offensive for some minds: What is the actual consequence of being wrong? Of not getting right the base reality of a situation? Usually, stasis is the enemy that is much great than false information. If people with 90% truth can take a step forward in the world, even if they mistakenly think they have 100% truth, what does it matter? They're learning more and acting more for that step taken. If the mistaken ground truth is false and importantly enough false, they'll learn it bc their experience is grounded in the reality the navigate anyhow. If they don't learn it, it's of no consequence. This is on my mind because I work in democratic reform, and I am acutely aware (from books like \"Democracy for Realists\", that eviscerate common assumptions about \"how democracy works\") that it often doesn't matter if we understand how democracy is working, so long as we feel like we do, enough to take steps forward and keep trying and learning. We literally don't even know how democracy works, and yet we've been living under it for centuries, to decent enough ends. I think often about the research of Donald Hoffman. His lab runs evolutionary simulations, putting \"creatures\" that see \"reality\" (of the simulation) against creatures that see only \"fitness\" (the abstraction, but also the lie, that is more about seeing what gets the creature living to the next click of the engine, whether that's truth or falsehood about the reality). [VIDE"},{"id":39499836,"author":"tibbydudeza","month":"2024-02","story":"Hallucination is inevitable: An innate limitation of large language models","stance":"reference","why":"TED talk on perception approximating reality.","quote":"For others here is a TED talk.","text":"The brain fakes it to approximate reality - more so for reasons of the limitations of the wetware it runs on than anything else. For others here is a TED talk. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13791721,"author":"psyc","month":"2017-03","story":"Is Consciousness an Illusion?","stance":"reference","why":"Hoffman's user-illusion idea of perception.","quote":"The user-illusion metaphor is a central idea in Donald Hoffman's work.","text":"The user-illusion metaphor is a central idea in Donald Hoffman's work. I'm not sure who borrowed it from whom. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27639332,"author":"copperx","month":"2021-06","story":"Ask HN: Words of encouragement for someone lost in life?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he just handwaves the idea that reality is not \"brains and neurons,","text":"> [0] - [VIDEO] While most of the talk is great, he just handwaves the idea that reality is not \"brains and neurons,\" nor what physicists try to model, but he makes no effort in explaining what's the difference between \"reality\" and \"physics.\" It would be great if someone who understood that last part could explain it."}],"r":[["VYhAGnsnO7w",1],["PGv5BqNL164",1],["TMbl_ofF3AM",1],["HMBl0ttu-Ow",1],["86Krv3gE-c4",1],["1Nh_vxpycEA",1],["wt-iVFxgFWk",1]]},"rFqL9CkNxXw":{"wy":"HN readers point to it as a clear explanation of a theoretical fuel-free propulsion concept from a channel they respect.","q":{"x":"Prof. Kipping is well known for his Cool Worlds youtube channel, which is fantastic.","i":34370113,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34370113,"author":"mperham","month":"2023-01","story":"The Halo Drive: Fuel-Free Relativistic Propulsion (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Kipping explains his Halo Drive idea on his fantastic channel.","quote":"Prof. Kipping is well known for his Cool Worlds youtube channel, which is fantastic.","text":"Prof. Kipping is well known for his Cool Worlds youtube channel, which is fantastic. He created a video on his Halo Drive idea: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28231512,"author":"chii","month":"2021-08","story":"A Dyson sphere around a black hole","stance":"rec","why":"Theoretical light-based propulsion concept.","quote":"it's a very nice theoretical concept","text":"also, you can use light, instead of matter. Termed the \"halo drive\", it's a very nice theoretical concept: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20072527,"author":"jp555","month":"2019-06","story":"Ways to travel at nearly the speed of light","stance":"rec","why":"Explains fuel-free propulsion via a black hole gravity assist.","quote":"The halo drive concept is interesting.","text":"The halo drive concept is interesting. It uses a beam of light to extract gravity energy from a black hole and use to to maintain acceleration of your ship. Almost no fuel needed because its really just a kind of extreme gravity assist that we’ve been doing since what, the 1970s? [VIDEO]"},{"id":19330515,"author":"_Microft","month":"2019-03","story":"Black Hole Propulsion as Technosignature","stance":"reference","why":"Cites a specific point in the video on travel cost.","quote":"","text":"If you have the technology to reach the nearest binary blackhole ( \"you just have to pay the one-time fee of the cost to reach the nearest binary black hole system\" , at 16:12 min in this video: [VIDEO] ), you can most likely deal with the mirror/energy harvesting problem as well."}],"r":[["ZuvK-od647c",1],["JRuNA2eK7w0",1],["QPKKQnijnsM",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["Cj4y0EUlU-Y",1],["nvK8fua6O64",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["YGLNyHd2w10",1],["HI09kat_GeI",1]]},"uU_Q2a0S0zI":{"wy":"Recommended as the source for counterintuitive high-dimensional geometry and described as an eye opener.","q":{"x":"This was an eye opener to me.","i":41472454,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41472454,"author":"bgnn","month":"2024-09","story":"How does cosine similarity work?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hamming lecture showing why intuition fails in higher dimensions.","quote":"This was an eye opener to me.","text":"One cannot intuitively think about higher than 3 dimensions. Even for most their intuition is often wrong in 3D space. It's quite accurate for 1D and 2D. Richard Hamming has a whole section lecture to make everyone realize precisely this [1]. This was an eye opener to me. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36012762,"author":"time_to_smile","month":"2023-05","story":"Counterintuitive Properties of High Dimensional Space","stance":"rec","why":"Hamming's lecture on n-dimensional space, the source of the article's ideas.","quote":"I highly recommend checking out Hamming's lectures if you find this enjoyable.","text":"A good chunk of this comes directly from Richard Hamming's incredible The Art of doing Science and Engineering (a video of the specific lecture on n-dimensional spaces can be found here[0]) and yet I see no mention of this talk anywhere in the article, which is unfortunate. I highly recommend checking out Hamming's lectures if you find this enjoyable. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29971064,"author":"it_does_follow","month":"2022-01","story":"A high-dimensional sphere spilling out of a high-dimensional cube","stance":"rec","why":"Hamming lecture covering high-dimensional sphere and cube geometry.","quote":"Richard Hamming includes a section on this in his n-dimensional spaces talk","text":"To add to this list: Richard Hamming includes a section on this in his n-dimensional spaces talk from \"The Art of Doing Science and Engineering\" lectures [0]. Stripe Press also recently re-published a beautiful copy of the print version of these lectures [1]. 0. [VIDEO] 1. [LINK]"}]},"wE5G1kTndI4":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a methodical argument that many US city pairs make economic sense for high-speed rail.","q":{"x":"CityNerd identifies 56 train segments that should be built, using data and reasonable, precedented criteria","i":39982309,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39513637,"author":"theluketaylor","month":"2024-02","story":"Spain's plan to ban domestic flights where you can take a train in under 2.5 hrs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"City pairs suited for high speed rail","quote":"Highly recommended.","text":"The CityNerd youtube channel recently did a list of city pairs ideally suited for high speed rail based on Spain's own network. spoiler alert: the northeast corridor plays a pretty heavy part, but it's not the only place in the US high speed rail makes a ton of sense. Highly recommended. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40224160,"author":"alistairSH","month":"2024-05","story":"General Motors Streetcar Conspiracy","stance":"rec","why":"Good summary of city pairs for high speed rail","quote":"CityNerd does a nice summary of city pairs that should have high speed rail based on population and distance","text":"Mixed answer. One driving factor in the creation of the interstate system was military - we wanted the ability to easily move troops/supplies across the US without airlift or rail. Not sure that's still a valid need (as in, I don't know - would need to ask a general). Also, the US is huge. We do need some sort of road system to get across it. Building rail to everywhere is prohibitively expensive. That said, we should/could have good high speed rail in the densest areas. Like expand the Acela corridor CityNerd does a nice summary of city pairs that should have high speed rail based on population and distance... [VIDEO] tl;dr - DC-NYC tops the list (and include Philly and Balt). LA to SF is near the top. Chicago-DET-Toronto is up there. And on down the list. Basically, there's no good reason (other than momentum/history) to not build out solid rail networks over parts of the US. I don't think anybody reasonably expects an LA-NYC link any time soon because flying is a better option for now."},{"id":39982309,"author":"browningstreet","month":"2024-04","story":"San Francisco-to-L.A. overnight train inches closer to reality","stance":"rec","why":"Data-driven methodology picking 56 rail segments","quote":"CityNerd identifies 56 train segments that should be built, using data and reasonable, precedented criteria","text":"CityNerd identifies 56 train segments that should be built, using data and reasonable, precedented criteria: [VIDEO] His video description: \"Nothing that exists in North America fits the definition of true high-speed rail. If we actually ever started building a network, where would we start? And how would we know when we were done? This video details a methodology for answering all those questions, then counts up 56 city pairs we really should've had high-speed rail service between long, long ago.\""},{"id":45756324,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2025-10","story":"Uv is the best thing to happen to the Python ecosystem in a decade","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as proof many US city pairs justify HSR","quote":"There are plenty of city pairs where high speed trains do make economic sense","text":"> there are vast stretches of the country where trains just do not make economic sense There are plenty of city pairs where high speed trains do make economic sense and America still doesn't have them . [1] is a video \"56 high speed rail links we should've built already\" by CityNerd. And that's aside from providing services for the greater good instead of for profit - subsidizing public transport to make a city center more walkable and more profitable and safer and cleaner can be a worthwhile thing. The US government spends a lot subsidizing air travel. > So no, I don't think Europeans who haven't been in America have quite absorbed just how vast America is China had some 26,000 miles of high speed rail two years ago, almost 30,000 miles now connecting 550 cities, and adding another couple of thousand miles by 2030. A hundred plus years ago America had train networks coast to coast. Now all Americans have is excuses why the thing you used to have and tore up is impossible, infeasible, unafordable, unthinkable. You have reusable space rockets that can land on a pillar of fire . If y'all had put as much effort into it as you have into special pleading about why it's impossible, you could have had it years ago. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["6RhtiPefVzM",1],["Y6U728AZnV0",1]]},"ACkmg3Y64_s":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an absorbing look at the most elaborate model railroad ever built.","q":{"x":"You can watch a short tour of it at","i":17098722,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":30284879,"author":"netsharc","month":"2022-02","story":"Simutrans – Transport Simulator","stance":"rec","why":"Absorbing miniature world with remotely controlled cars","quote":"Here's your productivity black hole of the day","text":"Here's your productivity black hole of the day: [VIDEO] Although the exciting software is to remotely control the cars, written in Delphi, but they haven't published these (understandably). They're even working on an F1 race with cars that will decide to overtake themselves: [LINK] (subtitles available), he said, 16 core CPU, well, there you go, 16 car brains!"},{"id":17098722,"author":"jfim","month":"2018-05","story":"Tiny Trains: Neat Narrow-Gauge Rail Connects German Island to Mainland","stance":"rec","why":"Short tour of huge model train exhibit with airport and day/night cycle","quote":"You can watch a short tour of it at","text":"Coincidentally, Germany also has the world's largest model train exhibit, which even has a fully functional airport (with functional departure board, of course) and a full day/night cycle. You can watch a short tour of it at [VIDEO]"},{"id":13583125,"author":"Animats","month":"2017-02","story":"Victorian Era Drones: How Model Trains Transformed from Cutting-Edge to Quaint","stance":"rec","why":"Tour of the most elaborate model railroad in the world","quote":"Check out Minatur Wunderland, the most elaborate model railroad in the world.[1]","text":"\"Model Railroading is Fun\". Said so on the cover of every issue of Model Railroader magazine for decades. They're trying too hard. Check out Minatur Wunderland, the most elaborate model railroad in the world.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["eviSykqSUUw",2],["WU0gvPcc3jQ",1],["Uj-rK8V-rik",1],["3UxZDJ1HiPE",1],["5ESJH1NLMLs",1],["JOLtS4VUcvQ",1],["nnR8fDW3Ilo",1],["3TFz1xqp3us",1],["wM8Xli2KTzI",1],["fzBRYsiyxjI",1],["nsJZ_kKjXcE",1],["LSxbc1IN9Gg",1],["t1MqJPHxy6g",1],["Q2aEzeMDHMA",1]]},"HBluLfX2F_k":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it for changing how they think about distributions and for showing how criticality shows up across earthquakes, wildfires and other fields.","q":{"x":"Before I watched this I would default to thinking about most distributions as normal.","i":46354788,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":49277156,"author":"gopalv","month":"2026-08","story":"Your Key to Success Isn't More Luck or Hard Work","stance":"rec","why":"Nice video on knowing which kind of game you are playing.","quote":"Veritasium put out a very nice video[1] about knowing which sort of game we're playing.","text":"Your success eV is opportunities x success-rate. Out of the gate, hard work improves success rates, but not the count of opportunities. Connections improve opportunities, it doesn't do shit for success-rate. Luck does for both sides of the mix. As a startup founder in my 40s, the entire VP/SVP level in silicon valley is people one or two hops away for an intro, though admittedly a lot of the first hop there is through the other founders. The original paper about importance of weak ties is under appreciated, because people you work with tend to go on to better & bigger things - especially in the beginning of your career, if you are a nice person. Veritasium put out a very nice video[1] about knowing which sort of game we're playing. It is a power law game. There are only two takeaways I had, \"show up\" and \"get lucky\". There is no winning without showing up. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":46945467,"author":"kjshsh123","month":"2026-02","story":"Five disciplines discovered the same math independently – none of them knew","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on how criticality applies across fields.","quote":"Veritasium has a good video on how criticality applies to other things too,","text":"3Blue1Brown has a good video on phase transitions. [LINK] Veritasium has a good video on how criticality applies to other things too, [VIDEO]"},{"id":46194365,"author":"indigodaddy","month":"2025-12","story":"Strong earthquake hits northern Japan, tsunami warning issued","stance":"rec","why":"Scientists explain how stress builds and releases through earthquakes.","quote":"The video is a great watch btw.","text":"Various scientists in this video. The video is a great watch btw. [VIDEO] (stresses build up and are often released through many small, unfelt earthquakes (25:54). If these small movements don't dissipate the stress, it can accumulate and lead to a powerful chain reaction (26:25) * disclaimer I used YouTube's built-in AI to find/summarize the timestamps, as I couldn't remember offhand where it was when I previously watched this."},{"id":46354788,"author":"austy69","month":"2025-12","story":"I'm just having fun","stance":"rec","why":"Changes how to think about normal versus power-law outcomes.","quote":"Before I watched this I would default to thinking about most distributions as normal.","text":"Glad you mentioned the veritasium video on these. Here is the link: [VIDEO] Before I watched this I would default to thinking about most distributions as normal. It's really fun to think about whatever \"game\" you are playing - wether you are building a business or trying to win a carnival game - and consider if the results follow a normal distribution or a power law?"},{"id":46183512,"author":"dlojudice","month":"2025-12","story":"The AI wildfire is coming. it's going to be painful and healthy","stance":"reference","why":"Covers power laws, self-organized criticality, and a wildfire simulation.","quote":"one of Veritasium's latest videos [1] about power law, self-organized criticality, percolation","text":"The text reminded me of one of Veritasium's latest videos [1] about power law, self-organized criticality, percolation, etc... and it also has a wildfire simulation [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46066313,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-11","story":"Ilya Sutskever, Yann LeCun and the End of “Just Add GPUs”","stance":"reference","why":"Tangential citation within a scaling-laws argument.","quote":"","text":"> Won't scaling laws apply to it anyway? Yes, of course. Scaling Laws will always apply, but that's not really the point[0] The fight was never \"Scale is all you need\" (SIAYN) vs \"scale is irrelevant\" it was \"SIAYN\" vs \"Scaling is not enough\". I'm not aware of any halfway serious researcher that did not think scaling was going to result in massive improvements. Being a researcher from the SINE camp myself... Here's the thing: The SIAYN camp argued that the transformer architecture was essentially good enough. They didn't think scale was all you needed, but that the rest would me minor tweaks and increasing model size and data size would get us there. That those were the major hurdles. In this sense they argued that we should move our efforts away from research and into engineering. That AGI was now essentially a money problem rather than a research problem. They pointed to Sutton's Bitter Lesson narrowly, concentrating on his point about compute. The SINE (or SINAYN) camp wasn't sold. We read the Bitter Lesson differently. That yes, compute is a key element to modern success, but just as important was the rise of our flexible algorithms. In the past we couldn't work with such algorithms because of lack of computational power, but that the real power was the algorithms. We're definitely a more diverse camp too, with vary arguments. Many of us look at animals and see that we can do so much more with so much less[2]. Clearly even if SIAYN were sufficient, it does not appear to b"}],"r":[["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["snAhsXyO3Ck",1],["1YbcB6b4A2U",1]]},"Phl82D57P58":{"wy":"It is linked whenever Honnold or free soloing comes up, as a gripping and frightening example of extreme climbing.","q":{"x":"one of the most frightening videos I have ever seen","i":8837976,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":12279083,"author":"sgrytoyr","month":"2016-08","story":"Rock climber Alex Honnold doesn’t experience fear like the rest of us","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Honnold free-soloing El Sendero Luminoso.","quote":"I feel compelled to post this video whenever Honnold comes up.","text":"I feel compelled to post this video whenever Honnold comes up. Here he is free-soloing El Sendero Luminoso: [VIDEO] He really is something else."},{"id":8837976,"author":"sgrytoyr","month":"2015-01","story":"On El Capitan’s Dawn Wall, Climb Thought to Be Toughest Progresses Slowly","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Frightening free solo of El Sendero Luminoso by Alex Honnold.","quote":"one of the most frightening videos I have ever seen","text":"Fantastic achievement if they pull it off. Incidentally, Alex Honnold, one of the climbers interviewed in the article, is the star of one of the most frightening videos I have ever seen - him free-soloing El Sendero Luminoso: [VIDEO] Even though I know perfectly well that nobody fell to their death on that climb, and have done a fair bit of climbing myself, I can’t watch this video without having a powerful physical reaction. It’s mind-boggling what some people are capable of."},{"id":14793239,"author":"arcticfox","month":"2017-07","story":"Two days in an underwater cave running out of oxygen","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Honnold's ropeless climbs.","quote":"any of Alex Honnold's ropeless climbs deserves a link too","text":"I really hope that guy practiced repressing the instinct to lunge at a bad throw when juggling. Extremely rare occurrence with such a simple pattern, but ooph! I feel like any of Alex Honnold's ropeless climbs deserves a link too, for example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12279723,"author":"Fricken","month":"2016-08","story":"Rock climber Alex Honnold doesn’t experience fear like the rest of us","stance":"reference","why":"Honnold's preparation for the climb.","quote":"","text":"Here's a video that touches on Honnold's prep for his ascent of El Sendoro Luminoso. Given that it's likely the most difficult ropeless climb in history (and on limestone, which is far more prone to breaking holds than Yosemite granite), he wasn't exactly super meticulous: [VIDEO] At lower grades he has been known to just go for it."}]},"UWvRorX0KhQ":{"wy":"HN people share it as a classic example of sleight-of-hand mastery whose ending is mind-blowing.","q":{"x":"The manipulations are pretty amazing and then at the end it's mindblowing.","i":36945710,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36945710,"author":"onychomys","month":"2023-07","story":"“Cards as Weapons” by Ricky Jay (1977)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Card manipulation that ends mindblowingly","quote":"The manipulations are pretty amazing and then at the end it's mindblowing.","text":"I am not 100% convinced that this isn't just actual magic. The manipulations are pretty amazing and then at the end it's mindblowing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18557876,"author":"duxup","month":"2018-11","story":"Ricky Jay’s Magical Secrets (1993)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Card routine that builds and builds","quote":"was just floored, the whole thing just builds and builds and builds.","text":"I didn't know who Ricky Jay was but ages ago i ran across this youtube video and was just floored, the whole thing just builds and builds and builds. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10009351,"author":"greghendershott","month":"2015-08","story":"Show HN: Learn Magic Tricks Online","stance":"reference","why":"Classic Ricky Jay card manipulation clip","quote":"(Table faro shuffles are arguably what Ricky Jay is doing at the start of this classic","text":"52Kards on YouTube has many free tutorials at beginner, intermediate, and advanced levels: [LINK] Although I just dabble I think they're mostly very good. They're definitely easier to grasp than the books I remember as a kid. Recently he's moved to doing non-free material: [LINK] In my limited experience YouTube has almost everything. Search for, I don't know, \"table faro shuffle\", and you'll find a few meh results and one gem. (Table faro shuffles are arguably what Ricky Jay is doing at the start of this classic, if you don't believe it's just a camera edit: [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["VPjODKUxV5g",1],["24OXzIRIiMQ",1]]},"fW8amMCVAJQ":{"wy":"A go-to short explanation of how movements start and why early followers matter.","q":{"x":"Watch this 2 minute to see how this works, you may have seen this before","i":33655751,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":46455478,"author":"abirch","month":"2026-01","story":"I rebooted my social life","stance":"rec","why":"Advice to be a first follower when building social life","quote":"Look for opportunities to be a first follower","text":"1) Do something you enjoy *and* that others in your area enjoy. 2) Look for opportunities to be a first follower: [VIDEO] 3) Build a routine. E.g., this event will happen every 2nd Tuesday of the month."},{"id":33655751,"author":"memish","month":"2022-11","story":"Twitter to employees: all office buildings closed, badge access suspended","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how a lone dancer becomes a leader via first follower","quote":"Watch this 2 minute to see how this works, you may have seen this before","text":"What happens when Twitter survives despite reducing headcount by 80-90%? It's going to send a message that companies are more overstaffed than we thought. This may explain an underlying fear driving even some engineers to put their reputation on the line with confident declarations that Twitter is going to fail. Some were saying it wouldn't survive the night. It's now morning. Others saying it won't last the weekend or coming week. We'll soon find out. I would expect some bumps along the way. Twitter Blue was a bump. Perhaps Twitter is just the first to go lean before the recession. Elon merely the first dancer who is willing to look dumb. Now who's going to be the first follower to transform the lone nut into a leader? Watch this 2 minute to see how this works, you may have seen this before: [VIDEO] Now here's a counterbalance to the fear with an optimistic take: This is a correction. A small number of tech companies have been hoarding talent. The talent gets a 3 month severance and made available to the larger ecosystem."},{"id":26849988,"author":"yogrish","month":"2021-04","story":"Ask HN: What was the biggest leadership challenge of your career?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the importance of a first follower in leading","quote":"This might give you an idea of first followers.","text":"The challenge I faced earlier was to get things executed ( expected outcome) from different teams. When I found my first follower , the one who really buys my vision and takes it down, things started to roll faster. This might give you an idea of first followers. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30871084,"author":"psyc","month":"2022-03","story":"US will now offer gender-neutral X option on passports","stance":"reference","why":"Used to explain how movements reach a tipping point","quote":"I believe the core essence is simply this","text":"I have seriously and honestly asked myself this! In my own head and never publicly, of course. I don't believe it's subject to rational/utilitarian analysis. It has, gradually at first, accelerating to a tipping point, become fashionable (albeit in a very serious way) to seek to protect this category of person. Admittedly reductionist, but I believe the core essence is simply this: [VIDEO] I think protection, respect, dignity, rights for LGBTQ people is great! But as you note, there is something extra here. It seems like the entire population, which is mostly cis and hetero, is being asked... well, not asked, really. It's not a request anymore, is it? Strongly urged, to deconstruct and overhaul how we think and speak about sex and gender in all contexts and at all times. And this is a bit difficult to square with the goodness of protecting LGBTQ people. It's a little extra. I was going to write that the growth of this movement consists of cishet people (\"allies!\"), since it's not a recruitment drive to turn people. But then I realized that's not strictly true either. While I do not believe that sexuality and dysphoria are chosen, there is some fuzziness in the [TQ] part. I could, with high sincerity, choose to identify as agender, possibly a bit genderfluid, which under the current rules qualifies me to identify as non-binary, which under the current rules qualifies me to identify myself as transgender. I don't do that. I don't see it as being in my interest, but that could c"},{"id":16291232,"author":"zawerf","month":"2018-02","story":"Dancing mania","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how dancing crowds spread via followers","quote":"You can call it a disorder but it's also human nature.","text":"> Scientists have described dancing mania as a \"collective mental disorder\", \"collective hysterical disorder\", and \"mass madness\". You can call it a disorder but it's also human nature. I can see this instance being classified as a modern outbreak: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12009919,"author":"kevin","month":"2016-06","story":"Employee 1: Yahoo","stance":"reference","why":"Early followers are crucial to movements and startups","quote":"I'm always remind of Derek Sivers video on First Followers when I think of them.","text":"We're really excited about this new series on The Macro about the first employees at startups. First employees are often unsung heroes at companies, but often incredible crucial to getting them off the ground and setting the culture of the startup. I'm always remind of Derek Sivers video on First Followers when I think of them. [VIDEO]"}]},"lKQ0yaEJjok":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an inspiring summary of a computational model of mind and a first lecture in a series on the brain as a computer.","q":{"x":"stuff like talks by Joscha Bach on C3","i":16219459,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":20426583,"author":"jkachmar","month":"2019-07","story":"How much bandwidth does the spinal cord have?","stance":"rec","why":"First lecture in a series on the brain as a computer.","quote":"if you watch it and find it interesting you should check out the proceeding installments.","text":"I don't particularly like that essay because the author seems focused on the idea that \"your brain is a computer\" is a metaphor rather than a theory (see [1] for a more nuanced discussion). The author correctly points out that past eras developed metaphors to explain how the mind might work based on the technological innovations they were familiar with, but I think there's a lot more nuance in the computational theory of the mind. Namely that the notion of computation is much more abstract, and potentially more portable across disciplines, than some of the historical examples that the author of that Aeon piece brings up. Anyway, obviously I don't have any real answers but for whatever reason the brain-as-a-computer theory rings pretty true for me and I've enjoyed reading essays and watching talks about the topic [2] [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] ^-- This is the first part in an ongoing series of lectures that Joscha Bach has been giving at the Chaos Communication Congress; if you watch it and find it interesting you should check out the proceeding installments."},{"id":16219459,"author":"freeflight","month":"2018-01","story":"Can staying awake beat depression?","stance":"rec","why":"Inspirational talk suggesting AI may help explain the mind.","quote":"it's quite inspirational as it gives me hope that creating strong AI might actually help us understand what's going on under our own hoods","text":"> In general I'd say the best tactic is to learn as much as you can yourself (while remembering to be honest with yourself) and pursue general purpose coping strategies across the board (for example, mindfulness based cognitive therapy). Yup, that's exactly what I've been doing for years now. I've read through quite a bit of stuff ranging from Freud, Jung, B.F. Skinner to Edward de Bono and countless others in addition to lots of Wikipedia surfing and study reading. A rather common theme among a lot of them: Many of them went into studying psychology as an attempt to diagnose their own issues and often it feels more like reading philosophy rather than actual medical science. I wonder if we will ever be able to articulate something like a \"Grand Unified Theory of the mind\"? Considering how much of this is subjective to the individual and atop of that the individual's brain physiology, it seems like an impossible task. But then I see stuff like talks by Joscha Bach on C3 [0] [1] [2] and it's quite inspirational as it gives me hope that creating strong AI might actually help us understand what's going on under our own hoods a little bit better. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":13257609,"author":"kryptokommunist","month":"2016-12","story":"Mind as an emergent self-organizing process","stance":"rec","why":"Good summary of the author's functional model of the mind.","quote":"there is also a talk by the author which is a good summary of the article","text":"Well in a way the articles approach is similar to Piaget's in the sense that the approach is also functional. The hypothesis here is that the mind is just a information processing system and the research approach therefore is developing functional models. It will either work or not, you can actually test your models. This approach doesn't just model biological structures but independently tries to find functional explanations/mechanisms that could result in a mind like system. I think the articles model is one of the most sophisticated of the highly integrated ones out there. It really is worth reading, especially the explanation for emotion and motivation i found to be quite interesting. If you need a teaser to get you going, there is also a talk by the author which is a good summary of the article: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wtV5ev6813I",1],["8LGDM9exlZw",1]]},"pdJQ8iVTwj8":{"wy":"HN points to it as the main place to hear Lattner's motivations and design ideas for Mojo, often in Mojo threads.","q":{"x":"further emboldened my stance that if Mojo can succeed, Lattner is one of the best-positioned engineers to see it through","i":36293296,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":45142698,"author":"tony","month":"2025-09","story":"ML needs a new programming language – Interview with Chris Lattner","stance":"rec","why":"Another Lattner appearance on the Lex Fridman show","quote":"Chris Lattner also has a very good episode on Lex Fridman","text":"Chris Lattner also has a very good episode on Lex Fridman (Episode 381, June 2nd, 2023): - [VIDEO] - [LINK] - [LINK] He has two other episodes on the show: - [LINK] (Episode 131, October 18th, 2020) - [LINK] (Episode 21, May 13th, 2019)"},{"id":36571716,"author":"spacephysics","month":"2023-07","story":"Show HN: Python can make 3M+ WebSocket keys per second","stance":"rec","why":"Podcast introducing Mojo as a Python superset with C speed","quote":"Lex Friedman had a great podcast with the founder","text":"Mojo seems like a very promising solution, despite it being AI oriented. Lex Friedman had a great podcast with the founder, its first version was released about a month ago, and in two weeks got 10k discord users. A superset of python that, once finished, can run all native python code, but also adds language features to get closer to the “metal”, getting speeds of C libraries with the ability to properly debug and step through it. If this project goes the way it’s promised (the guy has great experience with language development), this could further cement python as it would solve its biggest criticisms. Very excited for it [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":36291609,"author":"teh_klev","month":"2023-06","story":"Mojo is a much better “Objective-C without the C” than Swift ever was","stance":"rec","why":"Lattner discusses Mojo and other topics","quote":"might be of interest to readers","text":"This episode of the Lex Fridman podcast, where Chris Lattner talks to Fridman about Mojo and other things, might be of interest to readers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36293296,"author":"spmurrayzzz","month":"2023-06","story":"Mojo is a much better “Objective-C without the C” than Swift ever was","stance":"rec","why":"Interview bolstered confidence in Lattner's ability to make Mojo succeed","quote":"further emboldened my stance that if Mojo can succeed, Lattner is one of the best-positioned engineers to see it through","text":"Lattner being behind Mojo always engendered some benefit of the doubt despite my initial gripes with the marketing claims, but his recent interview with Lex Fridman [1] further emboldened my stance that if Mojo can succeed, Lattner is one of the best-positioned engineers to see it through. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":49364104,"author":"jasode","month":"2026-08","story":"The Mojo language (by Modular, now Qualcomm) is now open-source","stance":"reference","why":"Lattner explains lessons from Swift's rapid open-sourcing","quote":"Deep link to Chris Lattner interview where he says he learned lessons from Swift being rapidly released as open source","text":">I don't understand what open source has to do with this. People being able to see your source code doesn't somehow prevent you from changing it however you want. Yes, they can voice their opinion on it, but you can also just ignore it. Deep link to Chris Lattner interview where he says he learned lessons from Swift being rapidly released as open source meant that early design decisions that were suboptimal caused quick revisions. This caused a lot of complaints when later revisions to Swift caused breakages. This caused a lot of stress for Chris & the Swift team so he says he didn't want to repeat that for Mojo. [VIDEO] Sure, one can simple redo the exact same playbook with Mojo and iterate out in the open with early versions, and when people complain again about their code breaking, he can \"just ignore it\" but he didn't like that experience with Swift."},{"id":37943284,"author":"V1ndaar","month":"2023-10","story":"Mojo is now available on Mac","stance":"reference","why":"Lattner's Mojo ideas compared to Nim","quote":"when he talks about his ideas behind Mojo it pretty much sounds like he's describing Nim","text":"I mean honestly, the closest language to Mojo really is Nim. In the latest Lex Fridman interview with Chris Lattner [0] when he talks about his ideas behind Mojo it pretty much sounds like he's describing Nim. Ok fair, he wants Mojo to be a full superset of Python, but honestly with nimpy [1] our Python interop is about as seamless as it can really be (without being a superset, which Mojo clearly is not yet). Even the syntax of Mojo looks a damn lot like Nim imo. Anyway, I guess he has the ability to raise enough funds to hire enough people to write his own language within ~2 years so as not have to follow random peoples whim about where to take the language. So I guess I can't blame him. But as someone who's pretty invested in the Nim community it's quite a shame to see such a hyped language receive so much attention by people who should really check out Nim. ¯\\_(ツ)_/¯ [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":36246775,"author":"ftxbro","month":"2023-06","story":"Do LLMs eliminate the need for programming languages?","stance":"reference","why":"Long Lattner interview, framed as part of marketing push","quote":"he did a long interview with lex fridman recently as part of the same marketing blitz","text":"Anyone who doesn't know, this is by Chris Lattner the famous hacker who made projects like LLVM, Clang, Swift. [LINK] He is writing this as part of a marketing blitz for his new platform Mojo [LINK] If you want to see more, he did a long interview with lex fridman recently as part of the same marketing blitz [VIDEO] maybe dang will put this into the recirculation queue and retitle it something like 'chris lattner writes blogpost' it will go to number one place on hacker news"},{"id":36293653,"author":"tipsytoad","month":"2023-06","story":"Mojo is a much better “Objective-C without the C” than Swift ever was","stance":"reference","why":"Lattner appeared on Lex Fridman recently","quote":"","text":"I think largely because swift and mojo have the same creator, chris lattner. He was a guest on lex fridman last week [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["SrU9YDoXE88",1],["IoL8g0W9gAQ",1],["I14b-C67EXY",1],["yCd3CzGSte8",1],["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1]]},"7ne0GArfeMs":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible and elegant tour of how indirect reasoning builds up cosmic measurements, with one commenter calling it their favorite Tao work.","q":{"x":"Tao’s oft given talk about the cosmic distance ladder has always been my favorite thing he has done.","i":42985071,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42985071,"author":"mathladder","month":"2025-02","story":"Terence Tao on how we measure the cosmos, Part 1 [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tao's cosmic distance ladder talk is the commenter's favorite of his work.","quote":"Tao’s oft given talk about the cosmic distance ladder has always been my favorite thing he has done.","text":"Cool collab. Tao’s oft given talk about the cosmic distance ladder has always been my favorite thing he has done. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39677600,"author":"luc4sdreyer","month":"2024-03","story":"Webb and Hubble confirm Universe's expansion rate","stance":"rec","why":"Nice video about the cosmic distance ladder.","quote":"Nice video about the cosmic distance ladder by Terence Tao","text":"> Nobody had a clue In the 3rd century BC, Aristarchus calculated that the Sun was between 18 and 20 times farther away from the Earth than the Moon, and proposed the Heliocentric model as a result. The true value is instead approximately 400 times. But it's incredible given that he didn't have lenses, the value of Pi, and that the Geocentric model was considered correct until 1800 years after his death. [LINK] Nice video about the cosmic distance ladder by Terence Tao: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33188183,"author":"DavidSJ","month":"2022-10","story":"Researcher discovers another astronomy book written by Galileo Galilei","stance":"reference","why":"Explains distance measurement via parallax.","quote":"","text":"This very parallax effect. We know how far the Sun is from the Earth, and the Earth has moved twice this distance from January 1 to July 1. This in turn allows us to extrapolate distance to some of the nearest stars by their apparent motion relative to the other, \"fixed\" stars. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31137027,"author":"meroes","month":"2022-04","story":"I stopped working on black hole information loss","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates indirect reasoning ladder in astronomy.","quote":"","text":"My understanding of cosmology and astronomy is there is an inherent \"ladder\", nesting of assumptions, or indirect argument structure. Like, IF the Moon is a sphere, then by the shadows Earth leaves upon it during eclipses, Earth is a sphere. This reasoning came before Aristotle knew the Earth was sphere. [VIDEO] This is still a way to do science. I'm willing to believe the experimenters dealt with the inherent novelty in a scientific way. What do you mean known celestial body?? We've only ever \"been\" to 2. We don't get to run all the experiments you'd like. As long as the process is self-correcting, it works. You have not shown this method to be uncorrecting. Better and better standards are used to strengthen these claims. And it is expected some degree of future corrections will occur. That's the nature of the beast."}]},"AGUwcs9qJXY":{"wy":"Recommended as a concise, broad-strokes history of racial injustice, including the lasting effects of redlining.","q":{"x":"There’s a great 18-minute video from Bob the Tomato, AKA Phil Vischer, which lays out the sordid history in broad detail.","i":24359189,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24359189,"author":"filoeleven","month":"2020-09","story":"Police across Canada are using predictive policing algorithms, report finds","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Concise history of racial injustice from an evangelical Christian perspective","quote":"There’s a great 18-minute video from Bob the Tomato, AKA Phil Vischer, which lays out the sordid history in broad detail.","text":"There’s a great 18-minute video from Bob the Tomato, AKA Phil Vischer, which lays out the sordid history in broad detail. I like this in particular because he’s coming from an evangelical Christian background—a demographic that has 60% racial resentment, the highest in the country. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":42662652,"author":"395112342","month":"2025-01","story":"Meta's memo to employees rolling back DEI programs","stance":"rec","why":"Explains lasting effects of redlining","quote":"The creator of VeggieTales has a great video on this!","text":"Older injustice still has ramifications today. Take redlining for instance. That happened a long time ago. Redlining systematically and intentionally deprived non-white families of home ownership, while helping white families to own homes. But wealth begets wealth, so owning a home lets someone borrow money against it to start a business. When these people die, their children will inherit their wealth. As a result, the (grand)children of a family are still denied opportunities that they would've gotten, if not for redlining. The creator of VeggieTales has a great video on this! [VIDEO] P.S. Yes, a family who was able to get a home loan (redlining didn't affect them) might have squandered this wealth gambling, or maybe they didn't pass it onto their children, so some people unaffected by redlining may still end up in a similar place. Similarly, some families that were affected by redlining have still managed to accumulate wealth in spite of redlining. My claim is that the family that squandered their money still got the chance to squander was was given to them, and the injustice is that the redlined family was denied that opportunity."},{"id":23660137,"author":"joshuamorton","month":"2020-06","story":"Santa Cruz, California bans predictive policing in U.S. first","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You're making a lot of (intentional or not) dogwhistles. So let's dive in to a few of them: > Blacks kill more blacks than any other race or ethinicity.[1] Blacks also kill more whites, than whites kill blacks in pure numbers. Indeed, let's rephrase this: races in the US continue to be relatively segregated[0]. And crimes, even violent ones, are usually committed within-community. So the meme around \"black on black violence\" is a bit of a distraction unless you also are concerned about \"white on white\" violence. And if you are, organizations like stop the violence[1] do exist to address specifically violence within the black community. So you start by stating true, but ultimately misleading claims that are often used to justify racial prejudice. Then, you justify this opinion with two videos from black people. Neither Larry Elder nor Denzel Washington is an expert on incarceration or the US justice system. Larry Elder, in fact, is a right wing political pundit, so his income relies on his viewership. This calls into question his reliability. I'd suggest you watch the documentary 13th[2] or read up on these issues from actual experts. But I'll spend some time breaking down his video: He starts by noting that white men and black men are killed by police at around the same rate. This is, again true. But this means that Black people are killed at around 5 times the rate of White people. Then he restates the whole issue of black on black crime, which we've already discussed. He th"}],"r":[["aSEQfqNYNAc",1],["zAFcV7zuUDA",1],["OoJsPvmFixU",1],["XziuEdpVUe0",1]]},"NmGaXEmfTIo":{"wy":"Recommended as an eye-opening look at pre-computer automation and relay logic.","q":{"x":"The computer free automation of a jukebox from the 1970s is an eye opener","i":22775280,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23730653,"author":"milofeynman","month":"2020-07","story":"The Classic Sunbeam Radiant Control Toaster (2006)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Multi-part explanation of jukebox inner workings that hooked commenter.","quote":"I've been hooked. I really enjoyed the multi-part one where he explained the inner workings of a jukebox","text":"This video made me aware of his channel a few months back and I've been hooked. I really enjoyed the multi-part one where he explained the inner workings of a jukebox[0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22775280,"author":"lathiat","month":"2020-04","story":"Hardware Microphone Disconnect in Mac and iPad","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth teardown of 1970s jukebox automation without computers.","quote":"The computer free automation of a jukebox from the 1970s is an eye opener","text":"The computer free automation of a jukebox from the 1970s is an eye opener: (an in depth teardown and explanation) [VIDEO]"},{"id":27499169,"author":"eru","month":"2021-06","story":"Doom Running on an IKEA Lamp [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Example of doing automation with relays rather than chips.","quote":"Compare","text":"By today's standard Doom doesn't need much processing power. You could probably find exactly the right chip that only has exactly just as many bits of RAM as you need for the lamp's functionality. But that would probably be more expensive to develop and chip than just using standard parts? Even more radical: I suspect with a bit of cleverness you could probably do everything the lamp needs to do with some relays and transistors. But today, that would be more expensive. Compare [VIDEO] for the latter approach."}],"r":[["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["noQcWra6sbU",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["uYbdx4I7STg",1],["ra_9zU-mnl8",1],["4YYvBLAF4T8",1],["a9b9IYqsb_U",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["ZUvGfuLlZus",1],["DOWDNBu9DkU",1]]},"P4VBqTViEx4":{"wy":"Cited constantly on HN whenever a large company declines, as the canonical explanation of product people being pushed out by sales and marketing.","q":{"x":"Steve Jobs has the best explanation for this.","i":35745119,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35745119,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2023-04","story":"Intel Posts Largest Loss in Its History as Sales Plunge 36%","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jobs's account of why monopolies get overtaken by competition.","quote":"Steve Jobs has the best explanation for this.","text":"They boost the share price for a while, but then the competition takes over. Steve Jobs has the best explanation for this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32350093,"author":"deckard1","month":"2022-08","story":"Google’s video chat merger begins: Now there are two “Google Meet” apps","stance":"rec","why":"Jobs explains how monopolies let sales people displace product people.","quote":"Steve Jobs calls this out wonderfully here","text":"> resource curse, where countries with rich natural resources are prone to foundering Steve Jobs calls this out wonderfully here: [VIDEO] > [...] the same thing can happen in technology companies that get monopolies. Like, oh, IBM and Xerox. If you were a product person at IBM or Xerox, so you make a better copier or a better computer. So what? When you have a monopoly market share the company's not any more successful. So the people that can make the company more successful are sales and marketing people. And they end up running the companies. And the product people get driven out of the decision making forums. And the companies forget what it means to make great products. [...] the product sensibility and the product genius that brought them to the monopolistic position gets rotted out. By people running these companies that have no conception of a good product versus a bad product. They have no conception of the craftsmanship required to take a good idea and turn it into a good product. And they really have no feeling in their hearts usually about wanting to really help the customers."},{"id":49190797,"author":"conception","month":"2026-08","story":"\"Gravity is worth asking about.\"","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on product people running a company versus sales people.","quote":"Steve Jobs talking about Apple of the future.","text":"[VIDEO] Steve Jobs talking about Apple of the future. (His comments on product people running a company vs sales)"},{"id":41884111,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2024-10","story":"Worldcoin has a new Orb and is now just World","stance":"reference","why":"Old Jobs clip on sales and marketing people running companies.","quote":"From all external appearances, \"World\" is being run by sales and marketing people from its inception.","text":"I doubt it. For one thing, the ergonomics of an \"orb\" are horrible. This is a solution looking for a problem and seemingly designed by people who took the wrong lessons from Apple's success. Incidentally they have a typo in their announcement page[1]. > The Orb is the incluse[sic], secure and anonymous device that enables World ID holders to verify their humanness and uniqueness. I assume \"incluse\" is meant to be \"inclusive\". But what's \"inclusive\" about letting a startup with foggy monetization plans collect everyone's biometrics? Sounds like bullshit. [1] [LINK] Edit: I'm reminded of this old clip: [VIDEO] From all external appearances, \"World\" is being run by sales and marketing people from its inception."},{"id":41255965,"author":"dwallin","month":"2024-08","story":"Apple opens up NFC transactions to developers. There will be 'associated fees'","stance":"reference","why":"Steve Jobs on product people versus sales and marketing running a company.","quote":"","text":"Apple has had no problems with providing their macOS libraries, tooling, and associated updates for free, even with a drastically smaller user base, and it appears to have been sustainable for decades. The truth of the matter is Apple would almost certainly continue to do offer their iPhone services, updates, app store, etc; even if they were statutorily prohibited from making any money off of any of it, because in the end it makes for a better user experience and helps them differentiate and sell their phones at a premium. What is happening, is the more financial-minded types within Apple have realized they have substantial lock-in and market power, and they can leverage it to take a % of profits from other market entities. If you actually care about Apple products, and want them to continue to deliver great products well into the future, then the best thing that could happen would be for them to get the message and accept a loss in this battle. Otherwise you will see increased financialization continue until they are thoroughly hollowed out of great product/engineering culture, and collapse under their own weight, like so many before them. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40141810,"author":"rvba","month":"2024-04","story":"The man who killed Google Search?","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on product people being pushed out.","quote":"This is like in that Steve Jobs video about product people being kicked out","text":"They hired people who introduce Jack Welch methods. This is like in that Steve Jobs video about product people being kicked out and exchanged by ones who dont care about product: [VIDEO] They will not make good search. That is not their priority."},{"id":37336761,"author":"jack_riminton","month":"2023-08","story":"It's not just you – no one is posting on social media anymore","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on large companies caring about sales over product.","quote":"Reminds me of the interview with Steve Jobs where he talks about large companies not caring about product because of their market position.","text":"Reminds me of the interview with Steve Jobs where he talks about large companies not caring about product because of their market position. Soon all that really matters is sales and marketing, so all the people that were good at making good products leave or get drowned out by bad decisions [VIDEO]"},{"id":36003341,"author":"rglover","month":"2023-05","story":"Apple's Reality Pro headset compromised","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The vibe I got reading Tim Cook wasn't involved: [VIDEO] (wasn't expecting him to be because he's an ops/logistics guy, but that bit was a bit alarming)."},{"id":35545515,"author":"faeriechangling","month":"2023-04","story":"Intel and Arm Announce Multigeneration Collaboration on Leading-Edge SoC Design","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I think Steve Jobs himself said it best","text":"Think of why the iPhone existed in the first place. It was because Jobs saw the writing on the wall and knew that cell phones were bound to destroy the market for the iPod, Apple's moneymaker. Before making the iPhone, he made the Rokr and Jobs threw massive amounts of resources into the iPhone at large risk to Apple. He bought up the patent for multi touch back in 2005. I honestly think people overlook this aspect of Jobs leadership, basically from the time he returned on Apple he doubled down on mobile computing repeatedly throwing 50% of Apple's marketing budget into the unproven iPod for instance. Why was Jobs able to see the writing on the wall and not Intel? I think Steve Jobs himself said it best: [VIDEO] Intel itself was NOT totally blindsided by the mobile revolution. Intel Atom development started in 2004. Intel Atom could have dominated the market. There were Intel Execs pushing Atom. But all the R&D was pumped into the desktop and server business, the quality silicon was reserved for the desktop and server business, Intels execs didn't want these inexpensive chips for what were presumed to be peoples secondary computers to cannibalise its expensive desktop and server chips, and the strategic vision was simply bad. You can apologise for it but the mistake may have doomed the entire business and plenty of companies did not make the same mistake. Intel wouldn't even exist if they didn't already make such a shift away from the memory market into the microprocessor mar"},{"id":34374115,"author":"smnrchrds","month":"2023-01","story":"Working for a Dating Website (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on product people at monopolies.","quote":"If you were a product person at IBM or Xerox, so you make a better copier or computer. So what?","text":"\"If you were a product person at IBM or Xerox, so you make a better copier or computer. So what? When you have monopoly market share, the company's not any more successful.\" - Steve Jobs [VIDEO]"},{"id":34421064,"author":"schlauerfox","month":"2023-01","story":"Microsoft to lay off 11,000 employees","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on product development versus marketing teams.","quote":"Steve Jobs on product dev vs marketing teams","text":"Yeah, effectiveness is something too in monopoly tech firms. The Mythical Man Month [LINK] Steve Jobs on product dev vs marketing teams [VIDEO]"},{"id":33301255,"author":"ItsMonkk","month":"2022-10","story":"The Gervais Principle, or the Office According to “The Office” (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It also reminds me a lot of the Steve Jobs Product vs Marketing clip[2].","text":"This reminds me a lot of the Conflict vs Mistake framing[0] which is expanded on by his Tragedy of Legible Technique article[1]. It also reminds me a lot of the Steve Jobs Product vs Marketing clip[2]. > When the Director of the CDC asserts an opinion, she has to optimize for two things - being right, and keeping power. If she doesn't optimize for the second, she gets replaced as CDC Director by someone who does. That means she's trying to solve a harder problem than Zvi is, and it makes sense that sometimes, despite having more resources than Zvi, she does worse at it. It seems to me like the issue is actually in rent-seeking, where the more rent-seeking you have available, the more the marginal mistake person converts into a conflict person. In a world of perfect competition there would be no room for conflicts, and only once you have freely available rents does the land-grabbing phase begin. When your rules are written by the conflicters, for the conflicters benefit, it's already to late. They'll certainly never compromise on the simple correct path. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31742758,"author":"lapcat","month":"2022-06","story":"Apple and MLS to present all MLS matches for 10 years, beginning in 2023","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on how monopolies rot product sensibility.","quote":"The product sensibility and the product genius that brought them to that monopolistic position gets rotted out.","text":"\"The same thing can happen in technology companies that get monopolies.\" \"The people that can make the company more successful are sales and marketing people, and they end up running the companies. And the product people get driven out of the decision making forums, and the companies forget what it means to make great products. The product sensibility and the product genius that brought them to that monopolistic position gets rotted out.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":30515467,"author":"ramesh31","month":"2022-03","story":"Mozilla Hubs","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on management and sales taking over companies.","quote":"Relevant, as this is exactly what happened","text":"Management and sales completed their takeover of Mozilla over the last 5 years. The Mozilla we knew for decades is dead and gone, and now there's a zombie consumer tech company left in its place. Relevant, as this is exactly what happened: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30250471,"author":"baby-yoda","month":"2022-02","story":"Apple in 2021","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on sales and marketing displacing product people.","quote":"their endless pursuit for revenue has pushed sales and marketing people to the front of the line","text":"just my humble opinion - i believe aapl has crossed the threshold into the territory which steve jobs famously described as the hallmark of the Scully era[0] - their endless pursuit for revenue has pushed sales and marketing people to the front of the line and product people operate in their shadows. of course in a company as big and wide ranging as aapl, there are so many competing priorities and things to focus on but, IMO, tim cook is a real life paperclip maximizer. early on in his tenure there was some criticism that he was a \"bean counter\" and would ruin things cause he wasn't Steve, but rather than cutting costs as a traditional beancounter type would, he has been ruthless in pursuing what works to continue the endless revenue climb. there are certainly many things they could work on but choose not to, or could do better, but when you're in Cook's position and you are the preeminent Elephant (far larger and less capable of dance than Gerstner could have ever imagined), you have no choice but for changes in direction to be slow but large in magnitude. and at such a massive scale, while aapl could stumble out a new product/service and vacuum up tens of $millions, there are fewer and fewer opportunities which will clear their IRR. Taking some liberties interpreting here, but hence why they feel the need to extract value from App Store payments, turn a blind eye to scam apps, Google as default $earch, etc; their gravitational pull is so powerful that, as a proper paperclip"},{"id":27709333,"author":"nbzso","month":"2021-07","story":"First Look: macOS Monterey Public Beta","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I was in shock, when realised that everything that I love about Mac Desktop since beginning of OSX era will be showed away in the name of vertical integration. Now I am ok. Catalina is the last Apple OS that I will run on my computers. Figma is in the browser. Blender is working under Linux perfectly. Windows is horrible, but if used properly for specific tasks it will perform. The big problem was to move away from Logic, but where there's a will, there's a way. I am deeply thankful for all those years of Desktop Bliss, but Apple nowadays is marketing and shareholders driven company and this is the opposite of Jobs philosophy. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25097883,"author":"NalNezumi","month":"2020-11","story":"Intel's disruption is now complete","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on product versus sales people and Xerox.","quote":"Essentially what Steve Jobs said about product vs sales & marketing people and Xerox","text":"Essentially what Steve Jobs said about product vs sales & marketing people and Xerox, but more regional/department version. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23792861,"author":"whywhywhywhy","month":"2020-07","story":"Valve secrets spill over in new Steam documentary app","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I feel pretty strongly that, assuming you care about the art and creating things then Valve is a blueprint of how exactly not to do it, hang on let me finish. Building Steam was a genius business move, it has basically given them an insane amount of income for close to zero effort, I mean the client is insanely janky and has barely been updated from an actual experience point of view in over a decade but it doesn't matter because almost all PC gaming purchases are funneled through it. Now what this should have done is allow the company to stop caring about profits and being able to relish in building true art to a true un-compromised vision. But something has seriously gone wrong here and the company has struggled to ship close to anything meaty in 16 years. To put it in perspective lets look at auteur studios held in similar regard as Half-Life. (I'm ignoring side releases, just focusing on mainline games) - Half-Life: HL2 2004, HL:A 2020 - Kojima : MGS3 2004, MGS4 2008, MGS5 2014, Death Stranding (worth pointing out his studio was rebuilt from nothing for this too) 2020 - Rockstar : GTA:SA 2004, GTA4 2008, RDR 2010, GTA5 2013, RDR2 2019 If Valve hadn't had steam, the company would not still exist today with that output. If HL:A good, yes. But we put it next to Rockstars output in the same time and it's not even the same league. Something has seriously gone wrong within Valve and I think the charitable way to frame it is \"Oh they just care so much they only release when its "},{"id":23120449,"author":"Rury","month":"2020-05","story":"Intel accused by workers of prioritizing chip output over safety","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on why monopolies lose product focus.","quote":"Probably explained by","text":">it feels increasingly like IBM of 2003 Probably explained by: [VIDEO] On another point. I'm not sure how much this is true, but I've read that most of AMD's chips are made from the same basic building blocks, and their different CPUs are mostly just a matter of how many these blocks are stitched together along with whatever tuning/other hardware. Such a design is probably very scalable, which simplifies manufacturing and reduces costs. Whereas I heard intel chips have more separate customizations requiring more separate tooling for each of their chipsets, which complicates manufacturing..."},{"id":23009397,"author":"Rury","month":"2020-04","story":"The Anti-Amazon Alliance","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on sales and marketing taking over growing companies.","quote":"Companies get taken over by sales/marketing teams as a company grows in size","text":"I think the problem is actually what Steve Jobs had once mentioned [1]. Companies get taken over by sales/marketing teams as a company grows in size, and the product/customer focused people get removed from the decision making. The quality of the companies products then stagnate or rot, as the product people are always in a rush to meet the requests of upper management - who of which have no idea what it takes to make a good product. So everyone's being micromanaged to meet stupid metrics - rather than the outcome - a good product. As so it's not so much about micromanagement, as so much as having the best decision makers throughout the company. But none of this matters much when you're big, or have a monopoly... [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vgT4Y30DkaA",1],["RPdB_zdyMbM",1],["iD9xk3SDSYc",1],["3Fx5Q8xGU8k",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["Xe5AE-qYan8",1],["8pBPZMUcsh0",1],["yFIa-Mc2KSk",1],["bpAi70WWBlw",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["PdO3IP0Pro8",1],["3Ea3pkTCYx4",1],["XGbPHp6QfkQ",1]]},"wMFPe-DwULM":{"wy":"Cited constantly when discussing explaining to non-experts, the limits of explanation and what a 'why' question really asks.","q":{"x":"mhartl has already posted this excellent video","i":1866899,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":1866899,"author":"DevX101","month":"2010-11","story":"If Richard Feynman applied for a job at Microsoft (2002)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman explains what why means in scientific inquiry","quote":"mhartl has already posted this excellent video","text":"Of anyone I've read or seen (in person or from videos), Feynman has the best fundamental grasp of meta-knowledge. By meta-knowledge, for lack of a better term, I mean understanding what it means to know something. mhartl has already posted this excellent video: [VIDEO] , where he explains what the word \"why\" means in scientific inquiry. When we answer a \"why\" question, we don't really explain a concept in its entirety. At best, we're able to remove a layer of skin off the onion, but no one has ever really reached the center. I suppose science at it's heart is really just the elucidation of intermediate cause and effect scenarios. For the question \"why did the ball fall?\", \"Jimmy dropped it\" is a perfectly valid answer. So is \"Jimmy's motor neurons passed an action potential threshold, causing the muscles in his wrist to contract\". So is \"the ball moved along the curvature of space caused by the earth\". How far do we go?"},{"id":7010609,"author":"jliechti1","month":"2014-01","story":"Beware techies talking gobbledegook","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman explains why some things cannot be explained without shared framework","quote":"Feynman explains this really well when trying to answer a question about magnets","text":"Sometimes it's not always possible to explain something in sufficient detail to someone who has no concept of what you are trying to explain. Feynman explains this really well when trying to answer a question about magnets: \"Of course, it's an excellent question. But the problem, you see, when you ask why something happens, how does a person answer why something happens? For example, Aunt Minnie is in the hospital. Why? Because she went out, slipped on the ice, and broke her hip. That satisfies people. It satisfies, but it wouldn't satisfy someone who came from another planet and who knew nothing about why when you break your hip do you go to the hospital... when you explain a why, you have to be in some framework that you allow something to be true. Otherwise, you're perpetually asking why.\" \"I can't explain that attraction in terms of anything else that's familiar to you. For example, if we said the magnets attract like if rubber bands, I would be cheating you. Because they're not connected by rubber bands. I'd soon be in trouble. And secondly, if you were curious enough, you'd ask me why rubber bands tend to pull back together again, and I would end up explaining that in terms of electrical forces, which are the very things that I'm trying to use the rubber bands to explain. So I have cheated very badly, you see.\" The key quote being: \"I really can't do a good job, any job, of explaining magnetic force in terms of something else you're more familiar with, because I don't u"},{"id":1866564,"author":"mhartl","month":"2010-11","story":"If Richard Feynman applied for a job at Microsoft (2002)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Feynman's characteristic style of answering questions","quote":"I highly recommend this video","text":"For those who think this doesn't sound at least a little bit like Feynman, I highly recommend this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14566496,"author":"jancsika","month":"2017-06","story":"If you can’t explain something in simple terms, you don’t understand it","stance":"reference","why":"Full video shows the quote was misread in the article","quote":"But watching the video in full[1], one realizes he is saying something different","text":"This Feynman quote from the article is put in the wrong context: > I really can’t do a good job, any job, of explaining magnetic force in terms of something else you’re more familiar with, because I don’t understand it in terms of anything else you’re more familiar with. The article implies this is a case of the scientist expressing that he didn't understand a thing. But watching the video in full[1], one realizes he is saying something different: \"It's a force which is present all the time and very common and is a basic force. [...] I can't explain that attraction in terms of anything else that's familiar to you. For example if we say that magnets attract like as if they are connected by rubber bands I would be cheating you because they're not connected by rubber bands-- I should be in trouble if you soon ask me about the nature of the band. And secondly, if you were curious enough you would ask me why rubber bands tend to pull back together again, and I would end up explaining that in terms of electrical forces which are the very things that I'm trying to use the rubber bands to explain. So I have cheated very badly, you see.\" In other words, for some phenomena the only simple examples are themselves instances of that same phenomena. So the only possible analogies are themselves merely tautologies. I've noticed something less sweeping though similarly absurd with the internet. As more and more of people's daily lives depend on internet technologies, it becomes more difficul"},{"id":12253029,"author":"fatman13gg","month":"2016-08","story":"How to Have Healthy Relationships as a Developer","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on mismatched knowledge frameworks","quote":"Like Feynman said in this video","text":"Like Feynman said in this video [VIDEO] , when trying to explain how magnate works to the reporter. I think it's hard for someone else to actually understand the problem when you two are not in the same knowledge framework. Cause then she/he will be perpetually asking the why and how question and being not satisfied. edited."},{"id":10064915,"author":"unstabilo","month":"2015-08","story":"Marathon Editing Brings New Rigor to Wikipedia Physics","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman explains why magnets cannot be explained to a layman","quote":"Feynman couldn't explain to a layman how magnets work, but explained why not","text":"> Was it Einstein who said, \"Any scientist who cannot explain what they are doing to an eight year old is a charlatan\"? No. It's commonly attributed to Einstein, but of course he didn't say that, because he knew better. There are things you just can't explain at a certain level. For example Feynman couldn't explain to a layman how magnets work, but explained why not: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":5209075,"author":"snuze","month":"2013-02","story":"How do you tell a non-technical person that they can’t understand?","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on being unable to explain magnetic force simply","quote":"This reminds me of Richard Feynman answering a question about magnets.","text":"This reminds me of Richard Feynman answering a question about magnets. I really can't do a good job, any job, of explaining magnetic force in terms of something else you're more familiar with, because I don't understand it in terms of anything else that you're more familiar with. Transcript: [LINK] Video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4495566,"author":"tjoff","month":"2012-09","story":"Judge in Oracle v. Google Explains What Jurors Must Not Do","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman describing the problem of explaining to laymen","quote":"There's a short clip of Feynman describing the problem here","text":"Having programming experience is absolutely not a requirement for understanding the technicalities of what software does No, but having experience and knowledge about computers in general is required to make a decision on, say, whether a mouse-over event is innovative. There's a short clip of Feynman describing the problem here: [VIDEO] And yes, people are that clueless about \"computer stuff\" that they could just as well have been from another planet."},{"id":4419092,"author":"nano_o","month":"2012-08","story":"\"Sometimes science must give way to religion\"","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on limits of explanation","quote":"Here is, I think, Feynman explaining it","text":"Sometimes it may be better not to explain than to resort to cosmic molasses and moonlight (terms taken from the NYT quote in the article). Here is, I think, Feynman explaining it: [VIDEO] It seems to also apply to explaining through religion."},{"id":3045763,"author":"defen","month":"2011-09","story":"\"How much does a website cost?\" – a survey of web designers","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on explaining hard physics to laymen","quote":"Hopefully he didn't ask Feynman!","text":"> Imagine a curious older relative asking a physicist what light is and receiving \"Well, it's sort of like one thing, but it's really not\" as a response. Hopefully he didn't ask Feynman! [VIDEO]"},{"id":2719103,"author":"lutorm","month":"2011-07","story":"Theory suggests wrinkling of wet digits evolved for a reason","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on levels of why","quote":"Your comment reminds me of Feynman responding to the question of why magnets attract each other","text":"I would argue that it (unlike all the other things that did not evolve) did evolve for the reason that it was useful. It's not like all of a sudden there was someone that had wrinkly fingers and that \"stuck around because it was useful\". In all likelihood, there were many incremental changes that were progressively selected for and the \"reason\" for that selection is that it was useful. It seems to me that it's perfectly acceptable language to say that a cause is the \"reason\" for an effect, even if it's not possible to assign intent. Your comment reminds me of Feynman responding to the question of why magnets attract each other ( [VIDEO] ) which, while inscrutable from a physical point of view, seems not to be a fruitful way of approaching questions. There are always levels of deeper understanding, but you have to take it one level at a time."},{"id":2245085,"author":"Jach","month":"2011-02","story":"Why nothing can go faster than the speed of light","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman refusing to explain with rubber bands","quote":"Bringing in arrows where they don't belong, or in the magnet case \"rubber bands\", is cheating the asker.","text":"Yeah, I believe he proved a correspondence between the arrows and the Schroedinger equation (not in that book) at some point (and it's not hard to come up with a character of the proof). The grandparent is referring to something like this video, though, which you mentioned at the end: [VIDEO] Bringing in arrows where they don't belong, or in the magnet case \"rubber bands\", is cheating the asker. I'm not familiar enough with GR to know if arrows are a useful representation like they are with complex amplitudes."},{"id":2149226,"author":"naveensundar","month":"2011-01","story":"Oxford announces new degree in Computer Science and Philosophy","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on where explanations bottom out","quote":"Feynman on a related problem","text":"It is all a matter of where we stop asking questions. Mostly, good philosophers keep on pushing and end up with something ridiculously hard like Chalmer's hard problem of consciousness. But most of us refuse to recognize that this is even a problem: the brain produces it. Just because we answer one of (what,when,why,how,where) does not mean the whole problem is solved. Feynman on a related problem : [VIDEO] Unfortunately, this tradition of honest and deep questions and answers has mostly sort of tapered off..."},{"id":2021928,"author":"iwwr","month":"2010-12","story":"Why I love math","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on why analogies fail to explain magnetism","quote":"Feynman on magnetism","text":"Any specialized field becomes obscure to the uninitiated. While it may be possible to use metaphors to give them a glimpse into the field, it's ultimately a false picture. And so, analogies should be used to whet appetites, not attempt to explain expert knowledge. Feynman on magnetism: \"I can't explain that attraction in any of the terms that's familiar to you. For example, if we said that magnets attract as if they were connected by rubber bands, I would be cheating you[...] and if you were curious enough, you would ask me why rubber bands tend to pull back together again and I would end up having to explain that in terms of electrical forces.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":1631021,"author":"ostso","month":"2010-08","story":"Ask HN: What is Gravity?","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on how to answer why questions","quote":"Here is Richard Feynman's answer to a similar question","text":"Here is Richard Feynman's answer to a similar question (this isn't about gravity but about this type of question): [VIDEO]"},{"id":1403870,"author":"Jach","month":"2010-06","story":"IAmA theoretical physicist. Ask me anything (via reddit)","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on answering why questions via magnets","quote":"Here's Feynman's take on magnets (really, on answering 'why' questions)","text":"I stopped reading after it appeared he was advocating collapse theory. About the only non-confusing quantum mechanics I've read so far that's been light on math has been Feynman's stuff like QED and this series: [LINK] On a meta-note, I don't really think this is anything but noise. If you want to really, really understand the physics, you have to do the math, and if you just want a high-level concept I don't see how in the space of a single reddit post you could take away loads of confusion or adequately explain a concept without cheating the person. Here's Feynman's take on magnets (really, on answering 'why' questions): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vuEQixrBKCc",1],["fPrvnlvnu-k",1],["JxAXlJEmNMg",1]]},"wfzSE4Hoxbc":{"wy":"Cited in threads on probability and quantum theory as a strong argument that probabilities can be eliminated from physics.","q":{"x":"Worth a watch especially if you balk at this idea just to to see a strong counter argument.","i":35937841,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":33184677,"author":"ThomPete","month":"2022-10","story":"Probability (1963)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed commenter's view on origin and role of probability.","quote":"It was a big awakening for me once I realized not only where probability theory came from","text":"Any theory that is explainable and hard to vary does not need probability. Can you explain how probability is used in statistical mechanics in a way to contradict what I am saying? P.S. Thanks for being a sport and talk about this in a truly respectful way. If you want to understand where I am coming from, I can really recommend David Deutsch talk about probability here [VIDEO] It was a big awakening for me once I realized not only where probability theory came from (not physics) and how engrained it is in our thinking."},{"id":35937841,"author":"andrewgleave","month":"2023-05","story":"The World is Built on Probability [1988/2023]","stance":"rec","why":"History of probability and why many-worlds has none.","quote":"Worth a watch especially if you balk at this idea just to to see a strong counter argument.","text":"David Deutsch’s “Physics Without Probability” covers the history of probability, it’s legitimate and misconceived uses and concludes that according to MWI there is no such thing in reality - it’s basically that probabilities correspond to how measures of the multiverse proportion themselves as differentiation occurs. I watched it a few years ago so may be misremembering bits but I think that is the gist… Worth a watch especially if you balk at this idea just to to see a strong counter argument. [VIDEO] Edit: link"},{"id":24102500,"author":"scribu","month":"2020-08","story":"Algorithmic Theories of Everything (2000)","stance":"reference","why":"Deutsch's work eliminating probabilities from physics.","quote":"Contrast with the recent work from David Deutsch, which eliminates all probabilities from physics.","text":"IMHO, a better title would be \" Probabilistic Theories Of Everything\" Contrast with the recent work from David Deutsch, which eliminates all probabilities from physics. Intro to Constructor Theory: [VIDEO]"}]},"0j0xzuh-6rY":{"wy":"HN cites it in TikTok ban debates as the accessible summary of how Douyin and TikTok differ.","q":{"x":"tl;dw: Chinese users get more wholesome content, particularly children, along with usage limits.","i":35260801,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39800580,"author":"tivert","month":"2024-03","story":"India's TikTok Ban Is a Cautionary Tale for the U.S.","stance":"rec","why":"Explains differences between Douyin and TikTok","quote":"Here's a video about the differences","text":"> There’s an equivalent called Douyin. Having used neither, I don’t know what’s the difference, if any. Here's a video about the differences: TikTok in China versus the United States | 60 Minutes: [VIDEO] Douyin is much healthier. Chinese children cannot use Douyin for more than 40 min a day, and it promotes positive content like science experiments you can do at home. American kids can use TikTok as much as they want, and so are addicted. I don't use it, but I'm almost certain it doesn't just show prosocial educational content to kids, but mostly mindless entertainment and influencer ads. The result is the most popular career Chinese kids saw they want to grow up to be astronauts, but American kids want to be social media influences."},{"id":35260801,"author":"snozolli","month":"2023-03","story":"Kids Are Not Okay","stance":"rec","why":"Douyin versus TikTok differences","quote":"tl;dw: Chinese users get more wholesome content, particularly children, along with usage limits.","text":"60 Minutes piece on Douyin (domestic users) versus TikTok (foreign users): [VIDEO] tl;dw: Chinese users get more wholesome content, particularly children, along with usage limits."},{"id":44024891,"author":"hagbard_c","month":"2025-05","story":"Why Are There So Many 'Alternative Devices' All of a Sudden?","stance":"reference","why":"Expert comparison of Chinese and US youth content","quote":"Chinese children are time limited to 40 minutes per day","text":"No, it just moved eastwards and gained a few extra stars. Compare the Chinese iteration of TT (Douyin) with the international version. Chinese children are time limited to 40 minutes per day and get presented 'science experiments you can do at home, museum exhibits, patriotism videos and educational videos' according to a 'tech expert' interviewed on the subject [1] while western girls get told they may be boys and vice-versa if they happen to feel some unease with their growing bodies during puberty. They get inundated in 'influencer' content trying to peddle them the latest make-up trends, the latest fashion fads and more such consumerist drivel. From the interview with the 'expert': “There’s a survey of pre-teens in the U.S. and China asking, ‘what is the most aspirational career that you want to have?’ and in the U.S., the No. 1 was a social media influencer, and in China, the No. 1 was astronaut,” Harris said. “You allow those two societies to play out for a few generations and I can tell you what your world is going to look like.” Of course I did not only mention the CCP as a nefarious actor in the 'social' landscape, did you miss that? I pointed out the (decidedly home-grown) tech moguls as being just as guilty and while they may have tried to virtue signal with the 'progressives' when they were in power they're certainly not part of the 'red scare´ unless they're like Lenin's 'capitalists who will sell the Soviets the rope they will use to hang them'. I don't think th"},{"id":42342359,"author":"wyldberry","month":"2024-12","story":"TikTok divestment law upheld by federal appeals court","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"At best this comment implies there's actual an actual smoking gun related to what functionally are nation-state capability and operations to influence populations external to their own, specifically related to TikTok/ByteDance. If you have an AP government level understanding of how China and the USA work, then you can probably understand that this is an intersection of: government laws, great power competition, and wealth seeking behaviors. When you control the algorithm, everyone downstream of it is at the mercy of the power structures and incentives of your top level leadership [0]. Consider the following: 1.) How Chinese companies interact with the CCP: they are 100% subservient with a CCP party member who can pull the CEO in at anytime and assign them tasks related to studying Xi thought and other party material, as well as demand the company do anything for the state. 2.) What great power competition looks like including economic warfare (tariffs, massive state subsidies to protect national defense, influence operations for allies, securing raw materials, reducing reliance on rivals, etc). 3.) How espionage in general works in support of political aims, and especially how each nation uses it to further their goals. For China, it's always been about vacuuming up as much data as possible, and then later on finding use cases for it. The disruption side of their practices are successful if they make chaos in an adversary that forces them to spend time on dealing with that i"},{"id":40099519,"author":"jebby","month":"2024-04","story":"House approves sell-or-be-banned TikTok measure","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This was around the time I decided that I didn't care what happened to TikTok.","text":"[VIDEO] [TikTok in China versus the United States | 60 Minutes] This was around the time I decided that I didn't care what happened to TikTok."},{"id":33837730,"author":"gjsman-1000","month":"2022-12","story":"South Dakota first to ban TikTok on state-owned devices","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with tech expert on TikTok's influence","quote":"CBS 60 Minutes Interview with an IT expert just 3 weeks ago","text":"So he made it up thinking it was fake - but now it has been independently shown to be likely true. In which case he made up a conspiracy theory then demonstrated to be accurate when he thought it was a joke. [LINK] [LINK] CBS 60 Minutes Interview with an IT expert just 3 weeks ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33972368,"author":"jzmorganchase","month":"2022-12","story":"Bipartisan Legislation to Ban TikTok","stance":"reference","why":"Content on Chinese version is very different","quote":"The recent 60 minutes story specifically says the type of content is completely different","text":"This is not true. The recent 60 minutes story specifically says the type of content is completely different because it is heavily moderated in China which is the total opposite of what we see in the US or elsewhere outside of China. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33657533,"author":"ZainRiz","month":"2022-11","story":"The FBI alleges TikTok poses national security concerns","stance":"reference","why":"Compares Douyin and TikTok content for children","quote":"Here's a segment by 60 Minutes explaining how they show their own citizens educational, beneficial content","text":"If you read the first paragraph of the article, the primary concern mentioned is not surveillance, it's influencing the American public in ways that benefits China and harms the US. Psy-ops effectively Here's a segment by 60 Minutes explaining how they show their own citizens educational, beneficial content inspiring them to be better citizens, while the rest of the world gets the degenerative content we usually associate with ticktok. And China could easily further adjust their ranking algorithm further to highlight the views it wants us to have. All social media has the power to do this, but China has both the most adversarial incentive and a demonstrated willingness to do so. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["6bqBsHSwPgw",1]]},"3QnD2c4Xovk":{"wy":"Recommended as a simple, non-mathematical way to understand key exchange.","q":{"x":"Diffie-Hellman explained to me like I'm 5 using colors.","i":11974776,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11974776,"author":"zappo2938","month":"2016-06","story":"How to Backdoor Diffie-Hellman","stance":"rec","why":"Diffie-Hellman explained simply using colors","quote":"Diffie-Hellman explained to me like I'm 5 using colors.","text":"Diffie-Hellman explained to me like I'm 5 using colors. Public Key Cryptography: Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":5532333,"author":"bradford","month":"2013-04","story":"A non-mathematical explanation of one way functions","stance":"rec","why":"Non-mathematical paint mixing analogy for one-way functions","quote":"I liked this video, which uses a paint mixing analogy to explain the one way functions","text":"For non-mathematical explanations I liked this video, which uses a paint mixing analogy to explain the one way functions: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4109382,"author":"n3x","month":"2012-06","story":"Ask HN: How do you avoid literally Man-in-the-Middle actack?","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in a man-in-the-middle discussion","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] can't just proxy it. the attacker can force his own certificate, but as previously said, ssl ev should solve it by making the user aware something is wrong in the address bar."}],"r":[["8aGhZQkoFbQ",1],["pSm7BcQHWXk",1],["YEBfamv-_do",1]]},"9u0qlIoSSkQ":{"wy":"Cited as an example of impressive amateur-scale rocket work, and as a vertical-landing test that predates and rivals SpaceX's Grasshopper.","q":{"x":"this [1] test by Armadillo was more impressive in my opinion for the additional complexity involved","i":4965010,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":8538542,"author":"acous","month":"2014-10","story":"The Tyranny of the Rocket Equation (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Test flight footage from an Armadillo rocket","quote":"here's a cool video from one of armadillo's test rockets","text":"This exchange made me grin :) To add some value to my comment, here's a cool video from one of armadillo's test rockets: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4965010,"author":"gnarbarian","month":"2012-12","story":"SpaceX Grasshopper Takes Off and Lands Vertically [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Armadillo test with engine cut, chute deploy and successful landing","quote":"this [1] test by Armadillo was more impressive in my opinion for the additional complexity involved","text":"Bigger but this [1] test by Armadillo was more impressive in my opinion for the additional complexity involved in cutting the engine deploying a chute then cutting the chute and successfully landing from a turbulent freefall. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":1537822,"author":"matrixownsyou","month":"2010-07","story":"Ask HN:  Does reading HN ever make you feel like shit?","stance":"reference","why":"Armadillo rocket test flight as an example of impressive work","quote":"","text":"I felt the same way until i read a post from a HN user about \"being mediocre and it's OK\". Not being a rocket scientist doesn't mean i can't build great stuff too, i'll just never do something like this :) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bBC-nXj3Ng4",1]]},"BI-old7YI4I":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the go-to reference for the idea of living in Venus's atmosphere rather than on the surface.","q":{"x":"Caution: don't click unless you have nothing else to do today.","i":16703293,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":23132594,"author":"alistproducer2","month":"2020-05","story":"How Long Was Venus Habitable?","stance":"rec","why":"Isaac Arthur explores colonizing Venus","quote":"I enjoy Isaac Arthur's YouTube channel where he explores topics like colonizing Venus.","text":"I enjoy Isaac Arthur's YouTube channel where he explores topics like colonizing Venus. Here's the video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16703293,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2018-03","story":"Missions could see NASA spacecraft heading to Uranus and Neptune","stance":"rec","why":"Long, absorbing exploration of colonizing Venus","quote":"Caution: don't click unless you have nothing else to do today.","text":"Outward Bound: Colonizing Venus: [VIDEO] Caution: don't click unless you have nothing else to do today."},{"id":48193805,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2026-05","story":"Colonization of Venus","stance":"reference","why":"Isaac Arthur's take on Venus colonization","quote":"Obligatory Isaac Arthur reference [1].","text":"Obligatory Isaac Arthur reference [1]. I still think humanity's far future is in orbitals in space, not on planets and certainly not on planets as hostile as Venus is. I'm not sure how well living at 50km above the surface would work. You still need a lot of buoyance to float large structures. The atmosphere is also a solvable problem. One idea I've heard is using so-called \"fusion candles\". That is a fusion-powered device in the atmosphere that sends waste gas into space and waste matter to the ground in an equilibrium that keeps them airborne, all powered by fusion. You could extract carbon and/or oxygen this way from the plentiful atmospheric CO2. Still, if you ever got the atmosphere down to a non-hellish level at surface, the surface would still be covered with all sorts of exotics and metals, many of them toxic. You'd probably be looking at geologic timescales to rehabilitate it. But whenever these terraforming questions come up (often with respect to Mars), people really don't appreciate the scale and the energy budget required. The energy budget is many orders of magntidue what our civilization currently uses. If you have access to that much energy, there are far better options. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27729311,"author":"cletus","month":"2021-07","story":"Soviet Venus Images (2004)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Venus being a better terraforming target than Mars","quote":"","text":"It's wild we have any images of the surface of Venus at all given how hostile Venus is. Given how hostile it is, it seems unlikely we'll go back anytime soon. These images were really a product of the Cold War where the US focused on Mars. It's worth noting that Venus is still probably a better terraforming target than Mars [1]. This romantic notion of Mars has rooted itself deeply at this point (eg with Elon Musk) but Mars is basically a more distant and inconvenient Moon. Almost no atmosphere (in fact, just enough because dust storms will occasionally just coat all your equipment), the Sun is weaker and gravity is still very low. Venus's atmosphere is about 3% nitrogen and 97% CO2. It has about 100x the atmosphere of Earth, which means there's actually more atmospheric nitrogen than on Earth. And abundant CO2 means oxygen is abundant. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23132416,"author":"cletus","month":"2020-05","story":"How Long Was Venus Habitable?","stance":"reference","why":"Supports living in Venus's atmosphere rather than on the surface","quote":"","text":"So there a few comments scattered around about this so I'll address those concerns here. Quite a few people are scoffing at the idea of colonizing Venus (\"you'll get crushed, dude!\") but the idea has had a lot of people put thought into it and it's not as impractical as you may think. Consider: 1. Venus is easier to reach than Mars. The Delta-V is 0.4km/s and 0.7km/s respectively [1]; 2. Mars has practically no atmosphere and really low gravity. It's really not that much better than colonizing the Moon but a whole lot further away [2]. 3. You wouldn't live on surface of Venus, at least not initially. You'd live in the atmosphere [3]. 4. Energy will be plentiful on Venus, far more than on Mars or even the Earth, just from solar energy. [4] 5. Venus has nearly Earthlike gravity. So it's actually not as crazy as you might think and shouldn't be immediately dismissed. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO] [4]: [LINK]"},{"id":15706315,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2017-11","story":"Closest Temperate World Orbiting Quiet Star Discovered","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for floating habitats in Venus's atmosphere","quote":"","text":"Venus is pretty sweet as it is. Go high enough in the atmosphere, and you have about Earth atmospheric pressure and room temperatures. That altitude in the Venus atmosphere is effectively an Earth biosphere sized almost-Earthlike living space. One convenient circumstance: A breathable Earthlike atmosphere will float at that altitude, so it would be easy to live in floating balloon habitats. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qWKz3psejb0",1],["uUmIdq0D6-A",1],["-fny99f8amM",1]]},"LZPKTaVB1IU":{"wy":"Linked as a rebuttal when Lustig's or similar influencer nutrition claims come up.","q":{"x":"Here's Layne Norton's response to Lustig and that exact Huberman episode","i":38998307,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":44743381,"author":"oarfish","month":"2025-07","story":"Maintaining weight loss","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks Robert Lustig's claims contradicted by current science","quote":"A good debunk of his nonsense claims is here","text":"PSA: Robert Lustig is notorious for broadcasting incorrect views contradicted by current science. A good debunk of his nonsense claims is here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38998307,"author":"hombre_fatal","month":"2024-01","story":"Could inflammation be the cause of myriad chronic conditions? (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Layne Norton's rebuttal of Lustig's claims on the Huberman podcast","quote":"Here's Layne Norton's response to Lustig and that exact Huberman episode","text":"Here's Layne Norton's response to Lustig and that exact Huberman episode: [VIDEO] It'll answer your question."},{"id":39322940,"author":"jitl","month":"2024-02","story":"Consumption of 100% Fruit Juice and Body Weight in Children and Adults","stance":"reference","why":"Response video addressing the influencer claims about almonds and calories","quote":"Here’s some influencer content thats addressing that influencer content","text":"This guy says a lot of interesting things, and I like the sound of his voice. But, googling for stuff that really quantifies these effects isn’t turning up much. Like if 130 kcal of almond get absorbed in one part of the digestive tract, and 30kcal get absorbed farther down, it still sounds like 160kcal of almond is going into the body, and has to come out somewhere else, or it’s going to stay in there. Here’s some influencer content thats addressing that influencer content: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aMcjxSThD54",1],["DoA4Txr4GUs",1],["LQRAfJyEsko",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["XpDsk374LDE",1]]},"N_4R4X7AWtU":{"wy":"HN recommends it in threads about sanctions and Russian military electronics, as an explanation of Russia's semiconductor limits.","q":{"x":"See Asianometry: Why Russia Can’t Replace TSMC","i":31547634,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":39131725,"author":"mempko","month":"2024-01","story":"Most of Russia's War Chips Are Made by US and EU Companies (Intel, AMD, STM)","stance":"rec","why":"Details Russia's own chips and why it cannot replace TSMC.","quote":"The Asianometry YouTube channel has some details on Russia's own chips.","text":"Russia does build it's own chips but their fab tech is about 15 years behind the state of the art. The Asianometry YouTube channel has some details on Russia's own chips. [VIDEO] While they build their own chips, the video looks at why Russia can't replace TSMC for all of domestic use. But I suspect the whole local industry is oriented towards the war machine. I doubt you need 3nm chips for missiles."},{"id":31547634,"author":"breadloaf","month":"2022-05","story":"Captured Russian Weapons Are Packed with U.S. Microchips","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Russia struggles with semiconductor manufacturing.","quote":"See Asianometry: Why Russia Can’t Replace TSMC","text":"They are not doing that, because they have big troubles to pull off semiconductor manufacturing See Asianometry: Why Russia Can’t Replace TSMC - [VIDEO]"},{"id":30696383,"author":"freemint","month":"2022-03","story":"It happened to me: I accidentally attended a crypto bro dinner","stance":"reference","why":"Source disputing viability of Russia's 65nm chip production.","quote":"Since Russia's 65nm capabilities are disputed [1] to be viable for mass production.","text":"65nm is not plenty fast. Since Russia's 65nm capabilities are disputed [1] to be viable for mass production. That only leaves them with reliable 90nm fabs. This is 4GHz Pentium 4 at best. I have not seen any indication of video acceleration, so if a western CPU breaks and is replaced by a Russia made one it might no longer be able to play 4k video fluently. All the fabless semi conductor companies (Baikal, Elberus) only have recent designs for foreign nodes. Are you aware of any MCST chip that shipped in larger number on 65nm produced in Russia if this node is supposedly available since the early 2010s in Russia? I am asking like that because all Elberus chips smaller than 90nm seem to be fabbed by TSMC in recent years. [2] The Elberus chip supposedly on 65nm clocks at 0.8 GHz. Also I agree that the sanctions were well targeted not to hit Russia's poor but focus on the middle class upward instead. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["Zk11vI-7czE",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["fhOHn9TClXY",1],["lI7T650RTT8",1]]},"ZmWREtcDVBE":{"wy":"HN people link it in discussions of taxi drivers' spatial memory and how cabbies handle roadworks and traffic.","q":{"x":"There's a fantastic Tom Scott video on it","i":49241209,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49241209,"author":"phist_mcgee","month":"2026-08","story":"Taxi drivers rarely die of Alzheimer's","stance":"rec","why":"Cabbies remember roadworks and traffic patterns.","quote":"There's a fantastic Tom Scott video on it","text":"They also have to remember where roadwork is/was and what kind of traffic is best/worst at a given time. There's a fantastic Tom Scott video on it [VIDEO]"},{"id":34084143,"author":"rhdunn","month":"2022-12","story":"The Decline of the City Grid","stance":"rec","why":"Cabbie's knowledge versus GPS across London.","quote":"It was interesting to watch.","text":"There was a recent Tom Scott video ( [VIDEO] ) where he was using a GPS and Tom Hutley (a London cabbie) only relied on his London knowledge to get around, travelling the same distance after reaching certain landmarks. It was interesting to watch."},{"id":41973248,"author":"janosch_123","month":"2024-10","story":"Ask HN: What are you working on? (October 2024)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Sure, Tom Scott drives it in this (he doesn't talk about it being electric): [VIDEO] Here is a review: [LINK] Here is all the media: [LINK] My video tutorials are linked above."}],"r":[["cCOL7MC4Pl0",1]]},"nq_SpRBXRmE":{"wy":"HN mentions it as a favorite documentary scene and as a reference for the Pruitt-Igoe demolition.","q":{"x":"Koyaanisqatsi - Wikipedia: - Pruitt Igoe Scene","i":25629872,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25629872,"author":"kratom_sandwich","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: What is the most inspiring documentary film in your opinion?","stance":"rec","why":"Pruitt-Igoe scene from Koyaanisqatsi, a documentary favorite","quote":"Koyaanisqatsi - Wikipedia: - Pruitt Igoe Scene","text":"I guess this boils down to what your mean by \"inspiring\"; let me give you some of my favorites by category: > Movies about people doing stuff, usually investigating something - makes me want to get up and get stuff done [1] Cold Case Hammarskjöld - IMDB: [LINK] [2] Tickled - Trailer: [LINK] [3] Resurrect Dead: The Mystery of the Toynbee Tiles - Trailer: [LINK] (full movie is on Youtube, Too) [4] The Cove - Wikipedia: [LINK] > Movies about ... the human condition, human life on earth? IDK, but if you watch the trailer, you get what I mean. WARNING: Might evoke the urge to travel [1] Koyaanisqatsi - Wikipedia: [LINK] - Pruitt Igoe Scene: [VIDEO] [2] Samsara - Trailer: [LINK] [3] Anthropocene - Trailer: [LINK] (Check out his other movie \"Watermark\", too!) > Movies about the work of particular people: [1] Playing God - Trailer: [LINK]"},{"id":8453303,"author":"frik","month":"2014-10","story":"Beethoven transformed music, but has veneration of him stifled his successors?","stance":"rec","why":"Glass's well-known Koyaanisqatsi music","quote":"I like his well known Pruit Igoe/Koyaanisqatsi music","text":"I like his well known Pruit Igoe/Koyaanisqatsi music ( [VIDEO] ). But his recent work for a local small opera house named \"Kepler\" is off-putting ( [LINK] )."},{"id":28094764,"author":"Lammy","month":"2021-08","story":"Homelessness is about housing","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the Pruitt-Igoe demolition","quote":"Pruitt-Igoe is that housing project they implode in \"Koyaanisqatsi\"","text":"> They open by quoting an October 18, 1957 editorial in the St. Louis Post-Dispatch which says: \"If rooming houses are permitted to spread to the city's one- and two-family neighborhoods, there is not much use in talking brave words about fighting blight.\" RE: \"\"\"blight\"\"\" — [LINK] \"You can’t really understand Ferguson unless you understand J.D. Shelley. He was a middle-class black man from north St. Louis who in 1945 bought a home in a neighborhood just a few minutes east of Ferguson, unaware of the restrictive covenant that barred its sale to 'people of the Negro or Asian Race'. Alas, this move inflamed Louis Kraemer, who lived ten blocks away and was well aware of the covenant. Kraemer was temporarily vindicated when the Missouri Supreme court backed his lawsuit to enforce the covenant, but the United States Supreme Court overturned the Missouri ruling and forbade the state from enforcing such private agreements. In the wake of the Shelley v. Kraemer decision, blacks began to move out of crowded north St. Louis City, where many had been packed into high-rise projects such as the infamous Pruitt-Igoe, to north St. Louis County.\" Pruitt-Igoe is that housing project they implode in \"Koyaanisqatsi\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_o7qjN3KF8U",1],["ed2FWNWwE3I",1],["Wpzvaqypav8",1]]},"pT19VwBAqKA":{"wy":"Linked as the clearest technical explainer of differential privacy and the census, and of why aggregate data can still compromise privacy.","q":{"x":"Technical explanation by minutephysics","i":30635516,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30635516,"author":"achille","month":"2022-03","story":"Why the 2020 census has 9 fake people in a single house","stance":"rec","why":"Technical explanation of differential privacy in the 2020 census.","quote":"Technical explanation by minutephysics","text":"Technical explanation by minutephysics: [VIDEO] The amount of privacy is configurable, and researchers who would like to tune it (in either direction) can make a plea so the public can decide the amount of privacy loss they're willing give up in order to aid research. Each culture is different and has different tolerances for privacy and amount of intrusiveness."},{"id":26830731,"author":"Zacru","month":"2021-04","story":"Cohort IDs can be collected over time to create cross-site tracking IDs","stance":"rec","why":"Walkthrough of the privacy doom principle using census data.","quote":"Your comment reminded me of this minutephysics video about the census.","text":"Your comment reminded me of this minutephysics video about the census. [VIDEO] It a great walkthrough of the \"privacy doom principle.\""},{"id":22243995,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2020-02","story":"Google tracks individual users per Chrome installation ID","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> That would make the existence of anonymous data practically speaking impossible to have on the web For almost every type of data that is true. Transforming or substituting data doesn't make it anonymous; the patters in the data are still present. To produce actually anonymous data you have to do what the GDPR instructed: corrupt the data (\"rendered anonymous\") severely enough that the \"data subject is ... no longer identifiable\". You need to do something like aggregate the data into a small number of groups such that individual records no longer exist. Techniques like \"differential privacy\" let you control precisely how \"anonymous\" your data is by e.g. mixing in carefully crafted noise. > 8192 bucket While others have pointed out that this isn't actually limited to 13 bits of entropy for most people, there are at least two reasons that field is still very personally identifying. First, \"x-client-data on its own\" never happens. Google isn't wasting time and money implementing this feature to make an isolated database with a single column. At no point will the x-client-data value (or any other type of data they capture) ever sit in isolation. I used the IPv4 Source Address as an example because it will necessarily be present in the header of the packets that transport the x-client-data header over the internet. Suggesting that Google would ever use this value in isolation is almost insulting to Google; why would they waste their expensive developer time to create, capture, an"}],"r":[["bKGhvKyjgLY",1]]},"rpyBYuu-wJI":{"wy":"HN commenters link it in moka pot threads as a practical guide whose tips, such as starting with hot water, improved their coffee.","q":{"x":"There's a great video with coffee expert James Hoffmann on making coffee in a moka pot","i":29348260,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":34941345,"author":"ValentineC","month":"2023-02","story":"","stance":"rec","why":"Hoffmann's moka pot method","quote":"I like James Hoffmann's method for this","text":"I like James Hoffmann's method for this, which is surprisingly from a channel that's not his: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29348260,"author":"hardwaregeek","month":"2021-11","story":"The Humble Brilliance of Italy's Moka Coffee Pot (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Moka pot coffee guide featuring James Hoffmann","quote":"There's a great video with coffee expert James Hoffmann on making coffee in a moka pot","text":"There's a great video with coffee expert James Hoffmann on making coffee in a moka pot: [VIDEO] However a moka pot generally doesn't make espresso as much as strong coffee. If you want something that makes espresso, check out the 9bar: [LINK] Of course you'll want a good hand grinder for either, but especially espresso. You can't get anything close to espresso with pre-ground or a cheap grinder."},{"id":29348619,"author":"samplenoise","month":"2021-11","story":"The Humble Brilliance of Italy's Moka Coffee Pot (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Tricks that improved the commenter's moka pot coffee","quote":"I adopted a few tricks from either [1] or a similar video from James Hoffman","text":"I adopted a few tricks from either [1] or a similar video from James Hoffman’s Youtube channel some time ago (including the foaming milk with a French press one) and it vastly improved my lockdowns. The one useful thing they don’t say is (imo) it’s easier to get it right with a smaller size pot. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29351081,"author":"ValentineC","month":"2021-11","story":"The Humble Brilliance of Italy's Moka Coffee Pot (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Start with hot water so grounds heat less","quote":"recommend starting with really hot water at the bottom, so the coffee grinds have less time to heat up.","text":"James Hoffmann [1] and many others recommend starting with really hot water at the bottom, so the coffee grinds have less time to heat up. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27375202,"author":"ValentineC","month":"2021-06","story":"The 4:6 method for coffee-brewing (2017) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Hoffmann's hot-water moka pot method","quote":"James Hoffmann recommends using hot water in the moka pot","text":"James Hoffmann recommends using hot water in the moka pot: [VIDEO] I put mine (stainless steel) on my induction stove for 2, maybe 3 minutes maximum, and I've never encountered burnt coffee."},{"id":24127741,"author":"masonhensley","month":"2020-08","story":"The Humble Brilliance of Italy’s Moka Coffee Pot (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Moka pot brewing tutorial alongside personal tips","quote":"I use one every day.","text":"[VIDEO] I use one every day. Generally, fresh grounds, prewarm the water before assembly. I also like to wisk in a little sugar in the final cup after pouring out. (Don’t add sugar to the moka pot or it will caramelize/burn)"}]},"s19G6n0UjsM":{"wy":"HN points to it as a detailed, captivating talk by the Noria author, including a good explanation of deduplicating data between maps using unsafe.","q":{"x":"gave a great talk about the system","i":29617226,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29617226,"author":"Orou","month":"2021-12","story":"Noria: Dynamic, partially-stateful data-flow for high-perf web applications","stance":"rec","why":"Author's talk on the Noria system","quote":"gave a great talk about the system","text":"The author of Noria, Jon Gjengset, gave a great talk about the system: [VIDEO] The speeds achieved are impressive but you do not get consistency."},{"id":23442572,"author":"harikb","month":"2020-06","story":"There’s plenty of room at the Top: computer performance after Moore’s law","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed and captivating talks by Gjengset","quote":"His talks are very detailed[4] and captivating[3]","text":"Anecdotal - One of favorite YouTube live-streaming personalities, Jon Gjengset[1] is a Ph.D student at MIT CSAIL. His talks are very detailed[4] and captivating[3] [1] [LINK] Talks [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] Rust Details(my favorites) [4] [LINK]"},{"id":33613911,"author":"Jonhoo","month":"2022-11","story":"How PlanetScale Boost serves SQL queries faster","stance":"reference","why":"Shorter talk on partially-stateful dataflow","quote":"a shorter talk from a few years earlier at","text":":wave: Author of the paper this work is based on here. I'm so excited to see dynamic, partially-stateful data-flow for incremental materialized view maintenance becoming more wide-spread! I continue to think it's a _great_ idea, and the speed-ups (and complexity reduction) it can yield are pretty immense, so seeing more folks building on the idea makes me very happy. The PlanetScale blog post references my original \"Noria\" OSDI paper ( [LINK] ), but I'd actually recommend my PhD thesis instead ( [LINK] ), as it goes much deeper about some of the technical challenges and solutions involved. It also has a chapter (Appendix A) that covers how it all works by analogy, which the less-technical among the audience may appreciate :) A recording of my thesis defense on this, which may be more digestible than the thesis itself, is also online at [LINK] , as well as a shorter talk from a few years earlier at [VIDEO] . And the Noria research prototype (written in Rust) is on GitHub: [LINK] . As others have already mentioned in the comments, I co-founded ReadySet ( [LINK] ) shortly after graduating specifically to build off of Noria, and they're doing amazing work to provide these kinds of speed-ups for general-purpose relational databases. If you're using one of those, it's worth giving ReadySet a look to get these kinds of speedups there! It's also source-available @ [LINK] if you're curious."},{"id":18836947,"author":"dpezely","month":"2019-01","story":"Rust at speed – building a fast concurrent database [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Explains deduplicating data between two maps using unsafe","quote":"He explains this at roughly the 40 minute mark.","text":"He explains this at roughly the 40 minute mark. [1] \"There's 7 more lines of `unsafe` that avoids keeping both maps. You still keep two maps, but you de-duplicate the data between those two maps.\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["rAl-9HwD858",1]]},"vmwnhZmEZjc":{"wy":"Recommended as the protocol designer's own detailed explanation of SCP.","q":{"x":"This is a great video explaining SCP in detail","i":16235047,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":19914098,"author":"malgorithms","month":"2019-05","story":"Keybase and Stellar is live for everyone","stance":"rec","why":"Protocol designer's talk at Google.","quote":"Here's a good video of his talk at Google","text":"David Mazières (designer of the protocol) has given a number of talks. Here's a good video of his talk at Google, and I believe it's the one I watched a couple years ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16235047,"author":"sjs382","month":"2018-01","story":"2018 Stellar roadmap","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed explanation of the Stellar Consensus Protocol.","quote":"This is a great video explaining SCP in detail","text":"Crap. I'll reproduce the answer here, then: > Tl;dr: Stellar’s protocol (SCP) uses a decentralized state propagation where each node commits to a value if every node it deems trustworthy agrees while Ripple uses a supermajority vote (80%) among all validating nodes. Stellar achieves better decentralization than Ripple > A pretty old, but still relevant discussion on the bitcoin stack exchange [ [LINK] ] > Stellar started out as a fork of Ripple (now completely rewritten) so they are pretty similar in implementation and use cases. > Stellar uses the Stellar Consensus Protocol [ [LINK] ] (SCP) to achieve decentralization, while Ripple uses the XRP Ledger Consensus Process [ [LINK] ]. These are alternatives to Bitcoin's proof of work whose goal is to have every participant in the network agree on the current state. > In SCP, each node decides for itself which other nodes it trusts. When a lot of these sets of trusted nodes are overlapping, there is a high probability there is agreement on the state of the network. Broadly, a node will only accept a message if all of their trustworthy nodes have in turn accepted it. This is similar to the way internet certificates and DNS servers are working. In principle, there is no central authority appointing trustworthy nodes, but de facto, the network will reach an agreement on which nodes are trustworthy. This is a great video explaining SCP in detail [ [VIDEO] ]. There is also the white paper [ [LINK] ] for more formal mathematical defini"},{"id":27077143,"author":"max_","month":"2021-05","story":"Decentralized trust graph for online value exchange without a blockchain (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Stellar's quorum slices and Byzantine fault resistance.","quote":"Stellar uses a more sophisticated notion of \"quorum slices\" and is resistant to byzantine faults","text":"It seems so. Its the notion of \"Trust Lines\"[0] All this thing does is limit potential losses from fraud. Not eliminating. The main value proposition of a blockchain is to solve the \"principal-agent\" problem [1] and gis simply reduces the risk but doesn't remove it entirely. Plus some of the requirements on a node having to be online (in a decentalized, byzantine environment) all the time are unrealistic. This seems more like ripple. Stellar uses a more sophisticated notion of \"quorum slices\" and is resistant to byzantine faults [2] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}]},"zsjZ2r9Ygzw":{"wy":"Cited in encryption policy threads as a funny, accessible primer and for its fake Apple ad.","q":{"x":"John Oliver has a great segment[1]","i":11290983,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12355207,"author":"spdy","month":"2016-08","story":"Taking stock of the new French-German encryption proposal","stance":"rec","why":"Considered must-see on encryption policy.","quote":"Mandatory John Oliver on Encryption :)","text":"Elections are coming and everyone has to play tough. None of this is targeted at terrorist as is does not even remotely make sense its all about controlling the population. Let`s see when they propose to ban math. Mandatory John Oliver on Encryption :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":11290983,"author":"ccvannorman","month":"2016-03","story":"Encryption, Privacy Are Larger Issues Than Fighting Terrorism","stance":"rec","why":"Segment noting most encryption apps are not US-based.","quote":"John Oliver has a great segment[1]","text":"I am surprised that a search for \"math\" only turned up one result in this thread, about car accidents vs terrorist victims. Isn't it true that encryption legislation or policy is sort of irrelevant next to the very clear math that says encryption will always be ahead of decryption? Even in a (hopefully avoidable) dystopia where encryption is illegal, would that really stop technology companies from continuing to do what they've always done? John Oliver has a great segment[1] where he notes that the majority of cheap, available encryption applications aren't even US-based, and so it becomes nigh-impossible for our (or any) government to stop any pedestrian from encrypting. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18638466,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2018-12","story":"Australia’s vague anti-encryption law sets a dangerous new precedent","stance":"reference","why":"Humorous take on anti-encryption legislation.","quote":"Two relevant humor bits.","text":"Two relevant humor bits. * Honest Government Ad | Anti Encryption Law - YouTube || [LINK] * Encryption: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO) - YouTube || [VIDEO]"},{"id":11509808,"author":"studentrob","month":"2016-04","story":"Intelligence Committee Leaders Release Discussion Draft of Encryption Bill","stance":"reference","why":"Comment about democratic participation; video link not shown in text.","quote":"","text":"I agree voting from your IOT device is something that should happen. We will get there some day. To get to that point sooner, we should elect more people who are knowledgeable about technology. If you disagree or feel that is an impossible route, you may be able to find others who feel similarly. Personally I think people who feel this way are part of a minority and that limiting your interactions to a certain group is isolating and not productive. I am always looking for ways to engage people with different ideas, both to learn and to share what I've learned. Ultimately, I reject vox_mollis comment that participation in the democratic process is worthless or powerless. Just listen to This American Life's episode on \"Take the Money and Run for Office\" [1], > Barney Frank: If the voters have a position, the votes will kick money's rear end any time. I've never met a politician-- I've been in the legislative bodies for 40 years now-- who, choosing between a significant opinion in his or her district and a number of campaign contributors, doesn't go with the district. [2] Or look at how Lindsey Graham changed his mind in the encryption case [3]. Our representatives are not entirely useless. Similar to your day job or at your school, some people are good at what they do, and some are bad at it. That's no reason to throw the baby out with the bath water. We have the longest running democratic republic in the world. We should study it, contribute improvements by speaking up and vot"},{"id":11298155,"author":"Freak_NL","month":"2016-03","story":"FBI Wants It to Be Impractical to Deploy Strong Encryption Without Key Escrow","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to illustrate why everyone has something to hide.","quote":"Paraphrasing John Oliver","text":"> it's not about the average person, it's about someone with something to hide Keep in mind that the FBI would love it if everyone held that to be true. We all have something to hide, average person or not. No matter if you are a criminal, political activist, pervert, lawyer, priest, or just the baker down the street. Paraphrasing John Oliver: your banking statements, medical data, dick pics, private messages, dick pics, dick pics, and your secret diary are all things you most likely want to hide. ( [VIDEO] ) The FBI would not mind a situation where only people with technological know-how use GnuPG, LUKS with dm-crypt, etc., and the rest whatever came with their smartphone. Most criminals are not particularly tech-savvy, so if that group loses access to strong encryption by default, they largely gain what they want. Of course there is no practical way to make the use of strong encryption by individual citizens around the globe illegal, and they know this. They may however succeed in outlawing strong disk encryption available by default on store-bought devices. So when a suspect uses an Android or IPhone, and the FBI has his or her device, they want to be able to access its contents without the suspect's consent. Ideally, they also want the most popular messaging platforms (like Facebook's WhatsApp) to have a backdoor available, in order for the vendor to be able to comply with warrants for such data."},{"id":11311676,"author":"kelseydh","month":"2016-03","story":"Apple Encryption Engineers, If Ordered to Unlock iPhone, Might Resist","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of shifting public debate on encryption.","quote":"","text":"There is good evidence that public debate is starting to turn. Senator Lindsay Graham, a national security hawk, is beginning to state reservations with the FBI's strategy. That's a big deal: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11344657,"author":"Kristine1975","month":"2016-03","story":"Dancing on the Lip of the Volcano: Chosen Ciphertext Attacks on iMessage [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Notes the paper title references the Last Week Tonight Apple ad.","quote":"Also the title seems to be a reference to Last Week Tonight's Apple ad","text":"Blog post by one of the authors about the attack: [LINK] Also the title seems to be a reference to Last Week Tonight's Apple ad: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11388176,"author":"joelrunyon","month":"2016-03","story":"Apple wants the FBI to reveal how it hacked the San Bernardino killer's iPhone","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the faux-Apple commercial about older phone vulnerabilities.","quote":"","text":"I think you're reading this completely wrong. The FBI asked for access, Apple said no (because they knew the case was about precedent rather than capabilities). Apple knows the older phones had vulnerabilities (see this faux-apple computer - [VIDEO] commercial). This is the follow-up from Apple saying \"oh, you needed our help to crack it huh? How did you suddenly find a way to do it on your own without us as soon as you realized public perception wasn't proceeding as you hoped? EFF seems to think that the FBI is legally required to disclose the method ( [LINK] ) due to their VEP process."},{"id":11326543,"author":"bsimpson","month":"2016-03","story":"Johns Hopkins researchers poke a hole in Apple’s encryption","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"reminds me of John Oliver's fake Apple ad from last week","text":"“Even Apple, with all their skills — and they have terrific cryptographers — wasn’t able to quite get this right,” said Green, whose team of graduate students will publish a paper describing the attack as soon as Apple issues a patch. reminds me of John Oliver's fake Apple ad from last week: \"We're barely one step of hackers at any time,\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wJyUtbn0O5Y",1],["5TkIe60y2GI",1]]},"fLA_d9q6ySs":{"wy":"Recommended as a compact account of the Atari-Nintendo lawsuit and its technical and legal details, which shaped the game industry.","q":{"x":"Highly recommended to learn about the technical and legal details of the lawsuit that shaped gaming industry landscape to this day.","i":14824334,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14824334,"author":"AceJohnny2","month":"2017-07","story":"A Tale of the Mirror World, Part 6: Total War","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Wonderful 30-minute account of the Atari-Nintendo lawsuit and its technical and legal details","quote":"Highly recommended to learn about the technical and legal details of the lawsuit that shaped gaming industry landscape to this day.","text":"Another take about Atari's subsidiary Tengen and the Atari-Nintendo lawsuit is this wonderful 30minute video by Norman Caruso, the Gaming Historian: [VIDEO] Highly recommended to learn about the technical and legal details of the lawsuit that shaped gaming industry landscape to this day. Edit: And come to think of it, some of the details and wording in Maher's 2017 article seem pulled straight from Caruso's 2015 video. :\\"},{"id":31062548,"author":"franknine","month":"2022-04","story":"The games Nintendo didn't want you to play: Tengen","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how Atari obtained Nintendo's protection system source code via copyright","quote":"It talks about how Atari abuses the copyright system to gain access to Nintendo’s protection system source code.","text":"There's another episode from Gaming Historian specifically for Tengen: [VIDEO] It talks about how Atari abuses the copyright system to gain access to Nintendo’s protection system source code."},{"id":31063060,"author":"yosefjaved1","month":"2022-04","story":"The games Nintendo didn't want you to play: Tengen","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses Atari obtaining the code from the copyright office","quote":"Atari actually got the code illegally from the copyright office.","text":"Atari actually got the code illegally from the copyright office. Here's a video discussing that portion - [VIDEO]"},{"id":29184056,"author":"AceJohnny2","month":"2021-11","story":"Firefox is the 45th result when searching for “Firefox” on Windows Store","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Atari fighting Nintendo's curated platform approach","quote":"","text":"> Times have changed, though, and you could argue that now many people would like less app clutter and easier discoverability of the apps they actually want. Time and time again people & markets need to relearn this. The \"video game crash\" in the early 80s that collapsed Atari was because of so much shovelware that came out (with the infamous E.T. game being seen as the \"pinnacle\" of that era). Nintendo emerged from that rubble by being more selective about the games they allowed [1] on their platform, something which Atari fought them on tooth and nail [2]. (The amount of shovelware games on mobile platforms is a tragic repeat of that era.) One of the more memorable aspects of classic management classes like the case study of the Challenger Disaster is that you don't want more information, you want better information. Google's mission statement remains to \"organize the world's information and make it universally accessible and useful.\" How are they doing on that these days? How's the Play Store compared to the Apple App Store or Microsoft's Windows Store? [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["KrksBdWcZgQ",1],["jvPPXbo87ds",1],["fhOHn9TClXY",1],["dP1xVpMPn8M",1]]},"u69YSh-cFXY":{"wy":"HN refers to it as a vivid first-person account of anendophasia whenever inner monologues come up.","q":{"x":"I never miss a chance to reference this video.","i":44232979,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44232979,"author":"hackinthebochs","month":"2025-06","story":"What happens when people don't understand how AI works","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A woman vividly describes having no inner monologue.","quote":"I never miss a chance to reference this video.","text":"I never miss a chance to reference this video. A woman vividly describes he experience of not having an inner monologue: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44200201,"author":"cantor_S_drug","month":"2025-06","story":"I do not remember my life and it's fine","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"People with no internal monologue","text":"I have one suggestion to make to Marco Giancotti. Please read all of Oliver Sacks books. They will help you clarify your situation a lot due to some case studies he has mentioned in his books. Related : People with no internal monologue [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":24566131,"author":"henearkr","month":"2020-09","story":"What Is the Sound of Thought?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Numerous testimonies are unambiguous, such as this one","text":"I get that you are in disbelief, but this is actually a thing. Numerous testimonies are unambiguous, such as this one: [VIDEO] Like, the interviewee is able to read extremely fast (\"superhuman\", according to the interviewer), as a consequence to the absence of inner speech. This kind of details eliminate the possibility of the uncertainties you suggested."},{"id":23887011,"author":"jengleton","month":"2020-07","story":"OpenAI's GPT-3 may be the biggest thing since Bitcoin","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This interview made the rounds a while back","text":"Also interesting to note that a good portion of people lack an internal monologue. This interview made the rounds a while back: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41881619,"author":"jumping_frog","month":"2024-10","story":"What do you visualize while programming?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Imagine not having a voice in your head.","text":"Imagine not having a voice in your head. How are you gonna do rubber duck debugging within your head? [VIDEO]"}]},"-BLgS7m0W94":{"wy":"HN commenters link it when webcam quality comes up, as a quick rundown of why laptop webcams lag behind phones.","q":{"x":"Here's a good video that gives a run down","i":22813788,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22813788,"author":"spuz","month":"2020-04","story":"Tell HN: Toilet paper, hand sanitizer, and now web cams","stance":"rec","why":"Gives a rundown of why laptop webcams lag behind.","quote":"Here's a good video that gives a run down","text":"It's because the standard webcam interface is based on a slow USB 2 interface which can only support enough bandwidth for 720p video. To upgrade your webcam, laptop manufacturers would need to make significant changes to both the interface and the operating system. Add that onto the lack of economic incentive, it's just not worth the cost. Here's a good video that gives a run down: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23390379,"author":"Foivos","month":"2020-06","story":"Seven years later, I bought a new MacBook. For the first time, I don't love it","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as background on laptop webcam limitations generally.","quote":"it is not only Apple's problem","text":"A lot of it has to do with how laptop web cams work in general and it is not only Apple's problem [1]. A way to improve this situation a bit would be to be able to use the camera of an iPhone as a web cam, either over USB or wifi. You still have the compression and USB transfer, but at least you use the better optics of the phone. There are third party tools, such as EpocCam, that can do it, but require the installation of drivers. This should be a first party solution, in the same fashion as the \"take photo\" option from the right click menu on Mac OS, which uses an iPhone to take a photo and paste it directly on the open application of the computer. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22617930,"author":"dijit","month":"2020-03","story":"New iPad Pro with LiDAR Scanner and trackpad support","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why laptop webcams are poor: phones use DMA, laptops use USB.","quote":"phone webcams have DMA, laptop webcams are using USB.","text":"This might shed some light as to why: [VIDEO] tl;dr: phone webcams have DMA, laptop webcams are using USB."}],"r":[["YycAzdtUIko",1]]},"4VKZTmTP7oY":{"wy":"HN cites it as a concrete example of a maker helping save someone's life, and as evidence of Wu's work when she was omitted from press coverage.","m":[{"id":23611501,"author":"zuppy","month":"2020-06","story":"I Am Deleting the Blog","stance":"rec","why":"Positive outcome where she helped save someone's life","quote":"","text":"Wow, i’ve read all her 3 long blog posts about what happened and it angers me so much. I knew Vice was shit, but I had higher expectations from NY Times, Google or Twitter. Here are the other parts: - part 2: [LINK] - part 3: [LINK] There was a positive outcome too, though, check out this video where she helps save someone’s life (SFW): [VIDEO]"},{"id":30098653,"author":"SexyCyborg","month":"2022-01","story":"Naomi Wu video demonetized on YouTube","stance":"reference","why":"Her own example of being omitted from coverage","quote":"I'd fabricated the collars in Shenzhen, mailed the first batch, revised them","text":">But the engineering feats alone will never be the focus as a consequence. There's a line between \"never be the focus\" and \"we will erase you\". This sort of thing is constant- but I'll give you a recent example- NPR recently ran this story: [LINK] About an American lady who needed some parts for her iron lung and how no one would help her. Only the thing is, they knew I already had: [VIDEO] I'd fabricated the collars in Shenzhen, mailed the first batch, revised them and needed someone on the US side to to the final fitting. Martha told them this, a quick search told them this, hundreds of people told them this- did NPR revise the story to correct the omission of \"oh by the way this problem we were talking about was solved by this female engineer\". No of course not. I mean, it's NPR, you think they are going to get smut on their fingers writing about someone who looks like me? Never going to happen. Then the Razer mask story, I was central to it and some outlets gave me credit: [LINK] Others like the Verge flatly refused to mention my involvement- even jumping through some pretty crazy hoops to avoid it: [LINK] Again, hundreds of people called them out. But they did the math and decided that the stigma of mentioning someone who looked like me was worse. That's just the last few months- this has been a constant for years. I fully Open Sourced the best selling 3D printer on the planet, have the first five Open Source Hardware Association certifications granted in China- which pe"},{"id":29015496,"author":"themaninthedark","month":"2021-10","story":"Decades after polio, Martha is among the last to still rely on an iron lung","stance":"reference","why":"Shows her earlier work on collars","quote":"She also made a YouTube video of what she was doing at the time","text":"Twitter is a shifty way to present information and to talk.I looked around and found out that she sent some collars in 2019. She also made a YouTube video of what she was doing at the time [VIDEO] the video shows up if you search 'Martha Iron Lung' so it's not like it is hidden... A 2017 Wired article [LINK] states that Naomi was spearheading the effort then."},{"id":29015941,"author":"junon","month":"2021-10","story":"Decades after polio, Martha is among the last to still rely on an iron lung","stance":"reference","why":"Martha commented on the video","quote":"Martha herself commented on her video","text":"And the Wired article is the first result on Google for \"martha iron lung parts\". Even more, Martha herself commented on her video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29018853,"author":"aeyes","month":"2021-10","story":"Decades after polio, Martha is among the last to still rely on an iron lung","stance":"reference","why":"Pinned comment shows collars worked","quote":"Look at the pinned comment on the original video from 2 years ago","text":"> And the collars didn’t necessarily work either. They were shipped, not installed/tested. Are you sure about that? Look at the pinned comment on the original video from 2 years ago: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qQTjLWIHN74",1]]},"8dT6CR9_6l4":{"wy":"HN points to it as an entertaining analysis of AlphaZero's play in threads about chess engines.","q":{"x":"See GothamChess' very entertaining analysis","i":29277108,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29277108,"author":"ucha","month":"2021-11","story":"Acquisition of chess knowledge in AlphaZero","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining analysis of AlphaZero's uncanny pawn sacrifices against Stockfish.","quote":"See GothamChess' very entertaining analysis","text":"After watching some of the games AlphaZero played against Stockfish, I have to say I don't really agree that the moves make sense to a grandmaster... AlphaZero has this uncanny ability to sacrifice many pawns to restrict the movement of the opponent's pieces, something that you might see in advanced play but nowhere near the extent you see it with AlphaZero. Many more of the moves it plays are hard to make sense of compared to a classic engine such as Stockfish. See GothamChess' very entertaining analysis: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30192373,"author":"anton-107","month":"2022-02","story":"Top Chess Engine Championship","stance":"reference","why":"Related AlphaZero versus Stockfish match coverage.","quote":"","text":"Would be interesting to see AlphaZero in this contest, but I don't think it is open source [1] How AlphaZero Completely CRUSHED Stockfish [VIDEO]"},{"id":27194919,"author":"kohlerm","month":"2021-05","story":"Positions chess engines don't understand","stance":"reference","why":"Example of how far chess engines have progressed in understanding.","quote":"chess engines improved very much lately with regards to \"understanding\" chess positions.","text":"Just look at this [VIDEO] chess engines improved very much lately with regards to \"understanding\" chess positions."}],"r":[["tk862BbjWx4",1]]},"AtK_YsVInw8":{"wy":"Brought up as a comic touchstone in discussions of advertising, usually with a joke or a quick reference.","q":{"x":"George Carlin's skit is helpful here","i":10215641,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10215641,"author":"numair","month":"2015-09","story":"Ad Mocking Burning Man Corp. Influence Is So Accurate That Burning Man Might Sue","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin's skit on advertising as nonsense","quote":"George Carlin's skit is helpful here","text":"George Carlin's skit is helpful here: [VIDEO] It's just another ad. Advertising is mental cancer. Ignore it."},{"id":12806853,"author":"delegate","month":"2016-10","story":"CoinTent: A Sustainable Ad-Free Web","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin as an example of anti-advertising commentary","quote":"Advertising is a very bad thing.","text":"Just because advertising existed before, doesn't mean it has to continue to exist. Advertising is not a law of nature, it is a human invention and should be replaced by a new invention. Advertising is a very bad thing. It's a form of brainwashing in which people are manipulated or provoked to consume way beyond their needs. Its goal is to plant ideas into your head, without you even being aware of it. Curiously, the law of supply and demand in economics doesn't even mention the concept of advertising, yet it plays a huge role in demand creation. This 'inflated' demand provokes inflated supply, which creates even more demand (because everyone wants a popular thing), the feedback loop being limited by the speed of extraction of the natural resources and the capacity of the human body, but that can be augmented by increasing human population. There's too much shit produced in the world, too much waste created, too much useless information on the web and too many shitty websites and ecosystems, which exist solely due to advertising. I don't mind at all if they disappear or reduce in numbers. Good content and good products will find ways to survive. Bill Hicks [LINK] George Carlin [VIDEO]"},{"id":44549040,"author":"latexr","month":"2025-07","story":"Let me pay for Firefox","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> in the land of the free which is not the land of no cost. Maybe it is exactly what that means, and we’ve just been interpreting it wrong all this time. [VIDEO] > Free installation. Free admission, free appraisal, free alterations, free delivery, free estimates, free home trial, and free parking."},{"id":43871983,"author":"SoftTalker","month":"2025-05","story":"Taxes and fees not included: T-Mobile's latest price lock is nearly meaningless","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The whole \"price lock\" concept is a blatant marketing ploy in the first place. As always, \"buyer beware\" and read the fine print. \"Certain exclusions may apply.\" [VIDEO]"}]},"CnbVCNIh1NA":{"wy":"Cited as a leading example of structural editing whenever threads discuss alternatives to text-based code editors.","q":{"x":"Andrew Blinn gave a neat talk[1] pertaining to structured editing at Racketcon","i":24314587,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24076802,"author":"disposedtrolley","month":"2020-08","story":"MicroBlocks: Scratch and Smalltalk-inspired MCU VM for live embedded programming","stance":"rec","why":"Keyboard-driven structure editor keeping code syntactically correct.","quote":"Fructure is an example which looked promising!","text":"I've seen a few attempts at \"structure editors\" which are keyboard driven but limit what you can type so it's always syntactically correct. Fructure is an example which looked promising! [VIDEO]"},{"id":24314587,"author":"loosetypes","month":"2020-08","story":"Hazel: A live functional programming environment featuring typed holes","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on structured editing crediting Hazel.","quote":"Andrew Blinn gave a neat talk[1] pertaining to structured editing at Racketcon","text":"For folks interested in this sort of thing, Andrew Blinn gave a neat talk[1] pertaining to structured editing at Racketcon, which credits Cyrus’ work with Hazel. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28348175,"author":"disconcision","month":"2021-08","story":"Snap is Scheme disguised as Scratch [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter's own racket structured editor.","quote":"my own efforts in this space (a structured editor in racket)","text":"using a lisp here simplifies things from implementation and interaction model perspectives. you already have a simple, canonical AST out of the box, which in-principle suffices to support the entire language, not just a toy version. so it makes sense lisps are attractive targets for getting something rolling in this space, though it's certainly debatable whether it's long-term the best choice. (my own efforts in this space (a structured editor in racket), which i was able to get going as a novice programmer: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":22829161,"author":"spaethnl","month":"2020-04","story":"Simula: A VR window manager for Linux","stance":"reference","why":"Example of structural editing.","quote":"","text":"I'm not sure I agree with the parent, but to give an example for a non-rectangular UI for manipulating (programming) text: Combine structural editing[1] overlaid on some kind of 3d graph that shows how your entire codebase fits together. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21735588,"author":"threatofrain","month":"2019-12","story":"Lisp Programming in Vim with Slimv or Vlime","stance":"reference","why":"Structural editing as alternative to Vim.","quote":"I still have hope for structural editing","text":"In my mind the main reason why Vim still has relevance today is for seat-of-your-pants SSH'ing, which admittedly shouldn't be done but it still is. Outside of that... I still have hope for structural editing but I don't see Vim as the innovator in that space anymore. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21154413,"author":"disconcision","month":"2019-10","story":"How can we develop transformative tools for thought?","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter's own talk on a structured editor.","quote":"I have a talk about it here if anyone is interested","text":"I'm currently working on a structured editor which is at its core a customizable rewriting engine; you specify a grammar, and essentially all editor actions are rewrites on that grammar. My current target grammars are for simple LISPy languages because of the simplicity, but my original (and eventual) goal was/is as a tool for exploratory mathematics. I've always felt mathematical manipulation as more of tactile, synesthetic phenomena than a verbal/linguistic one; I feel like the current state of haptic input is not quite up to reifying this yet, though it is improving very rapidly these days! So I'm trying to tackle some of the software side, and seeing what can be done towards this goal with extant input devices. What I've done is pretty rudimentary so far, but I have a talk about it here if anyone is interested: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21156666,"author":"TuringTest","month":"2019-10","story":"How can we develop transformative tools for thought?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of structure editor.","quote":"","text":"> I still think excel is the leader in this category. Yes, definitely. The \"reactive functional\" programming paradigm of the spreadsheet is radically more understandable for end users than the imperative, as it doesn't require keeping a mental model of state changes in memory. Fortunately, React has popularized this reactive functional paradigm and it is no longer confined to spreadsheets. > I’m thinking the same elements presented in an environment perhaps more like Scratch could work An approach of visual building blocks could help for learning, though Scratch is imperative so it has all the trappings that make a language hard to learn for non-programmers, plus all the inconveniences of editing a visual language (much more cumbersome than editing text). I'd rather have a \"structure editor\"[1] for editing a textual language (like the spreadsheet, but with a tree representation instead of a grid one), which added enhanced visual representations over the base tree. You know, like the IDEs developers use, but with a functional reactive language that mixed code and data, like the spreadsheet does. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["gV5obrYaogU",1]]},"IKqXu-5jw60":{"wy":"HN uses it as an example of Cold War fear and persuasion, with some defending the advice as sensible against blast debris and others mocking it.","q":{"x":"Just getting under a desk or table away from any windows improves your chances of survival.","i":25076774,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":6820350,"author":"fennecfoxen","month":"2013-11","story":"For Nearly Two Decades the Nuclear Launch Code Was 00000000","stance":"rec","why":"Defends duck-and-cover as sensible advice against blast debris and flying glass.","quote":"one of the most misunderstood videos of all time, Bert the Turtle","text":"AND WHILE I'M HERE TALKING ABOUT NUCLEAR WEAPONS SYSTEMS and understandings thereof, allow me to rant about one of the most misunderstood videos of all time, Bert the Turtle (\"duck and cover\") -- [VIDEO] -- Witness the crowds of people saying \"lol but nuclear bomb! you're already dead!\" Well, maybe you're right and you're already dead, but IF YOU'RE NOT DEAD YET then there's going to be a BIG BLAST WAVE OF DEBRIS heading your way IN A MATTER OF SECONDS, so you'd better look for some shelter, pronto. And get away from any windows, too, or you'll end up like those poor people watching the Chelyabinsk meteor, getting LACERATED by flying glass."},{"id":28482646,"author":"fossuser","month":"2021-09","story":"Nuclear War Survival Skills (1987)","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how duck-and-cover made people comfortable with nuclear war.","quote":"See this","text":"That came later iirc - initially they were thinking of using them prior to running infantry in. I think there are old public test videos where they actually did this kind of thing. I also recall a group of scientists trying to elevate the understanding of the e-risk (pugwash conference?). Basically people were becoming too comfortable with the idea of nuclear war because they knew how to “duck and cover” and had a little bomb shelter in the yard. See this: [VIDEO] Dr. Strangelove is also worth watching for a satirical take from that time."},{"id":25076774,"author":"lwneal","month":"2020-11","story":"Nuclear war is unlikely to cause human extinction?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as drill that is laughed at but offers real survival value.","quote":"Just getting under a desk or table away from any windows improves your chances of survival.","text":"Today, the 1950's era \"Duck and Cover\" drills [1] are sometimes laughed at. However, in the event of an actual nuclear blast, some simple preparation would save lives. When a bright flash lights up the sky, people indoors will naturally run to the nearest window to see what happened. This is the wrong thing to do, because the bright flash of an explosion is followed a few seconds later by a pressure wave, turning the window glass into a fast-moving cloud of shrapnel. Just getting under a desk or table away from any windows improves your chances of survival. The advice is useful even if the flash is not caused by a nuclear weapon. Most of the injuries from the Chelyabinsk meteor [2] in 2013 were caused by window shrapnel. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23367575,"author":"jbay808","month":"2020-05","story":"Nukemap","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Cold War public belief that nuclear war was a real possibility.","quote":"","text":"What philosophy do you think drove the production of these videos, if people didn't think nuclear war affecting them and their children was a real possibility? [VIDEO]"},{"id":12725719,"author":"droopybuns","month":"2016-10","story":"Why We're Living in the Age of Fear","stance":"reference","why":"Example of fear used to persuade people.","quote":"","text":"Fear, like Sex, is a foundational human motivator that has been shown to persuade people to buy things. I don't believe there is anything new going on now. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30509432,"author":"hedora","month":"2022-03","story":"Updated rules on preparedness for nuclear explosion","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Duck and cover; still laughable after all these years","text":"Duck and cover; still laughable after all these years: [VIDEO] \"Fallout\" is far more useful than current covid information sites. It's sad how far the US gov't has fallen in 70 years: [LINK] Remember: In case of nuclear blast, stick your head between your legs and kiss your ass goodbye!"},{"id":5205834,"author":"sdoering","month":"2013-02","story":"Run. Hide. Fight.","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it seems as futile as these","text":"This so reminds me of the \"Duck and Cover\" Films ( [VIDEO] ). And imho it seems as futile as these. I will not go down the route, as to discuss the right to own an carry guns... as this is just as futile."}]},"Mq785nJ0FXQ":{"wy":"HN uses it as the canonical link whenever daily fantasy sports comes up.","q":{"x":"this link (John Oliver) needs to be posted eventually when discussing anything DFS related","i":10998687,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":10788988,"author":"akerl_","month":"2015-12","story":"Top fantasy sports player uses software, analytics to reap millions","stance":"rec","why":"John Oliver on whether daily fantasy sports is skill or chance.","quote":"Probably worth checking out","text":"Probably worth checking out [VIDEO] The relevant point being that not everybody believes they're a \"game of skill\" in the \"skill vs. chance\" slider which is relevant to the law. While there's no question that like every game, user actions (skill) can impact the outcome, how much it can impact the outcome is what determines whether something is classified as a game of skill or chance. Perhaps it's more clear if we say \"a game of primarily skill\" or \"primarily chance\". If daily fantasy sports is primarily skill-based, they can do it. If it's primarily chance-based, it's illegal in the US. Which is why they're pushing for it to be referred to as \"skill-based\"."},{"id":26534874,"author":"HenryBemis","month":"2021-03","story":"Fantasy Soccer Keeps My Kids Connected and Happy","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver presented fantasy sports as a gateway to gambling.","quote":"he kinda presented it as a gateway to gambling","text":"Perhaps this is something different, but I remember watching John Oliver - Last Week Tonight some years back and he kinda presented it as a gateway to gambling: [VIDEO] And a video (fake) ad with some nice comedians: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10851849,"author":"trequartista","month":"2016-01","story":"How the Daily Fantasy Sports Industry Turns Fans into Suckers","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver's take on the daily fantasy sports industry.","quote":"Here's John Oliver's take on the daily fantasy sports industry","text":"Here's John Oliver's take on the daily fantasy sports industry - [VIDEO] (probably some NSFW language)"},{"id":10998687,"author":"kriro","month":"2016-01","story":"Payment Processor to Stop Working with Daily Fantasy Sports Clients","stance":"reference","why":"Canonical John Oliver link for any DFS discussion.","quote":"this link (John Oliver) needs to be posted eventually when discussing anything DFS related","text":"Some questionable processors will step in. Stuff will get shut down, money will be frozen in accounts. This is online poker all over again. I think DFS has better chances of survival because they have some strong deals with people that know how to move and shake in Washington (NFL says hi). And of course this link (John Oliver) needs to be posted eventually when discussing anything DFS related: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JEYh5WACqEk",1]]},"R35gWBtLCYg":{"wy":"HN mentions it as a favorite homemade EV pickup, often as an honorable mention in electric truck discussions.","q":{"x":"There's an awesome Tesla pickup truck mod that I'd buy long before the Cybertruck","i":37321020,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37321020,"author":"daisystanton","month":"2023-08","story":"The end of cool small cars","stance":"rec","why":"Homemade Tesla pickup conversion.","quote":"There's an awesome Tesla pickup truck mod that I'd buy long before the Cybertruck","text":"Ughhhhh -- yes, I constantly dream of a pickup truck on a car-size chassis, and often mentally hack off the back of cars on my jogs to imagine what they'd look like as trucks (my version of imagining girls naked ;-)) * The Auzzies had an old version of this: [LINK] * There's an awesome Tesla pickup truck mod that I'd buy long before the Cybertruck: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38231753,"author":"lsllc","month":"2023-11","story":"Canoo's rugged new 'American Bulldog' electric pickup truck","stance":"reference","why":"Homemade Tesla pickup, mentioned among EV trucks.","quote":"honorable mention to Simone Giertz's Truckla","text":"No, but it's nice to see more entrants to the EV truck space. So now we have: * Ford F-150 Lightning * Tesla Cybertruck * Rivian R1T * Canoo American Bulldog Of course honorable mention to Simone Giertz's Truckla: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29635724,"author":"bellyfullofbac","month":"2021-12","story":"Homebuilt pickups: How underage teens skirt the law to drive in Sweden (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Suddenly the Truckla (Tesla pickup) makes more sense","text":"Suddenly the Truckla (Tesla pickup) makes more sense: [VIDEO] - the inventor is a Swede living in the US."}]},"TMoz3gSXBcY":{"wy":"HN points to it as a takedown of AI-assisted amateur physics, though a few commenters push back on it.","q":{"x":"are you another person who would benefit from watching Angela Collier's vibe physics video?","i":45119518,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45119518,"author":"southwindcg","month":"2025-09","story":"I asked ChatGPT a bunch of questions came up with solution Yang Mills Mass Gap","stance":"rec","why":"Angela Collier on non-experts doing physics with chatbots","quote":"are you another person who would benefit from watching Angela Collier's vibe physics video?","text":"Do you have a background in math and physics, or are you another person who would benefit from watching Angela Collier's vibe physics video? [VIDEO]"},{"id":49163818,"author":"DaiPlusPlus","month":"2026-08","story":"LLMs reward expertise","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Careful now","text":"> I have Claude break down high level physics \"as if I'm a farmer standing in a field\" - works beautifully. Careful now: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44831563,"author":"mattgreenrocks","month":"2025-08","story":"GPT-5","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is an excellent takedown of it","text":"He doesn’t. I think it’s the same mental phenomena that Gell-Mann Amnesia works off of. That interview is practically radioactive levels of cringe for several reasons. This is an excellent takedown of it: [VIDEO]"}]},"X36ye-1x_HQ":{"wy":"HN points to it as a demonstration of type-directed editing with typed holes, which some see as a likely future of programming.","q":{"x":"Something like that is likely the future of programming!","i":34217732,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34217732,"author":"still_grokking","month":"2023-01","story":"C++ at the End of 2022","stance":"rec","why":"Example of IDE-assisted type-driven programming as the future.","quote":"Something like that is likely the future of programming!","text":"> Basically, your IDE writes more of the code for you. I think this was, in general, a dead end in language research for various reasons, […] I think language researchers would strongly disagree. Something like that is likely the future of programming! For example: [VIDEO] From the video description: > In Idris, types are a first class language construct, meaning that they can be manipulated and computed like any other language construct. It encourages a type-driven style of development, in which programmers give types first and use interactive editing tools to derive programs."},{"id":42014167,"author":"dietr1ch","month":"2024-11","story":"Hazel: A live functional programming environment featuring typed holes","stance":"reference","why":"Shows type-driven development with holes in Idris.","quote":"Brady has a presentation about Idris where he shows Type-Driven development","text":"Brady has a presentation about Idris where he shows Type-Driven development (where you write code that could typecheck with some holes and you get the compiler to help you figure out the missing types for your \"whatever/something\" untyped variables) [VIDEO]"},{"id":33569217,"author":"nequo","month":"2022-11","story":"Type-Signature.com","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of interactive editing with typed holes.","quote":"","text":"Thinking in types and type relations sounds to me like Rule 5 taken to its logical conclusion. Types are your data structures, and they guide how you write your functions. Agda[1] and Idris[2] turn this idea into interactive editing with typed holes. You put a \"?\" in the code and the compiler fills the hole in for you based on the type that it infers for it. Haskell has a less expressive type system but it also has typed holes and Wingman[3] extends the Haskell Language Server to use them for interactive editing. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":20304559,"author":"mrgriffin","month":"2019-06","story":"Type inference has usability problems","stance":"reference","why":"Language creator derives zipWith without writing code.","quote":"Here's the creator of the language deriving the implementation of zipWith without writing any code","text":"Not OP, but my understanding is that it's a common approach in Idris. Here's the creator of the language deriving the implementation of zipWith without writing any code: [VIDEO] Some other examples include: - Haskell: [LINK] - Scala: [LINK] - Agda: [LINK] I was under the impression that justDoIt comes from Scala, but I can't find a reference for that."},{"id":23141456,"author":"vlaaad","month":"2020-05","story":"In Further Praise of Dependent Types","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I'm already having troubles following it after 8 lines of type definitions","text":"Damn, this area of programming sounds very interesting, but I'm so so lost in these type compositions... Is it this Idris video you are talking about? [VIDEO] I'm already having troubles following it after 8 lines of type definitions, while matrix transposition in the language of my choice is just `#(apply mapv vector %)` Sorry, I'm not trying to be dismissive, I just want the ergonomics of expressing my intentions to the computer be free of all this boilerplate while leaving compiler a possibility to give me some useful feedback when something might be wrong, are we really not there yet or are there languages aiming to make dependent types practical? You mentioned Coq, but I'm still not being able to find any code examples or some getting started guide in its official documentation..."}],"r":[["mOtKD7ml0NU",1]]},"ebeNeQFUMa0":{"wy":"HN cites it as proof that some octopuses really do crawl out of the water to hunt.","q":{"x":"Sir David Attenborough discusses the octopus here","i":36753196,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":18581585,"author":"nikofeyn","month":"2018-12","story":"The Octopus Is Smart as Heck, But Why?","stance":"rec","why":"Australian octopus crawling on land to hunt","quote":"it's fascinating.","text":"this is something a certain species in Australia does, as covered in the bbc's the hunt . [VIDEO] it's fascinating. they also filmed this in blue planet ii. [LINK] that clip unfortunately cuts off the first part where the shark caught the octopus, but the octopus put its arms in the sharks gills so it couldn't breathe and got away."},{"id":42111181,"author":"mncharity","month":"2024-11","story":"The Silurian Hypothesis","stance":"reference","why":"Amphibious octopuses as an example","quote":"Amphibious octopuses?[1]","text":"> experimental chemistry and physics would be harder to pull off underwater Amphibious octopuses?[1] In the widespread and connected shallow seas of a supercontinent world? [1] BBC Earth [VIDEO]"},{"id":36752555,"author":"mr_toad","month":"2023-07","story":"The Pacific Northwest Tree Octopus","stance":"reference","why":"Octopus species that crawls out of water to hunt","quote":"There is a species of octopus that does crawl out of the water to hunt","text":"There is a species of octopus that does crawl out of the water to hunt (Australian, of course), but they don’t climb trees. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36753196,"author":"PepperdineG","month":"2023-07","story":"The Pacific Northwest Tree Octopus","stance":"reference","why":"Abdopus aculeatus hunts crabs on land, narrated by Attenborough","quote":"Sir David Attenborough discusses the octopus here","text":">Octopuses are very much a water-only type of organism. They're not though. Octopi generally don't like being out of water, but they're capable of traveling on land short distances and in fact the Abdopus Aculeatus octopus regularly goes on land by choice in order to hunt crabs in different tidepools. Sir David Attenborough discusses the octopus here, which this octopus was one of the animals featured in The Hunt documentary: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["U5kIdtMJGc8",1]]},"fTTno8D-b2E":{"wy":"HN uses it as the standard example of sneakernet media distribution via hard drives.","q":{"x":"I'd recommend watching the video that is linked","i":12322346,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12322346,"author":"placeybordeaux","month":"2016-08","story":"The Cuban CDN","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how Cuba's weekly packet distributes media offline via flown-in drives","quote":"I'd recommend watching the video that is linked","text":"I'd recommend watching the video that is linked: [VIDEO] It talks about the mechanics, largely it looks like someone in Miami is consistently collecting stuff until they get close to 1TB or a week has passed, then that 1TB is flown out to Havana and it's copied and passed around after that."},{"id":48999916,"author":"movedx","month":"2026-07","story":"'VPNs are lawful technical tools,' says EU Court in landmark copyright ruling","stance":"reference","why":"Example of HDD-based content distribution in Cuba","quote":"Like they do in Cuba","text":"This is hard to read, but I think you're saying the Internet will see a shift to \"Sneaker Net\" like tactics where people torrent stuff, store it on HDDs, and just move those HDDs around? Like they do in Cuba: [VIDEO] For the most part, I agree."},{"id":35753874,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2023-04","story":"Internet in a Box","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how Cubans consume content without internet","quote":"This is how people consume content in Cuba","text":"This is how people consume content in Cuba [VIDEO] If you like this story, I recommend this documentary: [LINK] (Chuck Norris vs Communism)"}]},"frAEmhqdLFs":{"wy":"Mostly quoted as a joke in nuclear war and doom threads, with one commenter praising Lehrer's satire.","q":{"x":"Tom Lehrer is indeed a national treasure, and his satire is top shelf.","i":23224338,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23224338,"author":"Diederich","month":"2020-05","story":"Google deletes “communist bandits” from comments on Youtube","stance":"rec","why":"Gallows-humor Lehrer satire song","quote":"Tom Lehrer is indeed a national treasure, and his satire is top shelf.","text":"For those who aren't aware and/or won't look this reference: [LINK] As the other comenter noted, Tom Lehrer is indeed a national treasure, and his satire is top shelf. From the light-hearted \"The Elements\": [LINK] (watch for the surprise at the end) To the gallows comedy of \"We Will All Go Together When We Go\": [VIDEO] NB: I appreciate the irony linking to YouTube content in a 'WTF Google/YouTube' thread."},{"id":33273626,"author":"imagine99","month":"2022-10","story":"Nowhere to hide: How a nuclear war would kill you–and almost everyone else","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"here is a rousing, uplifting song which is guaranteed to cheer you up","text":"Adopting an attitude similar to Tom Lehrer in \"We will all go together when we go\" [1] may provide some peace of mind in this case. In any event, here is a rousing, uplifting song which is guaranteed to cheer you up: [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30509297,"author":"bobsmooth","month":"2022-03","story":"Updated rules on preparedness for nuclear explosion","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Either we all die or we don't[1], either way there's nothing you can do.","text":"Either we all die or we don't[1], either way there's nothing you can do. I'm sure there's things you can be worried about that you can actually affect. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30501047,"author":"catchclose8919","month":"2022-02","story":"Bank of China's Singapore operation stops financing Russian oil trades","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> why write like this? This comment makes no sense to me > transmission-poor You're insulting their communication skill, but some (I'd say possibly many) people actually enjoy reading/listening to this kind of communication . I actually really enjoyed it and found it funny and witty, and satisfying especially because it made me google for ~30sec to find out about Thucydides and Melos to be able to decode \"old Melian\", and then more to solve the puzzle of the \"Piper\" - although native Enlish speakers are more familiar with the The Pied Piper of Hamelin I'd guess or at least with the \"pay the piper\" expression. I actually find regular low-density conversations excruciatingly boring so tuning the density way up and sprinkling some puzzles in there improves my ability to focus and not tune out as I do in many conversations . It's a form of communications that helps AHDHed-techno-creatives like us follow and enjoy conversation and reading more actually, not transmission-poor but on the contrary more like transmission-enhanced , but probably only for a subset of people with brains wired a particular way. (Now that I think about it, even the sci-fi literature that I enjoy reading, eg. Charlie Stross, Vernor Vinge is a bit like this comment, although way more diluted probably to appeal to a greater audience... and yeah, I guess conspiracy theories are the lower end trash version of \"sci-fi appealing to ADHDed-techno-creatives\", so it kind of makes sense.) ...come to think about it, I"},{"id":21039128,"author":"acqq","month":"2019-09","story":"Soviet Officer Stanislav Petrov, 'The Man Who Saved the World' (2017)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"On an uplifting note, we will all go together when we go","text":"On an uplifting note, we will all go together when we go (this year is the 60th anniversary of the song recording): [VIDEO]"},{"id":13464700,"author":"Pica_soO","month":"2017-01","story":"Silicon Valley billionaires are ‘prepping’ to survive in underground bunkers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ah, the illusion that one can separate oneself from you human society and survive indefinitely. We had the biosphere, we had the sovjet cold war era bunkers, and under tests- the results where always the same. Insufficiently prepared, longterm psychological effects not calculated in - the buried alive envying the dead on the surface. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HI09kat_GeI",1],["kTKn1aSOyOs",1],["AcS3NOQnsQM",1],["ZXtimhT-ff4",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["l7rce6IQDWs",1]]},"isgu-VrD0oM":{"wy":"Used as background in nuclear energy threads for its account of French policy and the economics of the fleet.","q":{"x":"I would really recommend listening to discussion with Mark Nelson","i":47478463,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47478463,"author":"leonidasrup","month":"2026-03","story":"Italy, Belgium set to lose gas supply after biggest LNG plant bombed","stance":"rec","why":"Discussion with Mark Nelson on state of French nuclear industry","quote":"I would really recommend listening to discussion with Mark Nelson","text":"I would really recommend listening to discussion with Mark Nelson, managing director of Radiant Energy Group, about the state of the French nuclear power industry. \"Something's Rotten with French Nuclear\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":45259447,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2025-09","story":"EU court rules nuclear energy is clean energy","stance":"reference","why":"Long look at French nuclear site selection and policy history","quote":"Here's a long look","text":"I never disputed that it's a fact that China currently builds more renewables than nuclear. I said it is irrelevant . Those are different things. It's also not \"way\" more...unless you don't understand the irrelevance of nameplate capacity with intermittent renewables. China is also currently seeing the bottom drop out of their renewables industry, with over a third of the workforce laid off and massive drops in installs and production due to a reduction in subsidies. The EPR2 projects could not even have started in 2022, because he law that prohibits increasing nuclear capacity beyond the currently installed 63.2GW was only repealed in March 2023. And yes, reversing course so massively takes a little while, particularly when they still have to deal with a lot of the fallout of the failed \"soft exit\" policy. As to site selection: you disputed, I showed. Then you change the subject. The interviewee was the president of the French parliament, and he is quite specific. And he is not the only source, this is really well known...unless you bury your head in the sand. Here's a long look: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45259992,"author":"natmaka","month":"2025-09","story":"EU court rules nuclear energy is clean energy","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> China currently builds more renewables than nuclear. I said it is irrelevant. Those are different things No: nuclear and renewables are electricity-generating equipment types, and all the debate is about the proportion of renewables and nuclear in the final system. Seeing them as disconnected (in different universes) is not even funny. [LINK] > unless you don't understand the irrelevance of nameplate capacity with intermittent renewables. This perspective dates back a time when transporting electricity was expensive (lines, losses...), storing it also was expensive ( ), fossil fuels and nuclear were the only way to obtain gridpower... all this is obsolete. Explanations: [LINK] > China is also currently seeing the bottom drop out of their renewables industry Source? (I lived in China from mid-2017 to mid-2025) The renewables industry there is, as in nearly every nation, in much better state than nearly any other one. > The EPR2 projects could not even have started in 2022, because he law that prohibits increasing nuclear capacity beyond the currently installed 63.2GW was only repealed in March 2023 Nope. This law stated about active production capacity, and never forbade any reactor-building project. The very first EPR (Flamanville-3) project was running while this law was instated (2015) and did not stop. It simply forbade it to start without other reactor with at least a total equivalent powerplate value to be shutdown. Recent news: [LINK] > they still have to deal with a "},{"id":41604871,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2024-09","story":"Three Mile Island nuclear plant restart in Microsoft AI power deal","stance":"reference","why":"Background on France's law limiting nuclear capacity","quote":"Until March 2023, there was a law in France that made it illegal to increase nuclear capacity in absolute terms","text":"Yep. That was a one-time occurrence, due to (a) inspections being deferred during COVID and (b) France's long time catastrophic underinvestment in their nuclear fleet. Until March 2023, there was a law in France that made it illegal to increase nuclear capacity in absolute terms and mandated reduction to under 50% in relative terms. [VIDEO] But again, a one-time event, and it doesn't change the high capacity factors world-wide (over 80%) and the extremely high capacity factors in reasonably well run fleets ( > 90% in the US, and also inGermany when they still had nuclear )."},{"id":33496156,"author":"evilos","month":"2022-11","story":"EU electricity production by country and source","stance":"reference","why":"Mark Nelson explains how energy laws make French nuclear unprofitable","quote":"Mark Nelson talks about theses problems in this interview","text":"Essentially these plants are forced to be unprofitable via energy laws. For example, the nuclear plants are forced to sell their energy to energy traders at unprofitable low rates but those traders are never required to buy the energy and if the price of energy drops below this rate (AFTER it was bought) then the traders can just \"return\" the energy and the operators have to eat it. This happened during covid when energy prices collapsed due to lack of demand. These middlemen profit off of cheap nuclear energy instead of rate payers. In addition, the nuclear plants must pay a 23 euro per MWh tax that subsidizes the operation of renewable energy. Since the plants are forced to sell their energy below cost and subsidize renewable generation, they are not profitable and aren't given the proper funds to maintain/improve these plants. So then these plants look bad/expensive and the ministers in charge demand that these plants are closed down before the end of their life. Even though the upfront costs have already been paid and the electricity they make are far cheaper than renewable generation if you don't force these crazy disadvantages on them. Mark Nelson talks about theses problems in this interview: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bHIhgxav9LY",1],["bHJyoqDO0zw",1],["oI_X2cMHNe0",1]]},"kFT54hO1X8M":{"wy":"Recommended, including by Chris Lattner, as a history of CPU architecture and why performance gains slowed.","q":{"x":"The other talk Dr. Chris Lattner recommends watching is this one, and is very interesting","i":29586845,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29586845,"author":"mwattsun","month":"2021-12","story":"The Golden Age of Compiler Design in an Era of HW/SW Co-Design [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended by Chris Lattner; history of CPU architecture","quote":"The other talk Dr. Chris Lattner recommends watching is this one, and is very interesting","text":"The other talk Dr. Chris Lattner recommends watching is this one, and is very interesting David Patterson - A New Golden Age for Computer Architecture: History, Challenges and Opportunities [VIDEO] I didn't realize IBM would have went with the Motorola 68000 for the IBM personal computer if Motorola had been ready. That would have been awesome for me because I could have applied my Mac 68000 asm skills to the much larger IBM PC market."},{"id":35297692,"author":"panick21_","month":"2023-03","story":"Gordon Moore, Intel Co-Founder, Dies at 94","stance":"reference","why":"Patterson's perspective that Moore's law has ended","quote":"David Patterson perspective","text":"Actually Moores law is dead. His prediction was not transistors would get smaller. His prediction was initial that for 10 years was that density would double every year. And then later that it would double every 18 month. And this held longer then even he thought. But it has ended. While things still get denser, its no longer on that same trend. This interview with Moore explains it: [LINK] History by Cantrill: [LINK] David Patterson perspective: [VIDEO] Opposing point of view Jim Keller: [LINK] Patterson response to Keller: [LINK] There are talks by David Patterson, Jim Keller and"},{"id":31075374,"author":"leoc","month":"2022-04","story":"A New Golden Age for Computer Architecture (2020) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"As I've said before, I just find it hard to believe that the very foreseeable problems lurking at the intersection of speculation, cache and protected mode were really such an unexpected shock to the industry [VIDEO] . I have a jaded suspicion that in fact a fair few people had some idea, but they said nothing and didn't look closely because it would have been no fun, and in some cases not career-enhancing, to be the Jeremiah calling for major CPU deoptimisations. OTOH the very security-researcher heroes whose reputation is built on collecting trophies didn't seem to say much either, so maybe everyone really was completely blindsided?"}],"r":[["nWTvXbQHwWs",1],["yCd3CzGSte8",1],["WXuK6gekU1Y",1],["mZyvIHYn2zk",1],["5AqbCQuK0gM",1]]},"nAGHUkby2Is":{"wy":"Cited in ecology threads as background on how overgrazing prevents Scottish forests from regenerating.","q":{"x":"England and Scotland lack trees specifically because overgrazing prevented forests from regenerating after being cut for timber","i":32119118,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":27809993,"author":"iorek_dev","month":"2021-07","story":"Organic and regenerative agriculture are revitalizing rural Montana economies","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on restoring the Caledonian Forest, among sources on soil and nature balance.","quote":"Talk on the Caledonian Forest","text":"For those who are interested in the topic of soil reconstruction and balance in nature, some more sources: Documentary on the Loess plateau in China: [LINK] Talk on the Caledonian Forest: [VIDEO] Talk on using the \"herd effect\" in Africa: [LINK] A talk presenting a realistic view on soil reconstruction, what IMO is the best argument to convince farmers: [LINK]"},{"id":32119118,"author":"nerdponx","month":"2022-07","story":"The Great Green Wall","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Scotland lacking trees due to overgrazing preventing regeneration.","quote":"England and Scotland lack trees specifically because overgrazing prevented forests from regenerating after being cut for timber","text":"> the amount of grazing required for that is absolutely larger than anything we have today Who's to say that there wasn't more grazing (by area) back then? > Further, grazing makes fields more robust if done property. I'm not a farmer, but I know that this depends a lot on what is doing the grazing. Sheep for example apparently graze the grass down really far, and prevent the growth of bushes and tree saplings that are otherwise important for balancing the ecosystem. In the US in the 1800s, there was outright armed conflict between sheep and cattle ranchers over this issue: [LINK] And as far as I know, England and Scotland lack trees specifically because overgrazing prevented forests from regenerating after being cut for timber: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23470461,"author":"Litost","month":"2020-06","story":"Britain has gone two months without burning coal to generate power","stance":"reference","why":"Ted talk on restoring Scotland's Caledonian forest by protecting land from overgrazing.","quote":"related Ted Talk here","text":"\"To be fair much of Scotland used to be covered in forestry and has been denuded. There are some interesting projects showing what happens if the land is simply protected from over grazing (particularly by deer).\" Interesting this popped up, as I'm studying an Ecosystem Restoration Design Course and the first week was studying exactly this, related Ted Talk here - [VIDEO] But yes, overgrazing is the main culprit there (and also in a lot of other places that have suffered ecosystem collapse)."},{"id":23170025,"author":"floatrock","month":"2020-05","story":"Moths have 'secret role' as crucial pollinators","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Keystone species are fascinating and show how we really need more systemic thinking. The one that tons of people have seen is how reintroducing wolves into Yellowstone changed the course of rivers, made them less eroding, and generally increased ecosystem diversity: [LINK] Beavers are another one: [LINK] Remember how we learned a lot of westward expansion in US colonial days was led by mountainmen trapping beavers to use their pelts in fancy european hats and we kept going further west because we kept killing off the beavers while demand for fancy hats kept going up? Turns out when you remove all those beaver ponds that would slow down water flow, you end up with a lot more erosion, incision, and flooding -- the landscape we have now. Fisherman think beaver ponds decrease fish populations because they tend to have warmer water, but this doesn't quite pass the thousands-years-of-coevolution-before-beaver-extermination sniff test... some experiments have showed that beaver ponds actually increase fish stock because they create lots of fish nurseries, and trout and salmon can instinctively swim up through beaver dams. Then when you consider how things like aquaponics incorporate fish because fish poop makes great fertilizer, it starts to become clear how important beaver ponds can be to nutrient cycling in these ecosystems. One of the most fascinating fish-based nutrient flows is salmon migrations... they're hatched upstream, put on most of their growth and biomass in the ocean,"},{"id":18230622,"author":"detritus","month":"2018-10","story":"Alarming study shows massive insect loss","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on restoring ancient Caledonian Forest.","quote":"this might be of some peripheral interest...","text":"Given your location, this might be of some peripheral interest... \"Restoring the ancient Caledonian Forest Alan Watson Featherstone TEDxFindhorn\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["q7pI7IYaJLI",1],["uUmIdq0D6-A",1]]},"outcGtbnMuQ":{"wy":"HN refers back to it as the primary source for what GPT-4 could do at launch, especially the sketch-to-website and multimodal demos.","q":{"x":"the TaxGPT example in the GPT-4 Developer Livestream [1] was powerful","i":35183571,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35183571,"author":"CrypticShift","month":"2023-03","story":"Microsoft 365 Copilot – your copilot for work","stance":"rec","why":"TaxGPT and coding examples in the livestream","quote":"the TaxGPT example in the GPT-4 Developer Livestream [1] was powerful","text":"the TaxGPT example in the GPT-4 Developer Livestream [1] was powerful. I'm hoping (soon?) for a Microsoft 365 assistant that can do the same trick on ALL your MS cloud data (docs/files/mails/notes...) Edit : the included \"Business Chat\" goes in the same direction (on what they call the Microsoft Graph) Also, there were interesting \"coding\" examples in the same livestream. So the coming GPT-4 (and 5) integration in the Power Platform [2] could be a game-changer for less technical staff. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":42847322,"author":"tkgally","month":"2025-01","story":"I trusted an LLM, now I'm on day 4 of an afternoon project","stance":"reference","why":"Brockman's coding demo of GPT-4","quote":"Greg Brockman’s demonstration of using it to write code","text":"Minor correction: GPT-4 was announced on March 14, 2023, less than two years ago. I don’t remember how much LLMs had been discussed as coding assistants before then, but it was Greg Brockman’s demonstration of using it to write code that first brought that capability to my attention: [VIDEO] Advances since then have indeed been remarkable."},{"id":37643089,"author":"Tenoke","month":"2023-09","story":"ChatGPT can now see, hear, and speak – openai.com","stance":"reference","why":"Shows OpenAI demoed multimodal capabilities before release","quote":"they showed the multimodal capabilities back in the GPT4 Developer Livestream","text":">I wouldn't say that OpenAI \"telegraphed\" their intentions They did telegraph it, they showed the multimodal capabilities back in the GPT4 Developer Livestream[0] right before first releasing it. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37680508,"author":"skilled","month":"2023-09","story":"GPT-4 generates simple app from Whiteboard photo","stance":"reference","why":"Whiteboard-to-app feature was already shown in the livestream","quote":"it was already revealed during the GPT-4 livestream","text":"Not sure this is that interesting since it was already revealed during the GPT-4 livestream back[0] in March. It’s my understanding that this feature is still rolling out to everyone, so if there are some use cases beyond “simple” - I am sure we’ll hear about it in a more detailed article / blog post. Not taking anything away, just pointing out the facts. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35842483,"author":"ZephyrBlu","month":"2023-05","story":"It looks like GPT-4-32k is rolling out","stance":"reference","why":"Dates the GPT-4 livestream to March 14th","quote":"The developer livestream was on March 14th","text":"I completely agree, I feel the same way as a dev. GPT-4 is not even 2 months old . The developer livestream was on March 14th: [VIDEO] . The time since GPT-4 already feels something like 6 months. So far I'm perpetually feeling behind."},{"id":35160839,"author":"jonas21","month":"2023-03","story":"GPT-4","stance":"reference","why":"Demos of coding and sketch-to-website cited as evidence of intelligence","quote":"creating a website from a crude sketch","text":"If passing the bar exam, writing code alongside a human and incorporating their feedback [1], or creating a website from a crude sketch [2] aren't akin to human intelligence, then what is? At some point, you have to go from saying it's just a parlor trick to considering that maybe we're just a parlor trick as well. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35162680,"author":"rvnx","month":"2023-03","story":"GPT4 is up to 6 times more expensive than GPT3.5","stance":"reference","why":"Timecode of Be My Eyes mention in announcement","quote":"","text":"Maybe it's something else ? I am genuinely not sure what they mean, this is the timecode in the announcement: [VIDEO] It seems to say that they use Be my eyes \"to make the product better\", so it could be humans in the loop during captioning, or maybe they are using volunteers data from Be my eyes as training set ? Or it's completely opposite, and that Be my eyes is using the OpenAI APIs ? My guess is that it is both ?"},{"id":35268579,"author":"lonelyasacloud","month":"2023-03","story":"Robots Have Been About to Take All the Jobs for 100 Years","stance":"reference","why":"Demo turning a hand-drawn sketch into a working web page","quote":"it is shown being fed a photograph of a hand-drawn web page","text":"In one of GPT4's release demos, it is shown being fed a photograph of a hand-drawn web page. It then image analyses that, works out that it's a picture of a web page and turns it into a working web page ( [VIDEO] ). So here's the thing; what in that list of non-codegen tasks is more complicated than that which GPT can already automate?"}]},"qWJpI2adCcs":{"wy":"HN cites it for Acton's common-sense views on building engines, valuing curiosity and self-driven learning.","q":{"x":"Mike Acton has some pretty common sense views on this","i":19123379,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19123379,"author":"petermcneeley","month":"2019-02","story":"Create your own game engine but don't use it (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Common-sense view on building your own game engine in context.","quote":"Mike Acton has some pretty common sense views on this","text":"Mike Acton has some pretty common sense views on this: [VIDEO] Basically if your making your own game engine then use the context in which you are working. If you are going to end up with something generic like Unity maybe you should just use something like Unity from the start."},{"id":41865318,"author":"iamcreasy","month":"2024-10","story":"Try to fix it one level deeper","stance":"reference","why":"Acton says curiosity best predicts a good hire.","quote":"He said curiosity is the best indicator if a candidate will be a good hire.","text":"Mike Acton said the same thing in an interview. He said curiosity is the best indicator if a candidate will be a good hire. Casey Muratori was interviewing him at HandmadeHero Con back in 2016. Here is the snippet: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34968089,"author":"drainyard","month":"2023-02","story":"“Clean” Code, Horrible Performance","stance":"reference","why":"Casey interviews Mike Acton, showing who he is.","quote":"This video from 2015 where Casey is interviewing Mike Acton","text":"You could spend 2 minutes looking up who you are talking about. This video from 2015 where Casey is interviewing Mike Acton: [VIDEO] Also this course where he specifically talks about Numpy and pure Python: [LINK]"},{"id":14273464,"author":"zengid","month":"2017-05","story":"Ask HN: Is it a waste of time to teach yourself data science without a degree?","stance":"reference","why":"Mike Acton says he values self-driven learning over schooling.","quote":"I don't care what you learned in school.. I care about what you learned of your own volition","text":"As Mike Acton (Data Oriented Design Guru) once said in an interview \"I don't care what you learned in school.. I care about what you learned of your own volition\" [paraphrased from 1]. It never hurts to learn new things. Another HN poster suggested this channel for beefing up on linear algebra, and I absolutely love it [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["MmG2ah5Df4g",1],["nM9f0W2KD5s",1]]},"tWZOEFvczzA":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of what a very small crew can make with generative AI tools, while noting how much manual work it still takes.","q":{"x":"Corridor crew did a second video which is a lot better but still a lot of work.","i":38981745,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":37142433,"author":"etra0","month":"2023-08","story":"Opendream: A layer-based UI for Stable Diffusion","stance":"rec","why":"Anime made with AI techniques, plus explainer videos","quote":"Quite interesting if you ask me!","text":"Corridor Crew did a some sort of anime using this technique [1] and then they did two videos [2, 3] explaining the technology behind. Quite interesting if you ask me! There still are some issues with the eyes and a bit of flickering but at the speed everything is moving I wouldn't be surprised if this improves in a year or two. Needless to say, there's still a lot of artistry involved in such a process so anything is yet to be completely automated. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":39709086,"author":"roguas","month":"2024-03","story":"Devin: AI Software Engineer","stance":"reference","why":"Anime made by a very small crew using AI tools","quote":"anime done with very small crew","text":"> Just stranded without dignity or purpose, Perhaps dignity is seeking value that is scalable to entire humanity, hm? Isn't it dignified? If we cannot find value for fellow humans we can relax I guess? > We have outsourced art to computers, so people who don't understand art Ok, I am seeing a lot of bad faith arguments. Just because somebody doesn't have time to learn to craft and cruft of some very tedious discipline, doesn't really mean they don't understand it. You don't need to throw rocks all day to get an understanding of physics. But people had to do it in the past, now we calculate, simulate and then just do one throw. Also I am speaking as part time designer/artist. New AI tools are great, I could achieve similar results, but difficult bets were made, when i started. Now I just roll in images, some of them finetuned on my work, some are mix, some are completely nuanced. Many artists after initial sobbing(I was enthusiastic from getgo) started to learn and explore this tools. People bring amazing things to life, that just were impossible with their limited resources (take a look, anime done with very small crew [VIDEO] ). I much dislike the mindset of \"they took our jobs\"."},{"id":38981745,"author":"cdcox","month":"2024-01","story":"Midlibrary: Library of 4000 Midjourney artistic styles","stance":"reference","why":"Example of AI-assisted animation requiring much work","quote":"Corridor crew did a second video which is a lot better but still a lot of work.","text":"Linkin Park used it in 4 videos recently and seemed to use a combination of img2img and leaning into the glitchy style. [0] (These are 9-11 mo old so using much older models and techniques) Peter Gabriel used it extensively in some recent music videos. The artists behind it also leaned into the glitchy style but are probably using some pre trained stuff to keep style.[1] A writer/creative named Austin McConnell used AI art to make a 50 minute anime short to help market a book he wrote using AI [2]. Not sure how he kept consistency but this video got some flak and he has another video addressing his techniques. Corridor crew did a second video which is a lot better but still a lot of work. [3] I think a lot of projects using it are still kind of in the spec stage and are usually using a combination of loras, clip, generating multiple angles, and aggressive use of img2img and controlnets. And a few companies (Scenario [4] etc) are working on consistency. I think you won't see a ton of big projects using it yet because the tech is still early days and the early versions were really a bear to work with. A lot of people on YouTube are using it like theyd use stock art (YouTube thumbnails, backgrounds, ads, or story boards for writing focused/story YouTubes). There is still a stigma so a lot of people using it aren't announcing their use broadly, so I've found I usually have to stumble on their projects. Also, a lot of major companies aren't using it for that reason. I know Wizards"}]},"ug-CyGXMabg":{"wy":"HN uses it to show what the Nintendo PlayStation prototype hardware actually looked like, essentially an SNES with a CD-ROM drive.","q":{"x":"The first part of the video[1] is also worth watching.","i":12155830,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12155830,"author":"mschwaig","month":"2016-07","story":"Repairing the only known prototype of Nintendo PlayStation [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Teardown of the Nintendo PlayStation prototype with the owner's backstory.","quote":"The first part of the video[1] is also worth watching.","text":"The first part of the video[1] is also worth watching. It has the current owner explaining how he got it and some careful investigation of the device before the actual repair. Ben Heck has some impressive videos in general, watching him work always makes me wish I was better at electronics. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31648283,"author":"qbasic_forever","month":"2022-06","story":"Sega announces a tiny Sega CD retro console","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the Nintendo PlayStation prototype concept system.","quote":"The concept system actually materialized and was pretty underwhelming","text":"The concept system actually materialized and was pretty underwhelming: [VIDEO] The big issue is that most of those early consoles were designed with the expectation that games were on ROM chips directly connected to the CPU bus and mapped into memory so you wouldn't have to load all the assets in RAM (which was miniscule, a few dozen or low hundred kilobytes at most). If you just bolted a CD-ROM onto that architecture it can't be mapped into memory so now you have to cache things in RAM or architect your game to allow for seeking and getting them on demand. Sony did take all the learnings from the SNES CD-ROM and used it to design the Playstation hardware. They added 2 megabytes of RAM, a huge increase over the SNES, which worked much better with games on CD-ROM."},{"id":13389433,"author":"codesushi42","month":"2017-01","story":"Nintendo Switch","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the Nintendo PlayStation prototype hardware.","quote":"The Nintendo Playstation is not much more than an SNES with a CD-ROM drive and some extra memory","text":"He is completely wrong and must be confusing PS1 with the unreleased add-on, which would make more sense. The Nintendo Playstation is not much more than an SNES with a CD-ROM drive and some extra memory: [VIDEO] He is probably too young to have grown up with a PS1. I believe reading at the time that Sony poured close to $1bn into developing the PS1 hardware. This made sense-- the Sony brand then was what Apple is now, and this was their first full foray into the games industry. If you compare Star Fox running at 15fps on a SuperFX chip w/ untextured polys to Gran Turismo 2 running at 30fps, you will see why OP is spewing clueless garbage. The PS1 was a monster for its time. It blew away the hardware of Saturn and 3DO, when it came to 3D at least. 3D acceleration was new, and even PC gamers were jealous of what the PS1 had to offer at launch. And arguably it was not even dethroned by the N64 a year later. Get off my lawn."}]},"-F-IHvF5OCA":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the funny reference whenever someone gives an impenetrable technical presentation.","m":[{"id":38425929,"author":"qwertox","month":"2023-11","story":"Rockwell Retro Encabulator [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I thought you were referring to the scene of the presentation","text":"I was about to post this, it's excellent. I also like the depressing tone of the series, a bit like Mr. Robot. Wait, I've just clicked your link, I thought you were referring to the scene of the presentation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35604685,"author":"Rzor","month":"2023-04","story":"MiniGPT-4","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"For those that don't know this is from a show called Patriot.","text":"This post is double great and I will never forgive Amazon for canceling that show. For those that don't know this is from a show called Patriot. [LINK] Scene: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43543047,"author":"rrr_oh_man","month":"2025-04","story":"Show HN: Renault 25 – The 1980s Talking Car Experiment","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That read a bit like John Lakeman's piping speech.","text":"> Up front, McPherson struts with coil springs and a stabilizer bar provide decent handling, while at the rear, a semi-rigid axle with torsion bars and hydraulic shocks prioritizes comfort over sporty roadholding—a compromise criticized against the Citroën CX’s independent suspension or the Germans’ stiffer setups That read a bit like John Lakeman's piping speech. ( [VIDEO] ) The text itself, unfortunately, is clearly written by (an older version of) Chatgpt"}]},"0PxTAn4g20U":{"wy":"HN points to it as a rare Hollywood hacking scene that uses real tools and a plausible, era-appropriate exploit.","q":{"x":"The Matrix Reloaded features Trinity correctly using nmap to find an exposed SSH server","i":29841762,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":37184595,"author":"JohnMakin","month":"2023-08","story":"The Plumber Problem","stance":"reference","why":"Plausible hacking scene","quote":"Here is the scene, your link is a little hard to see what it's trying to show, but great reference","text":"Here is the scene, your link is a little hard to see what it's trying to show, but great reference: [VIDEO] this looks plausible enough."},{"id":29841762,"author":"jkingsman","month":"2022-01","story":"Memories of the “Sneakers” Shoot (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Accurate depiction of nmap and an era-appropriate exploit","quote":"The Matrix Reloaded features Trinity correctly using nmap to find an exposed SSH server","text":"While I completely agree with you, as a very minor point, The Matrix Reloaded features Trinity correctly using nmap to find an exposed SSH server, and then correctly using SSHNuke to exploit an era-appropriate CRC vuln -- [VIDEO]"},{"id":27141240,"author":"beermonster","month":"2021-05","story":"Movies every physics lover should watch (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Realistic nmap and SSH exploit in a film","quote":"You get to see[1] Trinity determine a vulnerable SSH is running, using nmap","text":"Oh, I don't know about Matrix. You get to see[1] Trinity determine a vulnerable SSH is running, using nmap, only to then exploit it to get #. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27146934,"author":"devenblake","month":"2021-05","story":"Movies every physics lover should watch (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Scene where Trinity uses nmap and sshnuke","quote":"scene from The Matrix Reloaded where Trinity uses nmap and sshnuke.","text":"Didn't know this but yup: [VIDEO] - scene from The Matrix Reloaded where Trinity uses nmap and sshnuke. Looks like there's a re-creation of the on-screen display here: [LINK] but the typing is faster than in the movie. Also, the Jurassic Park scene to which you're referring was a real picture of SGI IRIX's 3D file manager. It was visual fluff, but functional visual fluff."},{"id":13126580,"author":"darkarmani","month":"2016-12","story":"Mr. Robot Killed the Hollywood Hacker","stance":"reference","why":"Movie scene using nmap and SSH CRC32 exploit","quote":"Using nmap to find a vulnerable ssh server for the SSH CRC32 attack.","text":"> This started before Mr. Robot. Think Tron: Legacy. Think The Matrix: [VIDEO] Using nmap to find a vulnerable ssh server for the SSH CRC32 attack."},{"id":11478847,"author":"bnegreve","month":"2016-04","story":"Voltron: A hacky debugger UI for hackers","stance":"reference","why":"Realistic nmap use in a movie scene","quote":"The imaginary world in the matrix trilogy is inspired from the unix hacker folklore [1].","text":"It makes sense actually. The imaginary world in the matrix trilogy is inspired from the unix hacker folklore [1]. So if you're a hacker, it's not surprising that what's on your screen looks like what's on the screens in the Matrix. [1] as shown here [VIDEO]"}]},"1JpwkeTBwgs":{"wy":"Linked as the famous clip of Trump scolding Germany over Russian gas, whenever European energy dependence comes up.","q":{"x":"There was this famous Trump video where he opposed NATO members on the question of Nordstream 2","i":42973452,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42973452,"author":"ankbnwa","month":"2025-02","story":"Estonia to disconnect from the Russian-run electricity network on Saturday","stance":"reference","why":"Famous clip of Trump opposing NATO members on Nordstream 2.","quote":"There was this famous Trump video where he opposed NATO members on the question of Nordstream 2","text":"Yes, it is tiresome. There was this famous Trump video where he opposed NATO members on the question of Nordstream 2: [VIDEO] On social media it is spun as \"the Germans laughed at him\", when the person sitting in the middle opposite Trump is the Norwegian Jens Stoltenberg, NATO secretary and usually a willing US tool. It was all of Europe together."},{"id":42181819,"author":"alecco","month":"2024-11","story":"Two undersea cables in Baltic Sea disrupted","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Trump scolding German leaders over buying Russian gas in 2018.","quote":"in 2018 Trump did scold live on camera the German leaders for buying gas from Russia.","text":"Not op. But in 2018 Trump did scold live on camera the German leaders for buying gas from Russia. \"Germany is completely controlled by Russia\". [VIDEO] NOTE: I mostly don't like Trump. But the \"Russiagate\" angle is ridiculous."},{"id":32864251,"author":"parkingrift","month":"2022-09","story":"US Economy Is a Safer Bet Than ‘Dire’ Europe, Goldman Strategists Say","stance":"reference","why":"Clip of Trump criticizing Germany over Russian gas dependence.","quote":"why should the US stay in NATO when the EU is dependent on Russian energy","text":"Well his actual “speech” there said why should the US stay in NATO when the EU is dependent on Russian energy and also directly funding the Russian economy. [VIDEO] Trump was not uniquely correct about this. Obama warned Europe about this same thing."}]},"26ZDB9h7BLY":{"wy":"Used as a joke reference when someone claims to have had it harder than others.","m":[{"id":38783893,"author":"erellsworth","month":"2023-12","story":"The Psychology of Poverty: Where Do We Stand? [pdf]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This reminds me of the Monty Python Four Yorkshiremen sketch, with 4 rich dudes bragging about who had the poorest childhood[1]. One could just as easily compare your poverty with that of people in the poorest places in the world. Oh, your kids get to go to school? Our kids spend their days digging through rubbish heaps. Oh your kid was taken away by CPS? Our kid died from inhaling toxic fumes from melting down the old computer parts they found digging through the rubbish heaps. Oh, you have a debit card fee? We're don't even have ATMs, or banks. Poverty is a spectrum. Just because X is poorer than Y doesn't mean Y isn't also poor. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18606920,"author":"Radim","month":"2018-12","story":"Why parking minimums almost destroyed my town and how we repealed them (2017)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"For those who didn't catch your (brilliant and well-placed) Monty Python reference: Four Yorkshiremen , [VIDEO]"},{"id":18246507,"author":"Jaruzel","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: Is there a modern “power on to basic” computer, for kids to learn on?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This comment is just screaming out for this as a response: Monty Python's 'Four Yorkshiremen': [VIDEO]"}]},"9fbD753xE-U":{"wy":"HN cites it as proof that e-ink screens can be pushed to surprisingly high refresh rates, supporting arguments about interactive e-ink uses.","q":{"x":"Nook's screen can be coerced into doing some surprising stuff","i":19899625,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19899625,"author":"aasasd","month":"2019-05","story":"Alibaba’s UC Browser is dominating in markets with lower-end smartphones (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Nook screen can be coerced into surprising refresh rates.","quote":"Nook's screen can be coerced into doing some surprising stuff","text":"Its ‘Mini’ incarnation—or something like that—also dominated on my e-ink Nook Simple Touch by the virtue of working the best on a weak machine with a screen of limited capabilities (grayscale, infrared touchscreen aka no multitouch), under Android 2.1. And I was using it until about three years ago. IIRC Opera Mini also worked on that, but not much else. Guess it's time for me to mourn again that there are no e-ink devices with normal modern Android—the screen is noticeably easier on the eyes, even with the optional ‘ambient’ backlight (emitted from the sides, diffused through the glass). And the screen consumes little power, so the battery can be way smaller: Nook is lighter than my current phone despite having a bigger screen. As for the refresh rate, Nook's screen can be coerced into doing some surprising stuff: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14060965,"author":"bazinga888","month":"2017-04","story":"Lego Macintosh Classic with e‑paper display","stance":"reference","why":"Old Nook e-ink reader playing a game with hacked refresh.","quote":"2012 era Nook eink reader playing a game with hacked refresh","text":"I believe it's just due to the niche application space. 2012 era Nook eink reader playing a game with hacked refresh: [VIDEO] The tech can do better, but as I've heard it told, the market for it has been eink readers. Who needs to refresh their book at 120hz? is the thinking. Folks that want to game or have a \"retina\" resolution buy a tablet proper. Upping refresh has steep power costs too. Ereader marketers like their \"recharge once every 6-8 weeks!\" message."},{"id":10187876,"author":"simcop2387","month":"2015-09","story":"Raspberry Pi touch display","stance":"reference","why":"E-ink display running games suggests text editing is feasible.","quote":"people have gotten ps1 games working on e-ink displays","text":"No idea about the actual numbers, but people have gotten ps1 games working on e-ink displays. I'd think that'd indicate it's probably perfectly doable for text editing. [VIDEO]"}]},"9jh4Mpgbi4A":{"wy":"Only brought up as a joke, usually when someone uses the word literally or the thread is about auto-antonyms.","m":[{"id":35698127,"author":"NoZebra120vClip","month":"2023-04","story":"Deming Paradox: Operationally Rigorous Companies Aren't Nice Places to Work","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"See also, nerds favorite superhero","text":"I prefer to promote the term \"veritable\" as well as \"virtually\" which are good actual substitutes for what someone means when they mix up \"literally\". See also, nerds favorite superhero: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21152221,"author":"jraph","month":"2019-10","story":"Auto-Antonyms","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Do you know Captain Literally?","text":"It seems like a lost battle. If a person you don't know well uses literally, you already cannot know without context if they mean figuratively if the fact being qualified is remotely plausible. It's broken beyond repair. No hope for healing. Literally is no more. It's passed on. It has ceased to be. It's expired and has gone to see its maker. It's an ex-word. Do you know Captain Literally? [VIDEO]"},{"id":10682834,"author":"nocman","month":"2015-12","story":"Microsoft Edge's JavaScript engine to go open-source","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'm so sorry to hear about your accident.","text":"\"I literally blown away by their name\". ^^ I'm so sorry to hear about your accident. I hope your injuries were not serious! :-D [VIDEO]"},{"id":10682842,"author":"nocman","month":"2015-12","story":"Microsoft Edge's JavaScript engine to go open-source","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Wow, Microsoft should really be more cautious with their code.","text":"\"I was literally blown away by Visual Studio Code\" ^^ Wow, Microsoft should really be more cautious with their code. There seems to be an epidemic of injuries. I'm so sorry to hear about your accident. I hope your injuries were not serious! :-D [VIDEO]"}]},"ARTLckN9e7I":{"wy":"Referenced as a model of a contemplative, mood-setting sequence that shows a city away from the main plot.","q":{"x":"the long sequence in Ghost in the Shell just showing the city as it is, away from the main story","i":44296887,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44296887,"author":"Cthulhu_","month":"2025-06","story":"Dull Men’s Club","stance":"reference","why":"Long sequence depicting the city away from the main story","quote":"the long sequence in Ghost in the Shell just showing the city as it is, away from the main story","text":"There's high praise in some movies or animation where they depict the mundane; the long sequence in Ghost in the Shell just showing the city as it is, away from the main story ( [VIDEO] ), most of Studio Ghibli's films, a lot of Breaking Bad and especially Better Call Saul, where there's a lot of scenes of people just going about their day, John Travolta getting a can of paint and a pizza in the opening of Saturday Night Fever ( [LINK] ), etc."},{"id":38288940,"author":"the_af","month":"2023-11","story":"Blender 16yo winner of UK younganimator of the year","stance":"reference","why":"Contemplative sequence of Motoko riding a boat","quote":"(I love this movie!)","text":"Mamoru Oshii's style seems to heavily include this kind of sequences. Your link is from Innocence, right? The original Ghost in the Shell also has a sequence of Motoko simply walking and riding a boat, doing nothing but staring: [VIDEO] (I love this movie!)"},{"id":29545473,"author":"jasonwatkinspdx","month":"2021-12","story":"Neon Genesis Evangelion (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Mood-setting city sequence from the original film","quote":"this is the sequence I'm thinking of","text":"I hear that. The older GiTS really took its audience seriously. I mean a finale scene that's a conversation about Jameson and similar theories? Heady stuff for an anime. The original movie is one of my all time favorites because it hits such a poignant mood, and it's portrayal of a Hong Kong that's ravaged by climate change and technological advancement that isn't utopic was prophetic imo. Edit: re the last sentence this is the sequence I'm thinking of: [VIDEO]"}]},"DdmyUZCl75s":{"wy":"Used as the go-to citation in LeetCode and hiring debates that competitive programming skill is not a good signal for hiring.","q":{"x":"Peter Norvig discussed an analysis at Google that suggested doing well at coding contests was a negative indicator of job performance","i":35551318,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35551318,"author":"nitwit005","month":"2023-04","story":"On a Great Interview Question","stance":"reference","why":"Norvig on contest success as negative indicator of performance.","quote":"Peter Norvig discussed an analysis at Google that suggested doing well at coding contests was a negative indicator of job performance","text":"You did emphasize it was the best result, so it's hard to argue that you selected for it. Back in 2015 Peter Norvig discussed an analysis at Google that suggested doing well at coding contests was a negative indicator of job performance: [VIDEO] Presumably they do well at these sorts of questions, but that doesn't imply the same actual effectiveness."},{"id":31455827,"author":"nitwit005","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: What to do about ‘Good at programming Bad at Leetcode’","stance":"reference","why":"Google talk on contest participants and job performance.","quote":"There was a Google talk some years ago that brought up this same idea with regards to people who did programming contests","text":"There was a Google talk some years ago that brought up this same idea with regards to people who did programming contests: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30034962,"author":"MichaelMoser123","month":"2022-01","story":"On Leaving Facebook","stance":"reference","why":"Norvig on Google checking contests against job performance.","quote":"Peter Norvig says they were checking, if taking part in programming contests would correlate with good performance at the job [1]","text":">were hit with antitrust litigation starting around 2010 because they were all involved in colluding to reduce the ability of devs to switch jobs I didn't know that, really interesting detail. >no one wants to admit they don't know what they are doing. Interesting perspective, though I don't know about the vodoo part. They seem to be taking the matter quite seriously, at least google does. Take the matter of competitive programming: Peter Norvig says they were checking, if taking part in programming contests would correlate with good performance at the job [1]; I think having read somewhere that Norvig revised his negative judgement later on, as they had examined more data [2]. (actually that would mean that they didn't act on the data to begin with, well well). These companies are good at data processing, any data that is, at least they seem to be taking it dead seriously (disclaimer: I never worked at google, though I have managed to fail at interviewing there, however i am not sure if i would have accepted an offer ;-) [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":24182269,"author":"zerr","month":"2020-08","story":"A guide to learning algorithms through LeetCode","stance":"reference","why":"Norvig admits contest skill is unrelated to being a good hire.","quote":"Even Norvig admits it has nothing to do with being a good dev/hire","text":"> Obviously, each company can't blindly trust the interviewing practices of everyone's previous employer. Yet, they copycat each other on this. > after you've been programming for 10 years ...as I said, you're not desperate enough, so you become picky how you spend your time. > Companies aren't going out of their way to make it harder to hire good employees into their own companies. Lowering the churn rate has a higher priority. > The problems are designed to test CS skills. No, these problems are designed to test CP skills. Even Norvig admits it has nothing to do with being a good dev/hire: [VIDEO] > How else would you suggest reorganizing the interviews to test for CS skills? How Caltech, MIT, Harvard, ETH, etc... test for CS skills? Certainly not with CP riddles - unless you take a CP course, which indeed is a separate [optional] course at some of those universities for interview preps specifically."},{"id":16952852,"author":"umbs","month":"2018-04","story":"Competitive Programmer's Handbook (2017) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Norvig says contest skill correlates poorly with job performance.","quote":"Peter Norvig indicated that it correlates poorly with on the job performance (at least at Google) [1]","text":"As few comments already mentioned, post college, preparing for this type of thing immensely helps in job interviews. I find it very hard to clear job interviews and in 2017 alone, I appeared for ~25 job interviews. I'm very convinced that competitive programming skills gives you a leg up in job interviews. Many people may already may know this, but Peter Norvig indicated that it correlates poorly with on the job performance (at least at Google) [1]. I wonder why, then, companies still continue to do this style of interviews. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14116594,"author":"kyleschiller","month":"2017-04","story":"A Competitive Programmer's Handbook","stance":"reference","why":"Norvig on contest winners as a negative job signal.","quote":"Norvig seems to think it's a negative signal though.","text":"Agree with other comments about similarity to interviews. Norvig seems to think it's a negative signal though. [VIDEO]"}]},"GGgiGtJk7MA":{"wy":"Used to check the quote in context, since the full sentence referred to experts who are consistently wrong, and as a jibe in Brexit threads.","m":[{"id":20518778,"author":"Vinnl","month":"2019-07","story":"How Turkey Purged Its Intellectuals","stance":"reference","why":"Full sentence shows Gove was referring to experts consistently getting it wrong.","quote":"the full sentence was \"People have had enough of experts (...) who are consistently getting it wrong\".","text":"I really dislike people bringing that quote up, because the full sentence was \"People have had enough of experts (...) who are consistently getting it wrong\". [1] It's still not necessarily true, but by taking just part of that quote is misrepresenting what he said. Just thought that'd be good to keep in mind next time you might consider digging it up. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12196676,"author":"mattmanser","month":"2016-07","story":"Britain’s scientists are freaking out over Brexit","stance":"reference","why":"Full clip shows Gove's quote about experts who get it consistently wrong.","quote":"You watch the clip","text":"I voted remain. You watch the clip: [VIDEO] The actual quote is: \"People have had enough of experts from organisations with acronymns saying they know what is best and getting it consistently wrong\". By implication, those \"experts\" aren't actually experts because they get it consistently wrong. But sure, it's easy to mis-quote him and cut the rest of the statement to make the leave voters sound like knuckle draggers because that's the agenda a certain set of the remain voters wanted to push. Leave voters are stoooopppppidddddddd. That tactic didn't work well. Gove was talking about experts who get it consistently wrong. So many of these \"experts\" have suddenly gone \"oh, we wrong, X won't happen now after all\", all the threats, punishment budgets and dire consequences have dissipated like the nonsense fear-mongering they actually were. And we also now get a report about the IMF being irrationally pro-Euro, destroying Greece in the process, and we were supposed to listen to them as neutral voices about Brexit consequences? Turns out they had vested interests, just like so many of the other \"independent\" \"experts\". What we have now seen, and Brexit has really shone a light on this, is that the political elite across the world seem obsessed with European political integration and will spout utterly unsubstantiated rubbish to justify it. So, he was kinda right. Which is very disappointing. I voted remain."},{"id":15761343,"author":"orf","month":"2017-11","story":"Britain has abandoned its claim to be the world's fifth largest economy","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"All those experts we've \"had enough of\"[1], of course.","text":"All those experts we've \"had enough of\"[1], of course. If you think we've got enough faded glory already wait until we set sail on a Brexit low tide and immediately sink. Rule Britannia! 1. [VIDEO]"}]},"J6lyURyVz7k":{"wy":"Cited as a go-to reference in HN discussions about education and teaching to the test.","m":[{"id":46421435,"author":"fao_","month":"2025-12","story":"UK accounting body to halt remote exams amid AI cheating","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing well-known problems of learning only for the test.","quote":"","text":"> It's already easy enough to just throw the test material into the LLM and get a bunch of flash cards on relevant content and memorize that LLM summarisation is broken, so I wouldn't expect them to get very far with this (see this comment on lobste.rs: [LINK] ) Also, memorizing flashcards is actually, to some point, learning the material. There's a reason why Anki is popular for students. Ultimately, however, this comes down to the 20th+21st century problem of \"students learning only for the test\", which we can see has critical problems that are well-known: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23449856,"author":"CaptArmchair","month":"2020-06","story":"Ideas That Changed My Life","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as supporting material in a debate on American education.","quote":"","text":"Well, I read the article. It's not that great. Why? Because it's a mix 'n match of quotes, bits and pieces and observations cobbled together to support the thesis that digital technology will disrupt how information - and therefore our thinking about the world - is controlled. It's not that he's factually wrong about what he's saying. Rather, it's that the article lacks any references to a foundational understanding of historical dynamics and current affairs regarding economics, politics or economics. Everything he discusses has a historical context steeped in competing ideological, economical and political theories that have shaped the world since World War 1. And yet, he makes no mention that background whatsoever. On the contrary. For instance. > Like a fish in water, we’re unaware of the integration between our education system, the corporate structure, and our media environment. Uh. No. There's very much awareness about this among those who have participated in this debate and who are extremely worried about the state of education in America. And it's a debate about investing in public infrastructure, the advantages and drawbacks of free market dynamics in education and the long term impact on future generations. For instance, Mary Sue Coleman, president of the Association of American Universities and former president of the University of Michigan and the University of Iowa, wrote in the Washington Post back in 2016: > Public higher education is at a tipping point in the"},{"id":13303090,"author":"specialist","month":"2017-01","story":"When Finnish Teachers Work in America's Public Schools","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a related piece on standardized testing.","quote":"","text":"What portions of that link are relevant? All I see is a link dump. \"Signaling\"? As in virtue signaling? Is that your site? I approve of lesswrong, Brookings Institute, the Atlantic... And reviewing your comment history, I'm going to gamble on the hope that you're persuadable. You kinda \"sound\" like a libertarian, so you might enjoy this publication from criticalthinking.org: [LINK] First hit when I google \"fads in education\". Result! The intro is better than I could have said it: \"The history of education is also the history of educational panaceas, the comings and goings of quick fixes for deep-seated educational problems. This old problem is dramatically on the increase. The result is intensifying fragmentation of energy and effort in the schools, together with a significant waste of time and money. Many teachers become increasingly cynical and jaded. It is time to recognize that education will never be improved by educational fads, and that the manner in which educational trends are marketed guarantees that they will be transformed into fads. Fads by their nature are fated to self-destruction. Parents, educators, and citizen activists need to understand the problem of educational fads so that they can effectively distinguish substantive efforts at educational reform from superficial ones. Hence the motivation for this guide.\" It includes many fads I hadn't heard of. Its also about 10 years old, and it focuses on the classroom, so it doesn't include more recent nonsense lik"}]},"LlOSdRMSG_k":{"wy":"Posted purely as a joke reply to scenes of one party dismissing another, such as rival companies or high-status people brushing off others.","m":[{"id":45321550,"author":"joshstrange","month":"2025-09","story":"Vapor chamber tech keeps iPhone 17 Pro cool","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I will preface this with: Apple is _far_ from perfect, sometimes they even fail the “good” bar and I have serious issues with the direction they are going on some products/software. However, I see them as the lessor of two evils. I understand you might not agree, that’s just the conclusion I’ve come to. > Wrong. The vast majority of Apple fans don't care about Android. This is my experience as well. I prefer Apple products but I have Android phones/tablets (test devices) and know there are aspects where Android is better. That said, I have some friends who make it their life mission to mention any Apple/iPhone failing to me. I just don’t get it. I literally never bring it up, or if I bring up anything Apple related it’s just me excited about a new device I got, never in the context of “look how much better it is than Android”. Even then, I’ve learned to temper my excitement lest they see it as an opportunity to tell me all the problems/issues they have with it. I don’t follow Android news closely, I don’t share “gotcha’s” about the hardware/os, etc. It reminds me this scene in Mad Men [0], I literally do not think about Android or Android users but I know a couple Android users who seem to obsess over Apple products/users in a way I cannot relate to and do not understand. I can bet any negative Apple news will be relayed to me by them, I literally could not care less about the Android ecosystem issues (of which I see many). [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38747915,"author":"whalesalad","month":"2023-12","story":"Beeper Mini is now open-source","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> But the real issue boils down to human in/out group dynamics. Being excluded from group chats or having people get frustrated by the lone Android user in the group are powerful forms of peer pressure. This reminds me of the Don Draper \"I don't think about you at all\" scene from Mad Men. [VIDEO] There is an odd coincidence/overlap with friend groups and overall lifestyle/ideology and imessage vs android or blue vs green bubbles. I have like a few obscure friends who use android and they are all maga or counterculture weirdos. Meanwhile everyone else that I know (literally everyone, including a huge group chat for my gym weightlifting group, all close and extended family) uses an iPhone and iMessage. Back to don draper: Android users cry about being excluded, but the rest of us don't even think about android users. It's not Apple's responsibility or obligation to fix this."},{"id":36911601,"author":"joshstrange","month":"2023-07","story":"US iPhone market share jumps to 55% as Android shipments fall sharply","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Personally I don't understand the friends I have that use Android. They wear it as a badge of pride that they didn't give Apple money [0], ok... cool? None of them use a third-party app store, none of them use a custom rom, none of them really customize the phone at all past stock. I understand if you want to go the Android route to root/customise it but if you aren't going to do that then I really don't get the point. Google and Apple are, at worst, equal in how \"evil\" they are and in my opinion Apple comes out on top for more things that I care about. I also never say shit about their phone/computer choices but for some reason some of them find reasons to bring up my use of Apple products regularly. Makes me think of the scene in Mad Men \"I don't think about you at all\" [1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34390885,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2023-01","story":"Wyoming wants to phase out sales of EVs by 2035","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"California is the world’s fourth largest economy and has 12% of the US population (39 million compared to the 600k folks living in Wyoming). It’ll continue to be an economic powerhouse long after people complaining online about the state are gone. If complaining makes you feel better, do so, but it doesn’t change the facts. It’s certainly not going to hurt California [1]. Edit: I have never lived in California. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33950416,"author":"mLuby","month":"2022-12","story":"Be Wary of Imitating High-Status People Who Can Afford to Countersignal","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Doreen dropping the knowledge. I just learned another fun Coco Chanel quote: \"Those who create are rare; those who cannot are numerous. Therefore, the latter are stronger.\" Little known fact: before he was Pied Piper's cool attorney Dan in Silicon Valley, but after his first job at a Superstore, Ron LaFlamme was told that he wasn't thought about at all by Don Draper. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28765487,"author":"joshstrange","month":"2021-10","story":"Asahi Linux for M1 Macs: progress report for September 2021","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Everything Apple does they do because it makes sense for them. They are neither trying to help nor hinder third-party OSes. They just don't care. That's my read on this as well. They designed it so it would work well with their other hardware/software and this is what they landed on. I seriously doubt they paid even a second of thought to other OSs running on their hardware. Some people will call that \"user-hostile\" but it's much closer to apathy IMHO and as a macOS and Apple hardware user I'm fine with that. It's cool that Linux can/will run on the M1 but I'll never be doing that myself. It reminds me of that scene in Mad Men \"I don't think about you at all\" [0] but Apple's feeling isn't even as sinister or hostile as the comment made by Don. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9697624,"author":"pvg","month":"2015-06","story":"Reddit Bans Five Communities In New Anti-Harassment Campaign","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"/r/all is not the front page. Reddit is at a scale at which this will probably blow over, likely for a better Reddit. Other teacup storms like the removal of /r/jailbait, their handling of 'the fappening' and gamergate don't seem have affected reddit much at all. It seems almost more like this [VIDEO]"},{"id":6771817,"author":"pkulak","month":"2013-11","story":"Scroogled Keep Calm Mug","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Every time I see this \"Scroogled\" campaign I think about Microsoft and Google in an elevator","text":"Every time I see this \"Scroogled\" campaign I think about Microsoft and Google in an elevator: [VIDEO]"}]},"MCBdcNA_FsI":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence that lights-out factories exist, though one commenter notes it is outdated relative to current automation.","q":{"x":"some Chinese factories are so completely automated now that they don't bother turning on the lights","i":44780803,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":49045314,"author":"ffsm8","month":"2026-07","story":"Why Software Factories Fail","stance":"reference","why":"Notes video is outdated relative to current state of the art.","quote":"this is an outdated video for waht was state of the art a few years ago","text":"i mean youre most likely not wrong (i dont have insider knowledge of those places), however your previous comment said \"never want it to be dark\" - when the reality is that its normal operation _is_ dark. only when the automated QA detects issues or theyre doing random inspections are the lights turned up/on this is an outdated video for waht was state of the art a few years ago [VIDEO] again, i dont have insider knowledge so i cannot speak on whats SOTA in chinese automated factories beyond whats covered publicly"},{"id":46196848,"author":"kevlened","month":"2025-12","story":"The era of jobs is ending","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that lights-out factories exist.","quote":"China’s Dark Factories: So Automated, They Don't Need Lights\"","text":"> Find me a “lights out” factory. They don’t exist. \"Inside China's 'dark factories' where robots run the production lines\" [LINK] \"China’s Dark Factories: So Automated, They Don't Need Lights\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":44780803,"author":"Telemakhos","month":"2025-08","story":"A study of lights at night suggests dictators lie about economic growth (2022)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows automated Chinese factories that run without lights.","quote":"some Chinese factories are so completely automated now that they don't bother turning on the lights","text":"> the amount of light used when moving from last-gen 'heavy industry' to next-gen 'value add'/'light industry'/'design work'/whatever is going to be reduced Not to mention the automation of heavy industry leading to \"dark factories\": some Chinese factories are so completely automated now that they don't bother turning on the lights in large chunks of them. See [VIDEO]"}]},"UTduy7Qkvk8":{"wy":"Invoked as a joke in threads about ownership and entitlement.","m":[{"id":38728804,"author":"dylan604","month":"2023-12","story":"The right to use adblockers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Right, because the Do-Not-Track flag was respected oh so well. The only people that seem to care about flags are an invading colonizing system: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17567337,"author":"david927","month":"2018-07","story":"In 5 states, richest Americans live in a “new Gilded Age”","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Absolutely. Remember: technology will make everything move to fewer and fewer sources. If you wanted a book 20 years ago, you went to your local bookstore; now you go to one company. And so the people that get to this place first put their flag on it and claim it as their own. ( [VIDEO] ) I'm glad Jeff was there first (essentially) but we can still decide the future we want."},{"id":14333301,"author":"sangnoir","month":"2017-05","story":"The Right to Read (1997)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Step 1. Get the biggest, baddest gun Step 2. Plant a flag[0] Step 3. Declare the land now belongs to you and a descendants. 0. [VIDEO]"}]},"UlCYFh8U2xM":{"wy":"Cited, almost always critically, as evidence of Chinese influence over the WHO during the Covid pandemic.","m":[{"id":29118587,"author":"rndgermandude","month":"2021-11","story":"China says it will hold supporters of Taiwan's independence responsible for life","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The WHO for example is very adept at avoiding any position on Taiwan[2].","text":">Considering that Taiwan is already independent Not according to the PRC, and a lot of countries and international institutions either. Some (usually with close ties to China, but now always, e.g. Spain[1], probably because they do not want to give their own separatist movements any political ammunition) consider the ROC part of the PRC, others are deliberately vague on whether they consider Taiwan independent or even a nation, like e.g. the US[0]. The WHO for example is very adept at avoiding any position on Taiwan[2]. And then of course, there is this guy... [3]. (Just if you like some cringe about a serious topic) >China's goals of \"peaceful reunification\". (PR) China doesn't really talk about peaceful reunification. They officially consider Taiwan part of the country already, a part that is just a little rebellious at the moment. They are more talking about a peaceful end of the conflict, but recently use the word \"peaceful\" a lot less. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":26546809,"author":"SpaceRaccoon","month":"2021-03","story":"Why the Wuhan lab leak theory shouldn't be dismissed","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Could you explain why evidence that the virus evolved naturally contradicts the lab-leak theory? I'm all ears and waiting to hear the reasoning. As others have pointed out, lab-leak does not imply artificially developed. > I’m a bioscientist. And I'm a Bayesian analyst. Surely your position is that it is a coincidence that: - the virus appeared to originate in Wuhan - genome sequences from patients were 96% or 89% identical to the Bat CoV ZC45 coronavirus originally found in Rhinolophus affinis - The bats carrying CoV ZC45 were originally found in Yunnan or Zhejiang province, both of which are more than 900 kilometers away Wuhan - According to municipal reports and the testimonies of 31 residents and 28 visitors, the bat was never a food source in the city, and no bat was traded in the market - Wuhan is home to two laboratories conducting research on bat coronavirus - Within ~280 meters from the market, there was the Wuhan Center for Disease Control & Prevention (WHCDC). WHCDC hosted animals in laboratories for research purposes. In one of their studies, 155 bats including Rhinolophus affinis were captured in Hubei province, and other 450 bats were captured in Zhejiang province - one of the researchers described that he was once by attacked by bats and the blood of a bat shot on his skin. In another accident, bats peed on him. He was once thrilled for capturing a bat carrying a live tick Not conclusive by any means, but I have yet to hear reasoning by which we should exclude "},{"id":25299103,"author":"gruez","month":"2020-12","story":"China is the greatest enemy to freedom – US intelligence chief","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">The WHO seems to be owned by China: [VIDEO] From the video title alone: >Senior WHO official dodges questions about Taiwan’s WHO membership Isn't that... expected? Even the US (and most other western countries) doesn't recognize taiwan as a nation for the purposes of WHO/UN membership, so what's he supposed to do? Try to make a political statement and say \"taiwan is a country too!\", and piss off china in the process? What would such a statement achieve? >praises China That's also expected of an organization that's basically reliant on other countries to cooperate (provide infection numbers, virus samples, etc.). Praise is cheap, and scolding China is counterproductive. China's heavy handed measures to contain the outbreak might be a gross infringement of civil liberties, but from a public health perspective it was highly effective."},{"id":46729295,"author":"eska","month":"2026-01","story":"United States Completes WHO Withdrawal","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don’t know how people can defend this organization and criticize the US government for losing trust in it, when there’s a myriad of scandals like this one. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39417137,"author":"imiric","month":"2024-02","story":"Science fiction authors were excluded from awards for fear of offending China","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"remember how they controlled the narrative around COVID, the dubious WHO report in 2020, and this interview[2]?","text":"This is not new. China has always ran strong external influence campaigns. This has only ramped up in recent years under Xi. China has police presence around the world[1]. And remember how they controlled the narrative around COVID, the dubious WHO report in 2020, and this interview[2]? [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34953593,"author":"imiric","month":"2023-02","story":"Lab Leak Most Likely Origin of Covid-19 Pandemic, U.S. Agency Now Says","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"there have certainly been many instances of the WHO acting in favor of China's best interests","text":"If you're looking for definitive evidence, you're unlikely to find any. But there have certainly been many instances of the WHO acting in favor of China's best interests, and there are many criticisms of their SARS-CoV-2 research and report. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] I, as a layperson, found several issues with the report as well: [LINK] All of this mounts sufficient evidence for any reasonable person to be suspicious of the WHO, if not to entirely dismiss their claims."},{"id":29539802,"author":"sandstrom","month":"2021-12","story":"Vivaldi: Violating CoC on forum may close your email account","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This journalist cannot even mention the name \"Taiwan\" and \"country\" in the same sentence without this WHO guy (from Canada!) treating her like pariah.","text":"Good point! Similarly, there are also countries that are \"offensive\" from a Chinese point of view. This journalist cannot even mention the name \"Taiwan\" and \"country\" in the same sentence without this WHO guy (from Canada!) treating her like pariah. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29370551,"author":"willcipriano","month":"2021-11","story":"Why WHO skipped 2 Greek letters to name new variant ‘Omicron’","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I'm leaning in the direction of pandering.","text":"From this act alone and I am on board with your explanation, but with this[0] interview from last year I'm leaning in the direction of pandering. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28951952,"author":"jokethrowaway","month":"2021-10","story":"Fed to ban policymakers from owning individual stocks","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"While I think all western countries can easily be considered authoritarian countries, given most of them demand half of the profits of everyone doing anything productive on their land, you're terribly deluded if you think they rank anything close to what China is. China has concentration camps, kill businesses with a whip of the hand and apparently have half of the world (probably including your government) on their payroll. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28327515,"author":"account42","month":"2021-08","story":"Delta Air Lines doesn’t want to call delta variant the ‘delta variant’","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"WHO trying not to offend China by dodging questions about Taiwan","text":"A reference to the current Chinese dictator Xi \"Winnie-the-Pooh\" Jinping with the implication that the WHO is captured by Chinese interests. See also: WHO trying not to offend China by dodging questions about Taiwan: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26757817,"author":"imiric","month":"2021-04","story":"Scientists who say the lab-leak hypothesis for SARS-CoV-2 shouldn't be ruled out","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The WHO is also an organization of questionable trustworthiness and with suspicious subservience to China[1].","text":"> You are missing the important bit: The lab leak would have to be covered up by the Chinese authorities and the WHO would some how have had to be in on it. Is that so inconceivable? The Chinese government has historically obfuscated facts and runs one of the largest media control operations in modern history. The WHO is also an organization of questionable trustworthiness and with suspicious subservience to China[1]. But the WHO wouldn't necessarily had to have been complicit. This could've easily been covered up by Chinese authorities during the many months of blocking external researchers into the country[2]. The research in the report you linked to started in January 2021. As for the report itself, I skimmed a few pages and noticed some issues. My understanding is limited in this area, so please correct me if I'm wrong. 0. First of all, the conflict of interest of it being reported by WHO and Chinese researchers should be a factor in judging the validity of any of its claims. 1. From the arguments in favour of the intermediate host scenario (p. 115): > Although the closest related viruses have been found in bats, the > evolutionary distance between these bat viruses and SARS-CoV-2 is estimated > to be several decades, suggesting a missing link (either a missing progenitor > virus, or evolution of a progenitor virus in an intermediate host). Why would this suggest a missing link? Couldn't gain of function research accelerate the mutations of the virus to make it seem far d"},{"id":26129722,"author":"waterhouse","month":"2021-02","story":"US raises ‘deep concerns’ over WHO report on Covid’s Wuhan origins","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Senior WHO official dodges questions about Taiwan’s WHO membership; praises China","text":"\"Senior WHO official dodges questions about Taiwan’s WHO membership; praises China\": [VIDEO] Japan's deputy prime minister calls the WHO the \"Chinese Health Organization\": [LINK] > are we saying that the international group that entered china were some how in league with the CCP? There may be reason to expect that they or their superiors are ... biased."},{"id":25296649,"author":"OneGuy123","month":"2020-12","story":"China is the greatest enemy to freedom – US intelligence chief","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The WHO seems to be owned by China","text":"There is a difference between war mongering and legit concerns. The \"russia, russia\" thingy really seems to have been just media warmongering. But China seems to be more proactive, there are many stories of Chinese briberies. The WHO seems to be owned by China: [VIDEO] Also regarding US 2020 elections voting systems: \"SEC filings: The parent company of Dominion Voting Systems received $400 million from an Investment Bank in Switzerland that is 75% owned by the Chinese government. UBS Securities is a Swiss investment bank which owns 24.99% of UBS Securities Co LTD, a Chinese Investment Bank. The remaining 75% of UBS Securities CO LTD is owned by the Chinese government,” states the report.\""},{"id":23763946,"author":"fermienrico","month":"2020-07","story":"The U.S. is withdrawing from the World Health Organization","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"everyone should watch this WHO official who just hangs up when they're asked about Taiwan","text":"I don't know what the cost/benefit ratio is with WHO but as a non-expert, I found the WHO's leadership during this unprecedented times as weak, un-authoritative and frankly misguided/misinformed. Without stirring up conspiracy theories, everyone should watch this WHO official who just hangs up when they're asked about Taiwan: [VIDEO] That makes me think that the whole organization has been bought out and sold off. But, that's just my knee-jerk reaction and a gut feeling. That said, what's there to pay to be a member? Why not just stay in there like everyone else?"},{"id":23978786,"author":"kop316","month":"2020-07","story":"WHO says Covid-19 pandemic is 'one big wave', not seasonal","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"A senior WHO offical praises the PRC, even after it is come out the the PRC covered up the initial outbreak.","text":"Respectfully, I think there is a lot of mistrust in WHO is because the PRC deliberately withheld information in the 2002-2004 SARS Outbreak: [LINK] This is where the saying \"fool me once shame on you, fool me twice same on me\" comes into play. The below source shows the PRC covering up the COVID-19 outbreak: [LINK] I peronsally feel like the WHO should have been a lot more suspicious of the PRC and how they were reporting. However, it appeared like they took information from the PRC at face value in January, where containment could have spared much of this pandemic. Was it political? Much like someone else pointed out. [VIDEO] A senior WHO offical praises the PRC, even after it is come out the the PRC covered up the initial outbreak. It is very hard to not view that is political. To me, a lot of the mistrust of the WHO based on these sources are well founded."},{"id":22824980,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2020-04","story":"Japan to fund firms to shift production out of China","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"WHO is heavily influenced by China; here is an interview with a senior WHO official who was asked about Taiwan - he simply hangs up the phone","text":"In addition, WHO is heavily influenced by China; here is an interview with a senior WHO official who was asked about Taiwan - he simply hangs up the phone: [VIDEO]"}]},"VFkyV7d5t8o":{"wy":"Referenced as the memorable movie origin of the 3D file system browser and early VR depictions.","q":{"x":"Here's the scene from the movie adaptation","i":15516622,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15516622,"author":"throwaway2016a","month":"2017-10","story":"Fsv – 3D File System Visualizer","stance":"reference","why":"Movie scene of the 3D file system visualizer","quote":"Here's the scene from the movie adaptation","text":"Correction... too late to edit. The Chritchton book is just \"Disclosure\" not \"Full Disclosure\" incase anyone is curious. Here's the scene from the movie adaptation [VIDEO]"},{"id":10094979,"author":"harlanlewis","month":"2015-08","story":"Michael Crichton and Computers","stance":"reference","why":"Memorable VR scene from Disclosure","quote":"I don't remember much about the plot, but can't ever forget that VR.","text":"[VIDEO] I don't remember much about the plot, but can't ever forget that VR. <curmudgeon>The trends in \"expressive\" animated interface design make me think that dystopian future is still an aspiration for some designers. Just as soon as we all get headsets...</curmudgeon>"},{"id":8177917,"author":"doragcoder","month":"2014-08","story":"Ask HN: What Android Wear apps would you like to see?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Something that lets me scan a NoSQL database, like in the movie \"Disclosure","text":"Something that lets me scan a NoSQL database, like in the movie \"Disclosure\" A clip for those who don't know: [VIDEO]"}]},"ZITP93pqtdQ":{"wy":"Used as a punchline or example to mock hardline libertarian positions, not as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":36384960,"author":"avgcorrection","month":"2023-06","story":"Texas rules requiring water breaks for construction workers will be nullified","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> My point was that if pressed, the lawmakers responsible for overriding these \"bag bans\" would say something similar to you, essentially that bag bans are bad (though I suspect their reasoning would look very different). They would say something similar to me. Except the reasoning would be very different. So the only similarity is that they would say that it is “bad” (I would say that the ban is pointless per se). So they wouldn’t say something similar to what I said. Next up: “Wars are bad”. Did avgcorrection say that? Or a Libertarian? [VIDEO]"},{"id":32351102,"author":"gruez","month":"2022-08","story":"Traffic Fatalities Reach 16 Year High","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":">Licensing to drive seems like a scam How so? Don't you want to see some competency exhibited by people before they drive[1]? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27978304,"author":"snotrockets","month":"2021-07","story":"Mercedes-Benz teases super-efficient EV with over 1,000km of range","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"is it a comment from a real libertarian idiot or a case of poe's law?","text":"Reminds me both of this video [VIDEO] and the all too common headscratcher: is it a comment from a real libertarian idiot or a case of poe's law?"},{"id":16664492,"author":"java_script","month":"2018-03","story":"Game Workers Unite","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"GP means left-libertarians, for example anarcho-syndicalists like Noam Chomsky. Versus American libertarian [VIDEO]"},{"id":39649054,"author":"bastawhiz","month":"2024-03","story":"Uber must pay wheelchair user $35,000, provide accessible rides","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I can't even begin to take you seriously when you're arguing on behalf of the group who heckles their own political candidates because they believe in driver's licensing requirements. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28695535,"author":"gorwell","month":"2021-09","story":"Workers who keep global supply chains moving warn of 'system collapse'","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They become a parody of themselves, much like this Libertarian debate","text":"That combined with an ineffective leadership. Just look at what these groups spend their time squabbling over. This DSA meeting is reflective of how little chance they have of getting anything accomplished: [LINK] They become a parody of themselves, much like this Libertarian debate: [VIDEO]"}]},"_8yVOC4ciXc":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a concise explanation of GPT-3's scaling jump and surprising abilities at its release.","q":{"x":"Computerphile summed it up pretty well on GPT-3's release","i":34248453,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34707914,"author":"MichaelMoser123","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: HN for Finance?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains GPT-3 nearly learned addition and multiplication without anyone knowing how","quote":"they explain that gpt3 (almost) learned how to add and multiply, but they don't know how.","text":"maybe ask your favorite language model for advice. They say it's the answer to all questions, sort of like 42. My point is here: [VIDEO] - they explain that gpt3 (almost) learned how to add and multiply, but they don't know how. To me that's the thing. (the insight comes at 12:29, 15:23) in a sense that is kind of an admission that we don't know the heck what is going on."},{"id":34248453,"author":"CSMastermind","month":"2023-01","story":"Microsoft and OpenAI Working on ChatGPT-Powered Bing in Challenge to Google","stance":"reference","why":"Explains GPT-2 and GPT-3 scaling with parameters not leveling off","quote":"Computerphile summed it up pretty well on GPT-3's release","text":"> No one in 2018 suspected that large language models would smoothly scale up by simply increasing the number of parameters. [LINK] That was kind of the entire point of GPT-2. Computerphile summed it up pretty well on GPT-3's release: [VIDEO] Here's some quotes from that video: \"The thing about gpt2 is just that it was much bigger than anything that came before. It was more parameters and was kind of the point of that paper.\" ... \"They made gpt2 because the curve wasn't leveling off. We've gone 117 times bigger than gpt2 and they're still not leveling off.\""},{"id":28370895,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2021-08","story":"The Tesla Dojo Chip Is Impressive, but There Are Some Major Technical Issues","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates the jump in quality from GPT-2 to GPT-3","quote":"","text":"The industry collectively sank untold billions into the blind belief that neural algorithms will somehow turn into \"AI.\" The huge qualitative differences between GPT-2 and GPT-3 seem to suggest that they will, if you just keep adding orders of magnitude more connections and more data. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23731423,"author":"tripzilch","month":"2020-07","story":"GPT-3 Creative Fiction","stance":"reference","why":"Shows GPT-3 poems compared with human-written ones","quote":"yesterday I watched a youtube video about GPT3","text":"yesterday I watched a youtube video about GPT3 ( [VIDEO] ), and it showed two poems. One was human made, the other was from AI trained on that human's poems. Both poems were pretty good. But one of them had a metaphor about the moon reflecting in ocean waves, being distorted and taking on monstrous forms. I figured this had to be the human one, it was a novel description (because metaphor) of a very real experience (how the moon appears in reflection on the ocean)."},{"id":23857371,"author":"ChrisRR","month":"2020-07","story":"Using GPT-3 to generate user interfaces","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses a paper on GPT-3's ability to do arithmetic","quote":"They discuss a paper that studies the ability of GPT-3 to do arithmetic","text":"Your first point is actually covered in the computerphile video on GPT-3. They discuss a paper that studies the ability of GPT-3 to do arithmetic as it's part of the learned text database Check a look [VIDEO]"}]},"_Gsz7Gu6agA":{"wy":"HN posts it jokingly in threads about payments, taxes and cash, as a funny illustration of how digital transactions make low-level shenanigans harder.","q":{"x":"This is always a funny video clip about electronic payments making low level shenanigans more difficult.","i":30832331,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":48182990,"author":"mrandish","month":"2026-05","story":"Iran Starts Bitcoin-Backed Ship Insurance for Hormuz Strait","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory Sopranos clip of old school mobsters trying to sell","text":"I guess I'm just surprised they even bother trying to mask an obvious shake down under the euphemism \"insurance\" when it's such a trope. Obligatory Sopranos clip of old school mobsters trying to sell \"protective insurance\" to a Starbucks: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38536198,"author":"mindslight","month":"2023-12","story":"UK porn watchers could have faces scanned","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"surveillance tech, dispatchable forces with scaled-up weapons, and the economic advantages of cooperation/specialization.","text":"I don't really know what you're referring to from history, given that this level of technological development and fine-grained agile government/corporate perception/control is a pretty recent phenomenon. Our society isn't really kept in line any longer by force projection from small arms wielded by groups of humans, but rather surveillance tech, dispatchable forces with scaled-up weapons, and the economic advantages of cooperation/specialization. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30832331,"author":"lotsofpulp","month":"2022-03","story":"Crazy Eddie: The popular electronics chain that scammed America","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This is always a funny video clip about electronic payments making low level shenanigans more difficult.","text":"I am not sure what wiring to different jurisdictions allows you to do, but I was referring to the parallel books for the purposes of underreporting revenue for tax purposes or stealing from other business partners. The card processor always has your sales amounts available so come tax time or audit time, you will not be able to hide revenue, as seen on 1099-Ks. [LINK] This is always a funny video clip about electronic payments making low level shenanigans more difficult. The game nowadays is to do it legally, and getting involved in the political machinery. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22051205,"author":"lotsofpulp","month":"2020-01","story":"Visa, Plaid, networks, and jobs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"forcing accurate payment of taxes due to an an indisputable record being created of sales","text":"It also has the effect of forcing accurate payment of taxes due to an an indisputable record being created of sales: [VIDEO]"}]},"bT3G-kcKN70":{"wy":"Cited in Tesla Autopilot crash threads to show how deadly car-into-trailer crashes are and why underride guards matter.","m":[{"id":37802238,"author":"judge2020","month":"2023-10","story":"The final 11 seconds of a fatal Tesla Autopilot crash – A reconstruction","stance":"reference","why":"Shows skirts and underride bars save lives.","quote":"yes skirts and underride bars save lives","text":"While not what you're addressing, yes skirts and underride bars save lives: 2011: [VIDEO] 2013: [LINK] 2018: [LINK]"},{"id":19287289,"author":"drewrv","month":"2019-03","story":"Tesla driver again dies in crash with trailer, Autopilot not yet ruled out","stance":"reference","why":"Shows deadly car-into-truck crashes.","quote":"It freaked me out and I stay as far away as possible from semis on the road now.","text":"I was researching vehicle safety for a new car purchase a few years ago and came across this video: [VIDEO] It freaked me out and I stay as far away as possible from semis on the road now."},{"id":12027783,"author":"nostromo","month":"2016-07","story":"Tesla's 'Autopilot' Will Make Mistakes. Humans Will Overreact","stance":"reference","why":"Shows deadly underride crashes into semitrailers.","quote":"Rear ending a semi is one of the most fatal types of car crashes.","text":"> This is the immediate cause of the fatal accident involving under-running the semitrailer. Rear ending a semi is one of the most fatal types of car crashes. People should definitely not trust automatic cruise control when driving behind a trailer. And regular drivers should give themselves some extra space when driving behind a semi. [VIDEO]"}]},"cQ54GDm1eL0":{"wy":"Used as an early, widely seen example of deepfakes in discussions of fake video and its risks.","q":{"x":"It's already possible to produce fairly accurate fake video of people saying things, in their own voice","i":19169861,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":34503467,"author":"incomingpain","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What is the weirdest or most surreal (recent) technology you have seen?","stance":"reference","why":"Early deepfake example with a great message","quote":"It started off with mundane stuff with a great message","text":"Deep fakes certainly hit me wrong. Especially given how easy it is to do to celebrities. It started off with mundane stuff with a great message: [VIDEO] Then South Park guys, Trey Parker, doing absolutely hilarious deep fake video. They make Al Gore and Trump say poop. [LINK] But then basically the same time I saw deep fakes presented as true. Trump saying dumber things than normal. Biden appearing like he has late stage dementia. None of it true. As an outside observer of american politics... pretty damned concerning. But then I read a real statistic. 95% of deep fakes... are to make fake porn. Women who have never done porn now have tons of porn made of them. Worse yet, you can't do anything about it. You dont own the copyright. There is no copyright infringement. You cant sue for royalties because they assert its not you and you never did porn. So the USA/DHS decided they would try to police this. Try to get this all under control. They proceed to put a registered spy in charge of it who only policed 1 side of the aisle. So this technology is destabilizing the USA and heading directly toward... [LINK]"},{"id":20024719,"author":"_cs2017_","month":"2019-05","story":"Nancy Pelosi and Fakebook Dirty Tricks","stance":"reference","why":"Parody example that a deepfake ban might catch","quote":"Even if it means banning some parodies such as this","text":"Then what would prevent people whose videos were banned from suing Facebook because Facebook didn't get their intent correctly? And similarly, people appearing in videos that aren't banned could sue because Facebook didn't see the evil intent. Such lawsuits can be numerous and costly enough to kill the entire concept of hosting user uploaded videos. How to avoid this? Maybe it's better to drop the intent thing and just ban any videos that look real but aren't? Even if it means banning some parodies such as this [VIDEO] Even then we'll need to figure out how do it at scale, since a human reviewer will often not know if it's real. I don't think we can limit the ban to videos where you can see people's faces; you can create quite bad misinformation by showing a military boat of some country sinking a civilian ship etc."},{"id":19169861,"author":"stef25","month":"2019-02","story":"AI text generator not released for concerns about implications","stance":"reference","why":"Fairly accurate fake video of people speaking is already possible","quote":"It's already possible to produce fairly accurate fake video of people saying things, in their own voice","text":"Got news for you! It's already possible to produce fairly accurate fake video of people saying things, in their own voice [VIDEO]"},{"id":17136692,"author":"michaelbuckbee","month":"2018-05","story":"The US military is funding an effort to catch deepfakes and other AI trickery","stance":"reference","why":"Viewers on YouTube were deeply confused by it","quote":"has comments from people deeply confused by it on YT.","text":"Humans have such issues with confirmation bias [1] that I worry it's not going to matter. The last election already had algorithmically generated artificial news designed to sway people (note: I'm talking FB articles with headlines like \"$POLITICIAN just insulted $NICHE_AUDIENCE. STOP THEM.\" that linked to articles that were scraped/spun from other sources). They relied upon people not actually reading and just hitting like/forward/heart whatever and spreading the top level message. We are not going to be able to convince ourselves, never mind other people, that video (which has been the gold standard for \"truth\" for nearly a century) isn't actually real. Case in point: [VIDEO] (which was included in the article) has comments from people deeply confused by it on YT. 1 - [LINK]"},{"id":16863762,"author":"Arbalest","month":"2018-04","story":"Jordan Peele makes Obama call Trump a “complete dipshit” in PSA about AI video","stance":"reference","why":"Embedded video offered for people blocking scripts","quote":"So here is the video embedded in the article","text":"If you use a script blocker (like me) whitelisting to get the video is a pain. So here is the video embedded in the article [VIDEO] . If you're very strict on scripts and won't allow youtube to run theirs, there is always youtube-dl"},{"id":20057099,"author":"squarefoot","month":"2019-05","story":"Google – My Activity","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"What could possibly go wrong?","text":"\"They can build a full profile of me with all this data.\" What is really scary is that we already have all parts needed to build a \"real\" online person, except they're scattered around and not in the hands of a single entity. But let's say one day Google or anyone else with enough power and the same data about people buys the technology behind Lyrebird.ai and this: [VIDEO] What could possibly go wrong?"}]},"fn3KWM1kuAw":{"wy":"Used as a benchmark for robot agility, with debate over how much was choreographed or real-time.","m":[{"id":47071321,"author":"avereveard","month":"2026-02","story":"Martial arts robots dazzle at 2026 Spring Festival Gala [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier Boston Dynamics demo showing comparable skills years before","quote":"Heck check were they were 5 years ago","text":"Unfair reading bordering with propaganda. On one hand Boston Dynamics showed similar skill robot well before this demo, only without coreography, which is were most of the wow effect comes from here. [LINK] Heck check were they were 5 years ago [VIDEO] Things is american research is financed by outcome potential not for grandstanding, and free standing robot that can only do recorded coreography aren't that useful outside factory floors, and factory floors can use ceiling rails or wheels to better effect. So yeah video is suler cool, but there isn't much to it beyond that to read in terms of capabilities. You seem just to be projecting what the truth you want to be on top of a funny dance."},{"id":44912783,"author":"kohsuke","month":"2025-08","story":"AI-induced dehumanization (2024)","stance":"reference","why":"Used as stimulus showing robot's high socio-emotional capabilities in a study","quote":"","text":"So they run 5 different experiments to test the hypothesis, and they were nothing like what I imagined. For example, in one study, they divide participants into two groups, have one group watch [VIDEO] (that highlights the high socio-emotional capabilities of a robot), while the other watches [LINK] (that highlights the low socio-emotional capabilities of a robot) They are then asked if they agree or disagree with a (presumably hypothetical?) company's proposal to reduce employees' welfare, such as replacing a meal with a shake. Two groups showed a different preference. This makes me think about that old question of whether you thank LLM or not. That is treating LLMs more like humans, so if what this paper found holds, maybe that'd nudge our brain subtly toward dehumanizing other real humans!? That's so counter intuitive..."},{"id":25638324,"author":"chrisbentzel","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (January 2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Boston Dynamics showcasing coordinated dancing robots in a hiring post","quote":"We're pushing the boundaries of what robots can do, including coordinated dancing","text":"Boston Dynamics | Multiple Software Roles | Boston, Bay Area, and remote for some roles| Full-Time | [LINK] Boston Dynamics is a leading developer of mobile robots. We're pushing the boundaries of what robots can do, including coordinated dancing - [VIDEO] . We're focused on making our robots useful in the real world, such as for safe decommissioning of Chernobyl - [LINK] . We are hiring for many roles - [LINK] Software oriented roles include Full-Stack and Frontend Engineers, Controls and Computer Vision Engineers, SQA Manager, and SWE roles for people with networking and systems experience. Apply at [LINK]"},{"id":25737708,"author":"dblotsky","month":"2021-01","story":"Predictions Scorecard, 2021 January 01","stance":"reference","why":"Dancing robot as lab test of navigating a home","quote":"","text":"Aren't these effectively lab tests of robots that can navigate a home? [dancing] [VIDEO] [cleaning clutter] [LINK]"},{"id":25574199,"author":"kbaker","month":"2020-12","story":"Do You Love Me? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Pointing to a segment suggesting real-time footage","quote":"Looks pretty much real time.","text":"I don't know... watch the people walking in around 1:00 in the top left. Looks pretty much real time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36120057,"author":"rglover","month":"2023-05","story":"Paper Money Diehards Refuse to Fold","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I shared the Boston Dynamics thing in jest","text":"Not that you'll view it as valid but I'm using abductive logic. I shared the Boston Dynamics thing in jest, but I'd love to hear valid reasons for the existence of a bipedal robot that can run and scale over objects. Are these things being used to down dissidents a reality today? No, but it's a perfectly logical conclusion for what's down the pike. FWIW, too, the \"tinfoil hat\" thing doesn't work on me. I will smugly tighten mine as I look down upon the naive proletariat being manhandled as \"Do You Love Me\" [1] is played over the crowd control loudspeakers. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28192374,"author":"sxp","month":"2021-08","story":"OpenAI's Codex sure knows a lot about HN [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"still looks fake to me because it's at the level that I would expect to see from Hollywood CGI","text":"The skepticism is warranted for any bleeding edge technology. I wonder if there's another version of a Turing test when a technology can be considered sufficiently advanced when it's indistinguishable from a fake version you've seen in sci-fi. E.g, the Boston Dynamics' dancing robot video ( [VIDEO] ) still looks fake to me because it's at the level that I would expect to see from Hollywood CGI rather than a real tech demo. If I saw the video anywhere else but on the BD page, I would have enjoyed it and forgotten about it since it's an average CGI video."},{"id":25573840,"author":"gene-h","month":"2020-12","story":"Do You Love Me? [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it must also be said that these are preprogrammed maneuvers on a closed course","text":"Looks like Atlas may have had an accident during filming, there's a hydraulic fluid stain on the floor: [VIDEO] And it must also be said that these are preprogrammed maneuvers on a closed course..."}]},"n5QRgpjfarY":{"wy":"Cited as a shorthand example of a tone-deaf corporate response, especially around Blizzard and mobile gaming.","m":[{"id":19872372,"author":"Fnoord","month":"2019-05","story":"U.S. adults are spending big on video games, playing mostly on smartphones","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ah, suddenly \"You guys all have phones right?\" [1] makes sense. Its about \"follow the money\". [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19136472,"author":"duskwuff","month":"2019-02","story":"Activision Blizzard staff reportedly bracing for layoffs","stance":"reference","why":"Blizzard rep's 'do you guys not have phones' remark at BlizzCon","quote":"A Blizzard representative literally said \"Do you guys not have phones?\" during a Q&A session","text":"> The don't you guys have phones meme is direct response to the Blizzcon announcement that the next Diablo was going to be a mobile game not even produced by Blizzard. I mean, it isn't just a meme. A Blizzard representative literally said \"Do you guys not have phones?\" during a Q&A session, after someone asked whether the new Diablo game would be available on PC. [VIDEO] Fans were going to be disappointed either way, but this dismissive response is what really set off the reaction."},{"id":27115286,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2021-05","story":"eBPF on Windows","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It feels like that Blizzard developer Wyatt Cheng that got on stage and asked the PC gamer crowd: \"Do you guys not have phones?\" [VIDEO] The Windows Terminal guys are similar. \"Do you have any plans at all to make this work for users of Windows?\" \"No.\""}]},"nifUBDgPhl4":{"wy":"Shared as a fun example that Bach wrote secular, humorous music, usually in coffee threads.","m":[{"id":41036523,"author":"lanstin","month":"2024-07","story":"Caffeine suppresses cerebral grey matter responses to chronic sleep restriction","stance":"reference","why":"Bach cantata about a dad and coffee-drinking daughter","quote":"A little Bach Cantata about a grumpy old dad and his coffee drinking daughter","text":"On a cultural note regarding the threads, the moralistic make do with what god gives you side and the yay drink coffee and do stuff and be happy side have been around a long time. A little Bach Cantata about a grumpy old dad and his coffee drinking daughter: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39431909,"author":"MengerSponge","month":"2024-02","story":"Bach’s Cantata Calendar","stance":"reference","why":"Example of secular Bach cantata","quote":"They aren't all church-y though! The Coffee Cantata, for example","text":"The style has endured mostly in sacred music, so I get the association. They aren't all church-y though! The Coffee Cantata, for example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31941976,"author":"tzs","month":"2022-07","story":"The popularity of country music in rural sub Saharan Africa","stance":"reference","why":"Baroque cantata about coffee addiction with English subtitles","quote":"(Turn on closed captioning to see an English translation)","text":"What do you mean modern? And American? Such music has a long history. For instance Mozart's \"Leck mich im Arsch\" from 1782 [1]. The title is German for \"Lick me in the ass\". Or Bach's cantata \"Schweigt stille, plaudert nicht\" (German for \"Be still, stop chattering\") which glorifies addiction to coffee [2]. (Turn on closed captioning to see an English translation). [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"tNfGyIW7aHM":{"wy":"Used as a joke reply in threads with 'how to' titles, not as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":48438610,"author":"1123581321","month":"2026-06","story":"The gamers taking on the industry to stop it switching off games","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Just write a regulation that every game developer has to make a great game, not charge too much, support it for years, and give it away for free as soon as it's not popular. That way, we'll only have great games and archives of great free games. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37983465,"author":"harry8","month":"2023-10","story":"How to Make a CPU – A Simple Picture Based Explanation","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Definitely a worthwhile minute out of your day.","text":"Monty Python's \"Today on how to do it\" sketch is the classic of the genre maybe? Definitely a worthwhile minute out of your day. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12777688,"author":"huxley","month":"2016-10","story":"Building a Shop with Sub-Second Page Loads: Lessons Learned","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Monty Python's \"How to Do It\" skit: [VIDEO] \"... this week on 'How to Do It' we're going to learn how to play the flute, how to split the atom, how to construct box girder bridges and how to irrigate the Sahara and make vast new areas cultivatable, but first, here's Jackie to tell you how to rid the world of all known diseases.\" How to play the flute: \"Now, how to play the flute. (picking up a flute) Well you blow in one end and move your fingers up and down the outside.\""},{"id":7360209,"author":"brudgers","month":"2014-03","story":"How to debug small programs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"If I had stopped reading after a couple of paragraphs, I would not have been aware the article contained any \"actionable advice.\" Likewise if I had stopped after 3 paragraphs or four or even five. It is only after six paragraphs of putting the student in their place - instead of just linking to StackOverflow's own prose describing good questions - that \"First, turn on all compiler warnings\" makes an appearance. Is this good advice? Not really. Manipulating compiler setting is a homework task in itself for a student. What could be simpler than setting compiler warnings? It's all right here: [LINK] However, there's a bit of a logical error in this method. [0] Apparently what a student is supposed to do after turning them on is return to stackOverflow and ask another question. Well at least the author recognizes that turning on compiler warnings isn't really progress toward debugging except in the sense that debugging consists of asking questions on StackOverflow. Yet in fairness, it is hard to call this bad advice to a student struggling to finish an assignment given the next piece of \"helpful advice\" is: \"If your program still has a bug, obtain a rubber duck.\" The reason I struggle with this being better or worse than the first piece of advice is that at least rubber ducks don't make sport of trolling students with homework questions. [1] According to the article, obtaining a rubber duck will allow the student's code to compile. All that's left is to refactor, write some tests"},{"id":1682553,"author":"fondue","month":"2010-09","story":"How to Become a Programmer - wikiHow","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"What a terrific article! I found a video on how to play the flute, split an atom, how to construct a box girder bridge, and how to irrigate the Sahara desert and create vast areas of viable farmland, and finally how to cure all known diseases. [VIDEO]"}]},"uw8MACDZ3RI":{"wy":"HN uses it as one of many clips documenting San Francisco's decline, and some commenters criticize it as pushing a point of view.","m":[{"id":31710397,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2022-06","story":"Downtown S.F. on the brink: It’s worse than it looks","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among many clips documenting San Francisco decline","quote":"There are probably hundreds of these clips.","text":"CBS News. San Francisco Luxury Condo Overlooks City's Worst Squalor: [LINK] CBS News. KPIX Special Report: San Francisco's Tenderloin – A State of Emergency [LINK] ABC News. Here's why one of San Francisco's top officers says crime is here to stay [LINK] KRON 4. 70% of San Francisco residents say quality of life has declined: poll [LINK] Christopher Rufo. Chaos by the Bay: The Truth About Homelessness in San Francisco [VIDEO] NBC News. Saving San Francisco: Ep. 5 'Why Should I Live in Fear?' [LINK] There are probably hundreds of these clips. I am sure you can also dig up reasons why the DA was recalled. 850k people are unhappy. 40% of the residents want to leave: [LINK] When close to half of the population of a city wants to leave, probably a good idea to accept what's wrong with the city and try to look inward."},{"id":24184749,"author":"justchilly","month":"2020-08","story":"Remote work is reshaping San Francisco, as tech workers flee and rents fall","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with supervisor used as evidence of housing obstruction","quote":"Starting at 7:41 in this video an interview with the same supervisor.","text":"Example: Hillary Ronin is the supervisor for the mission. An area that was historically lower income minorities, now being displaced, in part due to a lack of housing units. She has fought for years to stop the construction of buildings that would add both affordable units and market rate units. \"Ronen fought to prevent the construction of a 75-unit building on the site of a laundromat. She argued that an environmental review of the building did not consider the impact of a shadow on a nearby schoolyard, even though an environmental review conducted by officials at the San Francisco Planning Department showed that the new construction, including its shadow, would not have an adverse impact on children at the schoolyard.]\" [LINK] Starting at 7:41 in this video an interview with the same supervisor. [VIDEO] The tax breaks that she is referring to were roughly $10mm/year. [LINK] The city's budget increased by nearly $6bn during the time the tax breaks were active (largely fueled by the growth from tech boom)."},{"id":34591225,"author":"rektide","month":"2023-01","story":"Chaos by the Bay: The Truth About Homelessness in San Francisco","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this video seems to be trying to make a point about the city, about it's failure","text":"There's an interesting counterplay of narration vs content going on when the video starts quoting the city. It starts showing (recalled) District Attorney Chesa Boudin speaking[1]: > The movement we built must continue to grow. It must demand that San Francisco make it easier to get help than it is to get high on our streets. Then overdubbing Chesea talking, it says: > Under the leadership of District Attorney Chesa Boudin, the city has released nearly half of all it's prisoners from the county jail and decriminalized tent camping, drug use, prosititution, and public defecation. Returning to Chesea's audio, > It must demand that we stop using the jail as the primary place to treat people suffering from mental illness. This is not my city & I don't live anywhere near here. It seems like a bad situation. I can have sympathy for what seems like a really bad situation. But this video seems to be trying to make a point about the city, about it's failure, and while I can agree that what's happening isn't working, while I can agree this seems terrible, I agree with Chesa Boudin here 100%. The counter-narration atop him, trying to shade him & whatever is afoot here, does not resonate. Jails are not the place. That said, we did just come from a segment that I do find some affinity for, a man who had lived on the streets as an addict, talking about how homelessness is not a crime, drug use is not a crime, but you are responsible for your actions & what you do. Eric Adams, NYC mayor, wa"}]},"vAEU-Lf60LA":{"wy":"Cited in Max Headroom threads as an example of the show's visual style being copied in film.","m":[{"id":41512245,"author":"tempaway4575144","month":"2024-09","story":"Max Headroom and the World of Pseudo-CGI (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"And the Max Headroom style was notably copied for the Back To The Future 2","text":"And the Max Headroom style was notably copied for the Back To The Future 2 \"80s Cafe\" scene, with Max Headroom style version of Reagan, Michael Jackson and Ayatollah Khomeini. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29327323,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2021-11","story":"The Max Headroom Incident","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Back To The Future - Cafe 80`s","text":"The Art of Noise with Max Headroom - Paranoimia (Official Video) [LINK] Coca-Cola Max Headroom | Max Headroom Coke commercials [LINK] Max Headroom on Sesame Street [LINK] The Headroom Collection on Letterman, 1986-1990 [LINK] Terry Wogan interviews Max Headroom [LINK] On Max Headroom: The Most Misunderstood Joke on TV [LINK] Back To The Future - Cafe 80`s [VIDEO] Altered Carbon Carnage: Who is Carnage? Who is Matt Frewer who plays Carnage? [LINK] Star Trek the Next Generation: A Matter Of Time [LINK] Matt Frewer Interview: Doctor Doctor, Max Headroom, Altered Carbon: Carnage, Orphan Black, Eureka [LINK]"},{"id":21613488,"author":"UncleSlacky","month":"2019-11","story":"Max Headroom broadcast signal intrusion","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"scene in Back to the Future II with Reagan, Michael Jackson and Ayatollah Khomeini","text":"The same effect was used in the \"80s Café\" scene in Back to the Future II with Reagan, Michael Jackson and Ayatollah Khomeini: [VIDEO]"}]}});
